rue] [17592186192584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257684 13194139533864 true] [17592186192584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257694 13194139533864 true] [17592186192584 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258907 13194139533864 true] [17592186192603 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257085 13194139533864 true] [17592186192603 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257664 13194139533864 true] [17592186192604 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257704 13194139533864 true] [17592186192604 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258897 13194139533864 true] [17592186192641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257583 13194139533864 true] [17592186192641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257586 13194139533864 true] [17592186198246 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258744 13194139533864 true] [17592186198246 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258747 13194139533864 true] [17592186198378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257844 13194139533864 true] [17592186203956 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258048 13194139533864 true] [17592186216973 1020 #edb/ref 17592186258261 13194139533864 true] [17592186217100 1020 #edb/ref 17592186257421 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256430 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1089 "q-10018" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1100 "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $9+0.9+0.09+0.009+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1113 #uuid "ccfa9d31-45ab-5f64-a4e2-3d1fa5018f4c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256429 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=9$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{0.9}{9}=0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1-0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{0.9} \\\\  & =\\frac{90}{9} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256431 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256431 13194139533864 true] [17592186256430 1110 #uuid "486b2df1-d1b4-528c-b25e-61fee88dc812" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256431 1034 "10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256431 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256431 1111 #uuid "110c7289-9e87-588c-8839-082ce62fa8b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256433 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1089 "q-100235" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1113 #uuid "d37a6373-85f7-5021-93bf-bde115d91e8c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256432 1145 "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(x-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(x-2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(-1)^{4}+4)^{2}}{(-1-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{-27} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256434 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256434 13194139533864 true] [17592186256433 1110 #uuid "baff0e2d-9b61-5780-9004-7951a0b3c029" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256434 1034 "-\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256434 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256434 1111 #uuid "3d496f36-d9ee-5b26-b44c-c1968857daf9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256436 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1089 "q-100236" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1100 "Find ${lim}_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1113 #uuid "91317a16-9fb5-5d43-9550-4a0b2404efdf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256435 1145 "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3) & =({lim}_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2)^{3})\\cdot ({lim}_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & ={({lim}_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2))}^{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & =((-2)^{3}+2)^{3}\\cdot (2\\cdot (-2)+3) \\\\  & =(-6)^{3}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =216.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256437 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256437 13194139533864 true] [17592186256436 1110 #uuid "32124313-f85d-5185-9fc8-c729515e34da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256437 1034 "216" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256437 1111 #uuid "b4271776-2565-508b-a4c0-05856a0d5c1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256439 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1089 "q-100264" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1113 #uuid "855a5e48-e137-5598-94f7-f4832740f0f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256438 1145 "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{({lim}_{x\\to 4}({x}^{2}-3x-5))}^{5}}-\\sqrt{{({lim}_{x\\to 4}(2x-7))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{({4}^{2}-3(4)-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2(4)-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(16-12-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(8-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-1}-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256440 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256440 13194139533864 true] [17592186256439 1110 #uuid "d92060c6-8853-5b65-a4a6-b4eb8d49cf80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256440 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256440 1111 #uuid "2e7f0189-8bdb-58cf-8aaa-952657ad5d46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256442 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1089 "q-100273" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fda731fa3c9a3401e9b9e2a7d4c103829d3fe3b14ac9d6f2872b76a319f6ae91.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1113 #uuid "c3c8ab44-204b-5d29-bcb7-af809bc21a89" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256441 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{3}-4(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256443 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256443 13194139533864 true] [17592186256442 1110 #uuid "540fe2c8-dadb-5db3-8dab-b24c95763f08" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256443 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256443 1111 #uuid "d5498add-bc06-5903-9efe-ef81027091af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256445 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1089 "q-100275" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e8aac08a40b3a318cb682afca1783a8d9563bf67f0b51bdff2ead8a9bf28117c.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1113 #uuid "beb0d5cc-39b2-586c-83f5-8cf543207207" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256444 1145 "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}={lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2} \\\\  & ={((-2)^{3}+4)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(-8+4)}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-2(2))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-4)}^{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2}{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & =16\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256446 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256446 13194139533864 true] [17592186256445 1110 #uuid "99476705-d581-5b54-a13c-b34652061ee2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256446 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256446 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256446 1111 #uuid "7d6a2bcd-3314-5ee1-8472-b7a09d44d3db" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256448 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1089 "q-100277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ddf4b2d129022e27627afa1b0946f18ef7e98ab2ffe20dea6d609bedce65f84d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1113 #uuid "d37402e0-45e5-5794-8d62-3744bcf93a6d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256447 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-3(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(3(2)-4(1))^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =-32\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256448 1110 #uuid "5df85a12-1e10-5d7f-ad90-818a82b74222" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256449 1034 "-32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256449 1111 #uuid "e708382f-3705-58a5-9a18-333922210b38" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256451 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1089 "q-100279" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a4f8b046dd85d258dd897e595b5dba1116e5df763e2e74a196c6b79e2a8a42a5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1113 #uuid "e66e2dc4-df2c-55ad-b6a2-790f10160f6d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256450 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-1)^{3} \\\\  & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2}))}^{5} \\\\  & =(3-4(1)^{2})^{5} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256452 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256452 13194139533864 true] [17592186256451 1110 #uuid "1a8505c7-dc76-5a01-b1fb-0b42c653f484" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256452 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256452 1111 #uuid "41725411-aa61-591c-bcf8-2a9591aba381" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1089 "q-100309" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b9a95898f98ad5289313a0f0fdea24fa8828d508564c8ce60cec986919f16f50.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)+1}{\\sqrt{f(x)+3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1113 #uuid "8e227243-5897-55da-a196-c0ae80dd04bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256453 1145 "From the graph, we get that  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nApplying the root rule, we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)+1}{\\sqrt{f(x)+3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)+1)}{\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)+1}{\\sqrt{(1)+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256455 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256455 13194139533864 true] [17592186256454 1110 #uuid "f09d06c1-b038-5f3e-9fc6-ebed92785b51" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256455 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256455 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256455 1111 #uuid "c2ba61a5-7ca0-5c0e-ad58-80bf00371d48" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256457 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1089 "q-100459" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1100 "If ${216}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{36})}^{6a-2}$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1113 #uuid "50d2f9b4-0b9f-53dd-8422-aae20f0d13ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256456 1145 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $216={6}^{3}$ and $36={6}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({6}^{3})}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}})}^{6a-2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2(6a-2)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}={6}^{-2(6a-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(3a+4) & =-2(6a-2) \\\\ 9a+12 & =-12a+4 \\\\ 21a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{8}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256458 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256458 13194139533864 true] [17592186256457 1110 #uuid "d70aba23-4e6c-5410-8e82-4fd86d1e5e21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256458 1034 "-\\frac{8}{21}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256458 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256458 1111 #uuid "bd368582-0175-5f86-aa74-163e6a090acf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256460 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1089 "q-100627" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1100 "If $-7{log}_{3}(x-26)=-42$, then $x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1113 #uuid "fb813bd0-6d03-5ce7-aeed-ba5a66fe6313" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256459 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{log}_{3}(x-26) & =-42 \\\\ {log}_{3}(x-26) & =\\frac{-42}{-7} \\\\ {log}_{3}(x-26) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(x-26) & =6 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}(x-26)} & ={3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}(x-26)}=x-26.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-26 & ={3}^{6} \\\\ x-26 & =729 \\\\ x & =755.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256461 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256461 13194139533864 true] [17592186256460 1110 #uuid "681993c1-a32e-56ef-8e99-9cd83ba96239" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256461 1034 "755" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256461 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256461 1111 #uuid "42bf02f2-51de-5cf2-b795-a860c928c6fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1089 "q-100631" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1100 "If ${log}_{8}x+2{log}_{8}x=2$, then what is the value of  $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1113 #uuid "64cb2061-188e-5a28-a695-11fcdda45850" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256462 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}x+2{log}_{8}x & =2 \\\\ (1+2){log}_{8}x & =2 \\\\ 3{log}_{8}x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{8}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $8,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}x & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {8}^{{log}_{8}x} & ={8}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${8}^{{log}_{8}x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={8}^{2/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256463 1110 #uuid "b23ee9cc-4294-5159-9632-fc1db62d39ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256464 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256464 1111 #uuid "07010dc9-1e29-58cf-80ff-96a56d9fba1d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256466 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1089 "q-100673" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{18}(41)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1113 #uuid "51214ed3-9217-5f35-8c62-58f93e8509fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256465 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{18}(41)$ by changing the base $18$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=18,$ $X=41,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{18}(41) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(41)}{{log}_{10}(18)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(41)}{\\operatorname{log}(18)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.612\\,784}{1.255\\,273} \\\\  & \\approx 1.285\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256467 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256467 13194139533864 true] [17592186256466 1110 #uuid "f19c826c-626c-5bc4-83fd-030e4431b402" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256467 1034 "1.285" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256467 1111 #uuid "c3dbb7fe-750e-5953-9a22-6daaaa63fb4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256469 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1089 "q-100674" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{15}(10)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1113 #uuid "70c8405e-c25e-581b-907c-1bd10f59fc85" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256468 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{15}(10)$ by changing the base $15$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=15,$ $X=10,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{15}(10) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(10)}{{log}_{10}(15)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(10)}{\\operatorname{log}(15)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1}{1.176\\,091} \\\\  & \\approx 0.850\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256470 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256470 13194139533864 true] [17592186256469 1110 #uuid "2da1e781-a4dc-50ed-92d1-c932089c7436" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256470 1034 "0.850" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256470 1111 #uuid "53710983-408a-5a6c-90ea-b34ff38359ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256472 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1089 "q-100675" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}(30)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1113 #uuid "c2f720ea-4efd-5c38-87f6-787d4de5f50b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256471 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}(30)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=30,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}(30) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(30)}{{log}_{10}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(30)}{\\operatorname{log}(8)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.477\\,12}{0.903\\,09} \\\\  & \\approx 1.64\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256473 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256473 13194139533864 true] [17592186256472 1110 #uuid "722b0dfa-4a0b-5cc5-9de6-df174301443b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256473 1034 "1.64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256473 1111 #uuid "28c51bc3-a49c-557b-86c0-c22af227fbd2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256475 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1089 "q-100676" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{22}(20)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1113 #uuid "2c2c3dd1-20e9-5368-9ec4-088bb43cb692" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256474 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{22}(20)$ by changing the base $22$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=22,$ $X=20,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{22}(20) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(20)}{{log}_{10}(22)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(20)}{\\operatorname{log}(22)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.301\\,030}{1.342\\,423} \\\\  & \\approx 0.969\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256476 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256476 13194139533864 true] [17592186256475 1110 #uuid "601db05c-084b-5144-a803-3fc02ba6ccb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256476 1034 "0.969" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256476 1111 #uuid "28a98f76-933b-551d-9eab-cfe391420e07" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256478 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1089 "q-100706" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1100 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1113 #uuid "b2471a67-c72a-575a-8f10-13bf4266225f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256477 1145 "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256479 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256479 13194139533864 true] [17592186256478 1110 #uuid "632752f1-0d8d-5dfe-b7aa-4366797c3958" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256479 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256479 1111 #uuid "a1d337be-4dbd-5c73-afde-d1f2e603c6c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256481 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1089 "q-100771" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1100 "Solve the equation ${log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36)={log}_{5}(-16v-28).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1113 #uuid "501f700f-e881-502f-a49c-ed376789d61f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256480 1145 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & ={log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {v}^{2}+36 & =-16v-28 \\\\ {v}^{2}+16v+64 & =0 \\\\ (v+8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}((-8)^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16(-8)-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}(100) & ={log}_{5}(100).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-8$ is a valid solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256482 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256482 13194139533864 true] [17592186256481 1110 #uuid "2552e629-ed36-5d2e-a592-f43ee66159f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256482 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256482 1111 #uuid "23c87e17-e068-58e6-b62b-35ecceb2fd6a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256484 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256486 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256488 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1089 "q-100989" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1100 "If $a=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=j-3k$, then $a-3b=$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 3, column 1: {{r3c1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1113 #uuid "ca9737e4-6571-57db-8572-7d99e7234d46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256483 1145 "First, notice that\n\n$$\nb=j-3k=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute $a-3b,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-3b & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =⟨-6,-8,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256485 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256485 13194139533864 true] [17592186256484 1110 #uuid "a47fbca2-eeb1-52f3-9480-f2d0f75bab35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256485 1034 "-6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256485 1111 #uuid "4572ec97-bfa4-5060-b8f4-cbf12f7c3e97" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256487 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256487 13194139533864 true] [17592186256486 1110 #uuid "ccd71f6f-8015-5fb7-b775-c33622baa306" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256487 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256487 1111 #uuid "ff6aa3ec-8276-54a8-b2c9-eaa021198cd3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256489 13194139533864 true] [17592186256488 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256488 1041 "r3c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256488 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256489 13194139533864 true] [17592186256488 1110 #uuid "6ceb37b1-8699-5b9c-9eba-dc3db48f29bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256489 1034 "11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256489 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256489 1111 #uuid "4e2c1459-2e82-5849-8376-a3f7512617f0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256491 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1089 "q-101283" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1113 #uuid "dcdef879-b89d-50b8-80c4-887502ec74b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256490 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4(3)-3}-3}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}-3}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{4x-3}-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{4x-3})^{2}-{3}^{2}}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x-3-9}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x-12}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4(x-3)}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4x-3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4(3)-3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256492 13194139533864 true] [17592186256491 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256491 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256491 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256492 13194139533864 true] [17592186256491 1110 #uuid "4b02b9b9-c5e3-5283-8cd8-754b72a740d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256492 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256492 1111 #uuid "d13820a1-ba4a-5136-8fb8-a1221aba9da4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256494 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1089 "q-101287" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1113 #uuid "36d75191-edf3-543e-8a14-a081f1ebcbf0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256493 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5(2)+6}-4}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}-4}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{5x+6}-4)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{5x+6})^{2}-{4}^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5x+6-16}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5x-10}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5x+6}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5(2)+6}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256494 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256495 13194139533864 true] [17592186256494 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256494 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256494 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256495 13194139533864 true] [17592186256494 1110 #uuid "ca6a3251-a073-53a9-83e3-b4e5a74fd2c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256495 1034 "\\frac{5}{8}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256495 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256495 1111 #uuid "3e12db33-cf16-5171-9f31-62a450f113d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256497 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1089 "q-101289" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1113 #uuid "e2dca2c7-e791-5c46-b7d1-0efafbd3a36b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256496 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3(0)+4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{3x+4}-2)(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{3x+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{3x+4-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{3x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x+4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(0)+4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256498 13194139533864 true] [17592186256497 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256497 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256497 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256498 13194139533864 true] [17592186256497 1110 #uuid "a9343017-3844-5b91-8ba3-a792c44abc57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256498 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256498 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256498 1111 #uuid "3605469f-e409-5a67-b3c3-c8fb261b444b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256500 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1089 "q-101625" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1100 "If $p+2=3(p-3)+6,$ then $p=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1113 #uuid "486b29b8-ce2e-59f6-a2b3-65c777f54464" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256499 1145 "First, we expand the parentheses on the right-hand-side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =3(p-3)+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3\\cdot p-3\\cdot 3+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3p-9+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must now bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $p$ on the left-hand side and $3p$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side. \n\nThe first step is to remove the $p$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $p$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =3p-3 \\\\ p+2-p & =3p-3-p \\\\ 2 & =2p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-3$ from the right-hand side. We do this by adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2p-3 \\\\ 2+3 & =2p-3+3 \\\\ 5 & =2p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{2p}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}p}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256500 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256501 13194139533864 true] [17592186256500 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256500 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256500 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256501 13194139533864 true] [17592186256500 1110 #uuid "12ffd4e0-bfb9-5b97-9e69-8e43ad3307ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256501 1111 #uuid "4de44592-363f-53b0-9452-63f02463612d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256503 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1089 "q-101719" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1100 "Given that $y=7-4{log}_{3}x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1113 #uuid "5712e498-177c-5ff5-9270-29807a04505c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256502 1145 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-4{log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =0-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} & ={(-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3})\\,|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256503 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256504 13194139533864 true] [17592186256503 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256503 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256503 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256504 13194139533864 true] [17592186256503 1110 #uuid "daadac8d-e9fa-50ed-9624-0e832b2c29a3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256504 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256504 1111 #uuid "e6ece883-5457-512b-817a-6a540f5cafb4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256505 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256506 13194139533864 true] [17592186256505 1089 "q-101730" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256505 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256505 1100 "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,5]$ is $-1,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,4p+1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,p+6\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256505 1113 #uuid "a9ca4c60-8351-5109-8b21-60704b041116" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256505 1145 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(-5)}{5-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(p+6)-(4p+1)}{5+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-3p}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=-1$ on the interval $[-5,5].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{5-3p}{10}=-1.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-3p}{10} & =-1 \\\\ 5-3p & =-10 \\\\ -3p & =-10-5 \\\\ -3p & =-15 \\\\ p & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256507 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256507 13194139533864 true] [17592186256506 1110 #uuid "accec121-d526-5711-937f-a3434f20da60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256507 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256507 1111 #uuid "92f610ab-f996-522b-87af-a4586c3c9d90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256509 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1089 "q-102077" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e495695d9def7518780fb2b9e44f11e890da4c70c2dff7c6525936b2684219e1.png)\n\nGiven the sets $S={8,10,11}$ and $T={10,12,15},$ find $S\\cap T.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1113 #uuid "fde102b2-05fb-5f0b-a8aa-3b7a81681d73" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256508 1145 "The intersection $S\\cap T$ consists of all the elements that are in both $S$ and $T.$ To represent $S\\cap T,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $S$ and $T.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5f807aaf7a026cc52f53f4a5f50afab269da5f2dfdcb835f6166891ada864f72.png)\n\nOnly the element $10$ is in both $S$ and $T.$ Therefore, the intersection of $S$ and $T$ is\n\n$$\nS\\cap T={10}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256510 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256510 13194139533864 true] [17592186256509 1110 #uuid "4623e151-7175-5517-b3ff-7afdb59a23b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256510 1034 "{10}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256510 1111 #uuid "074fa524-3867-542a-b63f-1113e37be26d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256512 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1089 "q-102106" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d9cf9a5c61cbf1796cf6682b129b4c05171c364c7b34e7cce176ba27e9c9cb24.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-1$\nII. - $x=2$\nIII. - $x=4$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1113 #uuid "6b31ab2b-c4c5-5adb-93e3-725f33b127ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256511 1145 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/95967eb82512aae261936a6c09e2f474c72c9a130d3ecb527f62408822d88c96.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256513 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256514 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256515 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256516 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256517 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256518 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256519 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256520 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256518 13194139533864 true] [17592186256512 1110 #uuid "cbf8d454-a5e4-5c07-b361-eea7a481c457" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256513 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256513 1111 #uuid "ce416271-d959-5f29-a613-74527db0fb36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256514 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256514 1111 #uuid "8803f1cd-97ad-5de2-a183-cf168dc86ebb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256515 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256515 1111 #uuid "a1943369-4326-5ce8-828f-6f497c0387c8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256516 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256516 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256516 1111 #uuid "1a6b88e9-537f-5014-904c-2f3c5e74a690" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256517 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256517 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256517 1111 #uuid "0fe4a6d4-d9a3-509d-a10f-a48daed15eb4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256518 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256518 1111 #uuid "3d948c4c-a856-5a84-a62a-0e142b8c5423" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256519 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256519 1111 #uuid "8b0aa4ce-2595-57a5-9575-ac87a8f8dd0a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256520 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256520 1111 #uuid "e969ed7d-a030-5bbe-b5ff-e06b6ecf3ffc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256522 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1089 "q-102613" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1100 "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=3$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1113 #uuid "0cb34dbc-b9db-5b93-9599-5e4c5f547a2f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256521 1145 "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{10(3+h)^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{90+60h+10{h}^{2}-90}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{60h+10{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(60+10h) \\\\  & =60+(0) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3) & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10{x}^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10({x}^{2}-{3}^{2})}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}10(x+3) \\\\  & =10(3+3) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256523 13194139533864 true] [17592186256522 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256522 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256522 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256523 13194139533864 true] [17592186256522 1110 #uuid "1ec0a6c9-bc80-514c-9741-89967e14c3d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256523 1034 "60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256523 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256523 1111 #uuid "82be2e6e-182f-52e7-b27f-4339541b6bf8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256525 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1089 "q-102961" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. - The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\nII. - $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\nIII. - The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1113 #uuid "02ff78bf-c744-52ed-b02f-687ebfd84d9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256524 1145 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the domain of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false since $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false since $f(-1)=\\sqrt[3]{-1}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256526 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256527 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256528 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256529 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256530 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256531 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256532 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256533 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256529 13194139533864 true] [17592186256525 1110 #uuid "ec2ed87d-8da5-5cf0-924f-c476dada74aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256526 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256526 1111 #uuid "c3adeb09-5140-5fca-9700-c994dedc9c7e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256527 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256527 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256527 1111 #uuid "48683ef0-cb9a-5a42-a5dc-8c5f4b4180fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256528 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256528 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256528 1111 #uuid "8e191c58-93fc-5a21-8269-2e823b43a26e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256529 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256529 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256529 1111 #uuid "1fab48ab-c6dd-5c37-b4c3-b974a79cd885" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256530 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256530 1111 #uuid "1a378210-8ecf-5ba8-af29-1ab0d287301c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256531 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256531 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256531 1111 #uuid "e2129c7c-0469-5c30-af1b-889b4fad5941" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256532 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256532 1111 #uuid "05eb3693-4523-5ff9-ae6d-14f814a75511" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256533 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256533 1111 #uuid "6564a1f4-1e4f-5275-86da-a58ccba15532" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256535 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256537 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1089 "q-10318" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1100 "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $-15{x}^{2}+kx-60=0$  has one distinct real solution.\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1113 #uuid "a8ca7739-ac12-5a8f-a94e-0d532b4daccf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256534 1145 "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=-15,$ $b=k,$ and $c=-60,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(k)^{2}-4(-15)(-60) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-3\\,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3600 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =3600 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{3600} \\\\ k & =\\pm 60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\pm 60.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256536 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256536 13194139533864 true] [17592186256535 1110 #uuid "cb5ec21e-e3fb-5152-965f-9a7b693fb214" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256536 1034 "-60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256536 1111 #uuid "aa8219dd-5426-535a-8746-a20de862801e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256537 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256538 13194139533864 true] [17592186256537 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256537 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256537 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256538 13194139533864 true] [17592186256537 1110 #uuid "17516546-529b-517f-a2ff-3650e714700d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256538 1034 "60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256538 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256538 1111 #uuid "c77f485b-642c-5f6c-a6a5-59814eca6ecb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256540 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1089 "q-103216" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1100 "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=-2$ and ${a}_{2}=0,$ then ${a}_{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1113 #uuid "9cc04cfe-5c1c-58c0-b534-2ea8f4897bac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256539 1145 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =0+(-2) \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256540 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256541 13194139533864 true] [17592186256540 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256540 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256540 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256541 13194139533864 true] [17592186256540 1110 #uuid "4a58f9f1-7f33-5634-9a0d-d31653864f38" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256541 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256541 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256541 1111 #uuid "36d47bf5-d562-56c1-981f-81ce1bfb0996" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256543 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1089 "q-103217" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1100 "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=4$ and ${a}_{2}=5,$ then ${a}_{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1113 #uuid "d61383ab-6ca0-5c21-90fc-da34d0ca7d99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256542 1145 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4+5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256544 13194139533864 true] [17592186256543 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256543 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256543 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256544 13194139533864 true] [17592186256543 1110 #uuid "7c87d266-0ee7-554e-9da5-6db1aebe77e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256544 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256544 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256544 1111 #uuid "8ffa1a3f-0c84-5504-a5b3-3da85ca9f1c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256546 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1089 "q-103222" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1100 "What is the fourth term of the following sequence?\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)\\,,\\,f(1)=-2\\,,\\,f(2)=0,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1113 #uuid "0798bf8d-4438-5b94-a6a0-6a186fe4b314" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256545 1145 "The fourth term is represented by $f(4).$\n\nTo compute $f(4),$ we first need to compute $f(3).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+2) & =f(n+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =0+(-2) \\\\ f(3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =-2+0 \\\\ f(4) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is $f(4)=-2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256546 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256547 13194139533864 true] [17592186256546 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256546 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256546 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256547 13194139533864 true] [17592186256546 1110 #uuid "309ffe6e-1a0d-5db1-8e6e-7d5c01c645e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256547 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256547 1111 #uuid "bbb57b6c-f1e6-5978-b142-a098b7bb0037" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256549 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1089 "q-103223" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1100 "If $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=-1$ and $f(2)=1,$ then $f(3)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1113 #uuid "22ea45b9-f009-53ac-920f-698b600ba579" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256548 1145 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =1+(-1) \\\\ f(3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(3)=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256549 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256550 13194139533864 true] [17592186256549 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256549 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256549 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256550 13194139533864 true] [17592186256549 1110 #uuid "0531825e-15d9-55ac-8251-aacc8bb9e54a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256550 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256550 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256550 1111 #uuid "7149c441-adba-55e5-97a8-21d1a37322d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256552 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1089 "q-103226" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1100 "If $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=0$ and $f(2)=2,$ then $f(4)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1113 #uuid "81f8219b-91a8-52ec-8e9f-7635a6f67073" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256551 1145 "To compute $f(4),$ we first need to compute $f(3).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+2) & =f(n+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =2+0 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4),$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =2+2 \\\\ f(4) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(4)=4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256553 13194139533864 true] [17592186256552 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256552 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256552 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256553 13194139533864 true] [17592186256552 1110 #uuid "a8c0e081-89dc-5965-9267-824eb2083173" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256553 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256553 1111 #uuid "c9442754-d4d9-588b-9302-dd7a2e482a5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256555 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1089 "q-104159" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1100 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9,$ then what is $f(\\sqrt{3})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1113 #uuid "146303a4-6968-5cfe-bc52-997b521163c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256554 1145 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3})={lim}_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256555 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256556 13194139533864 true] [17592186256555 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256555 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256555 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256556 13194139533864 true] [17592186256555 1110 #uuid "04bf3706-2aec-5d95-8407-21ebd5fa2088" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256556 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256556 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256556 1111 #uuid "d8503c3b-e02d-5ad3-9012-3f65dcd1c70a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256558 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1089 "q-104247" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/8dbc5d9ca713eadced5cdf2c3616bab04e6a7f5a88f305b6d9abb3e6b89f7463.png)\n\nAt which points is the function $y=f(x)$ above left-continuous but is not continuous?\n\n$x$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1113 #uuid "c868e391-1415-56f9-8288-1cafe3a76a66" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256557 1145 "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=0$, at $x=2$ and at  $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=0\\ne -2=f(2).\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=1=f(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=3$ only." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256559 13194139533864 true] [17592186256558 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256558 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256558 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256559 13194139533864 true] [17592186256558 1110 #uuid "4233fae9-564f-5755-a113-2112874404ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256559 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256559 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256559 1111 #uuid "b1609cda-4b6a-556a-a7cc-d54d04c54353" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256561 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1089 "q-104282" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/6a250c587a1c12845759eefdda74faac240c7cec2c63e1d6a6180b1b09292637.png)\n\nAt which points is the function $y=f(x)$ above not continuous but is right-continuous?\n\n$x$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1113 #uuid "84f94ec2-29d7-53da-9169-3c19a469e54b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256560 1145 "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- At $x=-2$  the function is neither left- nor right-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -1=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2=f(2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256561 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256562 13194139533864 true] [17592186256561 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256561 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256561 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256562 13194139533864 true] [17592186256561 1110 #uuid "cdfed29c-0003-53c7-9de5-749accda86d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256562 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256562 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256562 1111 #uuid "b715ad1a-ee55-5582-a671-bbfdbb363d8b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256563 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256564 13194139533864 true] [17592186256563 1089 "q-104652" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256563 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256563 1100 "Given that $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}}={t}^{n},$ what is $n?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256563 1113 #uuid "297ab20b-08c4-5fef-9db0-2144be93be03" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256563 1145 "First, we use that $\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}={t}^{-7}$, then we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and finally, we apply the power rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{t}^{-7}} & = \\\\ {({t}^{-7})}^{1/3} & = \\\\ {t}^{(-7)\\cdot (1/3)} & = \\\\ {t}^{-7/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n=-\\frac{7}{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256564 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256565 13194139533864 true] [17592186256564 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256564 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256564 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256565 13194139533864 true] [17592186256564 1110 #uuid "b04a25bf-fa89-50dc-84d6-5b32bd7d2347" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256565 1034 "-\\frac{7}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256565 1111 #uuid "38fc0134-3e32-5936-adb6-119a15f96bb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256566 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256567 13194139533864 true] [17592186256566 1089 "q-106046" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256566 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256566 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cd847efd8b91b1c622e93b1ee97035bcee803a79a603aa7b4beee8fae77abf06.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256566 1113 #uuid "79f2ac8f-706d-5c75-bc5d-c89a9e2fab4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256566 1145 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-x+k \\\\ y & =-(0)^{2}+2(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=5$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,5).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256567 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256568 13194139533864 true] [17592186256567 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256567 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256567 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256568 13194139533864 true] [17592186256567 1110 #uuid "f2a39660-f172-58c9-b080-c4ef45aa5957" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256568 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256568 1111 #uuid "29957c6e-c46a-5da1-952f-b90fda674c62" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256570 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1089 "q-106090" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f367a4318a1b9d3c2e582f515a2c57058067ed4546f588dc30282f7dd34aac89.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-1$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1113 #uuid "2b16aa53-4832-5f75-8c64-41571c8255c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256569 1145 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256571 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256572 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256573 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256574 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256575 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256576 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256577 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256578 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256578 13194139533864 true] [17592186256570 1110 #uuid "07029c89-c1ae-54b6-a2f2-8eca277ca242" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256571 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256571 1111 #uuid "6e9ab2bb-e1b9-5170-9c76-b31d705dee86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256572 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256572 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256572 1111 #uuid "611e0a96-0804-5d12-9945-f9de947f2ae4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256573 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256573 1111 #uuid "67487606-7811-5761-a83c-868f3a1183f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256574 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256574 1111 #uuid "2836c62e-4424-585e-b336-1f4274344dea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256575 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256575 1111 #uuid "e3842f0e-4a4c-51b1-8fd8-b5c2cbfcf200" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256576 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256576 1111 #uuid "b5842364-149e-5b82-ab88-a434e6e3a47a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256577 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256577 1111 #uuid "42d6f634-f8fc-582e-9020-d11ee4f8e8c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256578 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256578 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256578 1111 #uuid "dcb2485d-327c-5235-8e3e-ceed6d3a2ef4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256580 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1089 "q-107059" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1113 #uuid "cab1e7b8-4832-542d-b640-2493abb21591" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256579 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5-1}-2}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5} & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x-1}-2)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x-1})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x-1-4}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x-5}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5-1}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256581 13194139533864 true] [17592186256580 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256580 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256580 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256581 13194139533864 true] [17592186256580 1110 #uuid "294a7179-3399-56aa-8698-72d45ca63d34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256581 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256581 1111 #uuid "5392be73-c2fc-5483-9a69-e3f002bb5aa2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256583 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1089 "q-107246" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2d6d392d4e0f773f962289075ffa07cd26353ebc00ec50e7473a5c992824417f.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the solid shown above?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1113 #uuid "2a4b869e-ef91-5ea2-9f20-27bb98e30ff5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256582 1145 "The solid is a **right cylinder**. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has two parallel circular bases. So, it's a cylinder.\n\n- The axis that passes through the centers of the circles is perpendicular to the bases. So, it's right." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256584 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256585 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256586 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256587 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256588 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256589 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256585 13194139533864 true] [17592186256583 1110 #uuid "180de416-69ab-5c18-9a3a-a094db6648c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256584 1034 "Oblique cylinder" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256584 1111 #uuid "4c61f9f3-2a54-5019-a166-6ad0885eabb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256585 1034 "Right cylinder" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256585 1111 #uuid "a77fc4ff-c00e-5410-babf-3a6afcbd4f10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256586 1034 "Sphere" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256586 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256586 1111 #uuid "5bc115f3-10a1-5c32-9f8a-ca39b3d811c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256587 1034 "Pyramid" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256587 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256587 1111 #uuid "4ebaf498-6c55-523c-b224-d3a9c1b5fc5d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256588 1034 "Cone" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256588 1111 #uuid "4c6e5ea9-ddc3-5a4e-99b3-62670139ebd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256589 1034 "Prism" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256589 1111 #uuid "4a3728e3-ed0a-565b-81d5-e8fac6415c12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256591 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1089 "q-107412" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1100 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. - ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1113 #uuid "6d755804-fa3d-550b-b69e-19c5ae177d05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256590 1145 "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+2)(2x-1)}{(x-2)(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne \\pm 2,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=-\\frac{2x-1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=2.$ So statement II is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ | $2$ | $2.001$ | $2.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $298$ | $2\\,998$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-3\\,002$ | $-302$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 2$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 2$ from the right . Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9e96d6a81813c1c89a498d0206234f2685f0a53c1bab626ce7430d84f9c0f506.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256592 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256593 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256594 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256595 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256596 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256597 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256598 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256599 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256595 13194139533864 true] [17592186256591 1110 #uuid "c015e4bb-470c-5e78-af9e-eb498bed15fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256592 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256592 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256592 1111 #uuid "d0065993-5db1-59b7-b027-72e0e57586de" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256593 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256593 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256593 1111 #uuid "5e1463bc-1f3b-5336-a780-7bc5670543e0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256594 1111 #uuid "ae643bc0-1d01-5856-baa8-16a8ad37ccc7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256595 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256595 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256595 1111 #uuid "2f05f808-bfc8-53eb-aa7f-a85bd2ed8eae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256596 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256596 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256596 1111 #uuid "1854f7ef-55c8-5515-b0b5-534e35b760f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256597 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256597 1111 #uuid "5067d5e3-0ba8-5ccd-9a52-a879bfff4ca5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256598 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256598 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256598 1111 #uuid "ecdff9dc-3802-5ee7-8876-4d6bd1a9a576" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256599 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256599 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256599 1111 #uuid "83f1c6d3-6da2-5556-9017-c5aadc76a5a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256600 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256601 13194139533864 true] [17592186256600 1089 "q-10764" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256600 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256600 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5f6bf9cffbcfb9cf223f42cc1430570ee311541b969176bc303b41e4668dcca8.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows a correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256600 1113 #uuid "636eda17-e825-56ec-8d5a-51448a16fe2c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256600 1145 "As values of $x$ increase, the corresponding values of $y$ decrease rapidly, too. It seems like the points on the plot are scattered along an exponential curve, as shown below.\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the most appropriate regression model is *exponential.*" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256602 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256603 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256604 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256605 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256606 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256602 13194139533864 true] [17592186256601 1110 #uuid "532b01d3-9c91-5197-9c7e-20cd08c120d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256602 1034 "Exponential" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256602 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256602 1111 #uuid "e18d1e12-9df4-5acf-9419-c22b0b477135" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1034 "Cubic" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256603 1111 #uuid "a0c309b0-c958-544f-b676-1ca328b37e2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256604 1034 "Quadratic" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256604 1111 #uuid "97fe2927-499a-51d6-8932-ae6d072c6129" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256605 1034 "Linear" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256605 1111 #uuid "192857fc-12d6-59b9-9ad1-1c2a01a283d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256606 1034 "Logarithmic" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256606 1111 #uuid "0a22ee4c-874e-5337-b4c8-99eb56686ce2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256607 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256608 13194139533864 true] [17592186256607 1089 "q-107749" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256607 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256607 1100 "Find the sum of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n5+11+17+23+⋯+83\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256607 1113 #uuid "c01979e1-2f0d-57eb-bf22-2ecf15ad2571" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256607 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=5$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =11-5 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =5+(n-1)(6) \\\\  & =5+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we know, the last term is ${a}_{N}=83.$ Putting this value in the formula for the $n$th term obtained above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =6N-1 \\\\ 83 & =6N-1 \\\\ 84 & =6N \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${a}_{1}=5$, ${a}_{14}=83$, and $N=14$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{14} & =\\frac{14}{2}(5+83) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =616\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256609 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256609 13194139533864 true] [17592186256608 1110 #uuid "8532ae84-3559-5cd7-b556-eff814ccedbd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256609 1034 "616" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256609 1111 #uuid "37cfaf94-1a46-5072-bebb-e1a08259948a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256610 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256611 13194139533864 true] [17592186256610 1089 "q-107751" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256610 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256610 1100 "Find the sum of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n(-14)+(-20)+(-26)+(-32)+⋯+(-314)\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256610 1113 #uuid "06cac020-1582-549c-9faf-4dee975a31ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256610 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-14$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-20-(-14) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-14+(n-1)(-6) \\\\  & =-14-6n+6 \\\\  & =-6n-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we know, the last term is ${a}_{N}=-314.$ Putting this value in the formula for the $n$th term obtained above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =-6N-8 \\\\ -314 & =-6N-8 \\\\ -306 & =-6N \\\\ N & =51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${a}_{1}=-14$, ${a}_{51}=-314$, and $N=51$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{51} & =\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}\\cdot (-14-314) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}\\cdot (-328) \\\\  & =-8\\,364\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256612 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256612 13194139533864 true] [17592186256611 1110 #uuid "cdd765af-f333-566b-8af6-2696251ec62c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256612 1034 "-8\\,364" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256612 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256612 1111 #uuid "0f702305-766f-5dd0-8437-9e8a07a18a6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256613 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256614 13194139533864 true] [17592186256613 1089 "q-107760" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256613 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256613 1100 "Calculate the sum of the first $20$ terms of the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n13+17+21+⋯\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256613 1113 #uuid "34c81d61-3e3f-5632-9eae-10ea56529564" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256613 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=20$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=13.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =17-13 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=13$, $d=4$, and $N=20$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{20} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}(2\\cdot 13+(20-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}\\cdot (26+76) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =10\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =1\\,020\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256615 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256615 13194139533864 true] [17592186256614 1110 #uuid "894a8a89-af82-5b7b-828c-769b05f9756a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256615 1034 "1\\,020" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256615 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256615 1111 #uuid "af802da0-192c-5cc1-a289-cc9a48a4a188" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256616 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256617 13194139533864 true] [17592186256616 1089 "q-107794" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256616 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256616 1100 "Calculate the sum of the first $15$ terms of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n-1+(-\\frac{2}{5})+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}+⋯\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256616 1113 #uuid "e928ff1c-3648-56d1-a33b-01de56cefa49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256616 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=15$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=-1.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}-(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=-1$, $d=\\frac{3}{5}$, and $N=15$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}(2(-1)+(15-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (-2+8.4) \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 6.4 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256618 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256618 13194139533864 true] [17592186256617 1110 #uuid "81f67404-4664-57c5-a7ff-495c9b6fa304" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256618 1034 "48" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256618 1111 #uuid "cdb382b0-20e2-5cac-aa00-9a26b5d81f32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256619 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256620 13194139533864 true] [17592186256619 1089 "q-108192" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256619 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256619 1100 "Stephanie is a geologist who began studying land subsidence due to a geological fault. The table below shows the land subsidence  $C,$ in centimeters, $n$ weeks after the study began. How many centimeters of subsidence were there at the beginning of the study?\n\n| $n$ (weeks) | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C$ (centimeters) | $11$ | $13.5$ | $16$ |\n\nYou may assume that the relationship between $n$ and $C$ is linear.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256619 1113 #uuid "b7eae905-c57c-5087-add9-0cd565ddeaa1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256619 1145 "Notice that the number of centimeters increases by $2.5$ every $5$ weeks. In other words, the number of centimeters increases by\n\n$$\n\\frac{2.5}{5}=0.5\n$$\n\ncentimeters per week. Thus, if $A$ is the amount of subsidence at the beginning of the study, then we can model the total subsidence $C$ after $n$ weeks using the equation\n\n$$\nC=A+0.5n.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute one of the pairs of values from the table into this equation and solve for $A.$ After $n=10$ weeks the subsidence was $C=11$ centimeters. We substitute these values into the equation and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =A+0.5n \\\\ 11 & =A+0.5(10) \\\\ 11 & =A+5 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, there were $6$ centimeters of subsidence when the study began." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256621 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256621 13194139533864 true] [17592186256620 1110 #uuid "c2df93ae-3252-5ea5-9051-ef23a01b6c04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256621 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256621 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256621 1111 #uuid "2f18187b-0b51-5ce0-872d-df9e45afe42e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256622 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256623 13194139533864 true] [17592186256622 1089 "q-108374" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256622 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256622 1100 "Calculate ${log}_{125}(625)$ without using a calculator.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256622 1113 #uuid "4051d6e3-232c-59f8-9d4f-58de8b478ba5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256622 1145 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=125,$ and $X=625.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $5,$ we set $b=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{125}(625) & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(625)}{{log}_{5}(125)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}({5}^{4})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256624 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256624 13194139533864 true] [17592186256623 1110 #uuid "3f15f019-2ba2-5997-922b-a7d174fc823e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256624 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256624 1111 #uuid "f6bae5cc-a920-50b7-932f-de5a6e7134cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256625 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256626 13194139533864 true] [17592186256625 1089 "q-108681" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256625 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256625 1100 "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}=2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256625 1113 #uuid "4e293890-de10-5cd4-971c-ec26d5c69bb9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256625 1145 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{3}\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2y}{3x})}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256627 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256627 13194139533864 true] [17592186256626 1110 #uuid "f3f99b3d-1b4b-527c-a02a-4fbabeb0b9fb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256627 1034 "-\\frac{8}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256627 1111 #uuid "8cf7a164-cbcf-5dbf-b601-0f0124c38bf3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256628 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256629 13194139533864 true] [17592186256628 1089 "q-108753" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256628 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256628 1100 "What is the index of the term $104$ in the following arithmetic sequence?\n\n$$\n4,\\,9,\\,14,\\,…\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256628 1113 #uuid "97acde06-7ca9-5d94-b594-1bd2bd789eb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256628 1145 "Since ${a}_{1}=4$ and \n\n$$\nd=9-4=5,\n$$\n\nwe can construct the formula for the $n$th term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =4+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=104,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =5n-1 \\\\ 104 & =5n-1 \\\\ 105 & =5n \\\\ 21 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $105$ is the $21$st term of the sequence." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256630 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256630 13194139533864 true] [17592186256629 1110 #uuid "58811d5d-80b6-5d2c-a9b9-9f2b21d3a4c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256630 1034 "21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256630 1111 #uuid "e7ca1c0e-9830-57d1-87c2-d2f47a0da86d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256632 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256634 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1089 "q-10878" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1100 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}$ at the point $(3,9)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1113 #uuid "99463695-96b3-52fc-80ac-01a64fec0d6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256631 1145 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(3)=2(3)=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =-\\frac{1}{6}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}+9 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{19}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}x \\\\ x & =57.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(57,0).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256633 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256633 13194139533864 true] [17592186256632 1110 #uuid "39467e20-e20d-5502-9bf8-881bbc694917" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256633 1034 "57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256633 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256633 1111 #uuid "852cbd16-a914-5ad4-9056-4d9ec5c12889" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256634 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256635 13194139533864 true] [17592186256634 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256634 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256634 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256635 13194139533864 true] [17592186256634 1110 #uuid "a3b98be8-fbca-50cf-a182-625dd393f4fc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256635 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256635 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256635 1111 #uuid "46c65590-ea51-547b-a16f-ef7e7566fc67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256637 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1089 "q-10907" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1100 "Find ${f}^{''}(-1)$ if $f(x)=4{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1113 #uuid "307089e8-baf9-5482-9f9b-5553f2129d31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256636 1145 "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={(4{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4})}^{'} &  \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4{x}^{4}-4\\cdot 3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & ={(20{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3})}^{'} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 20{x}^{3}-3\\cdot 12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =80{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=-1$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(-1) & =80(-1)^{3}-36(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-80-36 \\\\  & =-116.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256637 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256638 13194139533864 true] [17592186256637 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256637 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256637 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256638 13194139533864 true] [17592186256637 1110 #uuid "ca84a72e-54ab-56a5-b51a-2a49c1154ad5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256638 1034 "-116" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256638 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256638 1111 #uuid "9d5f8d8b-bf63-5cc8-a242-6b35439d3217" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256640 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1089 "q-110040" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1100 "A website receives $v(h)$ visits every $h$ hours. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nWrite the units in the form \"[output units] per [input unit]\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1113 #uuid "c773c57f-ca50-5bf6-b884-84eac176a3dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256639 1145 "We have that the time $h$ is measured in hours and the number of visits is measured in visitors. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h: & \\text{hours} \\\\ v(h): & \\text{visitors}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ measures a change in $v$ over a change in $h.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}: & \\frac{\\text{visitors}}{\\text{hours}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ is measured in \"visitors per hour \"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256640 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256641 13194139533864 true] [17592186256640 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256640 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256640 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256641 13194139533864 true] [17592186256640 1110 #uuid "316d870c-3af4-574b-8b2e-fc81731c9bcd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256641 1034 "Visitors per hour" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256641 1111 #uuid "54f6f675-87c1-50bf-ae96-b0ca58aac52e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256643 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256645 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1089 "q-110144" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9d4a5a15c0fc8487aa827a275375c07f048f38ed76fd94b9c40223ff7c38fa9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$, where $g(x)=f(x+a)+b.$ What are the values of $a$ and $b?$\n\n$a$: {{a}}\n\n$b$: {{b}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1113 #uuid "ce12f197-2895-5a7b-ab67-64d3d897a2dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256642 1145 "Notice that every point on the graph $y=g(x)$ is two units *to the right* and six units *up* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $g(x)=f(x-2)+6,$ and therefore $a=-2$ and $b=6.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256643 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256644 13194139533864 true] [17592186256643 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256643 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256643 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256644 13194139533864 true] [17592186256643 1110 #uuid "8ab543ab-afd4-5116-a449-3a144754a7ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256644 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256644 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256644 1111 #uuid "bc6fd4af-ae32-5b31-9841-c3e3f540ec96" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256646 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1041 "b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256646 13194139533864 true] [17592186256645 1110 #uuid "cf29d8e0-883f-555e-b637-a654725199f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256646 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256646 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256646 1111 #uuid "e104378d-2249-50b8-a6f1-04beef5efc04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256648 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1089 "q-110234" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1100 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(4).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1113 #uuid "03236a87-fcdc-5f26-80c5-0425c864d161" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256647 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=1$ and ${f}^{'}(1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256649 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256649 13194139533864 true] [17592186256648 1110 #uuid "652c9ef4-abcb-555e-bd23-6001869c8931" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256649 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256649 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256649 1111 #uuid "211dd85d-2832-52ce-8e1e-34f5dc81217e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256651 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1089 "q-110277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1100 "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}\\frac{1}{9}(5)^{n-1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1113 #uuid "3e0062d6-cbd1-58f3-a005-e59a201ee54b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256650 1145 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{9},$ $r=5$ and $N=7.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}\\frac{1}{9}(5)^{n-1} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{7})}{1-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{7})}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{36}\\cdot (-78\\,124) \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\,531}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256652 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256652 13194139533864 true] [17592186256651 1110 #uuid "0d921919-d38f-5c37-be04-22304885e161" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256652 1034 "\\frac{19\\,531}{9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256652 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256652 1111 #uuid "7fc8b167-ef15-5146-9667-67a32a66e6bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256654 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1089 "q-110328" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=3x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1113 #uuid "1dda7663-69d0-5c1d-b3aa-82594e0c8df7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256653 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3x+\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2-3x \\\\ x & =4-12x+9{x}^{2} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-13x+4 & =0 \\\\ (9x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{9},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChecking the solutions, we notice that only $x=\\frac{4}{9}$ satisfies the equation $3x+\\sqrt{x}=2.$ Therefore, we discard $x=1$ and conclude that ${f}^{-1}(2)=\\frac{4}{9}.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3x+\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{9})}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256655 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256655 13194139533864 true] [17592186256654 1110 #uuid "c07f2208-d0a3-5e70-9c26-0d4c4b241668" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256655 1034 "\\frac{4}{15}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256655 1111 #uuid "c9e3c5ab-0da9-504a-888f-7bfd23b7937a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256657 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1089 "q-110335" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1100 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{4}2{(3)}^{n-1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1113 #uuid "032226b5-f50b-5569-a40d-8825ca8a0e5f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256656 1145 "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=3.$ Also, note that $N=5,$ since there are $5$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{(3)}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =2{(3)}^{0-1} \\\\  & =2{(3)}^{-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{3},r=3$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{5} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{5})}{(1-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{5})}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-242) \\\\  & =\\frac{242}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256658 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256658 13194139533864 true] [17592186256657 1110 #uuid "e145e81e-6d32-5816-8b4d-7fbdfeb2d2d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256658 1034 "\\frac{242}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256658 1111 #uuid "7fd952d3-2187-57f2-a6ea-3287343f7b91" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256660 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1089 "q-110539" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1100 "If ${81}^{2a}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}}$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1113 #uuid "f61369c5-9b9f-545c-8059-dd0509d17c85" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256659 1145 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $81$ as ${3}^{4},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{81}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}} \\\\ {({3}^{4})}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}} \\\\ {3}^{8a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{8a} & ={3}^{-(a-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8a & =-(a-1) \\\\ 8a & =-a+1 \\\\ 9a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256661 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256661 13194139533864 true] [17592186256660 1110 #uuid "40273622-2870-5090-97f5-a4f10a95baac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256661 1034 "\\frac{1}{9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256661 1111 #uuid "9aab07c9-d4d4-520d-8b13-0b521041dab9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256663 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1089 "q-110768" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1100 "If $\\operatorname{log}(2x)+5=5,$ then $x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1113 #uuid "7cfeb4b6-43fa-5ecb-97ca-0bb7ce89abb9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256662 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(2x)+5 & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(2x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $10,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(2x) & =0 \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}(2x)} & ={10}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${10}^{\\operatorname{log}(2x)}=2x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={10}^{0} \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256664 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256664 13194139533864 true] [17592186256663 1110 #uuid "5bf1c538-27c7-5ccf-8b87-dd65b49ef275" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256664 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256664 1111 #uuid "07f16a31-68cf-50b0-a4f8-6115e3e39831" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256665 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256666 13194139533864 true] [17592186256665 1089 "q-110798" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256665 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256665 1100 "Solve ${log}_{2}(x-2)={log}_{2}(2x-3)$ for $x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No real solutions\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256665 1113 #uuid "cbf7b752-cbd4-57de-8347-862618b357a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256665 1145 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & ={log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ x-2 & =2x-3 \\\\ -x & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(1-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2\\cdot 1-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, ${log}_{2}(-1)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256667 13194139533864 true] [17592186256666 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256666 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256666 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256667 13194139533864 true] [17592186256666 1110 #uuid "ba70de7f-710d-52b0-9dbf-4fe40595a65c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256667 1034 "No real solutions" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256667 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256667 1111 #uuid "c626e4c3-0071-5e2d-8f3c-bb8499222473" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256668 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256669 13194139533864 true] [17592186256668 1089 "q-111362" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256668 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256668 1100 "Given that  $(3{s}^{2}+2s+4)({s}^{2}+3s+2)=3{s}^{4}+a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+cs+8,$ calculate $a+b+c.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256668 1113 #uuid "55058b06-dd5d-5caa-a687-35d85c6cc189" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256668 1145 "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{s}^{2}+2s+4)({s}^{2}+3s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+3s+2)+(2s)({s}^{2}+3s+2)+(4)({s}^{2}+3s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+9{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+2{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+4s+4{s}^{2}+12s+8 & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+11{s}^{3}+16{s}^{2}+16s+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=11,$ $b=16,$ and $c=16.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=11+16+16=43.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256669 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256670 13194139533864 true] [17592186256669 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256669 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256669 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256670 13194139533864 true] [17592186256669 1110 #uuid "668faa38-775f-5725-a250-436066030f64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256670 1034 "43" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256670 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256670 1111 #uuid "9c396da7-833f-5cec-855b-b86b88810cce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256671 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256672 13194139533864 true] [17592186256671 1089 "q-112382" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256671 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256671 1100 "After taking a pill containing $300$ milligrams of a particular drug, the quantity of the drug in a patient's body decreases at a rate of $c(t)=300\\cdot {2}^{-0.7t}$ milligrams per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the patient consumed the pill. What will be the rate of decrease after $5$ hours? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256671 1113 #uuid "7a6d442f-4688-5203-a723-98f8e649492f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256671 1145 "Since we want to determine the rate of decrease in the quantity of the drug in the patient's body after $5$ hours, we have $t=5.$\n\nThus, the rate of decrease in the quantity of the drug after $5$ hours will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(5) & =300\\cdot {2}^{-0.7(5)} \\\\  & =300\\cdot {2}^{-3.5} \\\\  & \\approx 27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of decrease will be $27$ milligrams per hour." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256673 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256673 13194139533864 true] [17592186256672 1110 #uuid "57fa2f2d-5fc0-5725-9f07-9fc0207ec675" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256673 1034 "27" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256673 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256673 1111 #uuid "7d43a382-c034-5630-8dba-d0491c7e917d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256674 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256675 13194139533864 true] [17592186256674 1089 "q-112591" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256674 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256674 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/7b0ca55d089bea13471e9de6dd26093a1a9d477937106c92be26d5a469778c8a.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $x=-2$ is a simple root.\nII. - $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\nIII. - $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256674 1113 #uuid "f8190dc0-5e3e-595b-8144-fb26d1f0ac64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256674 1145 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the \n$x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=2$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256676 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256677 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256678 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256679 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256680 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256681 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256682 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256683 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256676 13194139533864 true] [17592186256675 1110 #uuid "2b6e0b01-1512-5734-99de-0dd680458bf2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256676 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256676 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256676 1111 #uuid "7d9b7624-8b63-5c92-9e87-99970e683bdd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256677 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256677 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256677 1111 #uuid "30a05358-5f44-575b-a9de-ce1822364fc6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256678 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256678 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256678 1111 #uuid "930f846a-0de4-5297-bf95-95ab3ae4dead" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256679 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256679 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256679 1111 #uuid "a56ef72d-5597-55cb-9d06-627f7b68b3dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256680 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256680 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256680 1111 #uuid "ffaf444c-54a3-5b3a-aced-cf563009d2c3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256681 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256681 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256681 1111 #uuid "a78c54fe-b2e5-5eb5-83ac-6e9175fcf819" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256682 1111 #uuid "19b0761b-be8e-589d-b2c9-ce99c5c71159" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256683 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256683 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256683 1111 #uuid "dec88327-3398-521e-bf43-d73f8b93ee45" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256684 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256685 13194139533864 true] [17592186256684 1089 "q-112786" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256684 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256684 1100 "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=4$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+32?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256684 1113 #uuid "e3bc7886-a3e3-5609-abf7-ad1cbadc0e93" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256684 1145 "Since $x=4$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ | $32$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-8$ | $-32$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-4}={x}^{2}-2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}-2x-8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-4)(x+2)(x-4) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256686 13194139533864 true] [17592186256685 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256685 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256685 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256686 13194139533864 true] [17592186256685 1110 #uuid "9160b3a0-d236-519e-9988-64bbfb4e234a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256686 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256686 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256686 1111 #uuid "89c9475a-c940-57e5-b093-63cdc1c0e4be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256687 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256688 13194139533864 true] [17592186256687 1089 "q-112805" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256687 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256687 1100 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $2?$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256687 1113 #uuid "65d26569-d024-5df5-80e7-ebdb725c458d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256687 1145 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $5{x}^{3}$ and $-15{x}^{2}$ is $5{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x-3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=3$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256688 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256689 13194139533864 true] [17592186256688 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256688 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256688 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256689 13194139533864 true] [17592186256688 1110 #uuid "070c4ccc-7e7e-523e-a68f-8973b72aec99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256689 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256689 1111 #uuid "537ab16a-e40c-5f97-a951-d58de51d5252" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256691 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1089 "q-112837" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1100 "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}-3x=0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1113 #uuid "6d21dfae-6d8c-52e5-8ced-deddffba77c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256690 1145 "We have $a=4,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=0.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-3)^{2}-4(4)(0) \\\\  & =9-0 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256692 13194139533864 true] [17592186256691 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256691 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256691 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256692 13194139533864 true] [17592186256691 1110 #uuid "3f66180a-6c98-506d-b01a-627d14bd739f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256692 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256692 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256692 1111 #uuid "c8ed3567-e2e2-5776-b081-4666187091d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256694 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1089 "q-112848" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation ${x}^{2}-6x+9=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1113 #uuid "651268f9-ded3-54c1-9fe8-21b92e019084" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256693 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=1,$ $b=-6,$ and $c=9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-6)^{2}-4(1)(9) \\\\  & =36-36 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is zero $(D=0),$ the equation has one distinct real solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256695 13194139533864 true] [17592186256694 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256694 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256694 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256695 13194139533864 true] [17592186256694 1110 #uuid "ecbbe986-b8f4-5dfd-8cb7-e10017d88391" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256695 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256695 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256695 1111 #uuid "5afaf879-395b-53d8-8fb1-82cbf20f0b1c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256697 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1089 "q-112849" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}-12x+9=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1113 #uuid "a95f3ae6-2de7-53c9-a211-7238246c0d4d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256696 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=4,$ $b=-12,$ and $c=9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-12)^{2}-4(4)(9) \\\\  & =144-144 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is zero $(D=0),$ the equation has one distinct real solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256697 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256698 13194139533864 true] [17592186256697 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256697 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256697 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256698 13194139533864 true] [17592186256697 1110 #uuid "31bc67ec-dff9-543a-85d5-e152981038ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256698 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256698 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256698 1111 #uuid "15c4698d-ad65-523e-bee5-6a8a35c0bf9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256699 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256700 13194139533864 true] [17592186256699 1089 "q-112867" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256699 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256699 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, What could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+10?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256699 1113 #uuid "9e4c35d0-80ea-555f-a42d-fa766d0cba07" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256699 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{-5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $8.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256700 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256701 13194139533864 true] [17592186256700 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256700 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256700 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256701 13194139533864 true] [17592186256700 1110 #uuid "99d9f829-2ab1-507e-a54e-e1c936f87e15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256701 1034 "-\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256701 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256701 1111 #uuid "f1100322-f9f9-5124-a50e-27f9ee343c35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256702 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256703 13194139533864 true] [17592186256702 1089 "q-112871" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256702 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256702 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, What could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-12{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}-3x+10?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256702 1113 #uuid "284a91b3-0aac-5ff1-be86-b1f45987e7b5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256702 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256703 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256704 13194139533864 true] [17592186256703 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256703 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256703 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256704 13194139533864 true] [17592186256703 1110 #uuid "2935c1f0-080f-504b-bd7e-60952c1de321" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256704 1034 "\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256704 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256704 1111 #uuid "51b3db7c-057e-5c4b-934f-a7beba01180a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256706 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1089 "q-112878" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, What **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{5}-5{x}^{3}+6?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1113 #uuid "25b2159c-942d-5822-b9a6-b71a06f24251" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256705 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $4$ does not take the above form. The value $4$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{4}{1},$ and the numerator $(4)$ is not a factor of $6.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256707 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256707 13194139533864 true] [17592186256706 1110 #uuid "26f63a45-44a6-5c2e-990a-9e8fb70acb02" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256707 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256707 1111 #uuid "d247b06a-64f3-560a-a0df-357046193290" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256709 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1089 "q-113033" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1100 "Simplify ${({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{26}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1113 #uuid "dbdb58f0-667e-5aa7-8bff-41bad7b32ac0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256708 1145 "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{26}=(1)^{26}=1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256710 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256710 13194139533864 true] [17592186256709 1110 #uuid "17777363-aed8-56fb-b2c6-70ad72085ac5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256710 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256710 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256710 1111 #uuid "663a2306-738c-57ca-bff9-92357aaf260b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256712 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1089 "q-113087" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1100 "$9{\\text{i}}^{-4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1113 #uuid "92f2f0b7-de4f-58b0-acaa-26e31d056305" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256711 1145 "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n9{\\text{i}}^{-4}=\\frac{9}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{4}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}=\\frac{9}{1}=9.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256713 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256713 13194139533864 true] [17592186256712 1110 #uuid "456339f4-a0d9-5d0b-a8d0-490f373da9dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256713 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256713 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256713 1111 #uuid "4fc8407b-d6ef-533c-b4f4-5ab84427489d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256715 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1089 "q-113092" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1100 "Find the roots of $f(w)=\\frac{{w}^{2}-3w-10}{2{w}^{2}-10w}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1113 #uuid "ee249868-10fd-54ab-8257-c7e4ccf74f80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256714 1145 "The roots of $f(w)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(w)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{w}^{2}-3w-10}{2{w}^{2}-10w}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(w) & =\\frac{{w}^{2}-3w-10}{2{w}^{2}-10w} \\\\  & =\\frac{(w+2)(w-5)}{2w(w-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(w+2)\\cancel{(w-5)}}{2w\\cancel{(w-5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(w+2)}{2w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+2 & =0\\,⟹\\,w=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(w)=0$ is $w=-2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256716 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256716 13194139533864 true] [17592186256715 1110 #uuid "74321424-c74a-5f83-a2a8-7acffeed9912" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256716 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256716 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256716 1111 #uuid "3ba8a4f9-6db3-5c67-98b1-6ca1d1ca2f8b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256718 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1089 "q-113479" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/65348161e386fac14a98e8440d32d3883b5865a6d1eecfcd58e12931730ad0b8.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1113 #uuid "fa5f1c4b-9c02-56f6-92b6-f8a6f5b1eec5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256717 1145 "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=2,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$ This means that $k=2$ and $b=1,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-1}+2.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(2,5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-1}+2 \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{a}{2-1}+2 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k=3+1+2=6.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256719 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256719 13194139533864 true] [17592186256718 1110 #uuid "bfc220ac-902f-57ac-b892-b71e8daa3106" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256719 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256719 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256719 1111 #uuid "3a8760a4-fc31-5c9a-9404-6afacf46f9b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256721 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1089 "q-113523" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1100 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3}.$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1113 #uuid "c37cf0e8-bbca-5ef1-ad17-b5e71311507a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256720 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x-1)+3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256722 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256722 13194139533864 true] [17592186256721 1110 #uuid "5d03e6ea-0b8d-5cd4-ad47-a3accdda2667" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256722 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256722 1111 #uuid "070582c7-9118-59ae-8fb8-cefbfc2e8342" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256723 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256724 13194139533864 true] [17592186256723 1089 "q-113547" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256723 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256723 1100 "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No vertical asymptotes\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256723 1113 #uuid "89758aa9-2520-5f08-bcf3-4dd38de13609" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256723 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x+1)+(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+1}}{\\cancel{(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256724 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256725 13194139533864 true] [17592186256724 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256724 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256724 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256725 13194139533864 true] [17592186256724 1110 #uuid "6ce9b096-dc68-52ed-9491-356c80fb6f40" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256725 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256725 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256725 1111 #uuid "5b59731b-ccb9-53c5-82cf-caaaeebe66fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256726 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256727 13194139533864 true] [17592186256726 1089 "q-113550" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256726 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256726 1100 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No vertical asymptotes\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256726 1113 #uuid "c1d02b50-c109-51fe-8f01-39a10ce1834c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256726 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{(x-3)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256728 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256728 13194139533864 true] [17592186256727 1110 #uuid "1ca49ee6-7b3e-50f6-b6b8-2580dda21a0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256728 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256728 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256728 1111 #uuid "91ab451d-c25c-5f68-b886-6bf28b6f2808" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256730 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256732 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256734 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1089 "q-114210" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1100 "Solve $-{m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}+25m+75=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1113 #uuid "62a71b17-2204-59c1-8d38-eaaccf24e868" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256729 1145 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n{m}^{3}+3{m}^{2}-25m-75=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}+3{m}^{2}-25m-75 & = \\\\ ({m}^{3}+3{m}^{2})+(-25m-75) & = \\\\ {m}^{2}(m+3)-25(m+3) & = \\\\ ({m}^{2}-25)(m+3) & = \\\\ (m+5)(m-5)(m+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(m+5)(m-5)(m+3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m+5)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=-5.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=5.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m+3)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=-3,\\,\\pm 5.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256731 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256731 13194139533864 true] [17592186256730 1110 #uuid "f6d70c21-c5c7-5a91-ad70-3da57be7affc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256731 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256731 1111 #uuid "e472f6af-282c-5f81-8a6f-aa842163264c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256732 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256733 13194139533864 true] [17592186256732 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256732 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256732 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256733 13194139533864 true] [17592186256732 1110 #uuid "e4280d61-9ff1-5d9c-a021-b2720a3231c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256733 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256733 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256733 1111 #uuid "38e65078-5b50-50dd-8580-957b11fe5c19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256734 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256735 13194139533864 true] [17592186256734 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256734 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256734 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256735 13194139533864 true] [17592186256734 1110 #uuid "771c2df9-b495-5230-a083-074b60a82938" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256735 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256735 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256735 1111 #uuid "01c18fee-8168-5be8-b443-ccc5b402231c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256737 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1089 "q-114253" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1113 #uuid "35cad922-9f73-5ba1-b352-62472240f419" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256736 1145 "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]}^{'}}{[8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2x-6}{\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{2(x+1)^{-1/2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{2(x+1)^{-1/2}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[x-3]}^{'}}{[2(x+1)^{-1/2}-1]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{-(x+1)^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 3}(x+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-(3+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256738 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256738 13194139533864 true] [17592186256737 1110 #uuid "a8edd50f-599e-5978-9f52-899e36acd0e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256738 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256738 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256738 1111 #uuid "45048763-18a9-5264-ab1c-dabdb5d833c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256740 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1089 "q-114605" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1100 "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{7x+4}=-9$\nII. - $\\sqrt{3x-2}=3$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{9x-5}=-2$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1113 #uuid "6afc5777-c42f-5d0a-931f-1320237e4acb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256739 1145 "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and III state that a square is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and III have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256741 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256742 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256743 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256744 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256745 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256746 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256747 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256748 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256743 13194139533864 true] [17592186256740 1110 #uuid "cbdd74f5-16f9-550f-8f1e-4eb6c3e50781" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256741 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256741 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256741 1111 #uuid "eac8cd01-c52e-5e9c-80fc-114b81d2daa2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256742 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256742 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256742 1111 #uuid "bcee4bb5-984a-5ee2-a65d-e06c31f0a27b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256743 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256743 1111 #uuid "1b153650-8e00-5782-898a-033beb61c782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256744 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256744 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256744 1111 #uuid "d204e465-e283-57f1-a505-024b3e1f71fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256745 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256745 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256745 1111 #uuid "b8d59aa3-7f16-5213-b7b6-2f801122b419" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256746 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256746 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256746 1111 #uuid "3ab33868-c654-53bf-a8df-b79b7cd623cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256747 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256747 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256747 1111 #uuid "b90288af-b97b-5a19-9f87-e5687fa1c355" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256748 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256748 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256748 1111 #uuid "db2f86aa-b780-5dcb-ba7e-cb54eaab2841" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256750 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1089 "q-114606" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1100 "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{7-2x}=-2$\nII. - $\\sqrt{x-3}=-7$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{6x-2}=5$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1113 #uuid "55431f3f-1e50-5d2e-ae53-dc462ee5ce94" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256749 1145 "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and II state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and II have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256751 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256752 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256753 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256754 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256755 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256756 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256757 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256758 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256756 13194139533864 true] [17592186256750 1110 #uuid "c061fa44-2283-5b2d-9a3a-45d58533becb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256751 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256751 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256751 1111 #uuid "3916e240-fb7f-535e-887a-c9e1c5a5d840" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256752 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256752 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256752 1111 #uuid "33d66366-35e9-5641-878c-f406fa2cff58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256753 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256753 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256753 1111 #uuid "ce5ccb7b-514f-53e2-8cda-6932d842e9ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256754 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256754 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256754 1111 #uuid "d56b2ee6-b94d-53ec-9172-b66232466e86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256755 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256755 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256755 1111 #uuid "0829c6b4-2de6-5d00-bbff-b771f8714cbb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256756 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256756 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256756 1111 #uuid "4fdeeb94-db0d-5f3f-8e17-5f724464727a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256757 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256757 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256757 1111 #uuid "47659802-aae3-56c1-b33e-41edb3963bd2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256758 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256758 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256758 1111 #uuid "0eacfd5e-10a2-54d0-b2b3-050e45056245" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256759 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256760 13194139533864 true] [17592186256759 1089 "q-114961" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256759 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256759 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256759 1113 #uuid "1bd10278-9b76-5fcf-980b-136026be1670" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256759 1145 "The major axis is the longest diameter of the ellipse, which is the line segment bounded by the points $(-3,1)$ and $(5,1).$ So, the length of the major axis is $5-(-3)=8.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256761 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256761 13194139533864 true] [17592186256760 1110 #uuid "d452168e-bfbc-5305-927c-94998c46c6d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256761 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256761 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256761 1111 #uuid "1f74fd72-dd28-524a-9c7f-1084f8649375" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256763 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256765 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256767 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256769 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1089 "q-11532" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1100 "Calculate $2H-3L$, where\n$H=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$L=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 1, column 2: {{r1c2}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 2: {{r2c2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1113 #uuid "68388deb-c99d-58dc-919b-11e840c4302b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256762 1145 "We multiply each entry of $H$ by $2$ and each entry of $L$ by $3,$ and then subtract the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2H-3L & =2[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 0 & 3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-0 & -4-3 \\\\ 4-6 & -2-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256764 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256764 13194139533864 true] [17592186256763 1110 #uuid "50d71f43-0198-534a-a76e-78a5c7ff66fc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256764 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256764 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256764 1111 #uuid "f00ea6a0-13f9-519c-9283-1433fc6754ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256766 13194139533864 true] [17592186256765 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256765 1041 "r1c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256765 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256766 13194139533864 true] [17592186256765 1110 #uuid "de2d9f41-421d-5340-a0d1-369099542203" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256766 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256766 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256766 1111 #uuid "12c3c137-8602-5f1d-96d8-8266ec5a6048" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256768 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256768 13194139533864 true] [17592186256767 1110 #uuid "8e14cc66-77c0-5c25-ba6e-f662695dd674" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256768 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256768 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256768 1111 #uuid "3e54d8f1-4f94-5d30-b05f-3a8b2466941f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256770 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1041 "r2c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256770 13194139533864 true] [17592186256769 1110 #uuid "440e4888-7883-5d03-99f9-c1a1ee936bb1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256770 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256770 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256770 1111 #uuid "a707a8e1-6dba-5c6c-96e5-30b0f82458c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256771 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256772 13194139533864 true] [17592186256771 1089 "q-115521" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256771 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256771 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256771 1113 #uuid "724e9328-9953-5c76-8644-8a4bbc630b36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256771 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{ln}^{2}x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{ln}^{2}x]^{'}}{{[x]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x})}{1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}x)^{2}}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}x]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{1} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256773 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256773 13194139533864 true] [17592186256772 1110 #uuid "23895971-4cf6-5c53-acb6-56e9293293d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256773 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256773 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256773 1111 #uuid "3f6ea9c2-57f9-5209-a617-348346886abc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256774 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256775 13194139533864 true] [17592186256774 1089 "q-115525" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256774 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256774 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256774 1113 #uuid "bb5aa37c-ec5b-54e4-abf6-b459e31e9391" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256774 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)]^{'}}{{[\\operatorname{tan}x]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}{{sec}^{2}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x{cos}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256776 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256776 13194139533864 true] [17592186256775 1110 #uuid "f6b5eeff-0c16-5b3c-866a-0de7f8c2e22a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256776 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256776 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256776 1111 #uuid "9f581248-f993-5ff1-a21c-eb63648ee127" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256777 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256778 13194139533864 true] [17592186256777 1089 "q-115529" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256777 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256777 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}2x}{\\operatorname{cot}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256777 1113 #uuid "96a5dc35-6a1c-5615-a025-affa6405f5ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256777 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}2x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}2x}{\\operatorname{cot}x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{cot}2x]^{'}}{[\\operatorname{cot}x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-2{csc}^{2}2x)}{-{csc}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}2x})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{{sin}^{2}2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{{(2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{4{sin}^{2}x{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2\\cancel{{sin}^{2}x}}{4\\cancel{{sin}^{2}x}{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256779 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256779 13194139533864 true] [17592186256778 1110 #uuid "888c9f31-93fc-54f1-8edb-6253391ea8fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256779 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256779 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256779 1111 #uuid "bcbb8b1b-e9f7-5e53-aba6-dd8c34f37f84" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256781 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1089 "q-11590" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d773ba5052937e20997d5039fe4de6958862c4553cbe0edd8fdfe12eeb5de8fa.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P({X}^{'}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1113 #uuid "18160ae9-a2e7-570f-b9a7-71d46ee99dc2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256780 1145 "The Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=0.925.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}^{'}) & =1-P(X) \\\\  & =1-0.925 \\\\  & =0.075.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256782 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256782 13194139533864 true] [17592186256781 1110 #uuid "c1cb15b4-dbff-5269-a982-d08e38e581a4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256782 1034 "0.075" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256782 1111 #uuid "94828c53-dd84-5288-9dd4-b29c5a5e99d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256784 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1089 "q-11603" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1100 "If $y=3{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}$, find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1113 #uuid "cee486c2-ed27-5e12-b275-9a37215d5458" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256783 1145 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=6x,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=12{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(6x)(({x}^{3}-1)^{4})+(3{x}^{2})(12{x}^{2}({x}^{3}-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =6x({x}^{3}-1)^{4}+36{x}^{4}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6x({x}^{3}-1)+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6{x}^{4}-6x+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(42{x}^{4}-6x)\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1$, we substitute $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=1} & =(42\\cdot {1}^{4}-6\\cdot 1){({1}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =36\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256785 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256785 13194139533864 true] [17592186256784 1110 #uuid "856d08f2-0c65-5782-b962-59c19dfd35d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256785 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256785 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256785 1111 #uuid "538fbf14-cdc0-5657-af83-f8c31618f4c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256787 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256790 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1089 "q-11670" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1100 "A nutritionist has $25$ patients. Of his patients, $10$ of them are smokers, and $15$ of them have diabetes. We randomly select a patient among the $25$ patients. \n\nDefine $A$ to be the event \"the patient is a smoker.\" What is ${A}^{'}$ described in natural language, and what is $P({A}^{'})?$\n\nComplement event: {{complement-event}}\n\nComplement probability: {{complement-probability}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1113 #uuid "d2dcf9c8-21c2-5756-902c-6ae34835148c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256786 1145 "The **complement** of an event $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'},$ is the *opposite* of $A.$ That is to say, ${A}^{'}$  is the set of outcomes in which $A$ *does not* happen.\n\nHere, ${A}^{'}$ is the opposite of the event \"the patient is a smoker.\" So ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"the patient doesn't smoke.\" \n\nNow, let's calculate the probability $P({A}^{'}).$  There are $25$ patients, and $10$ of them are smokers, so there are $25-10=15$ non-smokers. Therefore, \n\n$$\nP({A}^{'})=\\frac{15}{25}=0.6.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256788 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256789 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1041 "complement-event" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256789 13194139533864 true] [17592186256787 1110 #uuid "402bb104-7405-5d66-aabd-ca4094fb9864" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256788 1034 "The patient is a smoker" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256788 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256788 1111 #uuid "71109537-5d34-5063-b2cc-92d751d6c0ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256789 1034 "The patient is not a smoker" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256789 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256789 1111 #uuid "1694fcf5-640e-59e5-9571-f2a08d9ddcad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256791 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1041 "complement-probability" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256791 13194139533864 true] [17592186256790 1110 #uuid "b1f30e9b-cffc-5a2e-98ab-4d61c92e0d17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256791 1034 "0.6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256791 1111 #uuid "5a35fb8f-d89c-565c-9fb6-7a7ddaee8c0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256793 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1089 "q-116968" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1100 "What values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1113 #uuid "8fd2f5d3-ed41-5722-b4e0-f7436fc89d15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256792 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ is not in the range of the function." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256794 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256794 13194139533864 true] [17592186256793 1110 #uuid "7519cd05-eb6f-5c7c-be4e-fa766b0b5e78" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256794 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256794 1111 #uuid "bf497dd6-679d-5131-ac91-2d47cf6c54f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256796 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1089 "q-117039" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x?$\n\nI. - $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nII. - $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$\nIII. - $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1113 #uuid "4c2df21e-7d98-55cc-bb9d-c4c6dd3c0b6c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256795 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$  lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}x.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256797 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256798 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256799 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256800 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256801 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256802 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256803 13194139533864 true] [17592186256796 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256804 13194139533864 true] [175