c{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{4}{9}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256837 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256837 13194139533864 true] [17592186256836 1110 #uuid "1bdbc2fc-3af5-5eaa-a32a-7a2244e463f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256837 1034 "\\frac{4}{9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256837 1111 #uuid "9a40c3e6-33ed-5435-94e0-b07c6d7b3520" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256838 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256839 13194139533864 true] [17592186256838 1089 "q-117373" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256838 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256838 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x?$\n\nI. - $(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$\nII. - $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. - $(0,\\pi )$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256838 1113 #uuid "e42e861e-a315-5ce3-9bfd-ad15ea97bc42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256838 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256840 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256841 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256842 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256843 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256844 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256845 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256846 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256847 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256847 13194139533864 true] [17592186256839 1110 #uuid "a87b1ada-f070-5a16-8efc-b76311bcca9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256840 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256840 1111 #uuid "d44d8b18-33a5-5a24-b9bd-673cc51f97b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256841 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256841 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256841 1111 #uuid "58c68d78-b88a-515f-83c6-1c3d4bbca69c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256842 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256842 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256842 1111 #uuid "f5724fe7-92ec-56ed-a3bc-f56af12e586e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256843 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256843 1111 #uuid "99b79ba0-fff4-5683-abf9-c0c4c9a1e11b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256844 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256844 1111 #uuid "4f137b7e-1902-5bdc-a1d0-dc1767719059" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256845 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256845 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256845 1111 #uuid "2071b92f-10b1-5ac3-a15c-76aba9bb7fdf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256846 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256846 1111 #uuid "995ae627-9d8b-576a-bcee-28576e7ed6af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256847 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256847 1111 #uuid "63618dd2-0e11-5abc-82d7-1a61bb98c70c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256849 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1089 "q-117568" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1100 "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2x-1)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1113 #uuid "78732451-2db0-5c4e-aa11-0192b0cac8c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256848 1145 "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=\\frac{3}{2},$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|\\frac{3}{2}|=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256850 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256850 13194139533864 true] [17592186256849 1110 #uuid "f8ab1859-ecad-56e1-a2c1-455c5286b3f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256850 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256850 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256850 1111 #uuid "0e2e8619-b6be-52f0-85e5-f414a28c3328" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256852 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1089 "q-117575" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1100 "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-5\\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\pi )-3?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1113 #uuid "8910d732-402d-5d51-b841-b7875aafc6e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256851 1145 "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=-5,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|-5|=5.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256853 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256853 13194139533864 true] [17592186256852 1110 #uuid "0f3c7636-e490-520a-b803-7e863dbc2962" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256853 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256853 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256853 1111 #uuid "a8ce3e53-e1f5-5591-a4eb-114c90c884d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256855 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1089 "q-118638" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0beb4cb3deb6bf528f399d93f4fad9e9f4ddb141f7187608bdf9a9bd047eb9a7.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{YZ}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{WX}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1113 #uuid "28fafe7a-73c7-5a73-a89e-2b98b4be1492" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256854 1145 "By the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. Hence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WZ & =XZ \\\\ 2x+2 & =17-x \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256856 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256856 13194139533864 true] [17592186256855 1110 #uuid "70bc7d00-d148-5978-b0f0-bbea8032d6aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256856 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256856 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256856 1111 #uuid "b7c4db9d-25e2-5919-b3ae-fcdeff4ae1ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256858 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1089 "q-118780" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1100 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation ${x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}-9{x}^{7}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1113 #uuid "6548405c-a613-5075-b99b-e7774982d1db" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256857 1145 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ which has order $8.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $8.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256859 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256859 13194139533864 true] [17592186256858 1110 #uuid "42064ebd-20a9-5478-a842-ced33dee8f82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256859 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256859 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256859 1111 #uuid "5ca617c8-ae0c-50ae-9246-ed19fda55c63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256861 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1089 "q-118782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1100 "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $9x{y}^{''}+8{y}^{'}=4$\nII. - $\\sqrt{{y}^{''}+x}=3y$\nIII. - ${y}^{‴}+{x}^{2}{y}^{''}=3$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1113 #uuid "c6fe84cb-75e2-55de-b6df-ded3330a421e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256860 1145 "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation II is also a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256862 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256863 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256864 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256865 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256866 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256867 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256868 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256869 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256867 13194139533864 true] [17592186256861 1110 #uuid "257a23f9-a24a-5b27-a803-2fd9ff28766b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256862 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256862 1111 #uuid "5bd11e8a-9d28-5792-a51b-1ee774af6cb2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256863 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256863 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256863 1111 #uuid "3f1de0e4-6581-50d8-b4dd-417ce299ef49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256864 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256864 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256864 1111 #uuid "fd82d032-b6b4-5f9f-bb42-9bc13998c9b2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256865 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256865 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256865 1111 #uuid "6c9a1d31-55dc-5cac-9de6-5d54020933fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256866 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256866 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256866 1111 #uuid "a3956880-699f-50c3-98e2-75b8a47f798d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256867 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256867 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256867 1111 #uuid "89b70b1a-c0f9-503a-8b4f-42025621f37c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256868 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256868 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256868 1111 #uuid "a75eaa53-ae4e-5fee-a937-f991724017e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256869 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256869 1111 #uuid "81ccf827-073c-5b1e-8402-506130440329" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256871 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1089 "q-118786" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\nII. - $8{y}^{'}-xy=3$\nIII. - $3{y}^{''}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1113 #uuid "7d3e1743-cd34-559d-bad0-d34093686fd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256870 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{''}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256872 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256873 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256874 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256875 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256876 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256877 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256878 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256879 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256875 13194139533864 true] [17592186256871 1110 #uuid "39594d48-3439-593f-beb0-784efbc73181" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256872 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256872 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256872 1111 #uuid "39ee5fc1-03e0-57e5-bbbf-315d46f4703b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256873 1111 #uuid "f57d7578-82c4-5967-a523-96974dbf056c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256874 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256874 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256874 1111 #uuid "9d8e1b40-531d-5738-bd3c-ddd411adcbb3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256875 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256875 1111 #uuid "3dac5233-1cb8-538b-a95f-9c3184be17d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256876 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256876 1111 #uuid "c9953a88-e46e-55ac-b3c0-ab066edb021f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256877 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256877 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256877 1111 #uuid "b345a296-774e-57b4-8d0e-503947db627b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256878 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256878 1111 #uuid "9d54d347-85b6-56fb-b78d-46ec06bb4b9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256879 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256879 1111 #uuid "d2471a44-b1ef-5141-9fe5-a87c669720c3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256880 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256881 13194139533864 true] [17592186256880 1089 "q-118839" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256880 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256880 1100 "What is the distance between the points $A(8,-3,-1)$ and $B(12,5,-2)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256880 1113 #uuid "1ae8950d-1f55-5b0d-b4d8-6562835c999a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256880 1145 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(8,-3,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(12,5,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(12-8)^{2}+(5-(-3))^{2}+(-2-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256882 13194139533864 true] [17592186256881 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256881 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256881 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256882 13194139533864 true] [17592186256881 1110 #uuid "7ebf1136-0ad1-5236-b788-da4efd706743" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256882 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256882 1111 #uuid "db0f4fee-52ce-59ea-a878-1cc3251e9c31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256884 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1089 "q-118863" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{'}-8{e}^{y}=2$\nII. - $y{y}^{'}+3=\\operatorname{cos}y$\nIII. - $x{y}^{'}-8={e}^{y}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1113 #uuid "a3b6aeaa-a451-5b80-8bb6-433e2232b048" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256883 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-8{e}^{y} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =8{e}^{y}+2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=8{e}^{y}+2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{'}+3 & =\\operatorname{cos}y \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-8 & ={e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256885 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256886 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256887 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256888 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256889 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256890 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256891 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256892 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256890 13194139533864 true] [17592186256884 1110 #uuid "d629f59a-43b8-52d9-9357-8e959cb4368d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256885 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256885 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256885 1111 #uuid "3235ef37-0ffb-5843-b119-69f7fbde2c58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256886 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256886 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256886 1111 #uuid "df5f3e74-890b-5156-ba8c-31602fb54a0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256887 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256887 1111 #uuid "258eb886-5afd-548a-957e-221d02dc85f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256888 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256888 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256888 1111 #uuid "4e023746-ca48-5ec4-98d8-26a7b685ac4d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256889 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256889 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256889 1111 #uuid "ee38a5c3-7fe8-556b-bb81-d972d9343ec4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256890 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256890 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256890 1111 #uuid "471dddbc-f346-5688-a28d-86d6ac2d38da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256891 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256891 1111 #uuid "5fd83e73-697a-5565-aede-9bf0a4362b1c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256892 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256892 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256892 1111 #uuid "d79bd66a-a044-5058-9373-524b6b028706" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256894 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256896 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1089 "q-118955" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1100 "Consider the points $A(12w,2w,-4w)$ and $B(7w,-6w,2w).$ Given that the distance between $A$ and $B$ is $30\\sqrt{5},$ find the possible values of $w.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1113 #uuid "a478ded4-a3e7-57e8-9ba2-a2eb8cb92b12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256893 1145 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(12w,2w,-4w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(7w,-6w,2w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(7w-12w)^{2}+(-6w-2w)^{2}+(2w-(-4w))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5w)^{2}+(-8w)^{2}+(6w)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{w}^{2}+64{w}^{2}+36{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125}\\,\\sqrt{{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=30\\sqrt{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w| & =30\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |w| & =6 \\\\ w & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256895 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256895 13194139533864 true] [17592186256894 1110 #uuid "47bec473-b654-5bf5-8f7f-7ce0b93b538f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256895 1034 "-6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256895 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256895 1111 #uuid "0df561d3-5704-567e-9bea-30eaabf51a64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256897 13194139533864 true] [17592186256896 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256896 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256896 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256897 13194139533864 true] [17592186256896 1110 #uuid "0f8f41b8-b188-56fc-ab1f-eefeefda3503" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256897 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256897 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256897 1111 #uuid "ee79302d-cad7-5427-88cd-f0908fb97959" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256899 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1089 "q-119102" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1100 "Simplify the expression $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta .$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1113 #uuid "38ea29bc-c897-5316-8be5-65752445ef8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256898 1145 "First, we recall the identities $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the three quantities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta =1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256900 13194139533864 true] [17592186256899 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256899 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256899 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256900 13194139533864 true] [17592186256899 1110 #uuid "4afbf64c-d695-5ed2-b0e4-5d1a24b3779d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256900 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256900 1111 #uuid "d9b4e031-b7be-5d38-8cf4-7e4d0443b15d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256902 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1089 "q-119215" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1100 "Given that  $7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta =17\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,$ calculate $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta .$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1113 #uuid "8371e6c3-10d4-531f-8a1a-633dab884727" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256901 1145 "First, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }.$\n\nTo write the equation in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta $ only, we divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,$ and apply the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =17\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =17\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\ 7\\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta $ by dividing both sides of the equation by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{7} & =\\frac{17}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =\\frac{17}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256902 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256903 13194139533864 true] [17592186256902 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256902 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256902 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256903 13194139533864 true] [17592186256902 1110 #uuid "ed06f08f-7818-53e6-8631-641b2ed08fa9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256903 1034 "\\frac{17}{7}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256903 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256903 1111 #uuid "b08b20f9-5f5e-5aff-93b5-773bfab8b758" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256905 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1089 "q-119266" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1100 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1113 #uuid "5a14c4ac-9ae0-5be9-b8ff-f6429667be79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256904 1145 "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify, turn the division into multiplication, and then simplify again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta })÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }})÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256905 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256906 13194139533864 true] [17592186256905 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256905 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256905 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256906 13194139533864 true] [17592186256905 1110 #uuid "5ae3881d-9f37-5d41-87ad-00d04a4f2741" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256906 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256906 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256906 1111 #uuid "d4544c65-d138-5ebf-8267-be0c4b37fc56" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256908 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1089 "q-119376" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ff6cdd3a9cc650842bba6e546cc91fca3206279cb6d8f9d835357c3cdcb597ce.png)\n\nConsider the sample space $S$ with the Venn diagram given above. What is the value of a constant $b?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1113 #uuid "d531bcc0-5aad-57d1-a7e6-bdcbfb0a5d04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256907 1145 "From the given Venn diagram, we have $P(X)=\\frac{1}{b}$ and $P({X}^{'})=\\frac{2}{b}.$\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X)+P({X}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{b}+\\frac{2}{b} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{b} & =1 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256909 13194139533864 true] [17592186256908 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256908 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256908 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256909 13194139533864 true] [17592186256908 1110 #uuid "9a980036-158c-5b0c-a902-64f2cba3f24e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256909 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256909 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256909 1111 #uuid "bb8457db-f6e5-5ddd-b57d-62da772ce615" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256911 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1089 "q-119398" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/791a76ebbb34751b2ce345243c5de4baf95a32357ce48830e3ea43043011b5e1.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P({X}^{'}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1113 #uuid "307826c5-d518-52f5-8812-84ccd5425547" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256910 1145 "The Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=\\frac{2}{9}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}^{'}) & =1-P(X) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256911 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256912 13194139533864 true] [17592186256911 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256911 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256911 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256912 13194139533864 true] [17592186256911 1110 #uuid "a9a4000a-94f8-5b97-adfe-9613736db240" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256912 1034 "\\frac{7}{9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256912 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256912 1111 #uuid "a7449b96-505f-5add-86a5-d67ebf511a99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256914 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1089 "q-119406" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/c700c7a205ab674275e2e9f0b2dd79054853dfeaf33f6794d6d7e4196389274b.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P(X).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1113 #uuid "63041fbf-1c6b-5528-a9b1-540c3e1c23bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256913 1145 "The Venn diagram tells us that $P({X}^{'})=0.1234.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X) & =1-P({X}^{'}) \\\\  & =1-0.1234 \\\\  & =0.8766.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256914 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256915 13194139533864 true] [17592186256914 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256914 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256914 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256915 13194139533864 true] [17592186256914 1110 #uuid "2a4b6805-4f60-5a64-b64d-a1da46c28340" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1034 "0.8766" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256915 1111 #uuid "695bb784-c889-5b24-b8b4-10b5a10508ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256917 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1089 "q-119661" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1100 "Two fair $6$-sided dice are rolled. Define $A$ to be the event \"the sum of outcomes is a prime number\" Which of the following statements are true regarding this event?\n\nI. - $P(A)=0.42$ to $2$ decimal places\nII. - ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"the sum of the outcomes is an even number\"\nIII. - $P({A}^{'})=0.58$ to $2$ decimal places" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1113 #uuid "ccb354f9-ccd4-5d1d-9d93-05b8eecb5e81" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256916 1145 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. There are $36$ possible outcomes and $15$ of these have the sum of the outcomes equal to a prime, namely \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,4),(4,1),(2,3),(3,2),(1,6), \\\\  & (6,1),(3,4),(4,3),(2,5),(5,2),(5,6),(6,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{15}{36}=0.42\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false. The complement of $A$ is the event ${A}^{'},$ \"the sum of the outcomes is a composite number\".\n\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A)=0.42$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.42 \\\\  & =0.58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256918 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256919 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256920 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256921 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256922 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256923 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256924 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256925 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256920 13194139533864 true] [17592186256917 1110 #uuid "4b17352a-c753-5c59-820d-fe9d7e06eb41" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256918 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256918 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256918 1111 #uuid "59ad92fe-06ee-57cb-aec2-77491ac1bb6e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256919 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256919 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256919 1111 #uuid "940c2463-620e-5c9c-b7fe-91995fe351c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256920 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256920 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256920 1111 #uuid "3d67ee63-54ab-5bf4-8076-feb68e3aa3e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256921 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256921 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256921 1111 #uuid "f70090e3-98ef-5f3f-b28c-00eb47513251" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256922 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256922 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256922 1111 #uuid "bce7f2da-3789-5c2b-8e94-19ed4626c645" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256923 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256923 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256923 1111 #uuid "226a80ab-c24c-5168-9995-96b4cdccd0e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256924 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256924 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256924 1111 #uuid "653dfee8-c0bc-5bea-86ab-851d707ca336" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256925 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256925 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256925 1111 #uuid "e1c0e67e-d808-53cd-9858-cc01631827c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256927 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1089 "q-119699" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1100 "Three balls are drawn from a bag that contains $5$ white balls and $4$ black balls. Define $A$ to be the event \"at least one white ball is drawn.\" It is known that $P(A)=0.95$ to $2$ decimal places. To two decimal places, what is the probability of drawing three black balls?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1113 #uuid "76002dd4-3fa5-5b97-999e-21ec06af100c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256926 1145 "The event \"three black balls are drawn\" is the opposite of the event \"at least one white ball is drawn.\"\n\nSo, the event \"three black balls are drawn\" is the complement of $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'}.$\n\nSince we know that $P(A)=0.95$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.95 \\\\  & =0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256928 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256928 13194139533864 true] [17592186256927 1110 #uuid "4d625369-3894-5861-8ca4-9400a3db1aba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256928 1034 "0.05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256928 1111 #uuid "c0c7da23-01a0-51be-9e7e-72e594b04dd3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256930 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1089 "q-119781" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1100 "Which of the following statements must be true regarding any tetrahedron? \n\nI. - It has $4$ congruent faces, each in the form of a right triangle\nII. - Exactly $3$ faces meet at each vertex\nIII. - It has $4$ edges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1113 #uuid "d8bee717-f3b4-52d8-bbff-9f569aa6d04c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256929 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/adcc202ad86b782fd153566d6454269dcd50e8f1d9aca37bc42d2266c142ced8.png)\n\nLet's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. A tetrahedron is a regular polyhedron that \n\n- consists of $4$ congruent faces, each in the form of an equilateral triangle (not a right triangle), and\n\n- exactly $3$ faces meet at each vertex.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since there are $F=4$ faces, each in the form of an equilateral triangle, we have\n\n$$\nS=4\\cdot 3=12\n$$\n\nsides combined. Now, each edge of the polyhedron is created when two face sides coincide. So, the number of edges of the tetrahedron must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{S}{2}=\\frac{12}{2}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256931 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256932 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256933 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256934 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256935 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256936 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256937 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256938 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256938 13194139533864 true] [17592186256930 1110 #uuid "9ca5971d-3642-5e78-b611-8e6d7d3ff936" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256931 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256931 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256931 1111 #uuid "69302e63-a57e-5396-acc9-6dc6cea005b7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256932 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256932 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256932 1111 #uuid "89efa4a6-0420-51af-a849-84e77d8affb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256933 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256933 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256933 1111 #uuid "9a4e067d-c9c5-5fa9-be76-b089673d2924" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256934 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256934 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256934 1111 #uuid "1e257669-4e9a-5bdf-ac9d-4e4a6c397131" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256935 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256935 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256935 1111 #uuid "e0ea8bdd-632d-58e0-af4b-0c93e0683c00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256936 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256936 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256936 1111 #uuid "9e738797-cf84-5fab-a373-b7d0a87b64c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256937 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256937 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256937 1111 #uuid "60c0e146-2c9a-5ce1-aaac-193318cd408e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256938 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256938 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186256938 1111 #uuid "921f2784-76d0-5de5-864d-763447c6080c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256940 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1089 "q-120036" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1100 "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1113 #uuid "8d593e11-f74c-57da-a9e4-9637db488d53" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256939 1145 "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘})]-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={165}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}({330}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})=1-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256940 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256941 13194139533864 true] [17592186256940 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256940 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256940 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256941 13194139533864 true] [17592186256940 1110 #uuid "df3ccf03-d2f1-511e-9dd1-7c82faa09dd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256941 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256941 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256941 1111 #uuid "c946f316-e0f1-528f-b04f-487fc62bc3a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256943 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1089 "q-120087" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1100 "$10\\operatorname{sin}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}({150}^{∘})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1113 #uuid "72bde4a1-d04b-50b4-bd0e-183a00744709" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256942 1145 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={75}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {75}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}({150}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{sin}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}({150}^{∘}) & =5\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}({75}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘})]\\operatorname{csc}({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}({150}^{∘})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({150}^{∘})} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256944 13194139533864 true] [17592186256943 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256943 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256943 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256944 13194139533864 true] [17592186256943 1110 #uuid "127575f2-8f92-59b9-afdf-8307dff9b6e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256944 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256944 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256944 1111 #uuid "88ed0792-cb18-51e5-a9a3-54d22711fc17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256946 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1089 "q-120211" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1100 "Some drinks of different sizes and flavors were sold during a school party. The two-way frequency table below summarizes the sold drinks.\n\n|  | Soda | Water | Coca-Cola | Fanta | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Small | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ | $8$ | $30$ |\n| Large | $5$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $20$ |\n| Totals | $11$ | $8$ | $16$ | $15$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly selected sold drink is a small Fanta.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1113 #uuid "02046115-abbf-5f23-b026-4fef673ef48b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256945 1145 "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected drink is a small Fanta, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Small\"** and the column **\"Fanta\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Small\"** and the column **\"Fanta\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Soda | Water | Coca-Cola | Fanta | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Small | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ | $8$ | $30$ |\n| Large | $5$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $20$ |\n| Totals | $11$ | $8$ | $16$ | $15$ | $50$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8}{50}=0.16.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256947 13194139533864 true] [17592186256946 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256946 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256946 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256947 13194139533864 true] [17592186256946 1110 #uuid "ea8f1de7-1eee-58e2-b14a-6b1290a784d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256947 1034 "0.16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256947 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256947 1111 #uuid "2404a8fb-8cf7-5292-8507-88750527799b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256949 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1089 "q-120875" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1100 "Researchers surveyed students in School A on which superpower they would most like to have. The results of this survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Flying | Invisibility | Other | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $16$ | $18$ | $12$ | $46$ |\n| Male | $28$ | $12$ | $14$ | $54$ |\n| Totals | $44$ | $30$ | $26$ | $100$ |\n\nSchool B has $300$ students. Assuming the distribution of results is the same for both schools, estimate the number of School B students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1113 #uuid "2a26858a-19aa-5552-b214-8bb2060beda5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256948 1145 "To estimate the number of students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Male\"** and the column **\"Flying\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points at the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Male\"** and the column **\"Flying\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Flying | Invisibility | Other | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $16$ | $18$ | $12$ | $46$ |\n| Male | $28$ | $12$ | $14$ | $54$ |\n| Totals | $44$ | $30$ | $26$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing this number by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{28}{100}=0.28.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected School A student is a male and chooses to fly as his superpower.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of School B students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower, we multiply the total number of School B students $(300)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =300\\cdot p \\\\  & =300\\cdot 0.28 \\\\  & =84\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256949 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256950 13194139533864 true] [17592186256949 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256949 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256949 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256950 13194139533864 true] [17592186256949 1110 #uuid "e8f6fb75-fc6d-5913-93b2-5261fe6c7f00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256950 1034 "84" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256950 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256950 1111 #uuid "4500f0e5-fb34-5b1e-9aa7-f00bfd7572a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256952 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1089 "q-121065" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(2x)=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\n$a =$: {{a}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1113 #uuid "7be751c5-5761-5b27-a477-d52010e0eb74" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256951 1145 "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(2x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{30}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=15.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256953 13194139533864 true] [17592186256952 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256952 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256952 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256953 13194139533864 true] [17592186256952 1110 #uuid "0f5b13d7-90f7-5557-ad9b-bb080e3046c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256953 1034 "15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256953 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256953 1111 #uuid "8bf506eb-2b5f-58df-abc0-fd517d134b8a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256955 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256957 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1089 "q-121431" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1100 "The point  $(0,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+2).$ What points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1113 #uuid "cf204f4e-b758-5c5a-b337-1b3550b1b5b3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256954 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0+2,1)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(1))=(2,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-1+2)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0+2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(2)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+2 \\\\  & =-(1)+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256956 13194139533864 true] [17592186256955 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256955 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256955 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256956 13194139533864 true] [17592186256955 1110 #uuid "2c7eeb88-bccb-5e85-9269-bef59e4f4989" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256956 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256956 1111 #uuid "8a4290b4-1179-5391-8878-df28a5c914bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256958 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256958 13194139533864 true] [17592186256957 1110 #uuid "ad0cdf20-74e2-5ba8-bf91-ba3ecd430859" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256958 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256958 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256958 1111 #uuid "da777ae6-b120-55f4-8b2d-e46ca4e39c92" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256960 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1089 "q-121912" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1100 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2})+1=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1113 #uuid "18ed24b1-d375-51e8-ad2b-0848def589e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256959 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2})+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{{60}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & =-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={100}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={220}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={340}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={100}^{∘},{220}^{∘},{340}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$ is $220+340=560.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256961 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256961 13194139533864 true] [17592186256960 1110 #uuid "b9536be4-f080-5106-b857-a93e80b63cc1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256961 1034 "560" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256961 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256961 1111 #uuid "651f2754-926f-5093-8c02-3934d8e51f2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256963 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256965 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256967 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1089 "q-121937" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1100 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, $b=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ Find $a-(b+c).$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 3, column 1: {{r3c1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1113 #uuid "f65102c8-6fda-5ced-885c-0d555e3ecc9f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256962 1145 "To compute $a-(b+c),$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-(b+c) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-([\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256964 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256964 13194139533864 true] [17592186256963 1110 #uuid "96feac81-6330-5421-8ed4-439f33a296e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256964 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256964 1111 #uuid "f5e1e700-f53a-5f31-b1e5-83e7775f2629" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256966 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256966 13194139533864 true] [17592186256965 1110 #uuid "5eb92b71-ff02-5352-8087-3a7d6c5372bc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256966 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256966 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256966 1111 #uuid "20ab074f-0118-56c4-9b44-4bafeeac1c74" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256967 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256968 13194139533864 true] [17592186256967 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256967 1041 "r3c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256967 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256968 13194139533864 true] [17592186256967 1110 #uuid "77e61a13-55e4-51b6-9867-a32cbbee6a22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256968 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256968 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256968 1111 #uuid "a1a9057e-1899-5295-a74f-26c5f9dd16c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256970 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1089 "q-122052" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1100 "Given that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1113 #uuid "3757b1fa-bff3-5ebf-aae8-87bbb8f2ea11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256969 1145 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot (k-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(k-1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!} & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot (k-1)!}{(k-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\cancel{(k-1)!}}{\\cancel{(k-1)!}} \\\\  & =k(k+1)(k+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256971 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256972 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256973 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256974 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256975 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256972 13194139533864 true] [17592186256970 1110 #uuid "82f048d7-bf0c-5242-b17c-f6e97018bc83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256971 1034 "k(k+1)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256971 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256971 1111 #uuid "bca3db6d-c5c1-5f7d-92fc-cc4ceb7586cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256972 1034 "k(k+1)(k+2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256972 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256972 1111 #uuid "d08aad34-ac2d-5fdd-9ff8-ea9e5d1ceb98" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256973 1034 "(k+1)(k+2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256973 1111 #uuid "da27039f-cd4a-5922-b5be-9f0c9a1ec7b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256974 1034 "\\frac{1}{k(k+1)(k+2)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256974 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256974 1111 #uuid "5b3a2ccb-3f1f-546f-ad54-e9b6ad017387" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256975 1034 "\\frac{1}{(k+1)(k+2)}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256975 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256975 1111 #uuid "1c6d7373-1583-55db-bb75-706c41837842" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256976 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256977 13194139533864 true] [17592186256976 1089 "q-122063" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256976 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256976 1100 "Given that $n\\ge 2$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n-1)^{2}\\,(n-1)!}{(n-2)!}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256976 1113 #uuid "3768b9a9-d6ba-5788-9af2-24549ab90243" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256976 1145 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-1)! & =(n-1)(n-2)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n-2)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n-1)^{2}\\cdot (n-1)!}{(n-2)!} & =\\frac{(n-1)^{2}\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot (n-2)!}{(n-2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-1)^{2}\\cdot (n-1)\\cancel{(n-2)!}}{\\cancel{(n-2)!}} \\\\  & =(n-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256978 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256979 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256980 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256981 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256982 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256980 13194139533864 true] [17592186256977 1110 #uuid "e312be77-589a-5e40-87c8-4626331f81a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256978 1034 "(n-1)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256978 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256978 1111 #uuid "3e209561-4b0a-557c-802f-0a5efd206604" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256979 1034 "(n-1)^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256979 1111 #uuid "0a472835-c516-5910-805b-f7805cb08c00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256980 1034 "(n-1)^{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256980 1111 #uuid "aa2ee58b-07ea-5f37-926f-1986ccb81d64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256981 1034 "n(n-1)^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256981 1111 #uuid "629819e6-148e-5cb1-adf8-947e6d73b7dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256982 1034 "(n-1)^{2}(n-2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256982 1111 #uuid "ed37c071-0dab-5bae-9533-39575192858a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256984 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1089 "q-122079" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1100 "A shop sells $10$ fruit flavors and $12$ chocolate flavors of ice cream. How many choices of ice cream are there?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1113 #uuid "d8e036e3-cd37-5ffb-87e6-664a7ade6560" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256983 1145 "In order to find the total number of choices, we can use the **rule of sum**:\n\n$$\n10+12=22\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $22$ choices of ice cream." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256985 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256985 13194139533864 true] [17592186256984 1110 #uuid "ab2ca614-b982-5185-ba23-40e168a7197a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256985 1034 "22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256985 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256985 1111 #uuid "c7864dc5-1f1c-5878-8a1f-a85e3b704e18" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256987 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1089 "q-122144" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1100 "How many possible outcomes are there if a coin is flipped twice, a six-sided die is rolled three times, and a tetrahedral (four-sided) die is rolled once?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1113 #uuid "bfa71c25-63e9-530b-b8d9-c3e3f8a5bfb2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256986 1145 "For the coin, there are $2$ outcomes: \"Heads\" and \"Tails\".\n\nFor the six-sided die, there is a total of $6$ outcomes: $1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\nFor the tetrahedral die, there is a total of $4$ outcomes: $1,2,3,4.$\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total Possible Outcomes} & =\\text{Number of outcomes when the coin is flipped the first time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the coin is flipped the second time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the first time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the second time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the third time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the tetrahedral die is rolled the one time} \\\\  & =2\\times 2\\times 6\\times 6\\times 6\\times 4 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\times {6}^{3}\\times 4 \\\\  & =4\\times 216\\times 4 \\\\  & =3\\,456.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $3\\,456$ possible outcomes." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256988 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256988 13194139533864 true] [17592186256987 1110 #uuid "2fac9a33-1beb-5d7f-94e8-9a82cdb6858d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256988 1034 "3\\,456" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256988 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256988 1111 #uuid "d4240970-1c4e-5367-8994-1981ec63f032" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256989 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256990 13194139533864 true] [17592186256989 1089 "q-122146" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256989 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256989 1100 "In an ice cream shop offering twelve flavors of ice cream, half a dozen toppings, and half a dozen syrups, how many combinations are possible when selecting one flavor, one topping, and one syrup?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256989 1113 #uuid "7e6b5ed9-cae5-5916-92c5-34e0446c4eac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256989 1145 "For the ice cream flavor, there are $12$ possible choices (a dozen).\n\nFor the toppings, there are $6$ possible choices (half a dozen).\n\nFor the syrups, there are also $6$ possible choices (half a dozen).\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total Possible Choices} & =\\text{Choices for the ice cream flavor} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Choices for the topping} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Choices for the syrup} \\\\  & =12\\times 6\\times 6 \\\\  & =12\\times {6}^{2} \\\\  & =432.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $432$ possible choices." 13194139533864 true] [17592186256990 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256991 13194139533864 true] [17592186256990 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256990 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256990 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256991 13194139533864 true] [17592186256990 1110 #uuid "4c6dab44-269c-589b-89de-15a0d3593c80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256991 1034 "432" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256991 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256991 1111 #uuid "c7fa564c-942c-5555-8647-79e6cd11d597" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256992 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256993 13194139533864 true] [17592186256992 1089 "q-12218" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256992 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256992 1100 "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $5{x}^{2}+15x+k=0$ has one distinct real solution.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256992 1113 #uuid "3f53a0d5-edce-5e43-88e9-e77b8a039ddf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256992 1145 "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=5,$ $b=15,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(15)^{2}-4(5)(k) \\\\  & =225-20k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =0 \\\\ 225-20k & =0 \\\\ 20k & =225 \\\\ k & =\\frac{225}{20} \\\\ k & =\\frac{45}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\frac{45}{4}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256994 13194139533864 true] [17592186256993 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256993 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256993 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256994 13194139533864 true] [17592186256993 1110 #uuid "902ad4d0-cf99-5344-b4d5-6eeec1833710" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256994 1034 "\\frac{45}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256994 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256994 1111 #uuid "8524199f-2925-5235-89c3-8465d5e71310" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256995 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256996 13194139533864 true] [17592186256995 1089 "q-122326" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256995 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186256995 1100 "What is the diameter of a circle if its area is $\\frac{9}{25}\\pi ?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256995 1113 #uuid "f2d62cce-a90d-5450-806d-43401c6ea8b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256995 1145 "The formula for the area of a circle is $A=\\pi {r}^{2}$, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{25}\\pi  & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{25}\\cancel{\\pi } & =\\cancel{\\pi }{r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2\\cdot r=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256996 1000 #edb/ref 17592186256997 13194139533864 true] [17592186256996 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256996 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256996 1104 #edb/ref 17592186256997 13194139533864 true] [17592186256996 1110 #uuid "5974ae97-1a06-5e01-a932-830915741e4c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256997 1034 "\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186256997 1111 #uuid "112acee7-20bc-5d00-bf2d-194b800fa616" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256998 1017 #edb/ref 17592186256999 13194139533864 true] [17592186256998 1089 "q-123542" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256998 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186256998 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{csc}({90}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256998 1113 #uuid "966df727-409a-5141-bd11-436c96619e19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256998 1145 "The angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}({90}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}({90}^{∘})=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257000 13194139533864 true] [17592186256999 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186256999 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186256999 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257000 13194139533864 true] [17592186256999 1110 #uuid "e5d81087-399c-58da-b648-55b9f8dd1e58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257000 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257000 1111 #uuid "bc14ec51-2320-5036-9a6b-aa529fd9a71a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257001 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257002 13194139533864 true] [17592186257001 1089 "q-123544" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257001 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257001 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257001 1113 #uuid "147149ab-0381-536f-a9c7-1b31461b25be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257001 1145 "The angle ${0}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${0}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({0}^{∘})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({0}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257002 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257003 13194139533864 true] [17592186257002 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257002 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257002 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257003 13194139533864 true] [17592186257002 1110 #uuid "68fb5449-9e08-575e-bf36-b120ccb0ea69" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257003 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257003 1111 #uuid "fc8cdc6f-a69d-584f-b0fc-dabae251979e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257004 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257005 13194139533864 true] [17592186257004 1089 "q-123583" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257004 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257004 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257004 1113 #uuid "606b2a8d-a2fe-5af3-a7b5-53405c89d3ce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257004 1145 "The angles $\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $\\pi .$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\pi =-1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi =0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}\\pi }=\\frac{0}{-1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0+0=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257006 13194139533864 true] [17592186257005 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257005 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257005 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257006 13194139533864 true] [17592186257005 1110 #uuid "45720d87-6b35-533d-ae59-ce362ab3b985" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257006 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257006 1111 #uuid "a90d3cb0-00ad-5297-8b6e-e1655f932b2f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257008 13194139533864 true] [17592186257007 1089 "q-123586" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257007 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257007 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}0+\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257007 1113 #uuid "133173ff-502a-5d88-b605-7da6031fa128" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257007 1145 "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}0=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}0=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}0}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}0+\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1+1=2.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257009 13194139533864 true] [17592186257008 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257008 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257008 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257009 13194139533864 true] [17592186257008 1110 #uuid "a4bd8137-d637-56bb-a703-1a13466d8298" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257009 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257009 1111 #uuid "7a15362f-2b20-5913-95e9-c768b333513e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257010 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257011 13194139533864 true] [17592186257010 1089 "q-123598" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257010 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257010 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257010 1113 #uuid "92ff5704-f3f3-59c9-aa47-1a38090b6514" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257010 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${210}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257012 13194139533864 true] [17592186257011 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257011 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257011 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257012 13194139533864 true] [17592186257011 1110 #uuid "351223ef-4f05-520b-aa60-8facfda88416" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257012 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257012 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257012 1111 #uuid "db6b9649-01d4-59e3-b7fa-9f83f933b5fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257013 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257014 13194139533864 true] [17592186257013 1089 "q-123603" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257013 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257013 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257013 1113 #uuid "9e5a328a-ca83-5e2b-b8cf-4756149978bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257013 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit 