332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}=2.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257030 13194139533864 true] [17592186257029 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257029 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257029 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257030 13194139533864 true] [17592186257029 1110 #uuid "6a988b62-67d1-560e-a847-10ee6a20c795" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257030 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257030 1111 #uuid "b5f527be-209f-512b-970a-994469277415" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257031 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257032 13194139533864 true] [17592186257031 1089 "q-123630" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257031 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257031 1100 "$\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257031 1113 #uuid "8daec912-7336-52dd-9b2d-883a30398d0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257031 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=-1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257033 13194139533864 true] [17592186257032 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257032 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257032 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257033 13194139533864 true] [17592186257032 1110 #uuid "2b902f3e-bc98-54be-a58a-e2c56b53a2a8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257033 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257033 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257033 1111 #uuid "c1b72fb5-9e18-5b21-baa0-36b3d81c08eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257034 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257035 13194139533864 true] [17592186257034 1089 "q-123636" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257034 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257034 1100 "$\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257034 1113 #uuid "586e0eb6-86e4-58b1-b23d-9e2376c2b645" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257034 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=-1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257036 13194139533864 true] [17592186257035 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257035 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257035 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257036 13194139533864 true] [17592186257035 1110 #uuid "f0d773ae-97ec-58d4-8236-397edd3300f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257036 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257036 1111 #uuid "9fc8fd19-0e96-5099-b63c-88a3dde215af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257037 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257038 13194139533864 true] [17592186257037 1089 "q-123754" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257037 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257037 1100 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t-4,\\,\\,\\,y=1-2{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 4,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $b$ | $c$ | $-31$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257037 1113 #uuid "c56dbdf4-b093-5a0b-896e-fd12c3e29f0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257037 1145 "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=t-4.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=0-4=-4.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=1-2{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=1-2(2)^{2}=-7.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=1-2{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=1-2(3)^{2}=-17\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+(-7)+(-17)=-28.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257038 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257039 13194139533864 true] [17592186257038 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257038 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257038 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257039 13194139533864 true] [17592186257038 1110 #uuid "8884e2e3-45f2-59ba-a7ec-06e8f55db610" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257039 1034 "-28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257039 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257039 1111 #uuid "6b56962b-255e-51b5-8b0e-4c1c7dd1a168" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257040 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257041 13194139533864 true] [17592186257040 1089 "q-123830" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257040 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257040 1100 "Given that  ${a}_{n}=(2n)!$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257040 1113 #uuid "f7a7813f-e804-5d6c-b87c-980e27be4a3f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257040 1145 "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=(2n)!$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} & =\\frac{(2(n+1))!}{(2n)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2n+2)!}{(2n)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2n+2)\\cdot (2n+1)\\cdot (2n)!}{(2n)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2n+2)\\cdot (2n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(2n)!}}{\\cancel{(2n)!}} \\\\  & =(2n+2)(2n+1) \\\\  & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257042 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257043 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257044 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257045 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257046 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257047 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257048 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257049 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257047 13194139533864 true] [17592186257041 1110 #uuid "4f5fc15e-4bd1-594d-bf6e-a168bad9446a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257042 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257042 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257042 1111 #uuid "5ff178ed-6078-51ef-856c-8ebc0495c4b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257043 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257043 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257043 1111 #uuid "21b232e0-03f7-5eb2-adc6-bb5e6cb9d78d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257044 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257044 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257044 1111 #uuid "91faf9c1-42f4-57e9-9402-e0d79ddb9734" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257045 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257045 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257045 1111 #uuid "638f9b90-82b1-5356-974f-1720d2deaa40" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257046 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257046 1103 #edb/r