4 true] [17592186257153 1034 "(n+2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257153 1111 #uuid "f8153453-717a-5fa9-bdcd-0cb27d0e8fa9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257154 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257155 13194139533864 true] [17592186257154 1089 "q-124502" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257154 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257154 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n+6)!$ for any $n\\ge -3?$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257154 1113 #uuid "1e762a89-d904-530d-b468-be031bc28729" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257154 1145 "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n+6)!=(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+6)! & =(n+6)\\cdot \\overset{(n+5)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257156 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257157 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257158 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257159 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257160 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257156 13194139533864 true] [17592186257155 1110 #uuid "4e922b76-cdcd-576d-b343-fa35667ad6d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257156 1034 "(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)!" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257156 1111 #uuid "745c18ba-b4b2-5ca2-b01b-f674d042bf96" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257157 1034 "(n+6)\\cdot (n+4)!" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257157 1111 #uuid "fbc665a8-0d4b-52ee-8146-081448b0b6b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257158 1034 "(n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot (n+3)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257158 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257158 1111 #uuid "7dd3b879-c086-5ba1-b450-6fce908c41c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257159 1034 "(n+5)\\cdot (n+4)!" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257159 1111 #uuid "b64b5da1-db41-598c-a0fb-ea377f30778f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257160 1034 "(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257160 1111 #uuid "945bfc0d-31e2-5ee2-b31f-9eda3f097d12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257162 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1089 "q-124575" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1100 "Given that ${a}_{1}=10$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1113 #uuid "36e7cb14-a9b2-529b-981c-2ef8caf295b9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257161 1145 "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1-(-0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1+0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,000}{125} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257163 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257163 13194139533864 true] [17592186257162 1110 #uuid "24b1fc7d-8f4a-5ba5-be9f-ebcd2a012b8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257163 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257163 1111 #uuid "c8c91737-753a-5b7e-af1c-19314d43919a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257165 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1089 "q-124577" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1100 "Given that ${a}_{1}=-14$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1113 #uuid "d5e58701-9bd7-5e77-97f9-f02a87ad3341" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257164 1145 "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-14.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257166 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257166 13194139533864 true] [17592186257165 1110 #uuid "e9f4cc6b-4a73-57ba-92d8-20124206b052" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257166 1034 "-21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257166 1111 #uuid "bd517d52-a809-52ca-bfb2-6a41f1050cd9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257168 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1089 "q-124580" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1100 "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }45(0.1)^{n}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1113 #uuid "fe72df8e-159f-5c71-9114-1755109effd4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257167 1145 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=45(0.1)^{1}=4.5.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{45(0.1)^{2}}{45(0.1)^{1}}=0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }45(0.1)^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5}{(1-0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5}{0.9} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{9} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257169 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257169 13194139533864 true] [17592186257168 1110 #uuid "89695a32-33de-5291-a31b-c425b3e0c9b3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257169 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257169 1111 #uuid "f52124e0-1658-58b3-b02d-b5db6a8e4113" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257170 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257171 13194139533864 true] [17592186257170 1089 "q-124582" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257170 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257170 1100 "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257170 1113 #uuid "00c8c66f-75df-508a-b3d1-06c349afffda" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257170 1145 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}{15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257172 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257172 13194139533864 true] [17592186257171 1110 #uuid "6e203945-6008-5260-8b6a-c5753324154c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257172 1034 "30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257172 1111 #uuid "6a3ac2e8-a0b4-5eec-a2cb-2e94e0572103" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257174 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1089 "q-124585" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1100 "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1113 #uuid "9e0bc989-6c13-538f-9204-1071f5bfda3f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257173 1145 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}{15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257175 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257175 13194139533864 true] [17592186257174 1110 #uuid "bf43fe7a-26b2-5fc0-8910-6663e9a0868c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257175 1034 "10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257175 1111 #uuid "8947bc3a-7ecb-51af-ac74-5b49f9403f42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257177 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1089 "q-124586" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1100 "${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1113 #uuid "4d9ee369-331b-5825-8ec6-ff1d89ebe2f6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257176 1145 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}=16(\\frac{1}{16})=1 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}=16(\\frac{1}{32})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257178 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257178 13194139533864 true] [17592186257177 1110 #uuid "82825abf-7a8b-5dfe-821a-e491924b1552" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257178 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257178 1111 #uuid "4acbbefc-1b16-5c59-8b6e-56d7e6481078" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257180 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1089 "q-124587" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1100 "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1113 #uuid "51438042-d192-59cd-9eae-aac65ca4ba26" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257179 1145 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=12 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{24}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{24}{5})}{12}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257181 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257181 13194139533864 true] [17592186257180 1110 #uuid "02f66923-1333-5f92-8020-0f438e3502a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257181 1034 "20" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257181 1111 #uuid "fd9bc9cd-64be-5155-9556-0bcc2ac93274" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257183 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1089 "q-124710" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1100 "The angle $\\theta =-{675}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1113 #uuid "aef049cc-770d-5d6c-beb4-622b042039bd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257182 1145 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{305}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{675}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{315}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{675}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={45}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${45}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{675}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e9e985d1b9787a05c97ead1fa6d80ef411bc1a636bd7a670a221d663d7041f5c.png)\n\nWe see that ${45}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant I. Therefore, $-{675}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257184 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257185 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257186 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257187 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257185 13194139533864 true] [17592186257183 1110 #uuid "713cd814-f1b5-5784-a54e-b1bff8791608" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257184 1034 "Quadrant II" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257184 1111 #uuid "99fb90fd-4eef-58fd-8fca-8f1d47988f02" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257185 1034 "Quadrant I" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257185 1111 #uuid "6f26502d-c71d-5b2e-af32-b17010a3f756" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1034 "Quadrant III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257186 1111 #uuid "69115848-ea6d-5953-bb32-bccba4c3250e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257187 1034 "Quadrant IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257187 1111 #uuid "6b6483ef-d505-5969-b996-0b0edd3f1d72" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257188 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257189 13194139533864 true] [17592186257188 1089 "q-124719" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257188 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257188 1100 "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{27\\pi }{10}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257188 1113 #uuid "8ce00861-6b60-5530-adb7-2260647c73a2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257188 1145 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{27\\pi }{10}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{27\\pi }{10}+2\\pi =-\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$\n\n- $-\\frac{27\\pi }{10}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{13\\pi }{10}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{27\\pi }{10}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{13\\pi }{10}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/859964e94518cf2f6270c425cb6cc0db99bbde2af83c19b1f3cdae0083644b06.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{13\\pi }{10}$ lies in quadrant III. Therefore, $-\\frac{27\\pi }{10}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257190 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257191 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257192 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257193 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257192 13194139533864 true] [17592186257189 1110 #uuid "20e30312-fd1d-55eb-ad54-8557b97f1df8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257190 1034 "Quadrant II" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257190 1111 #uuid "997613b3-bfc8-5ed3-ba9e-bfe36a2278ab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257191 1034 "Quadrant I" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257191 1111 #uuid "e0afcaf6-96f9-5f04-b47a-1f7fc76fdae6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257192 1034 "Quadrant III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257192 1111 #uuid "267bc248-0e86-55b6-a42f-7d345142ce95" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257193 1034 "Quadrant IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257193 1111 #uuid "c4a8c567-83cd-5324-b82c-c56a02442ee8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257194 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257195 13194139533864 true] [17592186257194 1089 "q-124893" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257194 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257194 1100 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }(0.75)^{n-3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257194 1113 #uuid "a1070fac-dc84-5279-ab3e-7a24f1bc3b90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257194 1145 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{4}=(0.75)^{4-3}=0.75\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n{a}_{5}=(0.75)^{5-3}={0.75}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{{0.75}^{2}}{0.75}=0.75.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }(0.75)^{n-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.75}{1-0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.75}{0.25} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257196 13194139533864 true] [17592186257195 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257195 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257195 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257196 13194139533864 true] [17592186257195 1110 #uuid "d2c76489-c2c5-5932-a0c5-2b6042b00049" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257196 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257196 1111 #uuid "42da0ddb-f88f-5ff5-8e44-3d15fee0a2a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257197 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257198 13194139533864 true] [17592186257197 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257200 13194139533864 true] [17592186257197 1089 "q-125138" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257197 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257197 1100 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction.\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257197 1113 #uuid "bde60051-298e-59aa-a76e-9c9fabb0aba6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257197 1145 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+b & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+5 \\\\ 4+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2a+b| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(2a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257198 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257199 13194139533864 true] [17592186257198 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257198 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257198 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257199 13194139533864 true] [17592186257198 1110 #uuid "f4a95cd3-7d33-5b9e-86f2-7f568b3a6e4e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257199 1034 "\\frac{5}{13}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257199 1111 #uuid "186922e0-79e7-5bea-a1c9-9dc53e50e9f4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257201 13194139533864 true] [17592186257200 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257200 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257200 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257201 13194139533864 true] [17592186257200 1110 #uuid "05f5166b-2a10-5206-90d3-d3328b1a0f86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257201 1034 "\\frac{12}{13}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257201 1111 #uuid "fc68583d-2af0-5183-94e7-b79dc5a4617b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257202 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257203 13194139533864 true] [17592186257202 1089 "q-125241" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257202 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257202 1100 "Use the $p$-test to determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{{n}^{9}}\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257202 1113 #uuid "0cf29daa-b0e5-53b9-baa2-725e5b094a4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257202 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{9}}.\n$$\n\nThis is a $p$-series with $p=9.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\nNote that the factor of $18$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257204 13194139533864 true] [17592186257203 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257203 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257203 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257204 13194139533864 true] [17592186257203 1110 #uuid "c5648d9c-3272-5b32-a9f7-d1439002ed53" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257204 1034 "18" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257204 1111 #uuid "9223958e-a313-563a-b01a-410817ff2f1e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257205 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257206 13194139533864 true] [17592186257205 1089 "q-125247" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257205 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257205 1100 "Use the $p$-test to determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2{n}^{3}}\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257205 1113 #uuid "4212a544-54a4-5b52-9acc-dd7e93b7e947" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257205 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis is a $p$-series with $p=3.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\nNote that the factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257206 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257207 13194139533864 true] [17592186257206 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257206 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257206 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257207 13194139533864 true] [17592186257206 1110 #uuid "db64e579-2ebf-5f08-88cd-da538103aeb4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257207 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257207 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257207 1111 #uuid "d133462b-1549-5105-a881-96650fbe79ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257208 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257209 13194139533864 true] [17592186257208 1089 "q-125257" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257208 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257208 1100 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257208 1113 #uuid "79f1c9d5-2149-5539-a2fe-8aa88505e603" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257208 1145 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4}}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=4.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}} & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{1/3}}{{n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/6}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257210 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257211 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257212 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257213 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257214 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257215 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257216 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257217 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257215 13194139533864 true] [17592186257209 1110 #uuid "d57bfeb9-ff6f-5f21-8379-8a644a1e8993" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257210 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257210 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257210 1111 #uuid "3912c428-8308-54b9-90a5-343e36b58e8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257211 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257211 1111 #uuid "a229ba77-fdb2-518d-85ef-4ca70a20e0c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257212 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257212 1111 #uuid "9c355002-1b19-50ea-85dc-9abb1f0d9285" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257213 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257213 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257213 1111 #uuid "3018a463-e4d9-528d-ba7f-3117f1126da6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257214 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257214 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257214 1111 #uuid "0d42ce8b-b675-535e-a014-9de87dc59b8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257215 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257215 1111 #uuid "3dd98ab1-5c6a-574c-a40b-91694bdb0f1e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257216 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257216 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257216 1111 #uuid "1ed076b8-4cfc-5d46-88a3-654a35b95d60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257217 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257217 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257217 1111 #uuid "a547bd5d-34b0-54f5-bf2e-6957451f460e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257218 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257219 13194139533864 true] [17592186257218 1089 "q-125396" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257218 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257218 1100 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{4{n}^{3}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[5]{{n}^{3}}}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257218 1113 #uuid "71e47f74-abbf-5c16-a637-21fb96159208" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257218 1145 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{{n}^{5/4}} \\\\  & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{4{n}^{3}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}\n$$\n\n \nand this is a $p$-series with $p=3.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[5]{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{1/2}}{{n}^{3/5}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/10}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series divergess by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257220 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257221 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257222 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257223 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257224 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257225 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257226 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257227 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257227 13194139533864 true] [17592186257219 1110 #uuid "6458c492-81ff-5d30-91f6-062ce4df0fc0" 13194139533864 true] [1759218