1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{n}^{5}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{7}}}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257228 1113 #uuid "e8a9197c-7c16-580e-b5da-6dee677da60d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257228 1145 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=2.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{n}^{5}}=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=5.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{7}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\cdot {n}^{1/3}}{{n}^{7/4}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/12}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{12}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257230 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257231 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257232 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257233 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257234 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257235 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257236 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257237 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257235 13194139533864 true] [17592186257229 1110 #uuid "334686ca-1c54-5ac9-b7f3-b0b671401645" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257230 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257230 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257230 1111 #uuid "7819d449-01e9-5b16-a26a-433627b519ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257231 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257231 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257231 1111 #uuid "9d253ade-e26c-5024-98c2-c679cd173dd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257232 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257232 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257232 1111 #uuid "2918cd0c-06c0-5882-9896-90f1887dbc68" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257233 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257233 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257233 1111 #uuid "e4953611-76ff-5149-b100-68d565619ece" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257234 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257234 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257234 1111 #uuid "13ba2729-bc00-58be-9786-4da25b30bf6c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257235 1111 #uuid "453f20c4-494d-50fe-8398-c5c545279843" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257236 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257236 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257236 1111 #uuid "59f9339a-fb24-56d6-816d-d28aa94c5e16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257237 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257237 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257237 1111 #uuid "dc2ee59a-25b0-56bb-adc8-e30a6848104a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257238 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257239 13194139533864 true] [17592186257238 1089 "q-127614" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257238 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257238 1100 "If\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257238 1113 #uuid "4b0686b1-334e-545d-8566-76f487f19d14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257238 1145 "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (x-2)-6\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=-32$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x+32 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257240 13194139533864 true] [17592186257239 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257239 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257239 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257240 13194139533864 true] [17592186257239 1110 #uuid "71885dd8-5771-5616-abe8-685fda47e1b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257240 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257240 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257240 1111 #uuid "18c6267b-b6e1-588e-a95e-c1c0f10bdcac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257241 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257242 13194139533864 true] [17592186257241 1089 "q-127701" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257241 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257241 1100 "Calculate the minor of the entry ${p}_{32}$ in the matrix\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257241 1113 #uuid "f7eee920-abd1-5e92-872a-5f2d162a508d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257241 1145 "The minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ (third row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}8 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 3 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8\\cdot 6-4\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =48+20 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257242 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257243 13194139533864 true] [17592186257242 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257242 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257242 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257243 13194139533864 true] [17592186257242 1110 #uuid "0f71b00e-de6c-5483-9764-a413e5bd29b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257243 1034 "68" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257243 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257243 1111 #uuid "cedb97a5-a477-5288-a35c-c5df85b5d92d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257245 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1089 "q-130520" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b9c13f97b40699c11e5b3f4f1c77457177cf83418ce08d7a30420117b816f687.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is given by the graph above. Calculate $f(-1)+f(3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1113 #uuid "7c33c986-dc79-5316-96bc-a5c56e151eff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257244 1145 "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-1,$ we have $y=0.$ So, $f(-1)=0.$\n\n- When $x=3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(3)=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1)+f(3)=0+(-2)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257246 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257246 13194139533864 true] [17592186257245 1110 #uuid "438f7e7f-aba9-5f26-8a88-a8e64341593f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257246 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257246 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257246 1111 #uuid "7eddc1dd-c230-5bc7-af48-2e6fe476b329" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257248 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1089 "q-130569" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1100 "Evaluate $f(2)$, where $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1113 #uuid "13175d1d-ea3b-53e4-8a6a-5d9e08d8c7ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257247 1145 "We substitute $x=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x} \\\\ f(2) & =\\sqrt{2+7}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}+16 \\\\  & =3+16 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257249 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257249 13194139533864 true] [17592186257248 1110 #uuid "d7960ed4-5ae5-5275-b08e-12ab6f8ca413" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257249 1034 "19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257249 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257249 1111 #uuid "9b977c7e-eb1a-5dec-87f2-788ad6289b79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257251 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1089 "q-131287" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1113 #uuid "3d19938b-2667-5667-8ce7-7a27acfa3c8e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257250 1145 "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $0.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $0.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $0,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257252 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257252 13194139533864 true] [17592186257251 1110 #uuid "994bc45e-12a8-51c1-a1ea-6029cb1bf4be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257252 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257252 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257252 1111 #uuid "b50f64b3-2e2a-557c-bffc-304beb447550" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257254 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257256 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1089 "q-131292" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1113 #uuid "2ff2b93a-a0bc-53af-ab2c-ee40e773dd3a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257253 1145 "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257255 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257255 13194139533864 true] [17592186257254 1110 #uuid "27dad36a-52c3-50b4-a3c5-7ee3df9f5369" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257255 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257255 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257255 1111 #uuid "e334ffef-75e9-5487-9f3a-77355634a401" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257257 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257257 13194139533864 true] [17592186257256 1110 #uuid "8f502a90-720e-5765-9320-6a3936f57944" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257257 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257257 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257257 1111 #uuid "c598337d-157e-5bbe-8042-0760485016e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257258 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257259 13194139533864 true] [17592186257258 1089 "q-131295" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257258 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257258 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257258 1113 #uuid "623629e1-214d-5c86-b00a-3914400f8f18" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257258 1145 "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above equation assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nIn part 5, we found that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2,$ and from part 3, we found ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}) & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 0+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-0+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257260 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257260 13194139533864 true] [17592186257259 1110 #uuid "ae9fd745-e806-5ad6-895e-42e00c4eb393" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257260 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257260 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257260 1111 #uuid "c581f746-f4b4-565e-828e-9a9c760c5c80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257261 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257262 13194139533864 true] [17592186257261 1089 "q-131296" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257261 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257261 1100 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=6,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257261 1113 #uuid "ad6f060b-5cea-5986-996d-da86ba31d49c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257261 1145 "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}Cf(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot 2 & =6 \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257263 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257263 13194139533864 true] [17592186257262 1110 #uuid "81345881-d6c3-5516-a270-9dc397aaefa6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257263 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257263 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257263 1111 #uuid "d315cf83-135e-579e-a342-0047cef82766" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257264 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257265 13194139533864 true] [17592186257264 1089 "q-131299" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257264 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257264 1100 "What are the points of discontinuity of $f(x)?$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257264 1113 #uuid "40aecb4c-2e61-5d25-88a2-3f0a2a1cdd82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257264 1145 "The point of discontinuity of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of this point without taking our pencil off of the page." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257266 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257266 13194139533864 true] [17592186257265 1110 #uuid "111b6e45-7881-53ea-9f81-b27ffe1c218f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257266 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257266 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257266 1111 #uuid "345f47e2-55a9-53ee-b1e4-65537b163cf4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257268 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257270 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1089 "q-131334" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1100 "**In ascending order**, what are the points of discontinuity of $g(x)?$\n\n$g(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1113 #uuid "b49288e9-8609-57aa-a4ab-46cd78301ba3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257267 1145 "The points of discontinuity of $g(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these points without taking our pencil off of the page." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257269 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257269 13194139533864 true] [17592186257268 1110 #uuid "f7438fd0-3b41-5961-8782-b5d7f5ecf71d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257269 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257269 1111 #uuid "b34b8521-9fd5-518c-8ea6-3f5b18e43f35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257270 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257271 13194139533864 true] [17592186257270 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257270 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257270 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257271 13194139533864 true] [17592186257270 1110 #uuid "7ee8d153-f1d7-52a2-bfdf-7f602f427519" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257271 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257271 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257271 1111 #uuid "99802942-acec-5b70-82d3-4643460a87e3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257273 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1089 "q-133024" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1100 "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$f(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1113 #uuid "e6095b63-ddc6-568b-9380-729c9ade9f90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257272 1145 "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4\\sqrt{x}-2 \\\\ f(4) & =4\\sqrt{4}-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-2 \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257273 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257274 13194139533864 true] [17592186257273 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257273 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257273 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257274 13194139533864 true] [17592186257273 1110 #uuid "948fdae7-05ca-5624-a16e-022fb33b1775" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257274 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257274 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257274 1111 #uuid "513a423d-a88f-5e18-9d15-f24ca8887917" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257275 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257276 13194139533864 true] [17592186257275 1089 "q-133025" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257275 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257275 1100 "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257275 1113 #uuid "f1754b32-47e1-5590-8a91-bbc36a0323fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257275 1145 "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x>2,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=5-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257277 13194139533864 true] [17592186257276 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257276 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257276 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257277 13194139533864 true] [17592186257276 1110 #uuid "72b4136f-4387-5525-beff-16f19e2b0713" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257277 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257277 1111 #uuid "238dbe8d-4db0-537e-aa65-e66cdd944e03" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257278 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257279 13194139533864 true] [17592186257278 1089 "q-133026" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257278 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257278 1100 "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257278 1113 #uuid "6c8691c2-0337-5d26-bf63-fbc25bd0f7e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257278 1145 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-1 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257280 13194139533864 true] [17592186257279 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257279 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257279 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257280 13194139533864 true] [17592186257279 1110 #uuid "fe208381-c8c6-5813-a86d-b35b92287858" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257280 1034 "1/3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257280 1111 #uuid "80f8910e-5b03-5984-9910-47019bc5d244" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257282 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1089 "q-133027" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1100 "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-1,6],$ find the global minimum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{min}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1113 #uuid "84eeaa13-d68d-5cfc-8888-807b5ced7f65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257281 1145 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n* A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $h(x)=-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bc25d9452e02cfd80ae586368897ef3d917ab9bc5c8e7ea1b74c154f1d4ebb19.png)\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is ${h}_{min}=-2.$ The function reaches this value when $x=1,$ so the global minimum point is $(1,-2).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257283 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257283 13194139533864 true] [17592186257282 1110 #uuid "57a6ec67-c027-538e-9233-46de151c33f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257283 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257283 1111 #uuid "93e5b92f-ac10-506e-a57a-e16a167dbe6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257285 13194139533864 true] [17592186257284 1089 "q-133029" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257284 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257284 1100 "Find the value of $(f/h)(4).$\n\n$(f/h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257284 1113 #uuid "f6b2a0d8-4028-5a47-a42c-61c14cfc6c36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257284 1145 "The notation $(f/h)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $h(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/h)(4),$ we need to divide $f(4)$ by $h(4).$\n\nEarlier, we found the following values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =6 \\\\ h(4) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n(f/h)(4)=\\frac{f(4)}{h(4)}=\\frac{6}{1}=6.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257286 13194139533864 true] [17592186257285 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257285 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257285 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257286 13194139533864 true] [17592186257285 1110 #uuid "6df0ed28-1b60-5812-9a56-ad326420afaa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257286 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257286 1111 #uuid "d332894f-e277-5c10-8905-e605f713fc58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257287 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257288 13194139533864 true] [17592186257287 1089 "q-133030" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257287 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257287 1100 "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257287 1113 #uuid "d2f3209b-4bde-5973-b485-cd4659645aeb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257287 1145 "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$ \n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=1.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=1$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{1}-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257289 13194139533864 true] [17592186257288 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257288 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257288 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257289 13194139533864 true] [17592186257288 1110 #uuid "731f05d4-c4f7-5886-9532-afcb439d53c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257289 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257289 1111 #uuid "945b8d50-f440-5780-8ea7-c9e76f811169" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257291 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257293 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1089 "q-133031" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1100 "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [-1,1].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1113 #uuid "2339506b-6632-5223-9e01-443389461a63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257290 1145 "First, we graph the function $g(x)=3x-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/819903d614018e7945117ddd14b26cf669777c1d4f3742e095b1cebc212bf879.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-4.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,-4).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $2.$ This occurs at the point $(1,2).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-4$ and $2,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-4\\le y\\le 2,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-4\\le g(x)\\le 2.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257292 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257292 13194139533864 true] [17592186257291 1110 #uuid "06a77d84-2e97-5cb1-b50c-242f92a052c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257292 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257292 1111 #uuid "96ef80c8-2db1-5351-9296-57af895684bd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257294 13194139533864 true] [17592186257293 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257293 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257293 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257294 13194139533864 true] [17592186257293 1110 #uuid "ca89e50d-c4a3-56d4-a2ff-cc57d8270589" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257294 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257294 1111 #uuid "8c191908-eb0e-5337-9407-0cca774ce695" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257296 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1089 "q-133032" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1100 "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1113 #uuid "6672f6f6-d069-5e08-8c51-22e371f34dc7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257295 1145 "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=3x-1$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x-1)+k \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{3}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{3}+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-\\frac{1}{3}+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257297 13194139533864 true] [17592186257296 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257296 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257296 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257297 13194139533864 true] [17592186257296 1110 #uuid "4b320bbe-c8b3-5d1b-b588-89fe83588f77" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257297 1034 "1/3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257297 1111 #uuid "29abe2ec-1d6d-5b36-9243-f4f5463a1ce6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257298 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257299 13194139533864 true] [17592186257298 1089 "q-133093" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257298 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257298 1100 "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257298 1113 #uuid "4facbf96-c4c8-5f38-a25b-4b60a9204fda" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257298 1145 "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x>3,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=2x-5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =2(4)-5 \\\\  & =8-5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257300 13194139533864 true] [17592186257299 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257299 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257299 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257300 13194139533864 true] [17592186257299 1110 #uuid "d72cf376-ab9d-5294-aa38-2dbf171dad01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257300 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257300 1111 #uuid "9875c946-efae-5e29-b405-94de8fc3b770" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257301 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257302 13194139533864 true] [17592186257301 1089 "q-133094" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257301 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257301 1100 "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257301 1113 #uuid "dac5550f-7f7a-5ad7-9089-a64688784e6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257301 1145 "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257302 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257303 13194139533864 true] [17592186257302 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257302 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257302 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257303 13194139533864 true] [17592186257302 1110 #uuid "b3867252-3213-5383-8a8b-466b65ee7411" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257303 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257303 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257303 1111 #uuid "8de2c845-8e92-58ae-94f4-adea8d283336" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257305 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1089 "q-133095" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1100 "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5],$ find the global minimum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{min}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1113 #uuid "1d26441c-ac3b-59d3-b427-084701de0ed6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257304 1145 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n* A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $h(x)=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f76f7203f1ba17f8148bbc1140420034a631f46e6995a3bda5453ec3fd9a6f55.png)\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is ${h}_{min}=-4.$ The function reaches this value when $x=1,$ so the global minimum point is $(1,-4).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257306 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257306 13194139533864 true] [17592186257305 1110 #uuid "4123edc7-00ac-585c-92fc-0df39a14aa4e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257306 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257306 1111 #uuid "583857a8-0144-5997-9ea1-7321299750da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257308 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1089 "q-133098" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1100 "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1113 #uuid "db7e3081-13ac-5bac-97f6-3cdfeabac772" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257307 1145 "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$\n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=3.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=3$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(3) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257309 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257309 13194139533864 true] [17592186257308 1110 #uuid "1ba2c0da-5e9e-5cee-afa1-b64d8b64f6c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257309 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257309 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257309 1111 #uuid "2ec85d45-705f-58f2-9ecb-beeb96887d3c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257311 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257313 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1089 "q-133099" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1100 "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1113 #uuid "a61a79f3-a45a-5593-b4bc-eb0ce92364fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257310 1145 "First, we graph the function $g(x)=3x-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/47eb01a66773e5cf2b223cc3f8f4ff02caa424170a8e090ec0b1e45ed6d69741.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(0,-2).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $4.$ This occurs at the point $(2,4).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $4,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y\\le 4,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-2\\le g(x)\\le 4.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257312 13194139533864 true] [17592186257311 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257311 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257311 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257312 13194139533864 true] [17592186257311 1110 #uuid "9082cc46-6619-50c2-b109-27b601c47f11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257312 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257312 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257312 1111 #uuid "b93ae47e-c9a4-5c74-b810-01aab25b220c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257314 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257314 13194139533864 true] [17592186257313 1110 #uuid "7d698e12-f6f5-591d-a04a-2546aa9ce49f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257314 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257314 1111 #uuid "59a2bc2f-fe61-5929-96b2-b7033bdcf072" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257316 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1089 "q-133100" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1100 "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1113 #uuid "1c61acd1-ade2-5a44-8b2b-c8e5c8ab07c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257315 1145 "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=3x-2$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x-2)+k \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{3}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257317 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257317 13194139533864 true] [17592186257316 1110 #uuid "c06794a5-596c-5119-8f53-ec6c5b564819" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257317 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257317 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257317 1111 #uuid "607fe3ff-c70e-5e90-8be3-b217f42489ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257319 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1089 "q-133324" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1186087a359b3065400101c7f436c3e885bf937477bdd9f73fbdaab6771aa48a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3}).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1113 #uuid "7c776c27-a6a4-5d10-97a0-9fb241329ba9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257318 1145 "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-2,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=-2.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257320 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257320 13194139533864 true] [17592186257319 1110 #uuid "b4c911e3-9ea8-5569-8799-f7127763fe8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257320 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257320 1111 #uuid "93e8b85a-cbcf-55fd-b21d-724a4d35c312" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257322 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1089 "q-133326" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1113 #uuid "8c3c9278-79a7-5543-b4fd-fdc095dcc49a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257321 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{2-2}{(2)^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257323 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257323 13194139533864 true] [17592186257322 1110 #uuid "209517ec-e06e-50a2-b3b4-440b13e55c97" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257323 1034 "1/4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257323 1111 #uuid "039afb05-3dd1-55c1-8034-3ea673ee4a22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257325 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1089 "q-133327" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1113 #uuid "8555738f-0375-5e38-b16f-5d036c583f49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257324 1145 "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}[\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257326 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257326 13194139533864 true] [17592186257325 1110 #uuid "7e480ad3-4c1f-58fa-acab-2ec945f606c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257326 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257326 1111 #uuid "25f4ed6e-501b-5b5e-9497-a5e6e518ce62" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257328 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1089 "q-133428" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1100 "Which of the following expressions are **irrational**?\n\nI. - ${8}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}$\nII. - ${3}^{20}\\cdot 7.0523$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {2}^{-15}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1113 #uuid "9e4d6294-f5f5-58cd-9f5f-15ca164270ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257327 1145 "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Expression I is rational. Notice that $\\sqrt{36}=6$ is a rational number. The number ${8}^{5}$ is rational too. So, their product must be rational.\n\n- Expression II is rational. Since both ${3}^{20}$ and $7.0523$ are rational, their product must be rational.\n\n- Expression III is irrational. Since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational and ${2}^{-15}$ is rational, their product must be irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257329 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257330 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257331 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257332 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257333 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257334 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257335 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257336 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257333 13194139533864 true] [17592186257328 1110 #uuid "356dec81-7040-5fb4-a3fe-80097d3f0467" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257329 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257329 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257329 1111 #uuid "ed3b8032-3546-54b7-9d11-2ba01b471b1b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257330 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257330 1111 #uuid "1df3a48b-57b3-5f2d-941e-363407328668" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257331 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257331 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257331 1111 #uuid "4a90ccd6-f190-51f1-a576-e641b61d1e99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257332 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257332 1111 #uuid "81ca6aa0-5893-5596-9769-736c214e24b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257333 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257333 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257333 1111 #uuid "12cdbc21-5d48-577c-b672-10da61434ad8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257334 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257334 1111 #uuid "106cd227-8a53-5674-a963-1cd2ccac7aba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257335 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257335 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257335 1111 #uuid "44a6a700-fdf8-5f65-8367-a9a1f1bc0cd1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257336 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257336 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257336 1111 #uuid "2203de7e-d536-5ffd-945d-3aa3b477d87e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257338 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1089 "q-134130" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - Only one player has exactly $10$ years of experience.\nII. - There are two players aged $21.$\nIII. - None of the players has fewer than $6$ years of experience." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1113 #uuid "e2ae6b9a-e5a8-56dc-8196-49d72b8c47d7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257337 1145 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $10,$ namely, $(21,10).$ This means only one of the players has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/209759f5d70fd8587740d1da840f9da838a3c9a055a0d1e962f76c1cd6341fd3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $21,$ namely, $(21,10)$ and $(21,12).$ This means that two players are $21$ years old.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, one player has fewer than $6$ years of experience. The dot below the horizontal line represents this player.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257339 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257340 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257341 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257342 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257343 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257344 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257345 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257346 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257344 13194139533864 true] [17592186257338 1110 #uuid "4271acfa-9f19-528d-b7fe-83b00c07c70b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257339 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257339 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257339 1111 #uuid "2ccadedc-ac9d-501f-ac6f-af1d32f00024" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257340 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257340 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257340 1111 #uuid "d3c896c4-9756-5b21-afd7-840dfc0de007" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257341 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257341 1111 #uuid "58668d09-0f3a-5dc7-9e04-380e43c43f7b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257342 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257342 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257342 1111 #uuid "f10e9224-203c-5056-a43b-1139e16b53e6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257343 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257343 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257343 1111 #uuid "eddc8efc-da25-514e-ae6d-122bd4f49d5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257344 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257344 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257344 1111 #uuid "935540f8-c858-5bef-8d72-cd50516436da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257345 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257345 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257345 1111 #uuid "5aee3821-2280-5178-9380-34cbe7e976d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257346 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257346 1111 #uuid "5dde2dc4-e38c-5c9e-836a-729f2fd9d7be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257347 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257348 13194139533864 true] [17592186257347 1089 "q-134527" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257347 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257347 1100 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.1)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257347 1113 #uuid "fbd05050-bcf2-5bb4-9011-c5d2c51ceabc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257347 1145 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}\\approx 1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.1)^{5} & =(1+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & \\approx 1+5(0.1)+10(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.1)+10(0.01) \\\\  & =1+0.5+0.1 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.1)^{5}-1.6|}{(1.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & \\approx 0.65%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257349 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257349 13194139533864 true] [17592186257348 1110 #uuid "ca19158c-3589-51ce-9250-40e2ad38e966" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257349 1034 "0.65%" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257349 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257349 1111 #uuid "429a1aa9-f72c-50b7-a22c-27192a8b7efa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257351 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257353 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257355 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1089 "q-13468" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1100 "Determine the real roots of the cubic equation $2{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x=-3{x}^{3}.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1113 #uuid "73b7efc6-6b1f-5e8e-b511-0bc919992361" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257350 1145 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x=-3{x}^{3} \\\\ 5{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5x.$ We can factor out the $5x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x & =0 \\\\ 5x({x}^{2}-8x+7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${x}^{2}-8x+7$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x({x}^{2}-8x+7) & =0 \\\\ 5x(x-1)(x-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=7.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257351 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257352 13194139533864 true] [17592186257351 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257351 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257351 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257352 13194139533864 true] [17592186257351 1110 #uuid "5c144115-70f1-5567-83f5-e0fb71d3f663" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257352 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257352 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257352 1111 #uuid "14655ff6-bf2e-5fab-b23c-0740cbced876" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257353 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257354 13194139533864 true] [17592186257353 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257353 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257353 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257354 13194139533864 true] [17592186257353 1110 #uuid "71df7fd7-7b26-5c2d-8809-b504f56db558" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257354 1111 #uuid "35b6d5ca-5b61-555f-a534-35ad85260dc8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257356 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257356 13194139533864 true] [17592186257355 1110 #uuid "442f30a8-7af7-5011-bb14-90b9a46d8c25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257356 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257356 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257356 1111 #uuid "24dc3cdc-0c4d-5ea2-8e04-f8924a82a9d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257357 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257358 13194139533864 true] [17592186257357 1089 "q-134815" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257357 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257357 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cec25c6ab182494771824bae8c2182bf38b57e2d1e81d913e3e49916c8cc3d60.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - When $x=8,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\nII. - The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\nIII. - This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257357 1113 #uuid "d5d30017-4bac-5ef9-907f-2a33c3ed66b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257357 1145 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=8$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates  this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257359 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257360 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257361 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257362 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257363 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257364 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257365 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257366 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257362 13194139533864 true] [17592186257358 1110 #uuid "f244f4e1-13b7-5e68-bfa8-3655a8868455" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257359 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257359 1111 #uuid "1198c783-6d09-59c8-83de-ea02f8aaafbf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257360 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257360 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257360 1111 #uuid "597a71cf-9864-5336-b079-cb365d18d538" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257361 1111 #uuid "9eb50253-a975-5059-90ce-f2edaee71701" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257362 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257362 1111 #uuid "60f285b6-518e-544a-83b3-61a07eb08a67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257363 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257363 1111 #uuid "2d51af7a-49e9-5c4e-b237-6a5f7b502cfb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257364 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257364 1111 #uuid "0909f57e-a765-5887-be02-0912931e4062" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257365 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257365 1111 #uuid "a50c314e-f8f7-5634-8543-bfcb643fd0f9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257366 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257366 1111 #uuid "c2c32971-4119-5de3-be91-98e96faa25f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257368 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257370 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257372 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257374 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1089 "q-13550" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1100 "Find $K+L-(K+J)$, if\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 1, column 2: {{r1c2}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 2: {{r2c2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1113 #uuid "eb48a317-e064-5f22-b418-b65055f852ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257367 1145 "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K+L-(K+J) & =K+L-K-J \\\\  & =\\cancel{K}+L-\\cancel{K}-J \\\\  & =L-J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L-J & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-5 & 6-2 \\\\ 6-2 & 6-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257368 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257369 13194139533864 true] [17592186257368 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257368 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257368 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257369 13194139533864 true] [17592186257368 1110 #uuid "ca8e1263-f3fe-579d-8e20-d7beb1ea8ae8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257369 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257369 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257369 1111 #uuid "19fea0f1-db68-53b8-b64d-2d81bb965cd8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257371 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1041 "r1c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257371 13194139533864 true] [17592186257370 1110 #uuid "db11b31c-592b-587b-af27-7145a6680bc1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257371 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257371 1111 #uuid "87bc8ad6-e689-5792-9a80-b23182d9d445" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257373 13194139533864 true] [17592186257372 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257372 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257372 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257373 13194139533864 true] [17592186257372 1110 #uuid "5a458555-43c2-595b-9bea-97070415fc6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257373 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257373 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257373 1111 #uuid "25e2da5a-b65e-5688-8adf-7728bf6c3d29" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257375 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1041 "r2c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257375 13194139533864 true] [17592186257374 1110 #uuid "73f5c65a-f17f-5663-b1ae-b6481c8333c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257375 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257375 1111 #uuid "827ee7ec-62cb-5317-8a23-cb58c8957e2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257376 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257377 13194139533864 true] [17592186257376 1089 "q-13594" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257376 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257376 1100 "Given $f(x)=7x+3$ and $g(x)=2-5x,$ find $(fg)(1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257376 1113 #uuid "084cf399-5cc4-5ae8-9e6f-6bbf5eb1b6ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257376 1145 "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(1),$ we find $f(1)$ and $g(1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =7(1)+3=10 \\\\ g(1) & =2-5(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(1)=f(1)g(1)=10\\cdot (-3)=-30\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(7x+3)(2-5x) \\\\  & =-35{x}^{2}-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(1) & =-35(1)^{2}-(1)+6 \\\\  & =-35-1+6 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257378 13194139533864 true] [17592186257377 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257377 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257377 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257378 13194139533864 true] [17592186257377 1110 #uuid "7e585b38-6c42-5ef4-ad3e-c2eee41d3fb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257378 1034 "-30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257378 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257378 1111 #uuid "2df2d8f5-89b3-57e4-8f4a-d270b42092e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257379 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257380 13194139533864 true] [17592186257379 1089 "q-13595" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257379 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257379 1100 "Given $f(x)=x+2$ and $g(x)=x-5$, find $(fg)(-1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257379 1113 #uuid "8c2c26f6-ca4a-50fd-a1f5-f21eba02c0f0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257379 1145 "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)+2=1 \\\\ g(-1) & =(-1)-5=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-1)=f(-1)g(-1)=1\\cdot (-6)=-6\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(x+2)(x-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+2x-10 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-1) & =(-1)^{2}-3(-1)-10 \\\\  & =1+3-10 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257380 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257381 13194139533864 true] [17592186257380 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257380 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257380 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257381 13194139533864 true] [17592186257380 1110 #uuid "60051f1b-1cc5-57b3-9d12-2baaf0da0965" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257381 1034 "-6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257381 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257381 1111 #uuid "50417128-ff7a-5375-8f9b-6380919d7727" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257383 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257385 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1089 "q-13602" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ba20fb3746240004550da009e8cefa3437d559bb9f33e9cf42e97800fbddabec.png)\n\nFind the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints shown above.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1113 #uuid "30fb72b2-8ceb-5d3e-98ff-4e76c71e39e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257382 1145 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between the given points ${{(-2,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(4,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-2+4}{2},\\frac{3-2}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(1,\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257384 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257384 13194139533864 true] [17592186257383 1110 #uuid "24760f79-1c23-51bb-b2df-97129dc228c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257384 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257384 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257384 1111 #uuid "e75d8e1d-4242-5c15-8568-7a957c4a9719" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257385 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257386 13194139533864 true] [17592186257385 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257385 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257385 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257386 13194139533864 true] [17592186257385 1110 #uuid "7cc5099b-40eb-57c9-a42e-d9bb31902824" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257386 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257386 1111 #uuid "ffb907ea-d641-581c-98cf-1b962e5e9dcc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257388 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1089 "q-13607" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt{n}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1113 #uuid "f2feeaf1-eaac-5dbf-95f2-bd5007588656" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257387 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1+1/2}}=2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $2$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257388 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257389 13194139533864 true] [17592186257388 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257388 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257388 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257389 13194139533864 true] [17592186257388 1110 #uuid "f5cdc8b7-6bee-5aa6-a18a-5b3e707ce5bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257389 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257389 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257389 1111 #uuid "821eea6e-54b0-5192-84d9-7a02c98fde64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257391 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257393 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1089 "q-138998" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1100 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?\n\n$L$: {{L}}\n\nRoot test conclusion: {{root-test-conclusion}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1113 #uuid "166ee2ec-2929-59e4-ac37-3903890f3dc2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257390 1145 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{1+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{n(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}}{(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257392 13194139533864 true] [17592186257391 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257391 1041 "L" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257391 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257392 13194139533864 true] [17592186257391 1110 #uuid "c587d7f7-4217-5d39-b98b-a9d169ba528f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257392 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257392 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257392 1111 #uuid "8f537e09-63db-5400-87d5-4d6e62003142" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257394 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257395 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257396 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1041 "root-test-conclusion" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257396 13194139533864 true] [17592186257393 1110 #uuid "97e027c5-fe4d-5d1e-8776-99ade5e46f52" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257394 1034 "Diverges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257394 1111 #uuid "dd52aa37-3a3c-5f61-9789-314e2311d72c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257395 1034 "Converges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257395 1111 #uuid "7c19157b-eb38-5696-a3ca-5384bdc4700d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257396 1034 "Inconclusive" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257396 1111 #uuid "c8a60af4-dc48-5f40-a169-b2599e64fc50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257397 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257398 13194139533864 true] [17592186257397 1089 "q-14060" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257397 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257397 1100 "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $40+20+10+5+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257397 1113 #uuid "97aba825-3afc-5081-87e1-01c8ec528803" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257397 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=40$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{20}{40}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257399 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257399 13194139533864 true] [17592186257398 1110 #uuid "5eb399e4-3dec-5639-9a17-a37effe5f77c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257399 1034 "80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257399 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257399 1111 #uuid "5d59d8b0-4b56-5725-be31-52c36a0cb78c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257400 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257401 13194139533864 true] [17592186257400 1089 "q-14088" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257400 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257400 1100 "Determine the value of the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(-0.2)}^{3n}$  rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257400 1113 #uuid "308c69f7-025f-5fb5-b821-d31e639575ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257400 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6(-0.2)^{3(1)}=6(-0.2)^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=6(-0.2)^{3(2)}=6(-0.2)^{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6(-0.2)^{6}}{6(-0.2)^{3}}=(-0.2)^{3}=-0.008.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(-0.2)}^{3n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-0.2)^{3}}{1-(-0.008)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-0.008)}{1.008} \\\\  & \\approx -0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257402 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257402 13194139533864 true] [17592186257401 1110 #uuid "2c94b1fa-56ab-5b4b-9e3b-4d7264d2e3ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257402 1034 "-0.05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257402 1111 #uuid "00a19c25-9a8a-5f7c-8819-b75bf130ed1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257403 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257404 13194139533864 true] [17592186257403 1089 "q-14121" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257403 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257403 1100 "Find all the real solutions to the equation $3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9=0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257403 1113 #uuid "fe52eb5f-7e5b-51d0-b560-47f98d7ed314" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257403 1145 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9 & = \\\\ (3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2})+(27z-9) & = \\\\ {z}^{2}(3z-1)+9(3z-1) & = \\\\ (3z-1)\\cdot {z}^{2}+(3z-1)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (3z-1)({z}^{2}+9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(3z-1)({z}^{2}+9)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(3z-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-1 & =0 \\\\ 3z & =1 \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({z}^{2}+9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince a squared real number cannot be negative, the equation ${z}^{2}+9=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore,  $z=\\frac{1}{3}$ is the only real solution." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257405 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257405 13194139533864 true] [17592186257404 1110 #uuid "214026e5-fe18-58f7-8562-0bab5c37ec87" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257405 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257405 1111 #uuid "e0adb4eb-4e10-57f3-a3ac-5158e7bc1653" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257406 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257407 13194139533864 true] [17592186257406 1089 "q-14140" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257406 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257406 1100 "For the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257406 1113 #uuid "e2543d42-472f-5dce-a205-70e0b82fc936" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257406 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1\\,\\pm \\frac{6}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{12}{5},\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $24$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 12=24.$)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257408 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257408 13194139533864 true] [17592186257407 1110 #uuid "91cda7c9-e9f2-5ab9-99fe-1f74848e6195" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257408 1034 "24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257408 1111 #uuid "97261046-84b7-54d0-ae71-920ee699e7e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257409 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257410 13194139533864 true] [17592186257409 1089 "q-14177" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257409 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257409 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257409 1113 #uuid "a6a632e6-465f-5f69-9c0a-6eab5a5d00ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257409 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2+1/2}}=2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $2$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257411 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257411 13194139533864 true] [17592186257410 1110 #uuid "9bdd2145-2ded-52c3-b907-960924b52d8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257411 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257411 1111 #uuid "4ccc69ce-cc94-5927-8983-c3352e5d71af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257412 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257413 13194139533864 true] [17592186257412 1089 "q-14181" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257412 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257412 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[8]{{n}^{5}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257412 1113 #uuid "b428fa76-e87c-5e0d-99bc-e0b26ffcdc6e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257412 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/8}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{8}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $3$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257414 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257414 13194139533864 true] [17592186257413 1110 #uuid "6ce413f5-be82-5903-af44-36b68c1aca65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257414 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257414 1111 #uuid "0977bb5b-ffae-5c4b-bba5-09591162b930" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257416 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1089 "q-14183" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{\\sqrt[7]{{n}^{6}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1113 #uuid "406bddc0-a5c1-5c9c-a58e-9ea56b088d28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257415 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{6/7}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{6}{7}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $9$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257417 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257417 13194139533864 true] [17592186257416 1110 #uuid "c8af9183-c36f-5740-b5e0-1eae179145e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257417 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257417 1111 #uuid "9fbe7c5f-0d0f-5cb0-8356-4e360240f7cc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257419 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1089 "q-14186" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{n}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1113 #uuid "60fbd2f4-e4f7-5263-9d84-acda6fd29fa9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257418 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $9$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257420 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257420 13194139533864 true] [17592186257419 1110 #uuid "6a118c03-c82c-5554-b440-65048e0ca485" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257420 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257420 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257420 1111 #uuid "4f2b368c-30ff-533a-a028-f9520127f7c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257422 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257424 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1089 "q-142979" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f065f31c629cbd21a6beffe013c8786a4e8165f6dff09bde72cd3e61c8ae8a62.png)\n\nThe function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={e}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1113 #uuid "db023f5c-493e-5e6a-b5ba-9c2ad4393c03" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257421 1145 "Recall that the horizontal asymptote of $y={e}^{x-a}+b$ is $y=b.$ \n\nIn the figure above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=3.$ Hence, $b=3.$\n\nNext, we use the point $(5,4)$ to determine the value of $a.$\nSubstituting $(5,4)$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5-a}+3 & =4 \\\\ {e}^{5-a} & =1 \\\\ 5-a & =0 \\\\ a & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5$ and $b=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257423 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257423 13194139533864 true] [17592186257422 1110 #uuid "f3a192a2-9343-5383-8e4b-3367a8cbe673" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257423 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257423 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257423 1111 #uuid "51c66fe2-55c0-5652-b399-9a83c90d5f1f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257425 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257425 13194139533864 true] [17592186257424 1110 #uuid "85e2c635-2fde-5105-9f91-bec0aaf59d2f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257425 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257425 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257425 1111 #uuid "457b69e5-fe27-5686-a4ce-b0533ec2b17a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257427 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1089 "q-142991" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e1ada2941e9819e75218f9995d1913d535889c71b29efbf39ef73d91f9113205.png)\n\nThe function $h$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$5,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $h$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1113 #uuid "0ad51429-6a62-5eb4-a98f-df87784728a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257426 1145 "We have to solve $h(x)=5,$ or equivalently, $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3=5.$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3 & =5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $\\frac{3}{2}$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x})^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{9}+3 & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3+3 & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 2+3 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=9$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3=5$  is $x=9.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257428 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257428 13194139533864 true] [17592186257427 1110 #uuid "d4672edf-63c4-5c05-b468-eef9e9e3cb2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257428 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257428 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257428 1111 #uuid "1128e265-7219-5e31-98f1-7cc944d3f363" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257430 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257432 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257434 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257436 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1089 "q-142992" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1100 "What is the largest union of intervals on which the function describing the stock price is continuous?\n\n$x\\in [\\,\\,0\\,\\,,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cup $\n$($\n{{field-2}}$\\,,$\n{{field-3}}$)\\cup $\n$($\n{{field-4}}$\\,,\\,\\infty ).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1113 #uuid "9a1d8e21-fdef-5be3-a0f9-7a1cd257412c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257429 1145 "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(0,5.4)$, $(5.4,7.2)$ and $(7.2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[0,5.4).$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on $x\\in [0,5.4)\\cup (5.4,7.2)\\cup (7.2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257431 13194139533864 true] [17592186257430 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257430 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257430 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257431 13194139533864 true] [17592186257430 1110 #uuid "9355f3c0-18a6-5b69-80a1-0364e00808cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1034 "5.4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257431 1111 #uuid "043022d2-842f-5a0d-a3e6-a7431fabc0f6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257433 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257433 13194139533864 true] [17592186257432 1110 #uuid "1a799249-37a9-557d-b0c5-0430f1083a73" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257433 1034 "5.4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257433 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257433 1111 #uuid "e5bf4fa7-65cf-5ee1-a4bf-f773b42ad1bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257435 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257435 13194139533864 true] [17592186257434 1110 #uuid "879e8785-e2e8-5bd7-be03-6e8a499faf90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257435 1034 "7.2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257435 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257435 1111 #uuid "55261f6a-c49b-5feb-8d2c-d4962026c724" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257437 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1041 "field-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257437 13194139533864 true] [17592186257436 1110 #uuid "1298d7e4-33e3-5bbc-94f9-f7a723b0fb25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257437 1034 "7.2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257437 1111 #uuid "e76051ca-b880-583d-9253-e1380d6980a3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257438 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257439 13194139533864 true] [17592186257438 1089 "q-142994" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257438 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257438 1100 "What was the exact rate of change of the stock's value at week $9?$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ thousand dollars per week." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257438 1113 #uuid "3b81ed99-9a3f-5dfe-84ce-7ea4d4959a5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257438 1145 "The function ${h}^{'}(x)$ models the rate of change of the value of the stock, applicable in the domain of $h(x).$\n\nThus, the rate of change of the value of the stock at $x=9$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(9) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the stock price was $\\frac{1}{9}$ thousand dollars per week." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257440 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257440 13194139533864 true] [17592186257439 1110 #uuid "3f7aaad1-78f8-517c-9f4a-76f17a311294" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257440 1034 "1/9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257440 1111 #uuid "2e3b4618-9a7e-5db9-979c-581e6614ef24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257441 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257442 13194139533864 true] [17592186257441 1089 "q-143182" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257441 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257441 1100 "Given that  ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n)!}{{5}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n>2$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257441 1113 #uuid "866beddf-cf9f-5dad-a386-56b1a393c58f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257441 1145 "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n)!}{{5}^{n}}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{(2n+2)!}{{5}^{n+1}})}{(\\frac{(2n)!}{{5}^{n}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{n}(2n+2)!}{{5}^{n+1}(2n)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{n}\\cdot (2n+2)\\cdot (2n+1)\\cdot (2n)!}{{5}^{n}\\cdot 5\\cdot (2n)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{5}^{n}}\\cdot (2n+2)\\cdot (2n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(2n)!}}{\\cancel{{5}^{n}}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{(2n)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2n+2)(2n+1)}{5} \\\\  & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257443 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257444 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257445 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257446 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257447 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257448 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257442 1000 #edb/ref 1759218625745