db/ref 17592186257452 13194139533864 true] [17592186257451 1089 "q-143193" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257451 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257451 1100 "Given that  ${a}_{n}=2{n}^{5}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257451 1113 #uuid "9fa88825-edb5-50fd-b953-cabd8cb91f63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257451 1145 "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=2{n}^{5}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(n+1)^{5}}{2{n}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)^{5}}{{n}^{5}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{5} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}^{5} \\\\  & ={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ we have that $\\frac{1}{n}>0.$ Therefore, it follows that\n\n$$\n(1+\\frac{1}{n})>1,\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\nL={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{5}>1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257455 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257456 13194139533864 true] [17592186257452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257457 13194139533864 true