_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n+1}}{{(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{n+1-n} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{6})}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6} \\\\  & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257553 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257554 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257555 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257556 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257557 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257558 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257559 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257560 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257558 13194139533864 true] [17592186257552 1110 #uuid "64a9483f-15e7-582a-8f50-d98770d357aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257553 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257553 1111 #uuid "1da15512-9141-5658-adc0-2d796eabc0be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257554 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257554 1111 #uuid "23ce18da-bf69-52ea-aee1-4c548b5d7805" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257555 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257555 1111 #uuid "76758739-ba80-5f82-b136-044fff3dbf5b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257556 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257556 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257556 1111 #uuid "816d8401-ff89-562c-ae81-7b16d2a5bfcf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257557 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257557 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257557 1111 #uuid "323b0993-1575-5b72-888c-f46d1ee98748" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257558 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257558 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257558 1111 #uuid "1e01d71b-4a97-5dd2-989a-7f7537d1b14c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257559 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257559 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257559 1111 #uuid "2b4c4a50-ca75-5aaf-8889-0aa4c0f2819e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257560 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257560 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257560 1111 #uuid "d2a2de85-3c1d-53ef-b1a4-3dca83582c5d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257561 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257562 13194139533864 true] [17592186257561 1089 "q-143531" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257561 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257561 1100 "Find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$v(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257561 1113 #uuid "6a57fe21-061f-551f-9781-c27e61809783" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257561 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n4{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=4{(2)}^{2}=16.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257562 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257563 13194139533864 true] [17592186257562 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257562 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257562 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257563 13194139533864 true] [17592186257562 1110 #uuid "ca8409c4-5999-5423-bfeb-c04e340e43e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257563 1034 "16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257563 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257563 1111 #uuid "b5da6754-0d94-5766-8532-2c9f59a970a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257564 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257565 13194139533864 true] [17592186257564 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257567 13194139533864 true] [17592186257564 1089 "q-14358" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257564 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257564 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/781f3a15f9c0b8ac1d43e72f0911bc810dc9829f37bf64094a9d1d48f8a7e1f1.png)\n\nFind the midpoint of the line segment shown above.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257564 1113 #uuid "06a9a618-4104-5c38-8036-d75acee7cee4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257564 1145 "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between the given points ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-3,6)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{7-3}{2},\\frac{-4+6}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2},\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  & =(2,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257565 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257566 13194139533864 true] [17592186257565 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257565 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257565 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257566 13194139533864 true] [17592186257565 1110 #uuid "57926a54-2a90-573e-9bac-f527f0037ebc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257566 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257566 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257566 1111 #uuid "6747ecdd-9afe-5723-ad80-bae5a0753706" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257567 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257568 13194139533864 true] [17592186257567 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257567 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257567 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257568 13194139533864 true] [17592186257567 1110 #uuid "b15efcc2-8aec-5cd7-8978-77caf324cd6d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257568 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257568 1111 #uuid "b8e9e03c-28e2-5529-876b-ca1c1e0944d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257569 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257570 13194139533864 true] [17592186257569 1089 "q-14365" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257569 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257569 1100 "If the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(7,-4)$ and $(a,b)$ is $(2,1),$\nthen $a+b=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257569 1113 #uuid "5d86f908-7737-5796-8d80-bd80a4b73f99" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257569 1145 "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,{\\begin{aligned}a=-3 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257571 13194139533864 true] [17592186257570 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257570 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257570 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257571 13194139533864 true] [17592186257570 1110 #uuid "aec5bcbf-664d-5504-9bb2-d2b854602e61" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257571 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257571 1111 #uuid "be6ccc92-c990-5058-ada1-3e61078deb9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257572 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257573 13194139533864 true] [17592186257572 1089 "q-14388" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257572 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257572 1100 "Given the recursive sequence $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=1$ and $f(2)=3$, what is the value of $f(7)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257572 1113 #uuid "b8589e67-69ad-5bf0-a4d3-99bbd55f2e6c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257572 1145 "The recursion relation is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =3+1 \\\\ f(3) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =4+3 \\\\ f(4) & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, substituting $n=3,4,5$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5)= & f(3+2) & =f(3+1)+f(3)=f(4)+f(3)=7+4=11, \\\\ f(6)= & f(4+2) & =f(4+1)+f(4)=f(5)+f(4)=11+7=18, \\\\ f(7)= & f(5+2) & =f(5+1)+f(5)=f(6)+f(5)=18+11=29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(7)=29.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257574 13194139533864 true] [17592186257573 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257573 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257573 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257574 13194139533864 true] [17592186257573 1110 #uuid "41bc5adc-5b0b-54f7-8e1e-d70f29a06994" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257574 1034 "29" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257574 1111 #uuid "b2799b7b-24b4-5d16-80c2-ac7ae521d926" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257575 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257576 13194139533864 true] [17592186257575 1089 "q-14527" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257575 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257575 1100 "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ for the curve $y\\operatorname{cos}(x)=1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257575 1113 #uuid "d727380b-a283-52ef-aecd-bfe58ab6a644" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257575 1145 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $y\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{cos}(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x))+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =0 \\\\ y(-\\operatorname{sin}(x))+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x)}}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(0)}{\\operatorname{cos}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257577 13194139533864 true] [17592186257576 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257576 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257576 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257577 13194139533864 true] [17592186257576 1110 #uuid "c7dcdf7e-93c7-56a7-998e-b9fdf99520ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257577 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257577 1111 #uuid "2b0b3773-0d4b-5dc7-84da-e4733320c793" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257578 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257579 13194139533864 true] [17592186257578 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257581 13194139533864 true] [17592186257578 1089 "q-14614" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257578 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257578 1100 "The equation of a curve is $xy+{x}^{2}=1.$ The normal line at the point $(1,\\,0)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257578 1113 #uuid "02036e78-1a4a-5e3b-a210-404f9710dd18" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257578 1145 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(1,\\,0).$   Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2x & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(2x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,0)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2x+y}{x})}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+0}{1} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(1,\\,0)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257579 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257580 13194139533864 true] [17592186257579 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257579 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257579 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257580 13194139533864 true] [17592186257579 1110 #uuid "58ec7e67-5450-59cc-b81d-20c384c9bf65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257580 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257580 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257580 1111 #uuid "7755163d-cfe4-5031-9fba-514ea674855d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257581 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257582 13194139533864 true] [17592186257581 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257581 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257581 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257582 13194139533864 true] [17592186257581 1110 #uuid "7c3887d1-2962-5f88-bbeb-3413a3009764" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257582 1034 "\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257582 1111 #uuid "de3af54e-bd1a-5397-9399-69696aea8758" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257584 13194139533864 true] [17592186257583 1089 "q-148536" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257583 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257583 1100 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{3x+5}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{2-5x}{3x}.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257583 1113 #uuid "1ffc5e8a-01bf-55e5-8b55-8aa82dd13af8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257583 1145 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{3x+5}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{k}{3y+5}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{k}{3y+5} \\\\ x(3y+5) & =k \\\\ 3xy+5x & =k \\\\ 3xy & =k-5x \\\\ y & =\\frac{k-5x}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{k-5x}{3x}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257585 13194139533864 true] [17592186257584 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257584 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257584 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257585 13194139533864 true] [17592186257584 1110 #uuid "d00e7845-0434-54ae-a9c0-5ff550379e82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257585 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257585 1111 #uuid "80e0a1d7-1001-5c5f-9b7f-de98f181baed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257587 13194139533864 true] [17592186257586 1089 "q-148544" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257586 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257586 1100 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{6}{k-4x}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3x-6}{4x}.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257586 1113 #uuid "fed8ebe5-6227-5498-b8e6-525c8727e7e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257586 1145 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{k-4x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{6}{k-4y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{6}{k-4y} \\\\ x(k-4y) & =6 \\\\ kx-4xy & =6 \\\\ 4xy & =kx-6 \\\\ y & =\\frac{kx-6}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{kx-6}{4x}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257588 13194139533864 true] [17592186257587 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257587 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257587 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257588 13194139533864 true] [17592186257587 1110 #uuid "4810fec2-6560-5d2b-876a-62a56b696f00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257588 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257588 1111 #uuid "a74f047b-c214-5885-9d49-41a58bebc409" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257589 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257590 13194139533864 true] [17592186257589 1089 "q-152782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257589 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257589 1100 "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}(x)-5}{x}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257589 1113 #uuid "93b68d6a-209d-5990-a5e0-9593ec931e11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257589 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since $5\\notin [-1,1],$ this means that $\\operatorname{arcsin}(5)$ is undefined.\n\nTherefore, our limit is undefined, too." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257590 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257591 13194139533864 true] [17592186257590 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257590 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257590 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257591 13194139533864 true] [17592186257590 1110 #uuid "95f5d36a-0f55-50d1-bd47-29deb8c9696d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257591 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257591 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257591 1111 #uuid "9ad4d3e3-158b-5b02-b228-c155b6dacaad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257592 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257593 13194139533864 true] [17592186257592 1089 "q-152889" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257592 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257592 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}(6\\operatorname{arccos}(x))=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257592 1113 #uuid "324217ae-8df9-50ab-9ad5-02439e7104ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257592 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}(6\\operatorname{arccos}(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}(6\\operatorname{arccos}(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257594 13194139533864 true] [17592186257593 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257593 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257593 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257594 13194139533864 true] [17592186257593 1110 #uuid "1efa3dc0-deae-5f56-91c7-0f1843de34fc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257594 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257594 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257594 1111 #uuid "d92eda19-f935-5bfc-b945-ef9c1298c595" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257595 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257596 13194139533864 true] [17592186257595 1089 "q-152912" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257595 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257595 1100 "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x-3, & x<-3 \\\\ 0, & x=-3 \\\\ x+3, & x>-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-3$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x)$\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=f(-3)$\nIV. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257595 1113 #uuid "02eda226-1684-50b8-a1ed-83b979a1c53a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257595 1145 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that\n\n$$\nf(-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-3.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-x-3) \\\\  & =-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(x+3) \\\\  & =(-3)+3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-3$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=0\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=0=f(-3).$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257597 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257598 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257599 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257600 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257601 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257602 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257603 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257604 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257605 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257606 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257607 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257608 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257609 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257610 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257611 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257612 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257604 13194139533864 true] [17592186257596 1110 #uuid "5ae82df4-cd7c-58ff-a25e-011cdd4816bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257597 1034 "I, III, IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257597 1111 #uuid "1b4545dd-c671-5f4a-882e-fedfb495f197" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257598 1034 "II" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257598 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [1759218625