db/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257608 1111 #uuid "33239f59-1369-528c-a281-efcc02d63755" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257609 1034 "III, IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257609 1111 #uuid "dac95eac-3d37-5f19-aeac-9e0bf4d1d002" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257610 1034 "I, II" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257610 1111 #uuid "c2d3f792-7bce-5b94-984f-aba7e80ca910" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257611 1034 "I, II, IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257611 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257611 1111 #uuid "75049880-fd35-5317-930f-662f25d56abc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257612 1034 "II, III, IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257612 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257612 1111 #uuid "4523da4d-4d0c-50ec-941f-2b48feb414fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257613 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257614 13194139533864 true] [17592186257613 1089 "q-152928" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257613 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257613 1100 "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x+12, & x\\ne -2 \\\\ 4, & x=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)$\nIV. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257613 1113 #uuid "4dfe3453-c9ac-58b3-b12c-8c3fa9577459" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257613 1145 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(4x+12) \\\\  & =4(-2)+12 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(4x+12) \\\\  & =4(-2)+12 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=4\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=4=f(-2).$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257615 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257616 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257617 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257618 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257619 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257620 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257621 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257622 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257623 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257624 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257625 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257626 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257627 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257628 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257629 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257630 13194139533864 true] [17592186257614 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 tru