n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent\nII. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257674 1113 #uuid "21799ec1-c57d-5a61-9e39-d42822f17b26" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257674 1145 "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257676 13194139533864 true] [17592186257675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257677 13194139533864 true] [17592186257675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257678 13194139533864 true] [17592186257675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257679 13194139533864 true] [17592186257675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257680 13194139533864 true] [17592186257675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257681 13194139533864 true] [17592186257675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257682 13194139533864 true] [17592186257675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257683 13194139533864 true] [17592186257675 1040 #edb/r