139533864 true] [17592186257703 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257703 1111 #uuid "9c1fa0df-840a-5edd-95b9-8a1d0ed49477" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257705 13194139533864 true] [17592186257704 1089 "q-157136" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257704 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257704 1100 "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that ${b}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1.$\n\nI. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - $12\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is convergent" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257704 1113 #uuid "f21f9a92-e265-5a42-858b-c3523a61288f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257704 1145 "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is also divergent for $K\\ne 0.$\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any non-zero multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is true. The sum of a convergent series and a divergent series is divergent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Note that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is a **finite series.** Therefore, ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{50}{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257706 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257707 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257708 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257709 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257710 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257711 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257712 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257713 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257706 13194139533864 true] [17592186257705 1110 #uuid "4ad3a4e2-a944-5a9b-86c6-00785b7a28c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257706 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257706 1111 #uuid "22494098-8b65-5fa2-b2be-110fdcb3be8e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257707 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257707 1111 #uuid "121bb038-9e31-5a1d-b52a-6d4ad07f1776" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257708 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257708 1111 #uuid "70607e51-2474-5ba3-a376-fa8ab6de1919" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257709 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257709 1111 #uuid "967558d8-749b-573a-9452-5b2811814032" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257710 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257710 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257710 1111 #uuid "3753ca99-00a1-54cc-aba0-1684376b462e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257711 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257711 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257711 1111 #uuid "1af3e68b-7ab7-5b91-aa8f-bc70efb6cd63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257712 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257712 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257712 1111 #uuid "ea2380d0-c509-58f8-a973-f7d08f0df798" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257713 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257713 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257713 1111 #uuid "ab973a9d-f335-5266-ab60-179071c03b3c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257714 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257715 13194139533864 true] [17592186257714 1089 "q-157982" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257714 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257714 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation $\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+3x+5=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\nII. - The equation has only rational solutions.\nIII. - The equation has only irrational solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257714 1113 #uuid "c206bd09-cc76-538d-9898-d67fb118f9e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257714 1145 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $a=\\frac{1}{4},$ $b=3,$ and $c=5.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(3)^{2}-4(\\frac{1}{4})(5) \\\\  & =9-5 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $4={2}^{2}$ is a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is a nonzero perfect square, the equation must have two rational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257716 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257717 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257718 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257719 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257720 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257721 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257722 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257723 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257721 13194139533864 true] [17592186257715 1110 #uuid "74b083b9-77c7-56f1-b51c-d9c941c7b67b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257716 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257716 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257716 1111 #uuid "a5e19740-66b8-5f96-9e7c-a955bd088956" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257717 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257717 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257717 1111 #uuid "9246247b-5d70-5428-b3ec-20f913e45a67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257718 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257718 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257718 1111 #uuid "60a4c1e6-c071-57b7-a98c-bebcd5215080" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257719 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257719 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257719 1111 #uuid "99f81b75-1734-5fd0-bb7c-b9869b1723f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257720 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257720 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257720 1111 #uuid "cd3c4172-e661-5776-9d5f-2f7dda89e8be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257721 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257721 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257721 1111 #uuid "6a1ea6b8-c971-5fa6-893e-7075d3966731" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257722 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257722 1111 #uuid "1fcb7a2e-a779-5db8-81ad-e9a42dcf1107" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257723 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257723 1111 #uuid "020898bf-9988-5d10-90ea-8c9130f04e69" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257724 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257725 13194139533864 true] [17592186257724 1089 "q-158002" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257724 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257724 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation $2{x}^{2}+x-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\nII. - The equation has only rational solutions.\nIII. - The equation has only irrational solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257724 1113 #uuid "c784e4b5-c0e3-5a41-9985-7a5efc723545" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257724 1145 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-4.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(1)^{2}-4(2)(-4) \\\\  & =1+32 \\\\  & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $33$ is *not* a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true.\n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257726 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257727 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257728 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257729 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257730 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257731 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257732 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257733 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257730 13194139533864 true] [17592186257725 1110 #uuid "371c5200-94ba-5835-973b-4d663d34437a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257726 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257726 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257726 1111 #uuid "1a055fd9-0617-50fb-a775-e3d958e8e16c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257727 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257727 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257727 1111 #uuid "9feb2e74-f747-57d4-8b97-8116d0ee0c75" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257728 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257728 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257728 1111 #uuid "d2e517d8-1f68-5d43-83d2-6e039e6d0634" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257729 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257729 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257729 1111 #uuid "31acbb2d-ad20-5b0f-963a-864f1fdb0720" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257730 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257730 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257730 1111 #uuid "37fa1935-2489-5a76-a52d-a57af05e1c69" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257731 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257731 1111 #uuid "364d4895-7821-588d-8882-a142b42c777c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257732 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257732 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257732 1111 #uuid "0974579d-ff02-5b3e-ba9b-31d6d8583f06" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257733 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257733 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257733 1111 #uuid "d5702360-c7bf-55fb-bf9c-3597406e0e5e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257734 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257735 13194139533864 true] [17592186257734 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257737 13194139533864 true] [17592186257734 1089 "q-158540" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257734 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257734 1100 "The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ What points **must** lie on $y=3f(x+2)?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257734 1113 #uuid "6721ce60-bfa1-58ad-aee6-895ef577c707" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257734 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3f(x+2),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $2$ unit to the left and get the graph of \n$y=f(x+2).$  After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-2,1)=(-3,1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the y-axis and get the graph of \n$y=3f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,3\\cdot 1)=(-3,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3f(x+2).$ Substituting $x=-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3f(-3+2) \\\\  & =3f(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-3,3)$ must lie on $y=3f(x+2).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257736 13194139533864 true] [17592186257735 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257735 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257735 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257736 13194139533864 true] [17592186257735 1110 #uuid "e1178a48-950c-5de3-92d7-39990b834f2e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257736 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257736 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257736 1111 #uuid "16fa0f68-bcc1-512b-94af-8147b5a24f01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257737 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257738 13194139533864 true] [17592186257737 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257737 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257737 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257738 13194139533864 true] [17592186257737 1110 #uuid "ef6514f4-4875-53cd-b442-402cf686bbec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257738 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257738 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257738 1111 #uuid "0ab5f2fb-c4e9-501a-9315-d07cd5d66360" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257739 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257740 13194139533864 true] [17592186257739 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257742 13194139533864 true] [17592186257739 1089 "q-158542" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257739 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257739 1100 "The point  $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-1).$ What points **must** lie on $y=f(x)+2?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257739 1113 #uuid "56d9614f-42ba-58e8-a0d6-4b222638dad4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257739 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0-1,3)=(-1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,3+2)=(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0-1)=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257741 13194139533864 true] [17592186257740 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257740 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257740 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257741 13194139533864 true] [17592186257740 1110 #uuid "543cbfb7-1173-5a65-bc37-f5518618feb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257741 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257741 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257741 1111 #uuid "f25075c9-151d-51ec-ad1b-51c290720160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257743 13194139533864 true] [17592186257742 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257742 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257742 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257743 13194139533864 true] [17592186257742 1110 #uuid "4dbbd9a8-a3e8-5417-a704-9319b9fef99a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257743 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257743 1111 #uuid "74696480-27e8-50b8-8e68-1f5bc2c8821e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257744 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257745 13194139533864 true] [17592186257744 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257747 13194139533864 true] [17592186257744 1089 "q-158546" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257744 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257744 1100 "The point  $(2,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-2).$ What points **must** lie on $y=3-f(x)?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257744 1113 #uuid "9ce49de9-b37c-5069-a547-f0dd26c1d448" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257744 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=3-f(x):$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2-2,1)=(0,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=3-f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(0,-(1))=(0,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(0,-1+3)=(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2-2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(0)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ Substituting $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3-f(0) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2)$ must lie on $y=3-f(x).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257745 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257746 13194139533864 true] [17592186257745 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257745 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257745 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257746 13194139533864 true] [17592186257745 1110 #uuid "39489835-9589-58bd-9bad-441feaac60d4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257746 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257746 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257746 1111 #uuid "757ceea5-8485-572d-98f2-8cb47886efb2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257747 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257748 13194139533864 true] [17592186257747 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257747 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257747 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257748 13194139533864 true] [17592186257747 1110 #uuid "33a2d1a7-1d12-5bad-8745-cad969fa31d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257748 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257748 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257748 1111 #uuid "bb9424a8-f686-5da8-a145-a91ff0b23144" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257749 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257750 13194139533864 true] [17592186257749 1089 "q-15940" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257749 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257749 1100 "Find the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[10,13]$ given the following table of values for $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ | $\\,15\\,$ | $\\,17\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257749 1113 #uuid "be5e99bc-6a52-5455-9180-0bb659dfe8a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257749 1145 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(13)-f(10)}{13-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-1}{13-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257751 13194139533864 true] [17592186257750 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257750 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257750 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257751 13194139533864 true] [17592186257750 1110 #uuid "5e28de3b-4f3d-59d5-b4c1-13d8631a52fb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257751 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257751 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257751 1111 #uuid "327dfb30-a023-5c23-bba9-91f243856467" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257752 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257753 13194139533864 true] [17592186257752 1089 "q-160400" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257752 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257752 1100 "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\nI. - $3-\\text{i}$\nII. - $-1+2\\text{i}$\nIII. - $-2-4\\text{i}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257752 1113 #uuid "7049a950-6667-5e99-a83e-6c95051eae41" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257752 1145 "- The complex number $3-\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=3$ and the imaginary part $y=-1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(3,-1)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-1+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-1$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-1,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-2-4\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=-4.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,-4)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fafede137db90419fd849820668f36610ec675aa7a0e8a77d706872e33a149ef.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257754 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257755 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257756 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257757 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257758 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257759 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257760 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257761 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257761 13194139533864 true] [17592186257753 1110 #uuid "73392058-1d10-5e8b-90df-46ae072c8bf7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257754 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257754 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257754 1111 #uuid "6f19b5c4-e10c-5c70-8584-1c203dc7d03f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257755 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257755 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257755 1111 #uuid "a9d66bb6-1a71-5651-aa28-5c76ce9e870d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257756 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257756 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257756 1111 #uuid "f0ace6d7-933c-550c-839a-ce6ea37a0a5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257757 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257757 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257757 1111 #uuid "4cd1cb4e-2125-579f-a82b-a38e052eaddf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257758 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257758 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257758 1111 #uuid "c5c06e8a-a199-5dd2-b929-b6941f609134" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257759 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257759 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257759 1111 #uuid "4c28e77e-db15-5c0d-8069-14e040f64ecc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257760 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257760 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257760 1111 #uuid "3e3412e2-b865-5b94-9b3e-9b235d4aae3e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257761 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257761 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257761 1111 #uuid "252ca2ec-894b-5bb1-89e3-803daa8710f6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257762 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257763 13194139533864 true] [17592186257762 1089 "q-16355" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257762 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257762 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\n$a =$: {{a}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257762 1113 #uuid "83f30889-f878-583f-b150-a995dc476f5d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257762 1145 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{24}+\\pi n & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-\\frac{5}{24}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257764 13194139533864 true] [17592186257763 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257763 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257763 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257764 13194139533864 true] [17592186257763 1110 #uuid "dcb349ee-5aff-5464-b3b9-949ef5685eb8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257764 1034 "-\\frac{5}{24}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257764 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257764 1111 #uuid "bcd9ab5e-9ca8-5494-88f9-a90a5a2fef22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257765 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257766 13194139533864 true] [17592186257765 1089 "q-16364" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257765 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257765 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\n$a =$: {{a}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257765 1113 #uuid "6404a99e-7757-52ba-89dd-f5be5b3def0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257765 1145 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{1}{8}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257766 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257767 13194139533864 true] [17592186257766 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257766 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257766 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257767 13194139533864 true] [17592186257766 1110 #uuid "da0790fc-3f61-57e0-ae49-9f30ce6de3f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257767 1034 "\\frac{1}{8}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257767 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257767 1111 #uuid "53134b9c-2ec9-5e5a-adbc-78b1a7ed6b45" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257768 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257769 13194139533864 true] [17592186257768 1089 "q-166174" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257768 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257768 1100 "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n${f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}={lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257768 1113 #uuid "5be7f888-8103-5b32-9838-2ee1a31d74a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257768 1145 "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3) & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{[2x-{x}^{2}]-[2(3)-(3)^{2}]}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x+3}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{-({x}^{2}-2x-3)}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{-(x+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{x-3}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257769 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257770 13194139533864 true] [17592186257769 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257769 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257769 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257770 13194139533864 true] [17592186257769 1110 #uuid "4a784fbb-536a-5971-96a7-7d5e1444c0b9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257770 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257770 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257770 1111 #uuid "88b9dd30-548b-5770-8c2d-ad71a4396953" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257771 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257772 13194139533864 true] [17592186257771 1089 "q-166175" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257771 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257771 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-1)=${{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257771 1113 #uuid "7c3bccb0-4d19-5e29-b495-d2fd9d30979b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257771 1145 "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(-x-1) & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}x-{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}1 \\\\  & =-(3)-1 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257772 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257773 13194139533864 true] [17592186257772 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257772 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257772 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257773 13194139533864 true] [17592186257772 1110 #uuid "1e6539da-3322-59a4-a724-4cfc95dd35f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257773 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257773 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257773 1111 #uuid "968285cf-0e5f-56f0-b799-6231645ea515" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257774 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257775 13194139533864 true] [17592186257774 1089 "q-166178" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257774 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257774 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/17c57ba55e06207350ab93c8cac79bf22c48ba94e73ce4f914cd809b74b7d862.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above (not drawn to scale). Estimate the area $A$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n$A\\,\\approx \\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257774 1113 #uuid "a327301f-c4a9-5bc3-8650-35e0d214db46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257774 1145 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/41fde96921443be6c1efc28800e921a189fbe32d8a43667f6a85e7cf399eb745.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\approx (\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $0.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx (0.75+1+0.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257776 13194139533864 true] [17592186257775 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257775 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257775 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257776 13194139533864 true] [17592186257775 1110 #uuid "9705d313-ba96-5e54-999b-f76dc9705e0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257776 1034 "1.25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257776 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257776 1111 #uuid "7b9b9b9d-39a0-5ab6-9093-f6af0e0fb006" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257777 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257778 13194139533864 true] [17592186257777 1089 "q-166179" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257777 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257777 1100 "Write the expression for the Riemann sum from part 7 in sigma notation.\n\nThe right Riemann sum $\\,=\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(i-$\n{{field-1}} ${i}^{2})$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257777 1113 #uuid "ea206d90-e898-5894-9e09-0438d3e9d3d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257777 1145 "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nIn our case, we have $n=4$ subintervals, $a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $Δx=0.5.$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(0+i\\cdot 0.5)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5f(0.5i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(2(0.5i)-{(0.5i)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(i-0.25{i}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257779 13194139533864 true] [17592186257778 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257778 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257778 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257779 13194139533864 true] [17592186257778 1110 #uuid "b4e4b5a3-a5dc-57ea-b36b-782656ca2ec3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257779 1034 "0.25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257779 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257779 1111 #uuid "14882465-44da-51bd-9828-96748b5d62c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257780 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257781 13194139533864 true] [17592186257780 1089 "q-166547" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257780 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257780 1100 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=-{log}_{2}(9-3x)+4.$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257780 1113 #uuid "540e0beb-27dc-5689-9b70-9acb574ebe65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257780 1145 "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y={log}_{2}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-3x & =0 \\\\ -3x & =-9 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257782 13194139533864 true] [17592186257781 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257781 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257781 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257782 13194139533864 true] [17592186257781 1110 #uuid "2bf3ba6e-8536-5f0d-84b8-ba0c8d98e218" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257782 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257782 1111 #uuid "3f493eca-3a21-51c3-92f4-40a48a8146a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257783 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257784 13194139533864 true] [17592186257783 1089 "q-166947" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257783 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257783 1100 "If you deposit $$12,000$ into an account with a $8%$ annual interest rate, compounded quarterly, how many years will it take until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257783 1113 #uuid "c960aee5-3c8c-5166-a2ed-4e95f0b23d9f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257783 1145 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=12,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.08.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 18,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ 12,000{(1+\\frac{0.08}{4})}^{4t} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ (1+0.02)^{4t} & \\ge \\frac{18,000}{12,000} \\\\ (1.02)^{4t} & \\ge 1.5 \\\\ log\\,({1.02}^{4t}) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4tlog\\,(1.02) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t & \\ge \\frac{log\\,(1.5)}{log\\,(1.02)} \\\\ 4t & \\ge 20.475… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{20.475…}{4}\\approx 5.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember, payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $5$ or $5.25$ years.\n\n- After $5$ years, there is still less than $$18,000$ in the account, because $5<5.12.$\n\n- After $5.25$ years, there will be more than $$18,000$ in the account, since $5.25>5.12.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $5.25$ years ($5$ years and $1$ quarter) until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257785 13194139533864 true] [17592186257784 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257784 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257784 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257785 13194139533864 true] [17592186257784 1110 #uuid "0158252f-0174-5ffd-92f6-829d16529201" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257785 1034 "5.25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257785 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257785 1111 #uuid "36592917-0e89-59ea-b1c9-ec6fac2a0b0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257787 13194139533864 true] [17592186257786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257789 13194139533864 true] [17592186257786 1089 "q-167194" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257786 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257786 1100 "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $20b+400a=7\\,200.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257786 1113 #uuid "0afbd217-b5b5-5642-933f-a0e397418911" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257786 1145 "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=480-10a \\\\ 20b+400a=7\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=480-10a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 20(480-10a)+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600-200a+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600+200a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 200a & =-2\\,400 \\\\ a & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-12$ into $b=480-10a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =480-10(-12) \\\\ b & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-12,\\,b=600.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257788 13194139533864 true] [17592186257787 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257787 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257787 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257788 13194139533864 true] [17592186257787 1110 #uuid "0b772ad6-06db-5726-b6ce-d03788093751" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257788 1034 "-12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257788 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257788 1111 #uuid "825436cb-9673-51cf-85dc-e619d63d520d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257790 13194139533864 true] [17592186257789 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257789 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257789 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257790 13194139533864 true] [17592186257789 1110 #uuid "c9d309dc-1a7d-576d-82a8-c54ce41c71fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257790 1034 "600" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257790 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257790 1111 #uuid "99557b4b-cf24-5413-9953-5485c6e07968" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257791 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257792 13194139533864 true] [17592186257791 1089 "q-167195" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257791 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257791 1100 "Determine the weekly profit if the company produces $40$ couches in a week.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257791 1113 #uuid "2a5fdcf4-99c8-52a0-adf5-3a386b9372b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257791 1145 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-12{x}^{2}+600x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=40$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(40) & =-12(40)^{2}+600(40) \\\\  & =-19\\,200+24\\,000 \\\\  & =4\\,800\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$4\\,800.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257792 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257793 13194139533864 true] [17592186257792 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257792 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257792 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257793 13194139533864 true] [17592186257792 1110 #uuid "43acb66d-8004-5c0e-a631-d3d64fd7793b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257793 1034 "4800" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257793 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257793 1111 #uuid "354f5d76-8f03-5e6e-97fa-a25e850f3cf4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257794 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257795 13194139533864 true] [17592186257794 1089 "q-167197" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257794 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257794 1100 "Find the minimum number of couches the company needs to produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$7\\,200.$\n\nThe company needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ couches per week." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257794 1113 #uuid "9a0fc448-799b-505a-a910-4ee7e12527c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257794 1145 "From part 5, the required number of sofas $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n12\\cdot ({x}^{2}-50x+600)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $12,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-50x+600=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(600)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-50).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-30).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-50x=-20x-30x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-50x+600 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-30x+600 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-30+600 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-30x+600) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-30(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-20) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=20$ or $x=30.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $20$ couches per week to make a profit of $$7\\,200.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257795 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257796 13194139533864 true] [17592186257795 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257795 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257795 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257796 13194139533864 true] [17592186257795 1110 #uuid "2e13021a-bbe5-5777-8b5e-ea6b09aed017" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257796 1034 "20" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257796 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257796 1111 #uuid "d8069a5e-c350-59af-92e9-2784d9ed4e96" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257798 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257800 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1089 "q-167210" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1100 "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $30b+900a=6\\,000.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1113 #uuid "3745304a-337c-57fe-9274-0c5590dd8f9c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257797 1145 "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=240-20a \\\\ 30b+900a=6\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=240-20a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30b+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 30(240-20a)+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200-600a+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200+300a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 300a & =-1\\,200 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-4$ into $b=240-20a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =240-20(-4) \\\\ b & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=320.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257798 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257799 13194139533864 true] [17592186257798 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257798 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257798 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257799 13194139533864 true] [17592186257798 1110 #uuid "e2a946c9-fc0f-55fa-b14d-b6d33d7911be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257799 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257799 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257799 1111 #uuid "708a0248-6941-505b-81cb-fac6b0e13ef0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257801 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257801 13194139533864 true] [17592186257800 1110 #uuid "2a8ef031-bf8e-58d6-9c2c-ab33d25b38e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257801 1034 "320" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257801 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257801 1111 #uuid "a194f5e9-9385-5cd1-ad1a-5979b68b20d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257803 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1089 "q-167211" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1100 "Determine the weekly profit if the bakery produces $35$ cakes in a week.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1113 #uuid "1b9d6db3-e650-565d-8f8c-e2de39a23110" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257802 1145 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-4{x}^{2}+320x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=35$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(35) & =-4(35)^{2}+320(35) \\\\  & =-4\\,900+11\\,200 \\\\  & =6\\,300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$6\\,300.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257804 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257804 13194139533864 true] [17592186257803 1110 #uuid "c4fb357e-085a-5f3b-b537-38769b8780f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257804 1034 "6300" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257804 1111 #uuid "5a3e0464-63c9-5eef-bc9e-5c1c2afcd9f4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257806 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1089 "q-167213" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1100 "Find the minimum number of cakes the bakery needs to produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$6\\,000.$\n\nThe bakery needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ cakes per week." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1113 #uuid "7095629a-fbfb-5e1c-bf46-32aaaf63e2db" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257805 1145 "From part 5, the required number of cakes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4\\cdot ({x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(1\\,500)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-80).$ The required factors are $(-30)$ and $(-50).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-80x=-30x-50x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-30x-50x+1\\,500 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-30x-50x+1\\,500 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-30x)+(-50x+1\\,500) & =0 \\\\ x(x-30)-50(x-30) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-50)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=30$ or $x=50.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the bakery needs to produce $30$ cakes per week to make a profit of $$6\\,000.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257807 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257807 13194139533864 true] [17592186257806 1110 #uuid "8edca494-ef17-5230-bf6f-214bfc5a0fd7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257807 1034 "30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257807 1111 #uuid "3a1be701-b65f-52a8-b5ad-99d023b13697" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257809 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257811 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1089 "q-167226" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1100 "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $70a+9b=600.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1113 #uuid "8d63494e-5f74-557b-a601-72890b90758c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257808 1145 "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=75-5a \\\\ 70a+9b=600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=75-5a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70a+9b & =600 \\\\ 70a+9(75-5a) & =600 \\\\ 70a+675-45a & =600 \\\\ 25a+675 & =600 \\\\ 25a & =-75 \\\\ a & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-3$ into $b=75-5a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =75-5(-3) \\\\ b & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-3,\\,b=90.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257810 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257810 13194139533864 true] [17592186257809 1110 #uuid "1dbe85ba-bf16-554c-8d3e-5e4bd5bc731b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257810 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257810 1111 #uuid "ad23ad81-a361-5276-9f48-f95626f61826" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257812 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257812 13194139533864 true] [17592186257811 1110 #uuid "54828145-6d13-5a11-a9c1-05c77a89323a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257812 1034 "90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257812 1111 #uuid "a98733b2-2845-5316-abd1-cf18d0dca11a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257813 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257814 13194139533864 true] [17592186257813 1089 "q-167227" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257813 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257813 1100 "Determine the daily profit if the company produces $25$ luxury white chocolate boxes in a day.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257813 1113 #uuid "6efda865-3762-5c97-a1e1-be30152c52c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257813 1145 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-3{x}^{2}+90x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=25$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(25) & =-3(25)^{2}+90(25) \\\\  & =-1\\,875+2\\,250 \\\\  & =375\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$375.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257815 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257815 13194139533864 true] [17592186257814 1110 #uuid "74514ad4-4047-575e-b3bc-9e156f7147ab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257815 1034 "375" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257815 1111 #uuid "051bf4e8-235e-5157-adbf-b32ce8b74141" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257816 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257817 13194139533864 true] [17592186257816 1089 "q-167229" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257816 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257816 1100 "Find the minimum number of luxury white chocolate boxes the company needs to produce per day to make a daily profit of $$600.$\n\nThe company needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ luxury white chocolate boxes per day." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257816 1113 #uuid "dcb05104-1d9d-5307-88a8-33037d9f7de1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257816 1145 "From part 5, the required number of white chocolate boxes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3\\cdot ({x}^{2}-30x+200)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-30x+200=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(200)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-30).$ The required factors are $(-10)$ and $(-20).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-30x=-10x-20x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-30x+200 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x-20x+200 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x-20x+200 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-10x)+(-20x+200) & =0 \\\\ x(x-10)-20(x-10) & =0 \\\\ (x-10)(x-20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=10$ or $x=20.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $10$ luxury white chocolate boxes per day to make a profit of $$600.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257818 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257818 13194139533864 true] [17592186257817 1110 #uuid "7b3cb1c6-fc04-50f8-86d3-976bbe0f949b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257818 1034 "10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257818 1111 #uuid "eb1ba20d-813b-5415-993b-3a79e31ed28e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257819 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257820 13194139533864 true] [17592186257819 1089 "q-1700" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257819 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257819 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}({270}^{∘})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257819 1113 #uuid "c7e6e397-9e1b-53ed-9539-68c7fd844687" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257819 1145 "The angle ${270}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${270}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({270}^{∘})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({270}^{∘})=-1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}({270}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({270}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}({270}^{∘})}=\\frac{-1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{tan}({270}^{∘})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257821 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257821 13194139533864 true] [17592186257820 1110 #uuid "effc45c7-2b7f-53bd-9606-791d0a6f57ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257821 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257821 1111 #uuid "0ad12b9e-4974-5164-a400-ca66e15d7e60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257822 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257823 13194139533864 true] [17592186257822 1089 "q-170143" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257822 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257822 1100 "If $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}+1,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(3,8).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257822 1113 #uuid "c2e8234a-8f1b-5c06-89b8-b14ff16ffd00" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257822 1145 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt[3]{y}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt[3]{y})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{1/3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{-2/3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,8),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,8)} & =3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257824 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257824 13194139533864 true] [17592186257823 1110 #uuid "ceaa4d8c-4917-537a-a5bb-86d3ce2d66e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257824 1034 "12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257824 1111 #uuid "6f41737a-2e93-52b1-84d7-8ccbd56d94b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257825 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257826 13194139533864 true] [17592186257825 1089 "q-1702" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257825 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257825 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257825 1113 #uuid "8c6dfeb5-0fab-5a95-9a4e-38dfe7f97395" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257825 1145 "The angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257827 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257827 13194139533864 true] [17592186257826 1110 #uuid "5bedce63-0a7b-569f-93e6-21d59ce5641a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257827 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257827 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186257827 1111 #uuid "8712dda2-536b-5ec6-bc6c-f50d40c71e4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257828 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257829 13194139533864 true] [17592186257828 1089 "q-17280" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257828 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257828 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5e8383b56998815d4c981e72c946fdbf8de77ece15e52ca2ed5c7371c8f2c8af.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257828 1113 #uuid "6f7fded4-a7f4-5dbc-a96e-2d5c3e995127" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257828 1145 "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=4.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)=4(4)-12=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)={3}^{2}-2(3)+2=5\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257830 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257830 13194139533864 true] [17592186257829 1110 #uuid "a7063e60-d887-53de-8f03-68e4e7251e17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257830 1034 "\\frac{16}{25}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257830 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257830 1111 #uuid "8fbd1f6f-6266-508a-9271-24f3d4543ce2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257831 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257832 13194139533864 true] [17592186257831 1089 "q-178245" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257831 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257831 1100 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=9{x}^{3}+45{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $1?$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257831 1113 #uuid "947ba861-dcfa-53a6-bd12-92fc424fca88" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257831 1145 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $9{x}^{3}$ and $45{x}^{2}$ is $9{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =9{x}^{3}+45{x}^{2} \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor ${x}^{2}$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257833 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257833 13194139533864 true] [17592186257832 1110 #uuid "41937ddf-a832-5711-badd-e59e8e2d2e58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257833 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257833 1111 #uuid "c6efb5b2-ddf8-578d-834b-dc2f46f5aa6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257835 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257837 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257839 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1089 "q-178255" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1100 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-25x+25$ have multiplicity $1?$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1113 #uuid "d02e2a80-3a55-5677-827d-c57a97e96eb7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257834 1145 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-25x+25 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-1)-25(x-1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-25)(x-1) \\\\  & =(x+5)(x-5)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the roots $x=-5,1,5$ all have multiplicity $1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257836 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257836 13194139533864 true] [17592186257835 1110 #uuid "12baccc5-b6a0-5e9c-839f-3f5eb5d4b3da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257836 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257836 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257836 1111 #uuid "0f2efb63-e84b-5311-be2a-fc56be772094" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257838 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257838 13194139533864 true] [17592186257837 1110 #uuid "b097d600-e75a-5994-b87e-5bd7ab4f8ee0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257838 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257838 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257838 1111 #uuid "bf022ec4-e217-5247-875d-f03f80b7942e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257840 13194139533864 true] [17592186257839 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257839 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257839 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257840 13194139533864 true] [17592186257839 1110 #uuid "4c3cf54e-16d3-5ffb-938d-c284cc0baabd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257840 1111 #uuid "d624c35f-739a-5d07-a324-4ddd18d2817f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257842 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1089 "q-1786" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1100 "If a master combination lock has to be set to $3$ numbers and each number can range between $1$ and $40,$ including $1$ and $40.$ How many possible combinations are there?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1113 #uuid "8507db0d-b50f-5c25-ab7d-8dbff152c32a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257841 1145 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of combinations} & =\\text{Total number of options in the }1\\text{st position} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Total number of options in the }2\\text{nd position} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Total number of options in the }3\\text{rd position} \\\\  & =40\\times 40\\times 40 \\\\  & =64\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $64\\,000$ possible combinations." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257843 13194139533864 true] [17592186257842 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257842 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257842 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257843 13194139533864 true] [17592186257842 1110 #uuid "cda78ebf-0343-5a94-8ac1-a732e55d1311" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257843 1034 "64\\,000" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257843 1111 #uuid "0b274020-9c0a-590c-987a-a39babffa5e7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257844 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257845 13194139533864 true] [17592186257844 1089 "q-180277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257844 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257844 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a61a6232b2041bab3d455a7d765311ddfae25f674f8dbbf79696d2137355b159.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram shown above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space.\n\nGiven that every outcome is equally likely, find $P({A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257844 1113 #uuid "27fe13ff-12fc-5d42-8d48-6dd6fccb6a52" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257844 1145 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, ${A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'}$ is represented by the area outside the circle $B$ or outside the circle $A$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/6a3bb7b671e45aeb15d3239cae6fcedb7c36e52b15141d66b23e65066ab37edc.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in ${A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'}$ is \n\n$$\n2+5+1=8\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n2+9+5+1=17.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'})=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257846 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257846 13194139533864 true] [17592186257845 1110 #uuid "09e6db05-0c98-5a9e-b2fe-47e73fb3b8e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257846 1034 "\\frac{8}{17}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257846 1111 #uuid "8707755e-8a7e-5dff-a69b-97c7680a3a1d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257847 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257848 13194139533864 true] [17592186257847 1089 "q-1803" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257847 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257847 1100 "A word consists of $3$ letters, as shown below:\n\n| Letter | Letter | Letter |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n\nHere, each \"Letter\" can be any capital from A-H ($8$ in total). How many possible words are there?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257847 1113 #uuid "9727e37f-7458-5fb2-9794-a48d52770acf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257847 1145 "For each letter, there is a total of $8$ possibilities: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and H.\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of words} & =\\text{Number of options in the first slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the second slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the third slot} \\\\  & =8\\times 8\\times 8 \\\\  & ={8}^{3} \\\\  & =512.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $512$ possible words." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257849 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257849 13194139533864 true] [17592186257848 1110 #uuid "0196557c-c5ad-5730-928e-ed86abb7f003" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257849 1034 "512" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257849 1111 #uuid "03404903-6661-5ae8-b691-ba3920cab111" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257851 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1089 "q-1805" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1100 "A word consists of $4$ letters, as shown below:\n\n| Letter | Letter | Letter | Letter |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nHere, each \"Letter\" can be any capital from A-Z ($26$ in total). How many possible words are there?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1113 #uuid "89ba8929-6b23-5b4b-b596-3975abc434a8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257850 1145 "For each letter, there is a total of $26$ possibilities.\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of words} & =\\text{Number of options in the first slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the second slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the third slot} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of options in the fourth slot} \\\\  & =26\\times 26\\times 26\\times 26 \\\\  & ={26}^{4} \\\\  & =456\\,976.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $456\\,976$ possible words." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257852 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257852 13194139533864 true] [17592186257851 1110 #uuid "02e71e6a-8a94-5374-a840-cc367c91a48a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257852 1034 "456\\,976" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257852 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257852 1111 #uuid "11e67cf6-278c-59bb-bedf-08afe3f917f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257854 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1089 "q-1809" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1100 "Rio's Pizza offers a mix and match pizza on its menu. There are $4$ different meat types to choose from, $5$ different vegetables, $4$ different types of cheese, and $2$ different types of crust. How many different types of pizza can be made by choosing a type of meat, a vegetable, cheese and a crust?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1113 #uuid "013caba4-8d5e-572a-9ea0-5bedf0d0d6bd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257853 1145 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of pizzas} & =\\text{Number of type of meats} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of type of vegetables} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of type of cheese} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of type of crust} \\\\  & =4\\times 5\\times 4\\times 2 \\\\  & =160.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160$ types of pizza can be made with the given ingredients." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257855 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257855 13194139533864 true] [17592186257854 1110 #uuid "d535795e-b9e8-5397-b1c7-31bd591797df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257855 1034 "160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257855 1111 #uuid "3d69ac29-79f0-5fcb-975f-beec98dd346e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257857 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257859 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257861 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1089 "q-19049" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1100 "If $r=⟨0,-2,4⟩$ and $s=i-6j$, then $r+s=$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 3, column 1: {{r3c1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1113 #uuid "92ba06b2-5956-5b83-8cb1-36fb1d3ffbe6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257856 1145 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ns=i-6j=⟨1,-6,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTo compute the sum, we just add each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r+s & =⟨0,-2,4⟩+⟨1,-6,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,-2-6,4+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-8,4⟩ \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257858 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1041 "r1c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257858 13194139533864 true] [17592186257857 1110 #uuid "e926ece9-9c6a-5930-b891-d76a9c4b2b6a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257858 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257858 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257858 1111 #uuid "9f7b78d4-1cc0-5070-b66f-31145be45620" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257860 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1041 "r2c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257860 13194139533864 true] [17592186257859 1110 #uuid "a50714a1-8282-57e5-8d21-2bb9829fc692" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257860 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257860 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257860 1111 #uuid "c838a260-aad2-5399-833d-b5dbda46900c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257861 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257862 13194139533864 true] [17592186257861 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257861 1041 "r3c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257861 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257862 13194139533864 true] [17592186257861 1110 #uuid "019580bf-188b-5cfc-bd21-39b4613134ce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257862 1111 #uuid "e83ad01d-cddb-50a4-9e42-14abe9456bdf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257864 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257866 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1089 "q-19154" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1100 "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,23]$ is $1.5,$ find the value of $p.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ | $\\,18\\,$ | $\\,21\\,$ | $\\,23\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-{p}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,-13\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,13\\,$ |\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1113 #uuid "149a5bb5-23b4-56cd-ac8a-edc3c7f35e91" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257863 1145 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(23)-f(1)}{23-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-(-{p}^{2}-4)}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=1.5$ on the interval $[1,23].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22}=1.5.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{2}+17}{22} & =1.5 \\\\ {p}^{2}+17 & =33 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =33-17 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =16 \\\\ p & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=-4$ or $p=4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257864 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257865 13194139533864 true] [17592186257864 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257864 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257864 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257865 13194139533864 true] [17592186257864 1110 #uuid "f53d7482-1cba-564a-9af7-5083dcff0367" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257865 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257865 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257865 1111 #uuid "8877ffb7-f58c-5fbd-ba52-99c8f52a63f4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257866 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257867 13194139533864 true] [17592186257866 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257866 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257866 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257867 13194139533864 true] [17592186257866 1110 #uuid "9f890d1d-8aea-54ba-95ff-94ffdb406cff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257867 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257867 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257867 1111 #uuid "f11677ac-9262-56f2-8cbc-9d4cbc75e73a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257869 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1089 "q-19160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1100 "Consider the following table of values for $f(x).$ Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ is $3,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-11\\,$ | $\\,2p-1\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,p+10\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1113 #uuid "2e3ab4e9-a63e-5231-88df-c45b46948041" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257868 1145 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-2)}{3-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(p+10)-(2p-1)}{3+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-p}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=3$ on the interval $[-2,3].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{11-p}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11-p}{5} & =3 \\\\ 11-p & =15 \\\\ -p & =15-11 \\\\ -p & =4 \\\\ p & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257869 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257870 13194139533864 true] [17592186257869 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257869 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257869 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257870 13194139533864 true] [17592186257869 1110 #uuid "9a3ef0df-5c83-5dcc-979e-1d960c4c3f11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257870 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257870 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257870 1111 #uuid "7415d098-c067-5d3a-a6ae-5cd2e605e7b9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257872 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1089 "q-19262" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/25e78d632f4bbe23ebbf700946b1a05a6b4f9f578c1d8047ddb067842a820fa9.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(6,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}x,$ and the point ${A}^{'}$ with coordinates $(4,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1113 #uuid "075f5bd8-8f8e-5a44-8865-d55c0e9f72f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257871 1145 "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(6,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 6,\\operatorname{ln}6)=(4,\\operatorname{ln}6)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257872 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257873 13194139533864 true] [17592186257872 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257872 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257872 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257873 13194139533864 true] [17592186257872 1110 #uuid "54c917c5-7d48-5935-8b3a-9bf7e3ba9958" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257873 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257873 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257873 1111 #uuid "23d5ee2a-8c82-5e86-92da-fcd172396fdb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257875 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1089 "q-19306" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=5{e}^{t}+2\\operatorname{ln}t+{t}^{2},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=2.$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1113 #uuid "f100bda5-e5cc-5ecc-9c54-883833c98d6d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257874 1145 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{e}^{t}+2\\operatorname{ln}t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+\\frac{2}{t}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =5{e}^{2}+\\frac{2}{2}+2(2) \\\\  & =5{e}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =41.945… \\\\  & \\approx 42\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257876 13194139533864 true] [17592186257875 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257875 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257875 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257876 13194139533864 true] [17592186257875 1110 #uuid "e06156ff-7e9a-591e-81cc-e21a3cf5c67c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257876 1034 "42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257876 1111 #uuid "98f7b3ef-65a4-52b4-b5ee-e7567637249d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257878 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1089 "q-19761" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1100 "Charlie goes to buy a bike. There are $8$ black bikes, $6$ white bikes, and $7$ silver bikes. How many choices of bike does Charlie have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1113 #uuid "f8cb7c22-3b81-59c8-9e12-cf4c433c9fb7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257877 1145 "In order to find the total number of choices Charlie has, we can use the **rule of sum**:\n\n$$\n8+6+7=21\n$$\n\nTherefore, Charlie has $21$ choices of bike." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257878 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257879 13194139533864 true] [17592186257878 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257878 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257878 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257879 13194139533864 true] [17592186257878 1110 #uuid "b98cecff-487d-58d4-a352-f3f4ae326137" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257879 1034 "21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257879 1111 #uuid "5e959508-8320-5502-aa8d-9f88703c7ad1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257881 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1089 "q-19821" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1100 "Peter has $8$ shirts, $6$ pants, and $6$ pairs of shoes. If Peter wears one shirt, one pant, and one pair of shoes, how many different ways can Peter dress?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1113 #uuid "aaf4d311-c3be-544b-af6a-27bd8d3ca99a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257880 1145 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of outfits} & =\\text{Number of shirts} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pants} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pairs of shoes} \\\\  & =8\\times 6\\times 6 \\\\  & =288.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Peter can dress in $288$ different ways." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257882 13194139533864 true] [17592186257881 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257881 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257881 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257882 13194139533864 true] [17592186257881 1110 #uuid "d0663bbf-e9d2-5951-a932-2f4da6e5e822" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257882 1034 "288" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257882 1111 #uuid "8abc9de6-7201-5c0d-9f8d-5b05fad87efa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257884 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1089 "q-19824" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1100 "Matthew goes to the library to select a book. In the library, there are $12$ fiction books, $15$ science books, $19$ religious books, and $20$ history books. How many choices of book does Matthew have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1113 #uuid "e8193198-758f-58fd-af4b-ef906d9232ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257883 1145 "In order to find the total number of choices Matthew has, we can use the **rule of sum**:\n\n$$\n12+15+19+20=66\n$$\n\nTherefore, Matthew has $66$ choices of book." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257885 13194139533864 true] [17592186257884 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257884 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257884 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257885 13194139533864 true] [17592186257884 1110 #uuid "d920084c-b257-5056-a680-3d4357e1663f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257885 1034 "66" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257885 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257885 1111 #uuid "b9321b60-2a46-50c5-b364-64bd11aec43d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257887 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1089 "q-19877" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1100 "Given that $k\\ge 0$ is an integer, $\\frac{{((k+1)!)}^{2}}{k!}=$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1113 #uuid "4ee6a7d7-dbe7-5b40-954a-acea9efee6e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257886 1145 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(k+1)!=(k+1)k!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $k!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{((k+1)!)}^{2}}{k!} & =\\frac{{((k+1)k!)}^{2}}{k!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+1)^{2}\\,\\cdot (k!)^{2}}{k!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+1)^{2}\\,\\cdot k!\\cdot \\cancel{k!}}{\\cancel{k!}} \\\\  & =(k+1)^{2}k!\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257888 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257889 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257890 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257891 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257892 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257891 13194139533864 true] [17592186257887 1110 #uuid "f402311c-4280-5dc9-b696-2f17bc3e00bc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257888 1034 "\\frac{(k+1)^{2}}{k!}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257888 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257888 1111 #uuid "82a50d39-d29f-52e0-85f8-4f3954ebd28a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257889 1034 "(k+1)k!" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257889 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257889 1111 #uuid "b5ddf863-39cd-5d4b-b3a8-b634565b2e09" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257890 1034 "\\frac{k+1}{k!}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257890 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257890 1111 #uuid "53069e24-60e2-55cb-802d-a45c95d6ce5e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1034 "(k+1)^{2}k!" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257891 1111 #uuid "7390bed0-b01c-5f2a-befd-54ef50e8735e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257892 1034 "(k+1)^{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257892 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257892 1111 #uuid "d9a85205-4c01-59c8-80a2-d25bfd66faee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257893 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257894 13194139533864 true] [17592186257893 1089 "q-19882" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257893 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186257893 1100 "Given that $n\\ge 9$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257893 1113 #uuid "4a8f7cc8-f392-57e5-b3c9-254ee480518f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257893 1145 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+9)! & =(n+9)(n+8)(n+7)! \\\\ (n-8)! & =(n-8)(n-9)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n+7)!$ and $(n-9)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!} & =\\frac{[(n-8)\\cdot (n-9)!]\\cdot [(n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+7)!]}{(n-9)!\\cdot (n+7)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot (n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}}{\\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}} \\\\  & =(n-8)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+9) \\\\  & =(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257895 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257896 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257897 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257898 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257899 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257896 13194139533864 true] [17592186257894 1110 #uuid "520f903f-cc0f-5087-a651-ef4c311135ef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257895 1034 "(n+8)(n+9)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257895 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257895 1111 #uuid "6d4b7050-b0ec-506e-8d2d-3de366c66aef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257896 1034 "(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257896 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257896 1111 #uuid "77002e1b-3856-5764-ac6e-3589194b4e01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257897 1034 "(n+7)(n+8)(n+9)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257897 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257897 1111 #uuid "238bf615-e4f5-5bc6-8891-998d63d57915" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257898 1034 "\\frac{n+9}{n-9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257898 1111 #uuid "acbce194-3273-5052-9801-7566a12961bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257899 1034 "\\frac{(n+8)(n+9)}{n-9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257899 1111 #uuid "43d0cf0c-78ed-59b6-80b1-240f71aac532" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257901 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1089 "q-20183" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1100 "A ball is thrown up in the air. After $t$ seconds, its height is $h(t)=-16{t}^{2}+54t+10$ feet above the ground.\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the height when $t=1.5$ seconds?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1113 #uuid "9a791af4-1cbd-592a-a199-1e3e85cf8cfa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257900 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change of the height when $t=1.5$ seconds is equal to ${h}^{'}(1.5).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-16{t}^{2}+54t+10) \\\\  & =-32t+54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1.5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(1.5) & =-32(1.5)+54 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the height when $t=1.5$ seconds is $6$ feet per second." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257901 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257902 13194139533864 true] [17592186257901 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257901 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257901 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257902 13194139533864 true] [17592186257901 1110 #uuid "d9fbe8e7-03e3-57f1-8d03-b8f737b529ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257902 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257902 1111 #uuid "01a087fc-2640-5da9-891d-8d1a3aca9168" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257904 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1089 "q-20276" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1100 "Frank has a bag with $10$ different balls. In how many ways can Frank select a set of $8$ balls from the bag, considering that the order does not matter?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1113 #uuid "aaff4c14-16ea-5dec-8f92-9c9a5e0030ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257903 1145 "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ balls, so $n=10.$ We want to select $8$ of those balls from the bag, so we have $r=8.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential sets of $8$ balls is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{8} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-8)!\\,8!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{2!8!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot \\cancel{8!}}{2!\\cancel{8!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{90}{2} \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257905 13194139533864 true] [17592186257904 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257904 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257904 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257905 13194139533864 true] [17592186257904 1110 #uuid "505379a5-d388-5642-8c53-555ca99c500e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257905 1034 "45" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257905 1111 #uuid "9903e779-9ba3-5920-8450-53ab46f9cc63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257907 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1089 "q-20823" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1100 "A curve is defined as $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x.$ Find the average rate of change of $y$ over the interval $[1,4].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1113 #uuid "a2e31816-43ec-559b-a472-2ed0efb972fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257906 1145 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x.$  Applying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{[2({4}^{3})-{4}^{2}+5(4)]-[2({1}^{3})-{1}^{2}+5(1)]}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{132-6}{3} \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257907 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257908 13194139533864 true] [17592186257907 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257907 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257907 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257908 13194139533864 true] [17592186257907 1110 #uuid "d69339cc-4899-5ceb-bc91-5dc45b77c967" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257908 1034 "42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257908 1111 #uuid "a978ec35-daa3-5475-b451-48874291889c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257910 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1089 "q-21123" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1100 "The number of machines infected by a computer virus is represented by the function $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11,$ where $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus was first detected. What is the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1113 #uuid "95d04696-3038-5931-a2a7-b332a1053193" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257909 1145 "Let $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11$ be the number of machines infected by the computer virus after $t$ days. To find the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,7]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δt} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)-f(0)}{7-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[4(7)^{3}-2(7)^{2}+5(7)+11]-[4(0)^{3}-2(0)^{2}+5(0)+11]}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1309}{7} \\\\  & =187\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of growth over the first week is $187$ machines per day." 13194139533864 true] [17592186257910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257911 13194139533864 true] [17592186257910 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257910 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257910 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257911 13194139533864 true] [17592186257910 1110 #uuid "d381769d-59ee-54e7-87b7-fa734ad44d15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257911 1034 "187" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257911 1111 #uuid "4dab1977-579e-50a4-86c6-15428cba7f1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257913 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257915 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1089 "q-211287" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/282e65324ea1e7893da25ead1cc822c7c166df880c3aecc60face9adbbb7279a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1113 #uuid "4e52eb90-51ba-5a17-ba12-4144b8a682da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257912 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $3$ units to the left, and then\n\n- translating it by $2$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x+3)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,6).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+3).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2-3,6)=(-5,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units downward and get the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-5,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-5,6-2)=(-5,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2$ is $(-5,4).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257913 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257914 13194139533864 true] [17592186257913 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257913 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257913 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257914 13194139533864 true] [17592186257913 1110 #uuid "d8562ea8-b60f-5aab-8f71-8c5a505b05ec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257914 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257914 1111 #uuid "c5e182b6-70c4-58ac-8ad9-1fed5941e707" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257916 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1104 #edb/ref 17592186257916 13194139533864 true] [17592186257915 1110 #uuid "da23d844-cff0-5c78-a80e-fb76eedea37a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257916 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257916 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186257916 1111 #uuid "7471de03-1dd9-5e44-8b88-2757d3b13db9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257918 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257920 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1089 "q-211304" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/73da11bf77a8bc10c00f5636176321ed1b44bbf614c8d4658e574e8ae5f85831.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1113 #uuid "792abd45-247a-55f0-b21d-fe9a605db7a6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257917 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(3x)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(15,-5),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(3x)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(15,-5).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(15,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 15,-5)=(5,-5).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(3x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(5,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(5,-5+4)=(5,-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4$ is $(5,-1).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186257918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186257919 13194139533864 true] [17592186257918 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186257918 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [