 the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258187 13194139533864 true] [17592186258186 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258186 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258186 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258187 13194139533864 true] [17592186258186 1110 #uuid "06303f5e-9a77-5a76-93dc-2cd6a3f05bfe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258187 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258187 1111 #uuid "7cf57b73-5ad2-5eab-bf6d-fb4d095f013d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258188 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258189 13194139533864 true] [17592186258188 1089 "q-278543" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258188 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258188 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258188 1113 #uuid "8e85e93c-3cfa-5608-97e9-59cee6be71eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258188 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+2)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258190 13194139533864 true] [17592186258189 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258189 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258189 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258190 13194139533864 true] [17592186258189 1110 #uuid "e3d0c0c1-1bcd-51f6-8e4a-70dcf5913bcc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258190 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258190 1111 #uuid "f3b58eef-9b07-5e62-8dfd-06cdd9792dd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258191 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258192 13194139533864 true] [17592186258191 1089 "q-278551" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258191 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258191 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258191 1113 #uuid "5f2dc1ae-4892-50b9-981f-808f974086b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258191 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{2}(x-1)+(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+1=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258193 13194139533864 true] [17592186258192 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258192 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258192 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258193 13194139533864 true] [17592186258192 1110 #uuid "2bbf306f-0153-5b3f-845e-2e452538f0cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258193 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258193 1111 #uuid "e6bca57a-bbd7-5135-b4d1-5394e2880a4c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258194 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258195 13194139533864 true] [17592186258194 1089 "q-280142" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258194 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258194 1100 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}4{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258194 1113 #uuid "03feef15-6821-50d8-957a-59df60c1ee42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258194 1145 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=4,$ $r=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $N=5.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}4{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & =4\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{5})}{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{243}{1024})}{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\frac{781}{1024}}{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{3124}{1024} \\\\  & =\\frac{781}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258196 13194139533864 true] [17592186258195 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258195 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258195 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258196 13194139533864 true] [17592186258195 1110 #uuid "e8086ada-f6fe-58cb-9114-cce7f17fbdd9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258196 1034 "\\frac{781}{64}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258196 1111 #uuid "85ebfb2c-99c6-5637-b42c-761be5242059" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258197 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258198 13194139533864 true] [17592186258197 1089 "q-280149" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258197 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258197 1100 "Evaluate the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{3}{2}(4)^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258197 1113 #uuid "d76c89d6-e774-5178-8f95-00904170710b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258197 1145 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=4$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{2}(4)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}(4)^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=6,r=4$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{3}{2}(4)^{n} & =6\\cdot \\frac{(1-{4}^{5})}{1-4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{(-1023)}{(-3)} \\\\  & =6(341) \\\\  & =2046\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258198 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258199 13194139533864 true] [17592186258198 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258198 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258198 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258199 13194139533864 true] [17592186258198 1110 #uuid "e210c240-5654-5bf6-9793-aef1a6e8b3c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258199 1034 "2046" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258199 1111 #uuid "791e76a9-3d5f-57c1-b10b-b6d07ab33163" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258200 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258201 13194139533864 true] [17592186258200 1089 "q-280244" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258200 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258200 1100 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258200 1113 #uuid "38d95bd5-b1e1-544e-9f92-f7bc640d7469" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258200 1145 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{7}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=3,r=\\frac{1}{7}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}21\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n} & =3\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{7})} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{2401})}{\\frac{6}{7}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{7}{6}\\cdot \\frac{2400}{2401} \\\\  & =\\frac{1200}{343}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258202 13194139533864 true] [17592186258201 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258201 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258201 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258202 13194139533864 true] [17592186258201 