**Without** solving the equation, determine the sum of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258209 1113 #uuid "e0b72192-2fad-5822-a291-598e7afc9350" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258209 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=172,\\,c=-15\\,300.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{a} & =-\\frac{172}{1}=-172.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258211 13194139533864 true] [17592186258210 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258210 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258210 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258211 13194139533864 true] [17592186258210 1110 #uuid "3efb9e91-8a8a-5d4a-b7c3-c9aea8cd6ba0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258211 1034 "-172" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258211 1111 #uuid "22a949e2-f067-5d66-ab20-dc59a15d6625" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258212 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258213 13194139533864 true] [17592186258212 1089 "q-288813" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258212 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258212 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+11x-20=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258212 1113 #uuid "aaca8727-279c-5e4c-b712-d066e6241ce5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258212 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=11,\\,c=-20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-20)}{4}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258214 13194139533864 true] [17592186258213 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258213 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258213 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258214 13194139533864 true] [17592186258213 1110 #uuid "263e2c7c-5f78-50d0-9f40-04493a057cdb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258214 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258214 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258214 1111 #uuid "f2044120-320b-5665-8071-29b32ad9ca08" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258215 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258216 13194139533864 true] [17592186258215 1089 "q-288814" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258215 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258215 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-5x-14=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258215 1113 #uuid "939510b6-5bc7-5d08-b915-1376d3819fba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258215 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=7,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-14)}{7}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258216 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258217 13194139533864 true] [17592186258216 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258216 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258216 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258217 13194139533864 true] [17592186258216 1110 #uuid "60ce2755-627d-5f6b-adb2-bb7f7f424e14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258217 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258217 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258217 1111 #uuid "f16923e8-81d9-586a-aba2-f5c13f77c6c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258218 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258219 13194139533864 true] [17592186258218 1089 "q-288826" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258218 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258218 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+68x-1\\,041=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258218 1113 #uuid "ccac1a80-68f8-53f5-b849-c8a540e70082" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258218 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=68,\\,c=-1\\,041.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-1\\,041)}{1}=-1\\,041.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258219 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258220 13194139533864 true] [17592186258219 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258219 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258219 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258220 13194139533864 true] [17592186258219 1110 #uuid "5adcd2ec-d9e3-5e8d-8f85-09672d3a2271" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258220 1034 "-1\\,041" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258220 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258220 1111 #uuid "367a65ee-1249-516a-9dfb-c4291f663555" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258221 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258222 13194139533864 true] [17592186258221 1089 "q-29745" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258221 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258221 1100 "${\\int }_{-10}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258221 1113 #uuid "ea49163f-eaaf-5d9b-a819-45e18fcd5c81" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258221 1145 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-10}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}{|}_{-10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(-10)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2-50 \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258222 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258223 13194139533864 true] [17592186258222 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258222 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258222 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258223 13194139533864 true] [17592186258222 1110 #uuid "3e6f63af-0dee-5a8c-9f14-317d1122f8ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258223 1034 "-48" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258223 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258223 1111 #uuid "225a1fb0-b238-58ad-a91a-d398c30787f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258224 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258225 13194139533864 true] [17592186258224 1089 "q-29750" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258224 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258224 1100 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258224 1113 #uuid "8ba992bf-a932-50cb-b345-279ad6d9b588" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258224 1145 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2+1}}{-\\frac{1}{2}+1}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2{x}^{1/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}-1) \\\\  & =2(2-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258226 13194139533864 true] [17592186258225 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258225 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258225 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258226 13194139533864 true] [17592186258225 1110 #uuid "ae8feea0-bd5b-5959-9749-6a3add6e8c28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258226 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258226 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258226 1111 #uuid "761e4b32-9ff3-5679-b5ba-dc1e463c6577" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258227 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258228 13194139533864 true] [17592186258227 1089 "q-300969" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258227 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258227 1100 "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{\\,3{x}^{4}+5\\,}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258227 1113 #uuid "384b4b4a-1a21-5de5-b09c-7d8dcb9b51ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258227 1145 "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2x+8)}{{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and the denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2x+8) & =2{lim}_{x\\to -1}x+{lim}_{x\\to -1}8 \\\\  & =2(-1)+8 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{4}+5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3(-1)^{4}+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}} & =\\frac{6}{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258228 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258229 13194139533864 true] [17592186258228 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258228 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258228 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258229 13194139533864 true] [17592186258228 1110 #uuid "c85b5b07-734a-5431-a36d-96c50ba5499f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258229 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258229 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258229 1111 #uuid "40674303-c84e-53da-aa07-9a9b9e2be9fc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258230 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258231 13194139533864 true] [17592186258230 1089 "q-302152" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258230 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258230 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258230 1113 #uuid "03fac39f-7bd4-593c-8143-5d6d29276bf9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258230 1145 "The function ${log}_{6}(4x-2)$ is well defined for $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}(4\\cdot 2-2)}{2+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}({6}^{1})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258232 13194139533864 true] [17592186258231 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258231 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258231 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258232 13194139533864 true] [17592186258231 1110 #uuid "5a167e5e-f8ff-5323-922c-31faa60e4d63" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258232 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258232 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258232 1111 #uuid "e5f158ff-5efa-5a7b-be91-b288a1bd7ed3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258233 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258234 13194139533864 true] [17592186258233 1089 "q-302415" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258233 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258233 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258233 1113 #uuid "83f4f818-d835-5987-9636-e45c64049c02" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258233 1145 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258234 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258235 13194139533864 true] [17592186258234 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258234 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258234 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258235 13194139533864 true] [17592186258234 1110 #uuid "ba39c0e5-abab-533a-9258-7d7166fa507e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258235 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258235 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258235 1111 #uuid "70779499-2c86-54f2-81ee-5504191f9ca3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258236 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258237 13194139533864 true] [17592186258236 1089 "q-302430" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258236 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258236 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258236 1113 #uuid "001d04f8-b07b-5927-bb3f-243c2de00cc8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258236 1145 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258237 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258238 13194139533864 true] [17592186258237 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258237 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258237 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258238 13194139533864 true] [17592186258237 1110 #uuid "43f94853-1c0e-5c4c-9db2-3cf5d1d70da9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258238 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258238 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258238 1111 #uuid "31bd45ff-27ae-5ca3-a764-8f5b76e15666" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258239 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258240 13194139533864 true] [17592186258239 1089 "q-302976" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258239 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258239 1100 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m}=\\sqrt{5},$ what is the value of constant $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258239 1113 #uuid "cf9923c2-2a75-5d31-b240-73c84287294f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258239 1145 "Since we know that the limit of $\\sqrt{5x-m}$ exists, we can substitute $x=2$ into this expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m} & =\\sqrt{5(2)-m} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $\\sqrt{5},$ we can equate the above with $\\sqrt{5},$ and then solve for $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10-m} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 10-m & =5 \\\\ m & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258240 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258241 13194139533864 true] [17592186258240 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258240 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258240 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258241 13194139533864 true] [17592186258240 1110 #uuid "656f0b5e-8a20-5b17-b807-c16226a1ba77" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258241 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258241 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258241 1111 #uuid "f8ee5394-b2dd-50d9-b7b0-77c1f5409d4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258242 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258243 13194139533864 true] [17592186258242 1089 "q-3043" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258242 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258242 1100 "Solve for $a$ where $\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2.$\n\n$a$: {{a}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258242 1113 #uuid "2b6e17eb-b41f-58d1-bd2b-5852d16cb44e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258242 1145 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{2a+4}{5} & =4 \\\\ 2a+4 & =20 \\\\ a & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 8+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{16+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $a=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2$  is $a=8.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258244 13194139533864 true] [17592186258243 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258243 1041 "a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258243 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258244 13194139533864 true] [17592186258243 1110 #uuid "34d701d3-71f6-53e4-bd4d-224633ad5700" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258244 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258244 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258244 1111 #uuid "50506808-de9a-51bb-9395-185ea71aaaad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258245 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258246 13194139533864 true] [17592186258245 1089 "q-30612" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258245 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258245 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{2}x?$\n\nI. - The range is $y>0$\nII. - Its vertical asymptote is $x=2$\nIII. - It passes through the point $(2,1)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258245 1113 #uuid "5418831c-17ec-5431-913b-b0965c88ed86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258245 1145 "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{2}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fbc441f5915ef0dbe1e65ce1f748675e135ea8a2dc3a7d24dad56d55fca20eaf.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, we see that the range is all real numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vertical asymptote is $x=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Substituting $x=2$ into the equation of the curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}(2) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}({2}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258247 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258248 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258249 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258250 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258251 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258252 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258253 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258254 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258251 13194139533864 true] [17592186258246 1110 #uuid "0816cacf-8922-5aca-afbb-cbc4aa237440" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258247 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258247 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258247 1111 #uuid "620979e0-b1ef-525e-a283-e88692b6e5af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258248 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258248 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258248 1111 #uuid "de778815-cdbd-57af-b56c-86699d271222" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258249 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258249 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258249 1111 #uuid "c969f090-f90c-5e12-bfcb-3b0f97b43ade" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258250 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258250 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258250 1111 #uuid "92113b13-9afe-5b9c-9dad-982f3ab8fea1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258251 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258251 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258251 1111 #uuid "2f2fa861-14fc-5e57-8227-38e1af00edef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258252 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258252 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258252 1111 #uuid "7eef7db2-3ce0-5d08-970f-0e4cef942ffe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258253 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258253 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258253 1111 #uuid "b1be4ee3-a14c-54f0-9e69-0e8a5551cf51" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258254 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258254 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258254 1111 #uuid "209c21c4-7e6b-5776-9119-77f75ea9f42b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258255 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258256 13194139533864 true] [17592186258255 1089 "q-308798" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258255 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258255 1100 "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+3x$ at the point $(-1,-2).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258255 1113 #uuid "4147d71c-0780-5d7a-b644-4ac51dc7dac4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258255 1145 "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+3x)=2x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(-1)=2(-1)+3=-2+3=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258257 13194139533864 true] [17592186258256 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258256 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258256 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258257 13194139533864 true] [17592186258256 1110 #uuid "5df9d6fb-6be9-590e-a368-cd71009532f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258257 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258257 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258257 1111 #uuid "d6faeda4-9cd8-5fc1-a2cd-ee250d4c774f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258258 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258259 13194139533864 true] [17592186258258 1089 "q-310182" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258258 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258258 1100 "Find the slope $m$ of $y =\\sin \\left(\\pi \\left(x^{2} + 2x - 2\\right)\\right)$ at $x = 1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258258 1113 #uuid "4007a637-1203-5eab-9c3e-d86e260889f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258258 1145 "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (2x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (2x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (2\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =4\\pi \\cos(\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 4\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= -4\\pi\n\\end{align}\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-4\\pi}.\n\\]" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258259 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258260 13194139533864 true] [17592186258259 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258259 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258259 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258260 13194139533864 true] [17592186258259 1110 #uuid "a2a47021-fb80-56ac-9acd-3b46f185f95e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258260 1034 "$-4\\pi$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258260 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258260 1111 #uuid "b03bf73f-e8ce-5f5b-9004-f803d8be9067" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258261 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258262 13194139533864 true] [17592186258261 1089 "q-310788" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258261 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258261 1100 "Given that $-2{x}^{2}-4kx-8k=0,$ where $k$ is a **positive** constant, has exactly one real solution, what is the value of $k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258261 1113 #uuid "b9df0228-b059-53ad-bb1d-ba5e63872766" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258261 1145 "A quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is equal to zero: $D=0.$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na=-2,\\,b=-4k,\\,c=-8k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4k)^{2}-4(-2)(-8k) \\\\  & =16{k}^{2}-64k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need $D=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{k}^{2}-64k & =0 \\\\ 16k(k-4) & =0 \\\\ k & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be positive, we conclude that $k=4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258262 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258263 13194139533864 true] [17592186258262 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258262 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258262 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258263 13194139533864 true] [17592186258262 1110 #uuid "3e1c629d-9781-51b9-97e1-e229d8be1776" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258263 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258263 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258263 1111 #uuid "bcfe502a-e69f-59b8-bed5-dd062ff1b211" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258264 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258265 13194139533864 true] [17592186258264 1089 "q-31266" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258264 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258264 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258264 1113 #uuid "d6d25e57-c27a-5cc5-8667-e7f17ee59ccd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258264 1145 "Note that plugging in $x=-1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{[x+1]^{'}}{[3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(-1)^{2}-2(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258266 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258266 13194139533864 true] [17592186258265 1110 #uuid "7da96791-d575-5748-9560-bed41a152ebb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258266 1034 "\\frac{1}{11}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258266 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258266 1111 #uuid "abf12427-ef59-522b-a10d-49ebe034b6be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258267 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258268 13194139533864 true] [17592186258267 1089 "q-31272" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258267 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258267 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258267 1113 #uuid "5f6b45e4-aad3-52ce-a5c4-dc96f14dd157" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258267 1145 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8]}^{'}}{[{x}^{2}]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}^{'}}{[2x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(x+4)\\sqrt{x+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(0+4)\\sqrt{0+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{4(2)}]}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258269 13194139533864 true] [17592186258268 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258268 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258268 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258269 13194139533864 true] [17592186258268 1110 #uuid "d44d9737-467b-5c51-8ca8-3576f2404f16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258269 1034 "-\\frac{1}{16}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258269 1111 #uuid "25ff98e6-667e-58bd-98fc-d22141a55c7f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258270 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258271 13194139533864 true] [17592186258270 1089 "q-31288" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258270 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258270 1100 "Find ${f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ if $f(x)=-6\\operatorname{cot}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258270 1113 #uuid "81fa46b9-6e38-5bbb-a82c-d73296a4d0fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258270 1145 "First, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\operatorname{cot}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =-6(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =6{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =6{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6{(2)}^{2} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258271 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258272 13194139533864 true] [17592186258271 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258271 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258271 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258272 13194139533864 true] [17592186258271 1110 #uuid "34136657-7590-59a8-8251-ce13de8a18c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258272 1034 "24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258272 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258272 1111 #uuid "fe55bba9-dd10-5bc7-9f24-e71a53b0559b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258273 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258274 13194139533864 true] [17592186258273 1089 "q-31383" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258273 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258273 1100 "What is the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ in the product $(2x+y)({x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258273 1113 #uuid "cf4dcdbc-9168-55c3-a5fe-dc072fb8a7ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258273 1145 "We distribute both terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+y)({x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2}) & = \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2})+y({x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2}) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}y+6x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3} & = \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+4x{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ is $-3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258275 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258275 13194139533864 true] [17592186258274 1110 #uuid "a3ac9484-9a16-54ca-ad81-23e24fe0f882" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258275 1111 #uuid "66d3f27d-31a7-5ebb-a2b6-ef885bbff640" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258277 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258279 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1089 "q-315755" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4 \\\\ x={y}^{2}-10y+29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ of the system above.\n\n$(x,y)=($ {{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}} $)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1113 #uuid "5d374218-4fc1-5b1c-b8fc-aa9ccc1df000" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258276 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-10y+29 & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-10y+25 & =0 \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-5 & =0 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $(4,5).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258277 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258278 13194139533864 true] [17592186258277 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258277 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258277 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258278 13194139533864 true] [17592186258277 1110 #uuid "98a056b0-c3d0-51d6-975b-20443215dccf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258278 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258278 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258278 1111 #uuid "24270c0b-f6b0-5152-ae02-d754880a56f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258280 13194139533864 true] [17592186258279 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258279 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258279 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258280 13194139533864 true] [17592186258279 1110 #uuid "f3042e9c-e647-5a95-81fb-10468ab91b70" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258280 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258280 1111 #uuid "624c7ae5-bd38-54aa-be9a-0a6d0ecf556a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258281 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258282 13194139533864 true] [17592186258281 1089 "q-315758" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258281 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258281 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+2x-3 \\\\ y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf pairs $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ with ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the system above, what is the value of ${x}_{2}{y}_{2}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No real solutions\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258281 1113 #uuid "cf7ed733-de8f-586d-bd6c-3546abb19c44" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258281 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the obtained quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=-1\\cdot 2=-2,\\,c=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the discriminant of the quadratic is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system has no real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258283 13194139533864 true] [17592186258282 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258282 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258282 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258283 13194139533864 true] [17592186258282 1110 #uuid "b0195630-1fa8-58d5-b20c-3e9474d53208" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258283 1034 "No real solutions" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258283 1111 #uuid "e134657f-d454-513e-af3b-c71b4ee5796e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258285 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1089 "q-315764" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={y}^{2}-3y \\\\ x=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf pairs $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ with ${y}_{1}<{y}_{2}$ are the solutions of the system above, what is the value of ${x}_{1}{y}_{2}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1113 #uuid "2e2cc1ac-6bdd-51c7-9e50-11f12536a850" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258284 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-3y & =10 \\\\ {y}^{2}-3y-10 & =0 \\\\ (y-5)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,-2)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(10,5).\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{y}_{2}=(10)\\cdot (5)=50.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258286 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258286 13194139533864 true] [17592186258285 1110 #uuid "91e13cb0-b368-5ecf-ad50-5a5f174af71b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258286 1034 "50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258286 1111 #uuid "beecc13b-b00c-5d98-bd7b-d43e933947ee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258288 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258290 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1089 "q-315767" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=7 \\\\ x={y}^{2}+4y+11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ of the system above.\n\n$(x,y)=($ {{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}} $)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1113 #uuid "68136187-6129-51e6-90fd-f5ffc38cff9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258287 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+4y+11 & =7 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+2 & =0 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $(7,-2).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258289 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258289 13194139533864 true] [17592186258288 1110 #uuid "19a2f6e8-62c3-5307-aadb-70d34d8a2037" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258289 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258289 1111 #uuid "8863d29b-8e2a-5286-91cf-0c76e316cc04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258291 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258291 13194139533864 true] [17592186258290 1110 #uuid "76364189-2634-5706-b8d4-09600bad3673" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258291 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258291 1111 #uuid "42c10b4f-628c-5218-8d12-3cd70398ba78" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258292 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258293 13194139533864 true] [17592186258292 1089 "q-315776" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258292 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258292 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={y}^{2}-5y+6 \\\\ x=2y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf pairs $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ with ${y}_{1}<{y}_{2}$ are the solutions of the system above, what is the value of ${x}_{1}{y}_{2}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258292 1113 #uuid "96fa5c30-270f-5517-92f5-554b6af8cb5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258292 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5y+6 & =2y-4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-7y+10 & =0 \\\\ (y-5)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y$ in the second equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-4 \\\\  & =2(2)-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${y}_{2}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{y}_{2}-4 \\\\  & =2(5)-4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(6,5).\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{y}_{2}=(0)\\cdot (5)=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258294 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258294 13194139533864 true] [17592186258293 1110 #uuid "41185ab3-82a6-5e34-ba19-ade16b2c73b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258294 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258294 1111 #uuid "68a8f98f-9dff-58da-8426-3f2116ce5153" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258296 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258298 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1089 "q-315782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={y}^{2}+y+5 \\\\ x=-3y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ of the system above.\n\n$(x,y)=($ {{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}} $)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1113 #uuid "92e73856-4ae4-5727-a5f9-ecbb2cce4a26" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258295 1145 "First, we set the equations equal and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y+5 & =-3y+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+2 & =0 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3y+1 \\\\  & =-3(-2)+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $(7,-2).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258297 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258297 13194139533864 true] [17592186258296 1110 #uuid "6309eafd-2539-5eeb-8a2f-fb33e08e5c13" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258297 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258297 1111 #uuid "16fdb34f-cb52-52b3-8f12-8f1f4fc8cd02" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258299 13194139533864 true] [17592186258298 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258298 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258298 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258299 13194139533864 true] [17592186258298 1110 #uuid "873d2ca7-9ce3-5d5d-8074-201ffd7e6aa0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258299 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258299 1111 #uuid "50bdecd5-2ea0-5053-a2c9-36af6feb993a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258301 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1089 "q-315849" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(x,y)$ is a solution of the system above such that $x\\ne 0$, what is the value of $x+y?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1113 #uuid "d43a11ff-b10a-5b65-9970-066e56ae225b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258300 1145 "From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-6x.\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}-6x) \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}-12x \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12x & =0 \\\\ x(3x+12) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x$ in the first equation.\n\n- For ${x}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1} \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-6(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${x}_{2}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(-4)^{2}-6(-4) \\\\  & =16+24 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,0)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(-4,40).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require the solution such that $x\\ne 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & ={x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-4+40 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258302 13194139533864 true] [17592186258301 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258301 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258301 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258302 13194139533864 true] [17592186258301 1110 #uuid "9ca2b466-3788-5505-b7ed-22b8c756e13b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258302 1034 "36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258302 1111 #uuid "5d9b33ea-d06b-56b0-adff-af0c3dfb8852" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258304 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1089 "q-315861" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3{y}^{2}-4y \\\\ 8x-7{y}^{2}=5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(x,y)$ is a solution of the system above such that $x\\ne 0$, what is the value of $x+y?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1113 #uuid "7f964985-f603-54e9-95ca-111340622232" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258303 1145 "First, we simplify the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-7{y}^{2} & =5x \\\\ 3x-7{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x & =7{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, from the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx=3{y}^{2}-4y.\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =7{y}^{2} \\\\ 3(3{y}^{2}-4y) & =7{y}^{2} \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-12y & =7{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-12y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y & =0 \\\\ y(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y$ in the first equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =3{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =3(0)^{2}-4(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${y}_{2}=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =3{y}_{2}^{2}-4{y}_{2} \\\\  & =3(6)^{2}-4(6) \\\\  & =108-24 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,0)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(84,6).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require the solution such that $x\\ne 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & ={x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =84+6 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258305 13194139533864 true] [17592186258304 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258304 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258304 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258305 13194139533864 true] [17592186258304 1110 #uuid "b6f4edf2-6034-5d9e-8266-21ca7ae38846" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258305 1034 "90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258305 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258305 1111 #uuid "a501b122-c456-5b2c-befa-1a923f6cc418" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258307 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1089 "q-315865" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={y}^{2}+2y \\\\ 4x-3{y}^{2}=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(x,y)$ is a solution of the system above such that $y\\ne 0$, what is the value of $x-y?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1113 #uuid "e7347537-9022-5098-b0dd-a7d044f3e73e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258306 1145 "First, we simplify the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, from the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx={y}^{2}+2y.\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2({y}^{2}+2y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+4y & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y$ in the first equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =(0)^{2}+2(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${y}_{2}=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(4)^{2}+2(4) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,0)\\,\\text{and}\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(24,4).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require the solution such that $y\\ne 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & ={x}_{2}-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =24-4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258307 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258308 13194139533864 true] [17592186258307 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258307 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258307 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258308 13194139533864 true] [17592186258307 1110 #uuid "4dfff635-3e1f-572d-9f57-1ef58d08225b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1034 "20" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258308 1111 #uuid "22f30778-fb53-5312-8841-e16de75ddb46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258310 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1089 "q-316010" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1100 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1113 #uuid "b0bffb42-be09-55cb-8b31-881c330b35e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258309 1145 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{32}{x}^{-2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/5+1}}{(-\\frac{2}{5}+1)}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\,{x}^{3/5}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\,{(\\sqrt[5]{x})}^{3}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({(\\sqrt[5]{32})}^{3}-{(\\sqrt[5]{1})}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({2}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258310 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258311 13194139533864 true] [17592186258310 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258310 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258310 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258311 13194139533864 true] [17592186258310 1110 #uuid "42e20265-3fc6-5ad1-b14c-f0c396b19a7f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258311 1034 "\\frac{35}3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258311 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258311 1111 #uuid "5bdf05f2-2d0a-5785-8632-d7bb1bd2cd88" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258313 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258315 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1089 "q-316031" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1100 "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by the equation $s(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6}{{t}^{2}+1}$, where $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the position ${s}_{0}$ and velocity ${v}_{0}$ of the particle at the initial moment of time $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n${s}_{0}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}\\,\\text{and}\\,$\n${v}_{0}=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1113 #uuid "d6fb64ab-22ee-5c67-af5e-8451c17bd02b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258312 1145 "To calculate the initial position, we substitute $t=0$ it into the equation $s(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6}{{t}^{2}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =s(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(0+1)+6}{{0}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{t+1}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)-(\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6)\\cdot 2t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+1-2t(t+1)(\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)+6)}{(t+1)({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ we substitute $t=0$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{0}^{2}+1-2(0)(0+1)(\\operatorname{ln}(0+1)+6)}{(0+1)({0}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258314 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258314 13194139533864 true] [17592186258313 1110 #uuid "c6060556-4f2f-528e-867a-92246354b974" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258314 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258314 1111 #uuid "11d4230a-c6eb-5dc5-ad07-58e762322846" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258316 13194139533864 true] [17592186258315 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258315 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258315 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258316 13194139533864 true] [17592186258315 1110 #uuid "af6761b6-ac37-533b-acfa-de643b510281" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258316 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258316 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258316 1111 #uuid "34f275eb-583c-59aa-91f1-acad4dd146e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258318 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258320 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258322 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1089 "q-318377" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1100 "If $c=⟨4,2,6⟩$ and $d=⟨-1,5,5⟩$, then\n\n$c-d=⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}},\n{{field-3}}$⟩.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1113 #uuid "709b00dd-0479-5892-a6bc-f752585376ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258317 1145 "To compute the difference, we subtract each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c-d & =⟨4,2,6⟩-⟨-1,5,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4-(-1),2-5,6-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258318 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258319 13194139533864 true] [17592186258318 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258318 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258318 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258319 13194139533864 true] [17592186258318 1110 #uuid "33603e3b-595e-5033-8240-5f2bd5947655" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258319 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258319 1111 #uuid "a1403987-3894-5423-9bcc-4b82657f9801" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258321 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258321 13194139533864 true] [17592186258320 1110 #uuid "828e8116-4b8a-5fd6-a3eb-8d0be9348da6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258321 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258321 1111 #uuid "6b6ab8fc-95fb-5574-ab64-f987a6f978f0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258323 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258323 13194139533864 true] [17592186258322 1110 #uuid "3a341263-1c17-5be0-97fe-3840c5178af0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258323 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258323 1111 #uuid "253ff50e-1689-55cd-9278-d0a36f0f2a65" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258324 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258325 13194139533864 true] [17592186258324 1089 "q-319487" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258324 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258324 1100 "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=3+8\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258324 1113 #uuid "81300025-2eb5-531e-a8ef-a34d25e65f22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258324 1145 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fc3602d0d057902e1a510485ae8f02746afd80ea3bb7c2c8efc40ccfaf7b3dd4.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}(z)=arctan\\,(\\frac{8}{3})\\approx 1.21,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258326 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258326 13194139533864 true] [17592186258325 1110 #uuid "f998fc59-ebd1-51ac-aad9-bf3636d66256" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258326 1034 "1.21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258326 1111 #uuid "47c9d159-d38d-571e-8e23-96fca99598f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258327 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258328 13194139533864 true] [17592186258327 1089 "q-319495" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258327 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258327 1100 "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-7+5\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258327 1113 #uuid "1a69aadf-c1cd-5b5a-91d9-c39dafc5090a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258327 1145 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/86e7a97f9dcdec8a81d5362c79f15887da8ce13248c4758eafa49e960d8cf128.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{5}{7}) \\\\  & =0.620\\text{ }249\\text{ }…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi -0.620\\text{ }249\\text{ }…\\approx 2.52,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258329 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258329 13194139533864 true] [17592186258328 1110 #uuid "7a0f0d6c-b5f9-5ac1-8827-380546e14c22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258329 1034 "2.52" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258329 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258329 1111 #uuid "58558700-309c-52c4-af9e-3aa3f5fd3339" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258330 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258331 13194139533864 true] [17592186258330 1089 "q-319825" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258330 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258330 1100 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258330 1113 #uuid "dd0e54ab-23dd-5fbf-99e4-afe94b73a3d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258330 1145 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-1)}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{2(0-1)}{2(0)+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(-2)+\\operatorname{ln}(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258332 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258332 13194139533864 true] [17592186258331 1110 #uuid "8c4079ac-5969-5352-90bf-234b527c5450" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258332 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258332 1111 #uuid "48cf976f-c492-5116-a478-b5c3d499c7bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258334 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258336 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1089 "q-320153" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0b8cbae8855b443f0f06abd22ecbbf6336f0b5494e4a2ceb1b42ea77abfbbf15.png)\n\nExpress the complex number represented in the Argand diagram above in the form $bi+a,$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$i\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1113 #uuid "0425acaa-1c0b-5694-b107-74b9601dd896" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258333 1145 "The coordinates of the point are $(1,-5).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=1,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=1-5i.$ Finally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =1-5i \\\\  & =-5\\,i+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258335 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258335 13194139533864 true] [17592186258334 1110 #uuid "1fefbc6f-1f19-515b-82ae-260c313e48df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258335 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258335 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258335 1111 #uuid "407fd3df-d3d3-5c46-a5b2-64c625a711a8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258337 13194139533864 true] [17592186258336 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258336 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258336 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258337 13194139533864 true] [17592186258336 1110 #uuid "294c3075-7bff-58e6-ac44-1e6f9aa1e66d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258337 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258337 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258337 1111 #uuid "69f31a85-7a99-5202-8211-d6e74c6cb923" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258339 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258341 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1089 "q-320156" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/78f2b0b5784aee7237ad7a1912bf54f5371ec88fc504cee594618208f79b499b.png)\n\nExpress the complex number represented in the Argand diagram above in the form $bi+a,$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$i\\,-$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1113 #uuid "f4c8480d-b5e8-5a54-886d-eb152592bdcc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258338 1145 "The coordinates of the point are $(-6,3).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-6,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=-6+3i.$ Finally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-6+3i \\\\  & =3\\,i-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258340 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258340 13194139533864 true] [17592186258339 1110 #uuid "ea0da6aa-3703-5dc3-bd74-3a37024d0860" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258340 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258340 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258340 1111 #uuid "eadc3afc-965d-5497-a19e-5f6d336424a1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258341 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258342 13194139533864 true] [17592186258341 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258341 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258341 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258342 13194139533864 true] [17592186258341 1110 #uuid "af1ea0c3-ae93-594e-b961-0252edf69b5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258342 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258342 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258342 1111 #uuid "9664be6e-b39e-54ab-a099-49eb1d97c714" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258343 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258344 13194139533864 true] [17592186258343 1089 "q-320665" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258343 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258343 1100 "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258343 1113 #uuid "e1e3d3bc-82fc-5fa2-90ce-eb77683f685b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258343 1145 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258345 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258345 13194139533864 true] [17592186258344 1110 #uuid "1afda0f2-c499-5fb4-a591-efc3a186f139" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258345 1034 "45" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258345 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258345 1111 #uuid "ab2ed601-4938-54fd-9c7a-c1bcd1067fb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258346 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258347 13194139533864 true] [17592186258346 1089 "q-321138" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258346 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258346 1100 "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arctan}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258346 1113 #uuid "100a789f-1084-51be-ba86-3a2a27edfbe2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258346 1145 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4arctan\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=4\\cdot (-{30}^{∘})={-120}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258348 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258348 13194139533864 true] [17592186258347 1110 #uuid "3181f7fa-572e-51e1-934e-8f5364b3af9c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258348 1034 "-120" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258348 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258348 1111 #uuid "04ac59b0-6e4f-5dce-ae60-9b5f04dd2348" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258349 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258350 13194139533864 true] [17592186258349 1089 "q-321647" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258349 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258349 1100 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=25{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-x-5$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258349 1113 #uuid "6d0c7037-a792-5532-8a26-b8019a0ead40" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258349 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $25,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 5$, and $\\pm 25.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{25}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{5}{5},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{5}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{25}, \\\\ \\pm 5,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{25},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258351 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258351 13194139533864 true] [17592186258350 1110 #uuid "6be0c4e8-25bd-59ee-ae21-d0ce51fe84b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258351 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258351 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258351 1111 #uuid "427f79d7-8cbe-5fad-9c19-bda31862cb4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258353 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258355 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258357 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1089 "q-322683" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1100 "Write the vector $b=-7i+2j+5k$ in angle bracket notation.\n\n$b=⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}},\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1113 #uuid "e95cfe22-0303-57f5-8f01-714df12a8f83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258352 1145 "Remember that in general, if a point $B$ has coordinates $({b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=⟨{b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}⟩=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{x} \\\\ {b}_{y} \\\\ {b}_{z}\\end{aligned}]={b}_{x}\\,i+{b}_{y}\\,j+{b}_{z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =-7i+2j+5k \\\\  & =-7\\cdot i+2\\cdot j+5\\cdot k \\\\  & =⟨-7,2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258354 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258354 13194139533864 true] [17592186258353 1110 #uuid "2e2b2d3d-dc10-57bb-b4eb-b441ed7bed32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258354 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258354 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258354 1111 #uuid "be648c48-4047-584a-b794-e75cad2c3eac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258355 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258356 13194139533864 true] [17592186258355 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258355 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258355 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258356 13194139533864 true] [17592186258355 1110 #uuid "8681383d-8705-5781-b760-7f881834df21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258356 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258356 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258356 1111 #uuid "af0d2bc5-d722-511d-9c0b-71a05c581007" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258358 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258358 13194139533864 true] [17592186258357 1110 #uuid "f25949b9-4354-5131-a1b6-3792ad7c0e6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258358 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258358 1111 #uuid "4b38906a-c5ec-5e2c-aa82-e74377f9d2b7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258360 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258362 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258364 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1089 "q-323611" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1100 "The polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-10x-24$ has one of its roots in the interval $(2,4).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1113 #uuid "4acf3abe-b1ce-5c44-b7f4-f5eee1633c9e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258359 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-24$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-24}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 12,\\,\\pm 24.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(2,4)$ is $3.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}+6x+8).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-3)({x}^{2}+6x+8) \\\\  & =(x-3)(x+2)(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258361 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258361 13194139533864 true] [17592186258360 1110 #uuid "aec7e4e7-60c7-5348-97e4-6b83eb41af42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258361 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258361 1111 #uuid "052bb3bb-75e5-57ae-8dc4-4d7ca1d25494" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258363 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258363 13194139533864 true] [17592186258362 1110 #uuid "26471634-7f58-5bbf-b440-69354b9bced1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258363 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258363 1111 #uuid "ef634c1b-9d3d-5d79-a276-a4667af5e630" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258365 13194139533864 true] [17592186258364 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258364 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258364 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258365 13194139533864 true] [17592186258364 1110 #uuid "ea9f6c1c-9b24-557e-ac8d-62dfc574cbe3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258365 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258365 1111 #uuid "98b3f5f4-4a66-5a51-91f1-dc474368396a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258367 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1089 "q-323830" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1100 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=27{x}^{3}-54{x}^{2}+36x-8$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{2},1),$ the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ equals{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1113 #uuid "6dc510f3-7557-5168-b83b-b438937dc73f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258366 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $27.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\pm \\frac{1}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{9}, \\\\  & \\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{9},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{3},\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ are $\\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{2}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $27$ | $-54$ | $36$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $18$ | $-24$ | $8$ |\n|  | $27$ | $-36$ | $12$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{2}{3})(27{x}^{2}-36x+12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{2}{3})(27{x}^{2}-36x+12) \\\\  & =3(x-\\frac{2}{3})(9{x}^{2}-12x+4) \\\\  & =3(x-\\frac{2}{3})(3x-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(3x-2)(3x-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(3x-2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258368 13194139533864 true] [17592186258367 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258367 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258367 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258368 13194139533864 true] [17592186258367 1110 #uuid "233e0f41-f09e-5677-a93e-4488081bb7d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258368 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258368 1111 #uuid "263b2379-ca78-504d-b716-0871ff95c633" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258370 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258372 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258374 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1089 "q-326248" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1100 "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=1-{t}^{2},\\,1\\le t\\le 6.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{51}{2}$ | $62$ | $\\frac{245}{2}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-3$ | {{field-3}} | $-15$ | $-24$ | $-35$ |" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1113 #uuid "c623d73f-6289-5e32-add3-a8f8d30ef9c8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258369 1145 "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x={t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=(2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(2)=8-1=7.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=1-{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=1-(3)^{2}=1-9=-8.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(6),$ we substitute $t=6$ into $x={t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(6)=(6)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(6)=216-3=213.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n| **$t$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $7$ | $\\frac{51}{2}$ | $62$ | $\\frac{245}{2}$ | $213$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-8$ | $-15$ | $-24$ | $-35$ |" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258371 13194139533864 true] [17592186258370 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258370 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258370 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258371 13194139533864 true] [17592186258370 1110 #uuid "56edae5f-cb40-59c1-ab3c-e3780d816bb3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258371 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258371 1111 #uuid "2444c709-d123-5806-a9dd-efcb4142d7c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258373 13194139533864 true] [17592186258372 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258372 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258372 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258373 13194139533864 true] [17592186258372 1110 #uuid "dfb086a3-c35b-5665-aba1-7223cff63bb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258373 1034 "213" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258373 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258373 1111 #uuid "f9a2a163-0346-5e9c-9fe0-f4cad81b99e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258375 13194139533864 true] [17592186258374 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258374 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258374 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258375 13194139533864 true] [17592186258374 1110 #uuid "c4eaa414-d93f-5dbe-9d15-6c71f8462269" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258375 1034 "-8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258375 1111 #uuid "0a7835fe-8c51-5047-bad9-73b70954bbe8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258376 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258377 13194139533864 true] [17592186258376 1089 "q-328242" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258376 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258376 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258376 1113 #uuid "47d2cbf1-1164-5cb5-ab6c-facf3e8732c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258376 1145 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\pi +2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n \\\\ 6x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{1}{9}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258378 13194139533864 true] [17592186258377 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258377 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258377 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258378 13194139533864 true] [17592186258377 1110 #uuid "5c059408-4e1e-56d3-9bee-5e0446a61f1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258378 1034 "\\frac{1}{9}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258378 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258378 1111 #uuid "8481b97c-79bd-5680-a936-855eee350617" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258379 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258380 13194139533864 true] [17592186258379 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258382 13194139533864 true] [17592186258379 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258384 13194139533864 true] [17592186258379 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258386 13194139533864 true] [17592186258379 1089 "q-328346" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258379 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258379 1100 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the **exact solutions** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}(2x+{45}^{∘})=1,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258379 1113 #uuid "2ef87672-04a1-57b6-b8ef-a21f1ff660eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258379 1145 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{45}^{∘} & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-{45}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =n\\cdot {90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={0}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={90}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={180}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={270}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={270}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258380 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258381 13194139533864 true] [17592186258380 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258380 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258380 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258381 13194139533864 true] [17592186258380 1110 #uuid "6313b72d-4ece-5327-8d7f-7d8b27de6a3b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258381 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258381 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258381 1111 #uuid "6c158c77-0adb-5f06-8d4b-c07d37822cc7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258383 13194139533864 true] [17592186258382 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258382 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258382 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258383 13194139533864 true] [17592186258382 1110 #uuid "ee14a56f-6429-5c5e-a654-22356ab98625" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258383 1034 "90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258383 1111 #uuid "2f7935d6-915d-546d-9ca6-05f8521d9bd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258384 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258385 13194139533864 true] [17592186258384 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258384 1041 "field-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258384 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258385 13194139533864 true] [17592186258384 1110 #uuid "79538ab1-c604-5e0b-ab5a-c1801bb24957" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258385 1034 "180" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258385 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258385 1111 #uuid "797a29ae-b1ec-5db8-ac8e-934955f9ac1f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258386 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258387 13194139533864 true] [17592186258386 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258386 1041 "field-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258386 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258387 13194139533864 true] [17592186258386 1110 #uuid "c520450c-f7cf-5826-9d4e-e16ba09003e0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258387 1034 "270" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258387 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258387 1111 #uuid "f754096a-e449-5d46-aad7-4001bd2da0e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258388 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258389 13194139533864 true] [17592186258388 1089 "q-328449" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258388 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258388 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(4x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{24}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\\,x=a\\pi +\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258388 1113 #uuid "3b14cf9c-c1ee-5a69-a0d8-213d7f9bcecc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258388 1145 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(4x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n4x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,4x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n, & \\,4x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{24}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}, & \\,x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{11}{24}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258389 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258390 13194139533864 true] [17592186258389 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258389 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258389 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258390 13194139533864 true] [17592186258389 1110 #uuid "73c1882f-1dd2-54b0-974a-249353e6af9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258390 1034 "\\frac{11}{24}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258390 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258390 1111 #uuid "c9797fa8-ae3c-59f1-81ef-9302007dc2a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258391 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258392 13194139533864 true] [17592186258391 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258394 13194139533864 true] [17592186258391 1089 "q-332197" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258391 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258391 1100 "Express $\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}x$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258391 1113 #uuid "889a0c6a-4567-5269-8c45-169860a4a405" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258391 1145 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =0\\,\\operatorname{sin}x+1\\,\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258393 13194139533864 true] [17592186258392 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258392 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258392 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258393 13194139533864 true] [17592186258392 1110 #uuid "76bf53f9-dc9f-5679-8fdd-3f5f5d0ff6ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258393 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258393 1111 #uuid "30a15c72-9a15-5022-99b2-0c0968a3a7be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258394 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258395 13194139533864 true] [17592186258394 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258394 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258394 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258395 13194139533864 true] [17592186258394 1110 #uuid "5b15d72a-1a4d-5b32-bf7a-3349b514d1cc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258395 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258395 1111 #uuid "710df7e8-f09e-59ea-941f-dd92b0e48503" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258396 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258397 13194139533864 true] [17592186258396 1089 "q-332500" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258396 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258396 1100 "Using the fact that ${120}^{∘}={150}^{∘}-{30}^{∘},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258396 1113 #uuid "c17df135-ee84-5f51-838d-783803b3c840" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258396 1145 "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={150}^{∘}$ and $y={30}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({150}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}({150}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({30}^{∘})+\\operatorname{sin}({150}^{∘})\\operatorname{sin}({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258397 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258398 13194139533864 true] [17592186258397 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258397 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258397 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258398 13194139533864 true] [17592186258397 1110 #uuid "8d6cd35c-99df-53bf-a223-b226bd97ad44" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258398 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258398 1111 #uuid "51848a55-9dcd-5809-8d1d-b784e728b2d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258399 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258400 13194139533864 true] [17592186258399 1089 "q-334171" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258399 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258399 1100 "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258399 1113 #uuid "0de860f6-11f7-5006-bbaa-d1ca0fb6d2bc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258399 1145 "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258400 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258401 13194139533864 true] [17592186258400 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258400 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258400 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258401 13194139533864 true] [17592186258400 1110 #uuid "13b5cebc-ed49-5730-9a88-a8e0f04c3d34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258401 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258401 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258401 1111 #uuid "1c4fbdff-9f6e-56e1-a6c8-7bcfab6bbe8b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258402 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258403 13194139533864 true] [17592186258402 1089 "q-334189" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258402 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258402 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258402 1113 #uuid "9c04f322-6297-5a59-99f0-946aa65e8f91" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258402 1145 "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{5}-4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot 0=0\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258404 13194139533864 true] [17592186258403 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258403 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258403 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258404 13194139533864 true] [17592186258403 1110 #uuid "5fd90552-824a-5df9-b4cd-4c041b6d04fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258404 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258404 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258404 1111 #uuid "5268fcad-4ae8-573e-80fe-07a9521ab94c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258405 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258406 13194139533864 true] [17592186258405 1089 "q-334208" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258405 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258405 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/26e2e4a51d40e9ed8e6a8fab342935e944e750587534c7cedc1855cbfddfbfc5.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258405 1113 #uuid "521e4421-8c12-5483-a9ad-eb3663c791eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258405 1145 "From the diagram, ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}+5x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}+5x-6$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=2\\cdot 0=0\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258407 13194139533864 true] [17592186258406 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258406 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258406 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258407 13194139533864 true] [17592186258406 1110 #uuid "b713fe42-1112-55c0-a3e9-179614c29a7c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258407 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258407 1111 #uuid "a6bb6be2-87df-5a90-b8da-3fd783476439" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258408 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258409 13194139533864 true] [17592186258408 1089 "q-334343" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258408 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258408 1100 "Given that $3\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =10\\operatorname{csc}\\theta ,$ calculate $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta .$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258408 1113 #uuid "930639cf-caf6-5e8d-b822-7d58d5edf090" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258408 1145 "First, we recall the identities $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sec}\\theta  & =10\\operatorname{csc}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write the last equation in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }$ only, we multiply both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,$ and apply the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =\\frac{10\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =10 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta $ by dividing both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{3} & =\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258409 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258410 13194139533864 true] [17592186258409 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258409 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258409 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258410 13194139533864 true] [17592186258409 1110 #uuid "0c403739-b544-5818-95a2-e4df38d67d03" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258410 1034 "\\frac{10}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258410 1111 #uuid "20347862-221b-5860-8592-68978e928244" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258411 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258412 13194139533864 true] [17592186258411 1089 "q-336160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258411 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258411 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}t,\\,0<t<\\pi .\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n$v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258411 1113 #uuid "9e386cce-90e7-5313-a0f9-874041a6503c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258411 1145 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}t) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258412 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258413 13194139533864 true] [17592186258412 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258412 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258412 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258413 13194139533864 true] [17592186258412 1110 #uuid "335c7175-1070-581d-8e1f-409546a66e39" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258413 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258413 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258413 1111 #uuid "4500fac8-af20-5c20-911d-1f32dbb57955" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258414 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258415 13194139533864 true] [17592186258414 1089 "q-336189" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258414 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258414 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}t-8\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258414 1113 #uuid "d1f7c427-fd09-51c4-aeca-22ae40dad476" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258414 1145 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}t-8\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}t+8\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =6\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+8\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =6(0)+8(-1) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-8|=8\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258415 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258416 13194139533864 true] [17592186258415 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258415 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258415 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258416 13194139533864 true] [17592186258415 1110 #uuid "d79b4b85-5bf6-5426-8987-1d99cefbf281" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258416 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258416 1111 #uuid "400aa6d7-ad0b-55c5-9730-4139ca77b867" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258417 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258418 13194139533864 true] [17592186258417 1089 "q-3362" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258417 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258417 1100 "If ${27}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{81})}^{-2n+8},$ then $n=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258417 1113 #uuid "11121e1d-3771-5b73-8d84-a5287eac4550" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258417 1145 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $27={3}^{3}$ and $81={3}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{3})}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}})}^{-2n+8}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the power of quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{4(-2n+8)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}={3}^{-4(-2n+8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2n+2) & =-4(-2n+8) \\\\ 6n+6 & =8n-32 \\\\ 2n & =38 \\\\ n & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258418 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258419 13194139533864 true] [17592186258418 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258418 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258418 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258419 13194139533864 true] [17592186258418 1110 #uuid "e7b5992c-e506-553c-a960-95b6a05f6a33" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258419 1034 "19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258419 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258419 1111 #uuid "88946dd7-a497-59d4-9d3a-53ea96edf878" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258420 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258421 13194139533864 true] [17592186258420 1089 "q-336433" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258420 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258420 1100 "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by\n$v(t)=\\frac{t+3}{t+1}.$\nCalculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhen $a=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ the velocity is $v=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258420 1113 #uuid "2eb4629d-fc7e-5d92-9fc1-95bd60f68d87" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258420 1145 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t+3}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot (t+1)-(t+3)\\cdot (1)}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{t+1-t-3}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ (t+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ t+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ t & =-3,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $t=1$ since $t\\ge 0.$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=1,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{1+3}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\text{ m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $v=2\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258422 13194139533864 true] [17592186258421 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258421 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258421 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258422 13194139533864 true] [17592186258421 1110 #uuid "dbbe1e2b-3bac-52e4-9a4d-e5eeb164f79a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258422 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258422 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258422 1111 #uuid "45d1a1b1-aaed-55d9-93ee-6e710a836e33" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258423 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258424 13194139533864 true] [17592186258423 1089 "q-336903" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258423 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258423 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258423 1113 #uuid "e257b16a-a775-5397-9d95-12edd8b09eb7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258423 1145 "If we plug in $x=-4$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[(x+4)^{3}]}^{'}}{{[3(x+5)^{1/3}-x-7]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[3(x+4)^{2}]}^{'}}{{[(x+5)^{-2/3}-1]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{6(x+4)}{-\\frac{2}{3}(x+5)^{-5/3}} \\\\  & =-9{lim}_{x\\to -4}(x+4)(x+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9(-4+4)\\,(-4+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot 0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258424 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258425 13194139533864 true] [17592186258424 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258424 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258424 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258425 13194139533864 true] [17592186258424 1110 #uuid "65b65e21-4124-599c-8ef5-38bd7add3afa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258425 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258425 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258425 1111 #uuid "ee0f398f-597a-5b09-b835-c7b222771d92" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258426 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258427 13194139533864 true] [17592186258426 1089 "q-336955" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258426 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258426 1100 "If $f(x)=3{x}^{9}$ and $g(x)=-2{x}^{-11}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\cdot g(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258426 1113 #uuid "39ff6f11-126f-5d17-90bb-5fef510de3a4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258426 1145 "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot f(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (3{x}^{9})=3{x}^{11}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)$ and $g(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\cdot g(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{11}\\cdot (-2{x}^{-11})) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-6 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258427 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258428 13194139533864 true] [17592186258427 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258427 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258427 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258428 13194139533864 true] [17592186258427 1110 #uuid "f90da28f-f2df-5f89-bc5c-259715754e58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258428 1034 "-6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258428 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258428 1111 #uuid "eb907c0d-3b8a-5e38-bd9a-bee10c75c1dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258429 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258430 13194139533864 true] [17592186258429 1089 "q-337889" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258429 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258429 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{8(x-1)}+2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258429 1113 #uuid "eed63dc5-4938-533a-9ddd-3ca6174a3bcf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258429 1145 "We substitute $x=1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{8(x-1)}+2) & =\\sqrt[3]{8(1-1)}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{0}+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258430 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258431 13194139533864 true] [17592186258430 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258430 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258430 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258431 13194139533864 true] [17592186258430 1110 #uuid "2dc19a03-5ebe-5d9b-8e53-685a0cef23f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258431 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258431 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258431 1111 #uuid "3d50905c-b55c-5093-a351-7a4a8afb1004" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258432 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258433 13194139533864 true] [17592186258432 1089 "q-338361" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258432 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258432 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/14340a0938b61ef8c71a687bb280f601ade6aa062d6d4dd87d2786113692fac1.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(f(x)+2)^{4}}{(f(x)-5)^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258432 1113 #uuid "4a0d1c8d-bddb-5da2-84fd-afcb22c27644" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258432 1145 "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(f(x)+2)^{4}}{(f(x)-5)^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+2))}^{4}}{{({lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)-5))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)+2)=2+2=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(f(x)-5)=2-5=-3\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(f(x)+2)^{4}}{(f(x)-5)^{3}} & =\\frac{{4}^{4}}{(-3)^{3}}=\\frac{256}{-27}=-\\frac{256}{27}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258434 13194139533864 true] [17592186258433 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258433 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258433 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258434 13194139533864 true] [17592186258433 1110 #uuid "a30acf33-8dde-533b-bd36-a66e5c640b6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258434 1034 "-\\frac{256}{27}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258434 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258434 1111 #uuid "6a3feeeb-3691-52ea-bd09-ec47e1d1905a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258436 13194139533864 true] [17592186258435 1089 "q-338377" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258435 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258435 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a086a1f616aee596a021ee4ef6bdc8723309cf8959719e9f55d6f20b845bd760.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258435 1113 #uuid "399fc725-0978-537e-bb0c-8bab97a72cc3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258435 1145 "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=4.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3}={lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x))^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3))}^{2} \\\\  & ={((-2)^{2}+3)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(4+3)}^{2} \\\\  & ={7}^{2} \\\\  & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x))^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & ={(5-4)}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3} & =49\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258437 13194139533864 true] [17592186258436 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258436 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258436 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258437 13194139533864 true] [17592186258436 1110 #uuid "38611adc-fcfc-57f3-a7f8-c0d50374f43d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258437 1034 "49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258437 1111 #uuid "303d6888-a0f9-5d27-9bf3-15b38bc9ebd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258438 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258439 13194139533864 true] [17592186258438 1089 "q-338382" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258438 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258438 1100 "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 25}{x}^{-1/2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258438 1113 #uuid "58b9aff2-9496-5de6-9055-ee77ce1e5ffe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258438 1145 "Applying the root rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 25}{x}^{-1/2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 25}x)}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =(25)^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={({5}^{2})}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={5}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258439 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258440 13194139533864 true] [17592186258439 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258439 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258439 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258440 13194139533864 true] [17592186258439 1110 #uuid "96e2c7e3-8338-5551-8028-99ce0a4060df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258440 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258440 1111 #uuid "263f8f83-8f38-59a3-8479-d6660d0fddf4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258441 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258442 13194139533864 true] [17592186258441 1089 "q-338473" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258441 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258441 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}-2}{x-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258441 1113 #uuid "c474b397-28c9-5a8f-b7cf-50951877499f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258441 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}-2}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(2)}-2}{2-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}-2}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x}-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x-4}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(\\sqrt{2x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(2)}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258443 13194139533864 true] [17592186258442 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258442 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258442 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258443 13194139533864 true] [17592186258442 1110 #uuid "3bc45c87-8b02-5cbf-82c3-75765280b0a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258443 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258443 1111 #uuid "c1ac8c16-0300-57ea-be9d-c169520d5e13" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258444 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258445 13194139533864 true] [17592186258444 1089 "q-338485" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258444 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258444 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258444 1113 #uuid "5098a209-5e2a-59a9-840f-1d112ceb224b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258444 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & =\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3(0)+25}}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(5-\\sqrt{3x+25})(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3x+25})}^{2}}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{25-(3x+25)}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3x}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3x+25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3(0)+25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258446 13194139533864 true] [17592186258445 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258445 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258445 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258446 13194139533864 true] [17592186258445 1110 #uuid "76b70a58-b70f-5293-b8fb-3650a675967b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258446 1034 "-\\frac3{10}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258446 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258446 1111 #uuid "74d73477-f34f-5580-b9b7-c8a24304e0bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258447 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258448 13194139533864 true] [17592186258447 1089 "q-338487" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258447 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258447 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2}}{x-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258447 1113 #uuid "aa14dfc7-bd15-596f-a1c8-753114c04592" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258447 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2}}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(2)-2}-\\sqrt{2+2}}{2-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2}}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2})(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{3x-2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3x-2)-(x+2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x-4}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3x-2}+\\sqrt{x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3(2)-2}+\\sqrt{2+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258448 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258448 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258448 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258448 1110 #uuid "d824f055-2a42-57ab-b57c-c2792c42c0d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258449 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258449 1111 #uuid "a7a56ba9-d5c8-5edf-b051-a0fcda4a8487" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258450 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258451 13194139533864 true] [17592186258450 1089 "q-338517" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258450 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258450 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258450 1113 #uuid "7c248ff4-4847-5ae9-892d-0c9714b8f260" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258450 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-9)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x+1)-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{\\cancel{x-3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\,(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2) \\\\  & =(3+3)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2) \\\\  & =6(4) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258452 13194139533864 true] [17592186258451 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258451 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258451 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258452 13194139533864 true] [17592186258451 1110 #uuid "6b5640e3-e135-59a8-88e8-a44d496816e2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258452 1034 "24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258452 1111 #uuid "6a3764b5-0eaf-586c-8cf3-8754e6fd6227" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258453 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258453 1089 "q-338671" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258453 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258453 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258453 1113 #uuid "d11fc22e-27dc-5e16-bb71-552dd8da0121" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258453 1145 "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3(x+2)}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3(x+2)}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258455 13194139533864 true] [17592186258454 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258454 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258454 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258455 13194139533864 true] [17592186258454 1110 #uuid "2e8ec972-c67d-5fea-a1b3-1aac198c5c4a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258455 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258455 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258455 1111 #uuid "578b2cf5-183e-5a1f-b8c5-5dba90fd4584" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258456 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258457 13194139533864 true] [17592186258456 1089 "q-338679" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258456 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258456 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258456 1113 #uuid "ae096ba8-7305-5c9b-9b5b-b942d8e27c2c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258456 1145 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=0-3=-3.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258457 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258458 13194139533864 true] [17592186258457 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258457 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258457 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258458 13194139533864 true] [17592186258457 1110 #uuid "9be028ea-240c-5f99-b73d-942fa2a9e307" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258458 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258458 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258458 1111 #uuid "ea78c6f0-f62c-57c9-8f0a-9bae1cffea30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258459 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258460 13194139533864 true] [17592186258459 1089 "q-339133" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258459 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258459 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258459 1113 #uuid "fab1224a-731f-5aa7-8ae2-ee992a17f981" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258459 1145 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi } & =\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}(1)}{{1}^{2}+\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\cdot 0}{1+\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258460 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258461 13194139533864 true] [17592186258460 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258460 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258460 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258461 13194139533864 true] [17592186258460 1110 #uuid "a4b9627a-e7d2-5f0e-a6ad-61c7f1aa6553" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258461 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258461 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258461 1111 #uuid "4772e15f-3158-59c7-83b6-3713f61f26b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258462 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258462 1089 "q-339377" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258462 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258462 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258462 1113 #uuid "130e1293-e299-5dab-8944-efcd214f6324" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258462 1145 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ce2078de99b2c037b0ab6d5c5dcd4dab1e8aa7005417d87ca83176bf197fd5dc.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=\\frac{8}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258463 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258463 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258463 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258463 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258463 1110 #uuid "6954fc0f-4cd6-5486-b67a-aa9af0bf66de" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258464 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258464 1111 #uuid "0c5cac94-0da9-5a00-a571-d2ac997c806a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258465 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258466 13194139533864 true] [17592186258465 1089 "q-339584" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258465 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258465 1100 "If $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and $g(3)=16,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(4g(x)-3x\\sqrt{g(3)})=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258465 1113 #uuid "63cab282-4c4b-564d-875e-58dade96cb8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258465 1145 "Since $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x)=g(3)=16.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(4g(x)-3x\\sqrt{g(3)}) & =4({lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x))-({lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}3x)\\sqrt{g(3)} \\\\  & =4(16)-3(3)\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =64-9(4) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258467 13194139533864 true] [17592186258466 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258466 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258466 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258467 13194139533864 true] [17592186258466 1110 #uuid "61daeea4-8a05-5ace-93c2-681e77dda30d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258467 1034 "28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258467 1111 #uuid "b7f8b837-aa2a-5d9d-aa9b-832ee54e5b21" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258468 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258469 13194139533864 true] [17592186258468 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258471 13194139533864 true] [17592186258468 1089 "q-339731" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258468 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258468 1100 "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ 5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x) & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258468 1113 #uuid "77b4e96f-5072-5275-8cf8-c358fd213cd4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258468 1145 "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\pi -\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge \\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}(5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & =5+2\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5+2(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258470 13194139533864 true] [17592186258469 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258469 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258469 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258470 13194139533864 true] [17592186258469 1110 #uuid "512c6bf0-66f2-5e2b-aebd-63701a637a3f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258470 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258470 1111 #uuid "7b881693-bfa3-5064-accc-6349d95b1535" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258472 13194139533864 true] [17592186258471 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258471 1041 "field-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258471 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258472 13194139533864 true] [17592186258471 1110 #uuid "db98dfee-cb1f-54a6-a984-ac6ce65a2605" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258472 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258472 1111 #uuid "3e8d4895-61e5-563e-9628-0b60ef993e8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258473 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258474 13194139533864 true] [17592186258473 1089 "q-339827" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258473 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258473 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258473 1113 #uuid "e2bb04bd-6dcb-51fa-87c5-f2822ddf4961" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258473 1145 "The function ${log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)$ is well defined for $x>-7$ and $x<5,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x) & ={log}_{3}(2+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-2) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}(9)\\cdot {log}_{3}(3) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}({3}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258475 13194139533864 true] [17592186258474 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258474 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258474 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258475 13194139533864 true] [17592186258474 1110 #uuid "b52ad5f2-2eaf-5cc7-b3fc-39152ab8f994" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258475 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258475 1111 #uuid "2e4ec5d3-d8f6-557d-baf7-74248b61e78e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258476 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258477 13194139533864 true] [17592186258476 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258479 13194139533864 true] [17592186258476 1089 "q-339964" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258476 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258476 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+3x-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258476 1113 #uuid "c4bb0f1e-ed08-55ef-acdf-b88dc863724a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258476 1145 "Approaching $x=1$ from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}+3x-5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}[(x-2)^{2}+3x-5] \\\\  & =(1-2)^{2}+3(1)-5 \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+3-5 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=4$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_