 (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258622 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258622 13194139533864 true] [17592186258621 1110 #uuid "e2db9c67-a917-5a3d-8f0f-9bfae2afb4b9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258622 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258622 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258622 1111 #uuid "e7d861e0-b37e-5859-8382-862845635d7f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258624 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1089 "q-35252" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1100 "Find ${lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}(4y-1)^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1113 #uuid "dbe7843b-5fc3-5be1-af00-dea6856d2e57" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258623 1145 "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}(4y-1)^{2} & ={lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to 1}(4y-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{y\\to 1}(2{y}^{2}+1))}^{3}\\cdot {({lim}_{y\\to 1}(4y-1))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(2{(1)}^{2}+1)}^{3}{(4(1)-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3)^{3}(3)^{2} \\\\  & =243\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258625 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258625 13194139533864 true] [17592186258624 1110 #uuid "7e1331a0-8fbd-524a-8c07-5d64e2c40536" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258625 1034 "243" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258625 1111 #uuid "db5e330d-2cdf-5ad1-9ff4-ab4c886ff4bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258627 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1089 "q-35282" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1100 "Calculate ${lim}_{z\\to (-4)}\\frac{{({z}^{2}-10)}^{2}}{(z+6)^{5}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1113 #uuid "80fccaeb-23c0-5dec-b69b-a4bfad7c73ad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258626 1145 "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to (-4)}\\frac{{({z}^{2}-10)}^{2}}{(z+6)^{5}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{z\\to (-4)}{({z}^{2}-10)}^{2}}{{lim}_{z\\to (-4)}(z+6)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({lim}_{z\\to (-4)}({z}^{2}-10))}^{2}}{{({lim}_{z\\to (-4)}(z+6))}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{((-4)^{2}-10)}^{2}}{(-4+6)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{2}}{{2}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258628 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258628 13194139533864 true] [17592186258627 1110 #uuid "017ebeeb-f352-508f-8a30-1c71f2f15297" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258628 1034 "\\frac{9}{8}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258628 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258628 1111 #uuid "e5ea2b8d-d5ab-5793-a4d2-056df9235ce4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258630 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1089 "q-35295" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/95e092490e087b5245f05b352b4738beb1ce3d9d649ea289ded881d0728129e5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1113 #uuid "238f1bf7-e926-5a9a-b458-b3d344bdf40c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258629 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}f(x)=1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}(4(f(x))^{4}+10x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(1)^{4}+10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258631 13194139533864 true] [17592186258630 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258630 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258630 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258631 13194139533864 true] [17592186258630 1110 #uuid "e0d3983f-d0e2-5d03-9246-2bc06892aa79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258631 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258631 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258631 1111 #uuid "5bc01e6e-6ce6-5bc8-b813-3f42cc3847c3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258633 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1089 "q-353317" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1100 "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a third term equal to $27$ and a sixth term equal to $1.$ Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the terms of this sequence. Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1113 #uuid "c5b3ef5f-3bcf-5c98-a527-aed005bdad3c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258632 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{3}=27$ and ${a}_{6}=1.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=3$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}(r)^{2} \\\\ 27 & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\ 27 & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{9}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =243\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =243(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{729}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258634 13194139533864 true] [17592186258633 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258633 1041 "field-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258633 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258634 13194139533864 true] [17592186258633 1110 #uuid "293eeead-4ae0-5c88-9e68-19a4cafcedc9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258634 1034 "\\frac{729}2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258634 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258634 1111 #uuid "f62e55a8-541f-5460-be29-38471e8e82e0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258636 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1089 "q-35621" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e647ebf6e8332e9be5abd75e0f5ac5102c89c4a40590cef21a8ddfc65a17701d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1113 #uuid "18d96d23-85b4-508f-bbdc-a059435bb5b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258635 1145 "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4}={lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x))^{4}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(3(-1)^{2}-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x))^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x)))}^{4} \\\\  & ={(2-1)}^{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1))}^{2}{({lim}_{x\\to -1}(2-f(x)))}^{4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258636 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258637 13194139533864 true] [17592186258636 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258636 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258636 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258637 13194139533864 true] [17592186258636 1110 #uuid "4e9111b4-8506-5726-8c62-7d58cfb11fa0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258637 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258637 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258637 1111 #uuid "e2404cbf-3ca7-59ae-a93b-18a00631f502" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258639 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1089 "q-35630" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1100 "Calculate ${lim}_{y\\to 128}{y}^{-1/7}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1113 #uuid "71c89bbc-3485-5efc-ade9-48f584e4aa2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258638 1145 "Applying the root rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 128}{y}^{-1/7} & ={({lim}_{y\\to 128}y)}^{-1/7} \\\\  & =(128)^{-1/7} \\\\  & ={({2}^{7})}^{-1/7} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258640 13194139533864 true] [17592186258639 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258639 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258639 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258640 13194139533864 true] [17592186258639 1110 #uuid "17995870-8b7a-576c-a863-4cb40c562045" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258640 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258640 1111 #uuid "3c3a05bd-de3a-5c6b-8f3a-7f802aea3a86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258642 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258644 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1089 "q-36695" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1100 "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=0$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}x+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLeft limit: {{left-limit}}\n\nRight limit: {{right-limit}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1113 #uuid "98dc4d3c-dae5-5596-b008-46a065491e6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258641 1145 "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(-x+1) \\\\  & =-(0)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}x+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}0+1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258642 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258643 13194139533864 true] [17592186258642 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258642 1041 "left-limit" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258642 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258643 13194139533864 true] [17592186258642 1110 #uuid "b6d801d0-4de9-5e1e-8201-11f884ef6c79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258643 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258643 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258643 1111 #uuid "4ca66b35-65c0-58bf-b873-78af0e82f941" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258645 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1041 "right-limit" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258645 13194139533864 true] [17592186258644 1110 #uuid "f54c2141-d85b-57b8-b293-61693a3d01dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258645 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258645 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258645 1111 #uuid "b28aa2e0-30f0-557d-8d6a-a9a9b931d232" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258647 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258649 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1089 "q-36743" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1100 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+cx+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$\n\n$b$: {{b}}\n\n$c$: {{c}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1113 #uuid "3cbc081a-4656-5dca-b3b1-aa3f8536f992" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258646 1145 "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+b) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2(1)+b \\\\  & =1+2+b \\\\  & =b+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ b+3 & =2 \\\\ b & =2-3 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+cx+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{2}+cx+2) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+c(1)+2 \\\\  & =1+c+2 \\\\  & =c+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ c+3 & =2 \\\\ c & =2-3 \\\\ c & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258648 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1041 "b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258648 13194139533864 true] [17592186258647 1110 #uuid "fef77817-3574-5917-8751-f6dc6ac2eaef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258648 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258648 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258648 1111 #uuid "3627d8f3-7507-59fd-add7-0a30a7f9cd5f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258650 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1041 "c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258650 13194139533864 true] [17592186258649 1110 #uuid "8e135889-0014-59ef-a4ff-b04ac2c9f0e6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258650 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258650 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258650 1111 #uuid "fda01204-9e15-5935-8160-c3810f97cd32" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258652 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258654 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1089 "q-36744" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1100 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+cx+1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$\n\n$b$: {{b}}\n\n$c$: {{c}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1113 #uuid "03dad123-1833-5311-a4f2-ae8087879f79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258651 1145 "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $1,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $1.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-3x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x+b) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-3(2)+b \\\\  & =4-6+b \\\\  & =b-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =1 \\\\ b-2 & =1 \\\\ b & =1+2 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+cx+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-{x}^{2}+cx+1) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2}+c(2)+1 \\\\  & =-4+2c+1 \\\\  & =2c-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =1 \\\\ 2c-3 & =1 \\\\ c & =\\frac{1+3}{2} \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258653 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1041 "b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258653 13194139533864 true] [17592186258652 1110 #uuid "dbfb6eaf-b9f3-53be-be85-c1121d3491a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258653 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258653 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258653 1111 #uuid "32b89fbf-b014-5640-a34d-31a623a93f88" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258655 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1041 "c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258655 13194139533864 true] [17592186258654 1110 #uuid "e9df87ad-3a19-5812-8a5e-fef466e5ea8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258655 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258655 1111 #uuid "6c8cbf0f-8ac1-5598-b1c6-00543cf63c2c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258656 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258657 13194139533864 true] [17592186258656 1089 "q-3771" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258656 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258656 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258656 1113 #uuid "fe552b19-ecf8-5441-b376-c5bd68d19d28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258656 1145 "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{3x}-1]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{e}^{3x}}{1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =3(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258658 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258658 13194139533864 true] [17592186258657 1110 #uuid "e998e495-1f1f-5d85-87c2-c25c02c03dfd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258658 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258658 1111 #uuid "0aba405f-646d-527d-87c9-c54ec866fd50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258659 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258660 13194139533864 true] [17592186258659 1089 "q-38592" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258659 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258659 1100 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $k$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{x-1}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2x-k, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258659 1113 #uuid "6a0708c3-6443-5100-a620-9e95f2c04652" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258659 1145 "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}\\frac{k}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{-1-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}(2x-k) \\\\  & =2(-1)-k \\\\  & =-2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(-1)=-2-k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ -2-k & =-\\frac{k}{2} \\\\ -4-2k & =-k \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-4$ removes the discontinuity at $x=-1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258661 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258661 13194139533864 true] [17592186258660 1110 #uuid "ebe8e595-9b2a-5813-9735-4dac52b76b87" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258661 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258661 1111 #uuid "d8559109-d0d3-5d8a-9569-96affb8bc4a6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258662 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258663 13194139533864 true] [17592186258662 1089 "q-39069" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258662 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258662 1100 "What is the index of the term $-75$ for the arithmetic sequence below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-3,\\,{a}_{1}=-3,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258662 1113 #uuid "9ec80876-92cf-51cb-8c78-67cced1a623f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258662 1145 "We will convert the recursive formula to an explicit formula and then solve for the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=-75.$\n\nThe recursive rule for an arithmetic sequence takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+d.\n$$\n\nIn the given formula, we have $d=-3.$ We are also told that ${a}_{1}=-3,$ so we can write the explicit formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-3+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =-3-3n+3 \\\\  & =-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=-75:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-3n \\\\ -75 & =-3n \\\\ \\frac{-75}{-3} & =n \\\\ 25 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-75$ is the $25$th term of the sequence." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258664 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258664 13194139533864 true] [17592186258663 1110 #uuid "644a2064-f413-5926-b152-af6d8ab6d2f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258664 1034 "25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258664 1111 #uuid "8ae76a6d-e04f-580c-b80c-6fa055192eaf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258665 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258666 13194139533864 true] [17592186258665 1089 "q-39273" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258665 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258665 1100 "For the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - The domain of the function is $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\nII. - The graph of the function passes through the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\nIII. - $y\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258665 1113 #uuid "8c4a36da-599f-5664-ba9f-ac2ade1112ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258665 1145 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nNow, we analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is defined on all real numbers except $0.$ So, the domain of the function is $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. When $x=2,$ we get $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the curve.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the graph, we see that $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258667 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258668 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258669 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258670 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258671 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258672 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258673 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258674 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258674 13194139533864 true] [17592186258666 1110 #uuid "2a811596-4561-58d6-aa4d-f28d49154dd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258667 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258667 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258667 1111 #uuid "3f39dc82-820e-5548-a0dd-c19e651b8a80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258668 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258668 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258668 1111 #uuid "203714a3-86e4-5086-a47e-056427b64a5f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258669 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258669 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258669 1111 #uuid "68357f1e-38a3-5f15-ada5-2918d0749d0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258670 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258670 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258670 1111 #uuid "004fcd5a-d9cd-5ef1-9566-8524db4dcaa1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258671 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258671 1111 #uuid "fe083ecf-90fd-5715-aac8-b2ee888d250f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258672 1111 #uuid "ee09beba-184e-52a2-8655-5ddd98441138" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258673 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258673 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258673 1111 #uuid "32e51ac3-ebb0-5b54-8c8a-8c0e6a914bb7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258674 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258674 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258674 1111 #uuid "d09abb86-4cb1-5d8a-81b3-f062a0ae6192" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258675 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258676 13194139533864 true] [17592186258675 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258678 13194139533864 true] [17592186258675 1089 "q-39802" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258675 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258675 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258675 1113 #uuid "60689cc0-1330-59f8-9b56-7c01aabe3494" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258675 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{{x}^{2}(x+7)-(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{({x}^{2}-1)(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+1)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+1)}(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-1)(x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x+7)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-7$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-7$ and $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258676 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258677 13194139533864 true] [17592186258676 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258676 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258676 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258677 13194139533864 true] [17592186258676 1110 #uuid "a4838539-8ac7-5fe3-98da-c19d4d040c6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258677 1034 "-7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258677 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258677 1111 #uuid "f67911e8-8b1d-5d46-866b-cf157e0fa04f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258678 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258679 13194139533864 true] [17592186258678 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258678 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258678 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258679 13194139533864 true] [17592186258678 1110 #uuid "208f45e0-bf6c-5037-9987-d49ff6eb7dda" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258679 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258679 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258679 1111 #uuid "c68b2b34-ebf2-53f8-97aa-f1db5bbe7f2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258681 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1089 "q-39825" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1100 "Solve $\\sqrt{8x-5}=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1113 #uuid "193e9c3b-5823-5fe7-b42e-9a9b3e4a9d8b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258680 1145 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8x-5} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{8x-5})}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ 8x-5 & =3 \\\\ 8x & =8 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8\\cdot 1-5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{8-5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=1$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{8x-5}=\\sqrt{3}$  is $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258682 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258682 13194139533864 true] [17592186258681 1110 #uuid "d059153d-357b-56ea-b351-d096c5692bff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258682 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258682 1111 #uuid "fcf4dce6-1e7d-5176-950f-96b4537b78af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258684 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1089 "q-39830" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1100 "Solve $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1113 #uuid "0845673e-0a7d-58b4-9254-65ae15f4f4cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258683 1145 "First, we isolate the term $-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}$ by subtracting $8$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =6 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $-6$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2} & =12 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} & ={12}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =144 \\\\ x & =142.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{142+2}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{\\sqrt{144}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{12}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=142$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6$  is $x=142.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258685 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258685 13194139533864 true] [17592186258684 1110 #uuid "3d2b07a8-a82f-5b65-88a0-840cdbabae14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258685 1034 "142" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258685 1111 #uuid "d0798a66-db69-5497-b608-6cd7176099fb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258687 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258689 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1089 "q-39832" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1100 "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4}=4.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1113 #uuid "98003bca-fa53-57e1-a8e6-79179173f6b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258686 1145 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-4 & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-20 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging the solutions into the original equation. \n\n- For $x=-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-(-4)-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16+4-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-5-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{25-5-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values $x=-4,\\,5$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4}=4$  are $x=-4$ and $x=5.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258688 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258688 13194139533864 true] [17592186258687 1110 #uuid "fc8d9b98-e0a3-59ab-aa53-d00a41de0596" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258688 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258688 1111 #uuid "ef03f08c-e294-5878-842e-eb5f397b1825" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258690 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258690 13194139533864 true] [17592186258689 1110 #uuid "736a8b92-b3e8-56c3-8089-3512e8c5dd0c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258690 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258690 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258690 1111 #uuid "0b4e387d-4612-5b07-aae3-26f3b30882c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258692 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258694 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1089 "q-39833" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1100 "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37}=5.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1113 #uuid "d0f5b567-86e0-5c3b-94d7-e63615575a3c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258691 1145 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37} & =5 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37})}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+37 & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging the solutions into the original equation. \n\n- For $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}-8\\cdot 2+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{4-16+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=6$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{6}^{2}-8\\cdot 6+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{36-48+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values $x=2,\\,6$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37}=5$  are $x=2$ and $x=6.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258693 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258693 13194139533864 true] [17592186258692 1110 #uuid "700ead04-7a16-50e8-975b-a572438d1758" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258693 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258693 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258693 1111 #uuid "d3e1d694-1bdd-5948-8c96-890ceae62640" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258695 13194139533864 true] [17592186258694 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258694 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258694 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258695 13194139533864 true] [17592186258694 1110 #uuid "5ad8d745-e329-584d-8a11-ce5b919ce507" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258695 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258695 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258695 1111 #uuid "99449027-c0b9-5dd4-9166-86aaa4b8715c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258697 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258699 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258701 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1089 "q-3984" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1100 "Solve for $m$ where $2{m}^{3}-5{m}^{2}-18m=2{m}^{2}+{m}^{3}.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1113 #uuid "423dad75-ec6d-59c5-bce2-1028414952c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258696 1145 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(m)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{m}^{3}-5{m}^{2}-18m & =2{m}^{2}+{m}^{3} \\\\ {m}^{3}-5{m}^{2}-18m & =2{m}^{2} \\\\ {m}^{3}-7{m}^{2}-18m & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $m.$ We can factor out the $m$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}-7{m}^{2}-18m & =0 \\\\ m({m}^{2}-7m-18) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${m}^{2}-7m-18$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m({m}^{2}-7m-18) & =0 \\\\ m(m+2)(m-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $m=0,$ $m=-2,$ and $m=9.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258697 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258698 13194139533864 true] [17592186258697 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258697 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258697 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258698 13194139533864 true] [17592186258697 1110 #uuid "19d0e142-f90c-5c63-b1c3-353684dc6988" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258698 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258698 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258698 1111 #uuid "3105e6bd-21ef-530e-8e10-b909bcf733ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258700 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258700 13194139533864 true] [17592186258699 1110 #uuid "3351ea0b-eeff-5f58-becd-56fbf08f3f13" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258700 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258700 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258700 1111 #uuid "d23d4977-ec9c-564b-99ba-5632e8a3c200" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258702 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258702 13194139533864 true] [17592186258701 1110 #uuid "003a3369-96ec-559a-9958-0a3c80649b81" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258702 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258702 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258702 1111 #uuid "0700acea-40cc-5401-9f43-345fa1a20bad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258703 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258704 13194139533864 true] [17592186258703 1089 "q-40286" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258703 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258703 1100 "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a fifth term equal to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and an eighth term equal to $-\\frac{1}{16}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258703 1113 #uuid "44cf182f-9628-54ce-8c6a-39e321172edf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258703 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{5}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${a}_{8}=-\\frac{1}{16}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{8-5} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{16})}{(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(-\\frac{1}{16})(-2) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=5$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258705 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258705 13194139533864 true] [17592186258704 1110 #uuid "5b0833d4-4144-569c-b006-dceef4d41ca4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258705 1034 "-16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258705 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258705 1111 #uuid "db8b76fd-8b6b-55c3-8edc-c3327759fc25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258706 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258707 13194139533864 true] [17592186258706 1089 "q-40557" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258706 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258706 1100 "Which of the following values ​are possible outputs of $f(x)=5{(2)}^{2x+1}-3?$\n\nI. - $-3$\nII. - $0$\nIII. - $3$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258706 1113 #uuid "9867e83c-9088-5ac6-b716-bd2e12f821e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258706 1145 "The range of ${2}^{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{2}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{2}^{2x+1}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $5$ and then subtracting $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2x+1} & >0 \\\\ 5(2)^{2x+1} & >0 \\\\ 5(2)^{2x+1}-3 & >0-3 \\\\ f(x) & >-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)>-3.$ Thus, of the given values, only $y=0$ and $y=3$ are outputs of $y=f(x).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/27d552fe970877167abdcfd528c3dd6f517d9afb7ecf33d1eb00c8e8c2a332d7.png)\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258708 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258709 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258710 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258711 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258712 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258713 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258714 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258715 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258709 13194139533864 true] [17592186258707 1110 #uuid "ba7aa15e-4cac-5399-8271-9f448bc7cb70" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258708 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258708 1111 #uuid "ca8a3395-d6bb-562d-a159-c237ef1fe932" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258709 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258709 1111 #uuid 