g: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2-x}>0 \\\\ -3(3)^{2-x} & <0 \\\\ -3(3)^{2-x}-1 & <0-1 \\\\ f(x) & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)<-1.$ Thus, of the given values, only $y=-2$ is an   output of $y=f(x).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a40084132e9a34f28b151ed65812410ebef888e33b5394a5e3960996aff515ac.png)\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258718 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258719 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258720 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258721 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258722 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258723 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258724 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258725 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258721 13194139533864 true] [17592186258717 1110 #uuid "11e2a69c-fd58-538b-bd4f-6db8d91ddad1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258718 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258718 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258718 1111 #uuid "3878df5a-fd9e-5894-b398-627dc8f169f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258719 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258719 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258719 1111 #uuid "52b5bb8e-e56f-5632-908f-fae9f97d87ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258720 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258720 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258720 1111 #uuid "7ce0f1aa-ca34-51ba-842b-15b2eaccd753" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258721 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258721 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258721 1111 #uuid "e6157271-94a5-5f2d-aac5-c32cd9a60416" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258722 1111 #uuid "c838cc0d-4303-5766-864d-aa1c4dc04040" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258723 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258723 1111 #uuid "c552b19c-d3db-5d6c-9e64-40f372a6bd5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258724 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258724 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258724 1111 #uuid "c6aa2a47-ee9b-580c-bb16-ac8bc99d9108" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258725 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258725 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258725 1111 #uuid "5e47c367-ce18-5083-a725-7bab0581569d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258726 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258727 13194139533864 true] [17592186258726 1089 "q-41245" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258726 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258726 1100 "Find the minimum value of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}(x-2)+3.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No minimum value\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258726 1113 #uuid "8e61eb09-74d3-5874-8485-4b4618415641" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258726 1145 "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no minimum value." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258728 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258728 13194139533864 true] [17592186258727 1110 #uuid "97335ce5-3863-514c-8bff-f00a02924bb0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258728 1034 "No minimum value" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258728 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258728 1111 #uuid "1df260bd-367c-5988-822e-ba2c92b3ea18" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258730 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1089 "q-41246" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1100 "Find the maximum value of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{x}{2}+1)-1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No maximum value\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1113 #uuid "e7e52902-fed7-58d4-bb0e-42f5b74398ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258729 1145 "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no maximum value." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258731 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258731 13194139533864 true] [17592186258730 1110 #uuid "b147741f-ed90-57c9-8ffb-d0d72e684bcd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258731 1034 "No maximum value" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258731 1111 #uuid "a55e754a-d7fb-5292-89ba-7ca6df0dc0c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258733 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1089 "q-41478" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/34ba4a4dcd6655d68fbc20fb6192354ae755cc7eaf14a2459e9b7012ffe6d9ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given that $y=f(x)$ is periodic with period $4,$ what is the value of $f(62)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1113 #uuid "894785c9-b37d-573f-b85e-093f2abc4eb1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258732 1145 "Since the period is $4,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(62) & =f(62-4) \\\\  & =f(62-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(62-15\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =f(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258734 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258734 13194139533864 true] [17592186258733 1110 #uuid "1891fb5a-ae3c-53e5-bcfb-2e2ebdc3a191" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258734 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258734 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258734 1111 #uuid "7475235a-62e7-5fae-98a6-1664adf39916" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258736 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1089 "q-41911" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1100 "Two balls are drawn from a bag that contains two red balls and two black balls. Define $A$ to be the event \"no black balls are selected.\" It is known that $P(A)=0.25.$ What is the probability of selecting at least one black ball?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1113 #uuid "4ceee2f4-61b4-5f77-aefc-d5befcee1142" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258735 1145 "The event \"at least one black ball is selected\" is the opposite of the event \"no black balls are selected.\"\n\nSo, the event \"at least one black ball is selected\" is the complement of $A,$ denoted ${A}^{'}.$\n\nSince we know that $P(A)=0.25$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258737 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258737 13194139533864 true] [17592186258736 1110 #uuid "99a1e029-57c9-5535-a5a5-9b4e8d91d4df" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258737 1034 "0.75" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258737 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258737 1111 #uuid "720e1293-6223-51a2-8ae6-b8a8efbd2fe0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258739 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1089 "q-42070" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4decd7da6d460540762f6cb2c32fcd36bc7f3c9e24fb62af9244afe0c29dbba4.png)\n\nConsider the sample space $S$ with the Venn diagram given above. What is $P(A)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1113 #uuid "60abed17-1948-5b76-9695-4adb8b2b042e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258738 1145 "From the given Venn diagram, we have $P(A)=y$ and $P({A}^{'})=\\frac{y}{5}.$\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ y+\\frac{y}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{6y}{5} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,  $P(A)=y=\\frac{5}{6}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258740 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258740 13194139533864 true] [17592186258739 1110 #uuid "d1e24f39-9ec8-541f-9dc3-1a38b67d0f37" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258740 1034 "\\frac{5}{6}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258740 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258740 1111 #uuid "924ec03f-ea5a-5596-8164-807f45e54f59" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258742 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1089 "q-43575" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1100 "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\frac{5}{20}$ and $\\frac{7}{45}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1113 #uuid "6e1e45df-f294-5f60-b25f-93cd2088d874" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258741 1145 "To find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $20:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/7e03a139fc3afa7df4d7f32ecf42f8f30d19bfe0415725f47fe5be63301899b5.png)\n\nTherefore, $20={2}^{2}\\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $45:$\n\nTherefore, $45={3}^{2}\\cdot 5.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\ 45 & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is ${2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=180.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258743 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258743 13194139533864 true] [17592186258742 1110 #uuid "fc205521-7e24-5da7-8bbf-7fe31687c713" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258743 1034 "180" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258743 1111 #uuid "0c95150a-d601-580f-9756-c6f7a64b7c07" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258745 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1089 "q-43632" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1100 "What is the prime decomposition of  $126?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1113 #uuid "975f0bb3-e993-5f9b-a3ec-3b810e3cbf9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258744 1145 "To find the prime factorization of a number, we can use a factor tree.\n\nFirst, we need to express $126$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $126$ is $2,$ and $126=2\\cdot 63.$ We write these factors below $126$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b4f520ebadd00c650f5172e14943f67e67bc63f8e38a1ecd3fe86a7e7ab74ad4.png)\n\nWe draw a circle around $2$ because it's prime. \n\nNext, we need to express $63$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $63$ is $3,$ and $63=3\\cdot 21.$ We write these factors below $63$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\nWe carry on like this, stopping only when all of the endpoints are prime. By doing this, we get the following: \n\nSo finally, our prime factorization is\n\n$$\n126=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot 7=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258746 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258746 13194139533864 true] [17592186258745 1110 #uuid "b83c8ac8-1b0a-5849-87e0-b94e6262669e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258746 1034 "2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258746 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258746 1111 #uuid "8584c7f4-aee0-51c5-8848-9809d4b3fba5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258748 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1089 "q-43634" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1100 "What is the prime decomposition of $315?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1113 #uuid "6e736a28-8e89-585c-8f2b-ce912c35bf20" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258747 1145 "To find the prime factorization of a number, we can use a factor tree.\n\nFirst, we need to express $315$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $315$ is $3,$ and $315=3\\cdot 105.$ We write these factors below $315$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/35f5150e72285c0c706af351aa9827bf7010e22fa0dd49a07569466006c2bff1.png)\n\nWe draw a circle around $3$ because it's prime. \n\nNext, we need to express $105$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $105$ is $3,$ and $105=3\\cdot 35.$ We write these factors below $105$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\nWe carry on like this, stopping only when all of the endpoints are prime. By doing this, we get the following:\n\nSo finally, our prime factorization is \n\n$$\n315=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 5\\cdot 7={3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258749 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258749 13194139533864 true] [17592186258748 1110 #uuid "97b3c1fb-965d-5446-b945-9970eb8d409a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258749 1034 "{3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258749 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258749 1111 #uuid "21381e4c-dbdb-5dbf-9740-7260353e0ab7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258751 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1089 "q-44205" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1100 "Solve for $t$ where $\\frac{1-3t}{5}=-\\frac{4+t}{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1113 #uuid "fcdcd9f3-7122-5f59-93b6-53cc1a94a313" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258750 1145 "We transfer the negative into the numerator of the fraction, and proceed with cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3t}{5} & =-\\frac{4+t}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1-3t}{5} & =\\frac{-(4+t)}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1-3t}{5} & =\\frac{-4-t}{2} \\\\ (1-3t)\\cdot 2 & =5\\cdot (-4-t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-3t)\\cdot 2 & =5\\cdot (-4-t) \\\\ 2-6t & =-20-5t \\\\ 2-6t+5t & =-20-5t+5t \\\\ 2-t & =-20 \\\\ 2-t-2 & =-20-2 \\\\ -t & =-22 \\\\ -1\\cdot t & =-22 \\\\ \\frac{-1\\cdot t}{-1} & =\\frac{-22}{-1} \\\\ t & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258752 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258752 13194139533864 true] [17592186258751 1110 #uuid "5a52611a-e49a-575e-a27a-b6d409261943" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258752 1034 "22" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258752 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258752 1111 #uuid "9e802ab2-21bf-5bbc-9750-1cc6f48bf884" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258754 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258756 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1089 "q-44298" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1100 "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line $y=15x+5.$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1113 #uuid "8c4e56e6-52fc-51ba-afd3-0b7b7237eab0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258753 1145 "At the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =15x+5 \\\\ 0 & =15x+5 \\\\ -15x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{-15x}{(-15)} & =\\frac{5}{(-15)} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(-\\frac{1}{3},0).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258755 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258755 13194139533864 true] [17592186258754 1110 #uuid "da5b7cc0-e4c3-528c-aa88-a10de0acf241" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258755 1034 "-\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258755 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258755 1111 #uuid "2c4ee81a-ccfb-5af4-9d7f-6b289d2cd64d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258757 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258757 13194139533864 true] [17592186258756 1110 #uuid "59769b53-693c-54d6-bb7a-05801ddb3206" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258757 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258757 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258757 1111 #uuid "ea076972-7904-5cb7-87e8-ab329a78a15c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258758 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258759 13194139533864 true] [17592186258758 1089 "q-4460" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258758 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258758 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258758 1113 #uuid "7f232a13-ac59-5c7f-b9ee-ff0c688c0095" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258758 1145 "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(4x-2)}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+3(1)+4}+6(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+3+4}+6 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+6 \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(4x-2) & =4(1)-2=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2} & =\\frac{8}{2}=4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258760 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258760 13194139533864 true] [17592186258759 1110 #uuid "3d213a5a-61a1-56dc-a89c-7e909aa9753d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258760 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258760 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258760 1111 #uuid "ba9a8eb1-1589-50a7-aec1-2595510c8f04" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258761 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258762 13194139533864 true] [17592186258761 1089 "q-4469" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258761 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258761 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258761 1113 #uuid "b1b61dac-dcb8-5733-8ee2-72d827e766d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258761 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(5)-1}-3}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5} & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-1}-3)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x-1})}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2x-1-9}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2x-10}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2(x-5)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x-1}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(5)-1}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258763 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258763 13194139533864 true] [17592186258762 1110 #uuid "9f948605-0438-5761-a387-1a77da15a514" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258763 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258763 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258763 1111 #uuid "6c02b898-1aa1-52df-aa4d-6305b77d4cd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258764 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258765 13194139533864 true] [17592186258764 1089 "q-45301" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258764 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258764 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/de02555d435e957019036ace7b723225ff6f3dd905f7533a51a353bacc603fce.png)\n\nWhich of the following combinations of transformations map the segment $AB$ to a congruent segment?\n\nI. - A reflection in the $y$-axis, followed by a reflection in the $x$-axis.\nII. - A rotation of ${35}^{∘}$ clockwise around the point $B.$\nIII. - A dilation of scale factor of $4$ with center of dilation $A,$ followed by a translation of $3$ units to the left." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258764 1113 #uuid "41248f0a-a076-5b66-940a-eca2e5291a4b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258764 1145 "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n\n- Rotations\n\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Combination I maps $AB$ to a congruent segment. Indeed, reflections are rigid motions.\n\n- Combination II maps $AB$ to a congruent segment. Indeed, a rotation is a rigid motion.\n\n- Combination III does not map $AB$ to a congruent segment because dilation is not a rigid motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258766 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258767 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258768 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258769 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258770 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258771 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258772 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258773 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258771 13194139533864 true] [17592186258765 1110 #uuid "fd9b793c-a80d-5119-bd10-c96f2a5e5f39" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258766 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258766 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258766 1111 #uuid "0a479ba3-feb6-5453-9357-da6b1db12efa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258767 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258767 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258767 1111 #uuid "517f2865-77aa-509b-9701-a6cab8243aae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258768 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258768 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258768 1111 #uuid "4327f18a-dfb3-56fc-9926-b89eefc912f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258769 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258769 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258769 1111 #uuid "baf45562-a86a-54e7-9a49-5399e7399a16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258770 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258770 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258770 1111 #uuid "2174ef5f-f026-5344-af8e-44b86ef478b3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258771 1111 #uuid "114a3157-beae-521a-913f-6953e81d1d44" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258772 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258772 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258772 1111 #uuid "f32881c3-f0b8-5f3d-a989-e1a2d518e877" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258773 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258773 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258773 1111 #uuid "ec33a8d3-5f3f-525a-a94b-b70111b7d158" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258775 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1089 "q-45468" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1100 "Which of the following transformations map every polygon to a congruent polygon?\n\nI. - $(x,y)↦(x-1,y)$\nII. - $(x,y)↦(-y,x)$\nIII. - $(x,y)↦(2x,y)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1113 #uuid "ebee1808-fab9-59f7-b821-b1dbd643c058" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258774 1145 "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n\n- Rotations\n\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is a translation of $1$ unit to the right. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\n- Transformation II is a rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\n- Transformation III is a stretch with a stretch factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction. This is *not* a rigid motion. So, it does not map every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258776 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258777 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258778 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258779 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258780 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258781 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258782 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258783 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258781 13194139533864 true] [17592186258775 1110 #uuid "18468b5f-9d2d-55e6-b1a6-13894be795c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258776 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258776 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258776 1111 #uuid "5cd1f6ab-9e6f-5379-a843-509d13d54a28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258777 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258777 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258777 1111 #uuid "8b393e06-c6ab-55ad-84e1-b84baf9ce523" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258778 1111 #uuid "6cee4cc0-d3b3-5d78-b68e-a0edbb097628" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258779 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258779 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258779 1111 #uuid "64612897-434e-5af8-8a66-2a579b19b522" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258780 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258780 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258780 1111 #uuid "c485b055-36f4-5265-94ce-14a224fd55fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258781 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258781 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258781 1111 #uuid "9922f82f-d65d-53c8-b56b-c4df0a16c693" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258782 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258782 1111 #uuid "5adc6009-37d9-5443-bdf7-3a25923f800d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258783 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258783 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258783 1111 #uuid "ef89d4ba-070d-5810-9324-b577e0b6fd73" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258785 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1089 "q-46167" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1100 "The curve $y=4{x}^{2}-24x+24$ can be written as $y=a(x+b)^{2}+c.$ Calculate $a+b+c.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1113 #uuid "d537e63a-0b98-56d5-9a0e-cfd908ba035e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258784 1145 "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square. \n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-24x+24 & =4({x}^{2}-6x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+6 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+6 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2})-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+6 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-6x+6) & = \\\\ 4((x-3)^{2}-3) & = \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  $a+b+c=4+(-3)+(-12)=-11.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258785 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258786 13194139533864 true] [17592186258785 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258785 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258785 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258786 13194139533864 true] [17592186258785 1110 #uuid "15d73943-5b21-5284-b561-312dc91a8737" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258786 1034 "-11" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258786 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258786 1111 #uuid "783b0e30-4221-51cb-be22-d35124ccd74f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258788 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1089 "q-46814" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1100 "Martin surveyed some students in a school, asking them if they have cats or dogs at home. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1113 #uuid "f0371bfd-8501-506c-b9fa-24609b84ea8d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258787 1145 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\" Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(32),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9+10}{32}=\\frac{19}{32}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258789 13194139533864 true] [17592186258788 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258788 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258788 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258789 13194139533864 true] [17592186258788 1110 #uuid "d24908d8-a2f5-5436-aecb-56c6458ab874" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258789 1034 "\\frac{19}{32}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258789 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258789 1111 #uuid "ae0f137c-f1e6-5c07-842d-8713cd771e0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258791 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1089 "q-46833" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1113 #uuid "678f5c58-bbd6-5e36-9285-ca3cd7002851" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258790 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=2$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}} & =\\frac{2-2}{2(2)-\\sqrt{8(2)}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{8x})(2x+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{(2x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{8x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{4{x}^{2}-8x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{4x(x-2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{8x}}{4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+\\sqrt{8(2)}}{4(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258792 13194139533864 true] [17592186258791 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258791 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258791 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258792 13194139533864 true] [17592186258791 1110 #uuid "d277c78c-1469-51f9-96ba-677522683231" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258792 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258792 1111 #uuid "2a4b2d75-2e5e-5759-a73d-ce1d5a2549ae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258794 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1089 "q-46839" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1113 #uuid "d6fab19a-aae0-556f-9992-7a68d4d6de92" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258793 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=8$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}} & =\\frac{8-8}{4-\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{(4-\\sqrt{2x})(4+\\sqrt{2x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{{4}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{16-2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{-2(x-8)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2x}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2(8)}}{-2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258795 13194139533864 true] [17592186258794 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258794 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258794 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258795 13194139533864 true] [17592186258794 1110 #uuid "b0581553-e79c-501c-a973-086be2342b5f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258795 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258795 1111 #uuid "b68e11b3-a57c-5715-bb2f-6f584359c034" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258797 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1089 "q-46841" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 25}\\frac{x-25}{\\sqrt{x}-5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1113 #uuid "e08fbc7b-1ab0-52e1-b7cf-725d96e81d95" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258796 1145 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=25$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 25}\\frac{x-25}{\\sqrt{x}-5} & =\\frac{25-25}{\\sqrt{25}-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 25}\\frac{x-25}{\\sqrt{x}-5} & ={lim}_{x\\to 25}\\frac{(x-25)(\\sqrt{x}+5)}{(\\sqrt{x}-5)(\\sqrt{x}+5)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 25}\\frac{(x-25)(\\sqrt{x}+5)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{5}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 25}\\frac{(x-25)(\\sqrt{x}+5)}{x-25} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 25}(\\sqrt{x}+5) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25}+5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258797 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258798 13194139533864 true] [17592186258797 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258797 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258797 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258798 13194139533864 true] [17592186258797 1110 #uuid "d2114988-497a-53f9-a5a0-c96042ed491a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258798 1034 "10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258798 1111 #uuid "52445dd2-31d0-551a-bcb1-4c442de1e2b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258800 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1089 "q-46975" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1100 "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1113 #uuid "5b240cab-24fa-5335-a943-6cdfe8310c86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258799 1145 "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0,\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=2\\cdot 0=0\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258801 13194139533864 true] [17592186258800 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258800 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258800 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258801 13194139533864 true] [17592186258800 1110 #uuid "511ca430-2669-5d58-94b7-ee16594c46e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258801 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258801 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258801 1111 #uuid "62b06ea6-f6ee-5ce4-8188-756ab212a69e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258803 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1089 "q-47139" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1100 "The employees and the interns of a company were asked whether they commute to work by car or by bus. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Employee | Intern | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Car | $24$ | $16$ | $40$ |\n| Bus | $26$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $50$ | $30$ | $80$ |\n\nLet $I$ be the event that a randomly selected person is a **\"Intern\"**, and $B$ be the event that a randomly selected person commutes to work by **\"Bus\"**. Estimate $P(I\\cup B).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1113 #uuid "6ed5c80c-e475-58de-a971-282836200277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258802 1145 "First, note that $I\\cup B$ is the event that a randomly selected person is an intern or person who commutes to work by bus. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $I\\cup B,$ we\n\n- add up the numbers in the column **\"Intern\"** or the row **\"Bus\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the column **\"Intern\"** or the row **\"Bus\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Employee | Intern | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Car | $24$ | $16$ | $40$ |\n| Bus | $26$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $50$ | $30$ | $80$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(80),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(I\\cup B)=\\frac{16+14+26}{80}=\\frac{56}{80}=0.70.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258803 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258804 13194139533864 true] [17592186258803 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258803 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258803 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258804 13194139533864 true] [17592186258803 1110 #uuid "743e6ede-3996-50f5-bcb2-0cea97ae3446" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258804 1034 "0.70" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258804 1111 #uuid "c16e723f-f101-552c-b2b0-a44b2c6fdb5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258806 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1089 "q-47274" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1100 "Rounded to $3$ decimal places, find the value of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{log}(x)-4\\operatorname{log}(2x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1113 #uuid "10395e19-686b-58f5-b08a-69774a480eae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258805 1145 "First, we replace $x=4$ and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =2\\operatorname{log}(4)-4\\operatorname{log}(2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{log}(4)-4\\operatorname{log}(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required value to $3$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $6$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =2\\operatorname{log}(4)-4\\operatorname{log}(8) \\\\  & \\approx 2(0.602\\,060)-4(0.903\\,090) \\\\  & =1.204\\,120-3.612\\,360 \\\\  & =-2.408\\,240 \\\\  & \\approx -2.408\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258806 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258807 13194139533864 true] [17592186258806 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258806 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258806 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258807 13194139533864 true] [17592186258806 1110 #uuid "c90c1f9c-f963-5c96-a354-804058c647dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258807 1034 "-2.408" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258807 1111 #uuid "dbb38578-339f-547d-a018-7c880ceec33c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258809 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1089 "q-47299" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1100 "Rose surveyed $168$ children in Arizona High School. She asked them about their favorite topics in math, between geometry and algebra. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below. \n\n|  | Geometry | Algebra | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $28$ | $63$ | $91$ |\n| Boys | $56$ | $21$ | $77$ |\n| Totals | $84$ | $84$ | $168$ |\n\nLet $B$ be the event that a randomly selected child is a **\"Boy\"** and $G$ the event that a randomly selected child likes **\"Geometry\"**. Estimate $P(B\\cap G).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1113 #uuid "75eedbd1-62f9-545d-b2b8-ed88a19bd586" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258808 1145 "First, note that $B\\cap G$ is the event that a randomly selected child is a boy and likes geometry. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $B\\cap G,$ we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Geometry\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Geometry\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Geometry | Algebra | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $28$ | $63$ | $91$ |\n| Boys | $56$ | $21$ | $77$ |\n| Totals | $84$ | $84$ | $168$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(168),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(B\\cap G)=\\frac{56}{168}=0.333.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258810 13194139533864 true] [17592186258809 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258809 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258809 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258810 13194139533864 true] [17592186258809 1110 #uuid "df64faa5-9c44-5a00-8262-d22fb90135ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258810 1034 "0.333" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258810 1111 #uuid "554bd0ac-14d9-5d7e-b6fa-9a9fbd6f80d5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258812 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1089 "q-47306" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1100 "A group of $5$th grade and $6$th grade students at Benjamin Franklin Elementary School are randomly selected for a survey. They are asked about their favorite places to go on summer vacations between the beach and the countryside. The results of this survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Beach | Countryside | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$th grade | $55$ | $30$ | $85$ |\n| $6$th grade | $42$ | $33$ | $75$ |\n| Totals | $97$ | $63$ | $160$ |\n\nJohn Adams Elementary School has $250$ students in $5$th grade and $6$th grade. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same in both elementary schools, estimate the number of students at John Adams Elementary School who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations (or both). Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1113 #uuid "146b2ef3-9c02-5d19-b5a0-7ac41d8998b7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258811 1145 "To estimate the number of students who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"$6$th grade\"** or the column **\"Beach\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | Beach | Countryside | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$th grade | $55$ | $30$ | $85$ |\n| $6$th grade | $42$ | $33$ | $75$ |\n| Totals | $97$ | $63$ | $160$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(160),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{55+42+33}{160}=\\frac{130}{160}=0.81.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student is in $6$th grade or likes to go to the beach on summer vacations.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of John Adams Elementary School who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(250)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =250\\cdot p \\\\  & =250\\cdot 0.81 \\\\  & =202.5 \\\\  & =203,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258813 13194139533864 true] [17592186258812 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258812 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258812 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258813 13194139533864 true] [17592186258812 1110 #uuid "93ca5f3b-ab39-53ed-8854-3d49571cdace" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258813 1034 "203" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258813 1111 #uuid "ddcdc7a1-98ce-5f26-a400-e2c7ecdb290f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258815 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1089 "q-47307" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1100 "A group of randomly selected students from Chicago High School was asked which country they want to visit between Brazil, Spain, France, or Japan. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Brazil | Spain | France | Japan | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $12$ | $20$ | $9$ | $14$ | $55$ |\n| Boys | $14$ | $10$ | $20$ | $1$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $26$ | $30$ | $29$ | $15$ | $100$ |\n\nBoston High School has $400$ students. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same in both high schools, estimate the number of girls at Boston High School who wish to visit Brazil or France. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1113 #uuid "3636bb28-36be-554b-b1a3-2ece301c26d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258814 1145 "To estimate the number of girls who wish to visit Brazil or France, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the intersection of the row **\"Girls\"** and the column **\"Brazil\"** and the intersection of the row **\"Girls\"** and the column **\"France\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | Brazil | Spain | France | Japan | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $12$ | $20$ | $9$ | $14$ | $55$ |\n| Boys | $14$ | $10$ | $20$ | $1$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $26$ | $30$ | $29$ | $15$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{12+9}{100}=\\frac{21}{100}=0.21.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student is a girl who wishes to visit Brazil or France.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of girls of Boston High School who wish to visit Brazil or France, we multiply the total number of students in Boston High School $(400)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =400\\cdot p \\\\  & =400\\cdot 0.21 \\\\  & =84\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258816 13194139533864 true] [17592186258815 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258815 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258815 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258816 13194139533864 true] [17592186258815 1110 #uuid "e1976e7b-5e56-5bf2-a749-123a4c7460f8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258816 1034 "84" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258816 1111 #uuid "554267c3-b36b-53f1-96ea-a70a14233d73" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258818 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1089 "q-47405" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1100 "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,16)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $q-p.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1113 #uuid "2dd83d18-2e82-5500-9a29-406ec35162d7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258817 1145 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})=6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(2)=6(2)^{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{'}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(16) & =-\\frac{1}{24}(x-(2)) \\\\ 24y-384 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+24y & =386.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q-p=386-24=362.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258818 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258819 13194139533864 true] [17592186258818 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258818 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258818 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258819 13194139533864 true] [17592186258818 1110 #uuid "4d2e2314-4883-5c9a-a3d2-ba10b8b0172d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258819 1034 "362" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258819 1111 #uuid "520a9952-36d1-5b28-9d1a-d3da42f14a77" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258820 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258821 13194139533864 true] [17592186258820 1089 "q-47447" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258820 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258820 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\nII. - $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\nIII. - $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258820 1113 #uuid "4c510ba2-7634-5ceb-8cb3-a5b35044f36d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258820 1145 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258822 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258823 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258824 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258825 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258826 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258827 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258828 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258829 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258826 13194139533864 true] [17592186258821 1110 #uuid "71f95d56-7ec8-583a-a3ec-79cbc7ddc998" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258822 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258822 1111 #uuid "ebf2e9d7-31ca-5d03-bbcb-392994bc39ac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258823 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258823 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258823 1111 #uuid "063a9d47-c2f3-5ee8-888d-19388957813d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258824 1111 #uuid "354dfa5c-933f-56e0-b784-e3778f7b0c95" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258825 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258825 1111 #uuid "5f4242d4-2b03-5e93-8f4c-e52fbd49c565" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258826 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258826 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258826 1111 #uuid "ff96f702-8d09-5553-8e60-afeac0d3cb05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258827 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258827 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258827 1111 #uuid "efb3abad-5ae7-5dde-8f68-1f4ed8247a31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258828 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258828 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258828 1111 #uuid "7342fdd3-89f9-5282-9690-bc9b44b19fbb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258829 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258829 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258829 1111 #uuid "7f067afb-9173-5325-99a3-8593dd6e211c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258831 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1089 "q-47520" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/3bb1c35edab6cecb8bdc0021b7d931c4a8284d02385c0d2398e0b3ca4fb473c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. - The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\nII. - The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\nIII. - The function is continuous at $x=0$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1113 #uuid "8029b517-cfed-599d-a293-d42c50744670" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258830 1145 "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258832 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258833 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258834 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258835 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258836 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258837 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258838 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258839 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258835 13194139533864 true] [17592186258831 1110 #uuid "4546afc4-5ede-5282-82c2-2149674417a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258832 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258832 1111 #uuid "ad21724d-e6e8-5043-b9d8-2acf6ddcc615" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258833 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258833 1111 #uuid "03f8ebf2-c952-5934-a775-da2d2f676039" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258834 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258834 1111 #uuid "da5461dd-41c1-5e2f-8f1f-2660d57e5348" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258835 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258835 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258835 1111 #uuid "96a94397-5062-5d2c-9777-42904783f519" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258836 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258836 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258836 1111 #uuid "edcb216f-70e1-57f5-a503-0024f4c3a53b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258837 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258837 1111 #uuid "6d340422-abf5-5da9-8220-7695f2bc70a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258838 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258838 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258838 1111 #uuid "77742dde-dc9f-5bfd-887f-b5a25604d260" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258839 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258839 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258839 1111 #uuid "13e81348-444c-5b9f-b19f-580e1444d765" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258841 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1089 "q-47778" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1100 "What's the range of $f(x)={log}_{9}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nUse an interval in words, such as \"All real numbers\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1113 #uuid "f7b6a962-a925-5eb4-8eb3-c093275b7ef1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258840 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{9}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/40664f83ba162a93ed06970798d37798db90b85c21d44a9ed20f338df4e1b4e1.png)\n\nThe domain of $y={log}_{9}x$ is $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All real numbers.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258841 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258842 13194139533864 true] [17592186258841 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258841 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258841 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258842 13194139533864 true] [17592186258841 1110 #uuid "82c34cd0-ab73-5aef-b587-d37f07c81adf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258842 1034 "All real numbers" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258842 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258842 1111 #uuid "6574fd45-455a-5ce6-8ad8-68abbf105320" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258844 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1089 "q-48063" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f5ba5f7cc2ea5aaf405db1abff7e6f28ed4378ba6a5ec88d73c6861ede1d75de.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ exists\nIII. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1113 #uuid "56d623d9-78f0-5f10-8521-99d9ec2fc8e8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258843 1145 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ breaks at $x=2$,  it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258845 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258846 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258847 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258848 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258849 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258850 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258851 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258852 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258850 13194139533864 true] [17592186258844 1110 #uuid "ac779f9e-c0a9-531b-b21a-ca5587c63114" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258845 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258845 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258845 1111 #uuid "b9c5c917-cd9a-5116-977a-f13e8461778d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258846 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258846 1111 #uuid "cb2ad4e6-a361-5f9f-afac-1dbed637a8d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258847 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258847 1111 #uuid "6f0521a6-cb41-51e5-98d9-bd0a4835723b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258848 1111 #uuid "2fb5de3c-2a0f-5ffc-bb2d-47e383c8020a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258849 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258849 1111 #uuid "00c33181-3288-5ae5-8ba3-6a6e11b11119" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258850 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258850 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258850 1111 #uuid "b595ea90-16a9-5607-b40c-a6b2148a8777" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258851 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258851 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258851 1111 #uuid "58bfed97-d29c-5d97-afdb-29266e0fe8d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258852 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258852 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258852 1111 #uuid "469095ff-9ad6-5993-92ea-1f7061e39341" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258854 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1089 "q-48208" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1113 #uuid "3187ea03-2a2b-58df-8eac-de1e0d9676e0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258853 1145 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7)=0+7=7.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258855 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258855 13194139533864 true] [17592186258854 1110 #uuid "173daa7b-200e-5db0-a98f-a4d6280ca3f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258855 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258855 1111 #uuid "4384c527-a3e2-5dee-a56c-d7db6110ce47" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258857 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1089 "q-48216" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{3x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1113 #uuid "be4b5477-1b23-5f88-be13-f138d6d0f412" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258856 1145 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $3x≫\\operatorname{ln}x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{3x}=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258857 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258858 13194139533864 true] [17592186258857 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258857 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258857 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258858 13194139533864 true] [17592186258857 1110 #uuid "491ab714-6bce-5018-a106-c09c06b2cb82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258858 1111 #uuid "99cf18cd-d6f1-5c96-9586-f2af15c7606c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258860 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1089 "q-48219" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f00b1960549affb287d1120ba43639c047bb5ad419e7a172063b8d9ce514e3e5.png)\n\nAt which points is the function $y=f(x)$ above left-continuous but is not continuous?\n\n$x$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1113 #uuid "4650e469-b715-5b14-be32-6211971fc986" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258859 1145 "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=-2$, at $x=1$ and at  $x=2.$\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=4=f(1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=0\\ne 2=f(2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1$ only." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258861 13194139533864 true] [17592186258860 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258860 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258860 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258861 13194139533864 true] [17592186258860 1110 #uuid "be62c019-6d99-591d-a6a4-a1f610dc4692" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258861 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258861 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258861 1111 #uuid "027ed59a-c2bc-5f6e-9339-e71cc5646ad2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258862 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258863 13194139533864 true] [17592186258862 1089 "q-48221" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258862 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258862 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258862 1113 #uuid "5c4a4a0b-e1a5-5f41-82c8-6f7ce8f75158" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258862 1145 "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\nx{e}^{-2x}=\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258864 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258864 13194139533864 true] [17592186258863 1110 #uuid "699c09d3-0aa4-5110-9ac4-f1705eebb66d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258864 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258864 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258864 1111 #uuid "268d2a23-f9fc-5723-8101-b984f9a1d733" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258865 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258866 13194139533864 true] [17592186258865 1089 "q-4825" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258865 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258865 1100 "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x$ and $g(x)=9-2x,$ find $(fg)(-1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258865 1113 #uuid "bd6a8a55-36de-5b05-883a-a794caef72c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258865 1145 "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{2}+5(-1)=-3 \\\\ g(-1) & =9-2(-1)=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-1)=f(-1)g(-1)=-3\\cdot 11=-33\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(2{x}^{2}+5x)(9-2x) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+45x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-1) & =-4(-1)^{3}+8(-1)^{2}+45(-1) \\\\  & =4+8-45 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258867 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258867 13194139533864 true] [17592186258866 1110 #uuid "178b3ae7-011a-542f-917e-72857a90aa25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258867 1034 "-33" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258867 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258867 1111 #uuid "f84ad872-89b4-594c-b109-88bee658a266" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258869 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1089 "q-48508" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1100 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y = \\cos (5x-2 )$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5,1\\right).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1113 #uuid "a9b3f537-d998-5b44-ac6e-d3ede45445e6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258868 1145 "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac 2 5$ is given by $f'\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = 5x-2.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin u \\qquad \\style{font-family:inherit;}{\\text{and}} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\sin u \\cdot 5\\\\[5pt]\n&=-5\\sin u\\\\[5pt]\n&= -5\\sin\\left(5x-2 \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\dfrac 25,$ we find\n\n\\begin{eqnarray}\n \\eqalign{\n\nm=f'\\left(\\dfrac 25\\right)  & =- 5 \\sin \\left(5\\cdot \\dfrac 2 5-2\\right)\\\\[5pt] \n\t\t& =- 5 \\sin 0\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= 0.\n \n       \n    }\n\\end{eqnarray}\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5,1\\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align*}\ny-y_1&=m(x-x_1)\\\\[5pt]\ny-1&=0\\left(x-\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\ny-1&=0\\\\[5pt]\ny&=1.\n\\end{align*}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258870 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1041 "y" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258870 13194139533864 true] [17592186258869 1110 #uuid "1a7c8e57-06af-5956-8bd8-c3cc75ad39ab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258870 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258870 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258870 1111 #uuid "a075da18-342a-549a-b681-746e39c6bd3e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258872 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1089 "q-48608" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1100 "The birth rate of a krill population is modeled by $B(t)=5\\cdot {2}^{0.7t}$ thousands of tons per year, where $t$ is the time, in years, since $1998.$ What was the birth rate, in thousands of tons per year, for the krill in $2008?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1113 #uuid "9cc8a79a-7802-592e-80ef-d176d2e37ddd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258871 1145 "The function $B(t)$ models the birth rate for the krill, in thousands of tons, $t$ years since $1998.$ Since we want the birth rate in $2008$, then $t=10$ years.\n\nThus, the birth rate for the krill in $2008$ was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(10) & =5\\cdot {2}^{0.7(10)} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{7} \\\\  & =640.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the birth rate for the krill in $2008$ was $640$ thousand tons per year." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258872 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258873 13194139533864 true] [17592186258872 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258872 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258872 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258873 13194139533864 true] [17592186258872 1110 #uuid "88dc04f0-ba52-595f-b804-826cb8ee5d73" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258873 1034 "640" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258873 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258873 1111 #uuid "2781a87c-c459-54dd-ba3e-a5af81917eb6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258875 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1089 "q-48636" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1100 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x) = \\cos{\\left(x^3\\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,1\\right).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1113 #uuid "57478b30-a73d-57e4-8308-8dc05fea241e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258874 1145 "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 0$ is given by $f'(0).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = x^3.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =3x^2.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= -\\sin{u} \\cdot 3x^2\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=-3x^2\\sin(x^3).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\\begin{align}\nm=f'(0) & = -3(0)^2\\sin(0^3) = 0.\n\\end{align}\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\\begin{align}\ny - y_1 & = m(x - x_1)\\\\[3pt]\n\ny - 1 & = 0 \\cdot \\left(x - 0 \\right)\\\\[3pt]\n\ny - 1 & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n\ny & =1.\n\\end{align}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258876 13194139533864 true] [17592186258875 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258875 1041 "y" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258875 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258876 13194139533864 true] [17592186258875 1110 #uuid "cf82e01c-1269-551e-8e93-cecda9b6e244" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258876 1111 #uuid "5290779a-217c-5e77-a6ef-4d8a2c3e2fe3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258878 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1089 "q-48644" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1100 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y =\\cos \\left(\\sin x \\right)$ at the point $\\left(0,1 \\right).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1113 #uuid "3eae5d22-d1dc-5383-9678-4b5348d7df9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258877 1145 "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 0$ is given by $f'(0).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\nf = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =\\cos x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\sin u \\cdot (\\cos x)\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\cos x \\sin (\\sin x).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\\[\n\t\\eqalign{\n\tf'(0)&=-\\cos (0) \\sin (\\sin (0))\\\\[3pt]\n\t&=-1 \\cdot 0 \\\\[3pt]\n        &=0.\n}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[3pt]\ny-1 &= (0)\\cdot\\left(x-0\\right)\\\\[3pt]\ny&=1.\n\\end{align}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258878 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258879 13194139533864 true] [17592186258878 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258878 1041 "y" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258878 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258879 13194139533864 true] [17592186258878 1110 #uuid "f31279c8-8912-5d7a-a4fa-2d896f5d9f64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258879 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258879 1111 #uuid "ffd05dd0-edc0-5d5e-9bf3-895de08612ac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258880 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258881 13194139533864 true] [17592186258880 1089 "q-48700" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258880 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258880 1100 "The number of computers affected by the spread of some ransomware after $t$ days can be modeled by the function $f(t)=\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change in the number of infected computers when $t=9$ days?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258880 1113 #uuid "4df84652-8096-5b0a-ac62-74634ce27764" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258880 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ days is equal to ${f}^{'}(9).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{100(3{t}^{2})(t+1)-100({t}^{3}+2)(1)}{{(t+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-2}{{(t+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(9) & =100\\cdot \\frac{2(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-2}{{((9)+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1\\,458+243-2}{100} \\\\  & =1\\,699.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ is $1\\,699$ computers per day." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258882 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258882 13194139533864 true] [17592186258881 1110 #uuid "d2a308a1-1af3-5de6-8f8f-a49d604bfa0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258882 1034 "1\\,699" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258882 1111 #uuid "47b1dd97-14d2-5cc3-95b1-6cd4e9b6a358" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258884 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1089 "q-48866" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1113 #uuid "72cf5a4e-ff4b-5bf3-a1c6-4b089563c4f1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258883 1145 "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $3{x}^{2}+x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/80dcb33426c9981efdb4eddb4bbd33e75aeb5267cd776acfbda99758e010128a.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258885 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258885 13194139533864 true] [17592186258884 1110 #uuid "a44932b0-57d2-57a8-a5ab-685dc9746c7b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258885 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258885 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258885 1111 #uuid "859a9968-4603-567a-8951-158894e28316" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258887 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1089 "q-48869" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1113 #uuid "a6e8da81-1916-527c-8075-091e80f07bef" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258886 1145 "The function $\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n3\\le \\operatorname{cos}x+4\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2c4c732472ffcfa4a42e7a0dd7e1ff55a9b4d2f0d5b0b652b875efa284d9274d.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258887 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258888 13194139533864 true] [17592186258887 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258887 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258887 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258888 13194139533864 true] [17592186258887 1110 #uuid "09dbbc95-19f3-5123-b473-bbf74d305dc5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258888 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258888 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258888 1111 #uuid "1af60cbf-868c-5faa-828d-3f9f7ecac8ba" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258890 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1089 "q-49568" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1100 "Consider the following sequence: \n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1113 #uuid "71dac1d2-6ea4-558d-93e8-0311765a5a7d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258889 1145 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|>1$, the sequence diverges." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258891 13194139533864 true] [17592186258890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258892 13194139533864 true] [17592186258890 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258890 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258890 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258891 13194139533864 true] [17592186258890 1110 #uuid "d20d3612-8f26-5ea3-b4eb-4ce596ec8c19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258891 1034 "Diverges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258891 1111 #uuid "7493e4bf-895d-5e65-bb8f-bf2d383720b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258892 1034 "Converges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258892 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258892 1111 #uuid "209fa5c5-1466-5ce7-a8d2-8973f26a4e1f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258893 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258894 13194139533864 true] [17592186258893 1089 "q-49642" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258893 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258893 1100 "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{n}.$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258893 1113 #uuid "335820dd-ba2b-5e7f-92b7-d9a01208fa93" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258893 1145 "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{1}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{2}=0 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{3}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{(-1)}^{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we can see that the sequence of partial sums oscillates between  $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $0$ forever. \n\nSo the limit of the sequence of partial sums does not exist, and therefore the series is divergent." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258895 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258896 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258895 13194139533864 true] [17592186258894 1110 #uuid "8bafb7c5-b67d-5e0b-9773-76f093b2d804" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258895 1034 "Diverges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258895 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258895 1111 #uuid "3ab84687-4674-50da-a455-1f6360b70d58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258896 1034 "Converges" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258896 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258896 1111 #uuid "f0422631-e627-5668-a3fd-c50f7d2e4c0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258898 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1089 "q-49836" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1100 "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true? Assume that ${a}_{n}$ is well-defined for $n⩾1.$\n\nI. - $\\frac{1}{10\\,000}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=10}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1113 #uuid "9f068f64-42c9-5eab-a9dd-69dbbca65fcb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258897 1145 "Let's recall the properties of divergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is also divergent for $K\\ne 0.$\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any non-zero multiple of a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}$ is a **finite series.** Therefore, ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Removing a finite number of terms from a divergent series gives a divergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258899 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258900 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258901 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258902 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258903 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258904 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258905 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258906 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258902 13194139533864 true] [17592186258898 1110 #uuid "c39958ab-9a75-5986-89db-259aee63a2ce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258899 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258899 1111 #uuid "fb78b81a-08e0-5883-b8c1-d2b3762bd274" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258900 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258900 1111 #uuid "c88b193c-1e36-53f0-bba8-c3b41432dc35" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258901 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258901 1111 #uuid "2950368e-f30f-54a6-9cbc-e39d2b227801" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258902 1111 #uuid "9764d03e-d2a5-51c2-b135-b591e8355b42" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258903 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258903 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258903 1111 #uuid "ddf52af7-4622-5274-b8d9-df77922937e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258904 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258904 1111 #uuid "4be3504a-0f66-5048-9275-871a324af799" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258905 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258905 1111 #uuid "5c5027dd-4dbf-56e5-b293-30e24a303a20" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258906 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258906 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258906 1111 #uuid "13f34607-fa97-57d2-b597-fa8c4bd337b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258907 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258908 13194139533864 true] [17592186258907 1089 "q-49841" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258907 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258907 1100 "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=100}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - $1\\,000{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258907 1113 #uuid "f30cf6e1-a2f7-50a2-8c61-f5748c2a0e0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258907 1145 "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258909 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258910 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258911 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258912 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258913 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258914 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258915 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258916 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258911 13194139533864 true] [17592186258908 1110 #uuid "32524753-7bf7-51bd-b7ec-0c1ce05625a2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258909 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258909 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258909 1111 #uuid "5a35aa78-d547-5a55-8a2f-3f86e4c0afb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258910 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258910 1111 #uuid "8644a362-1e72-5a40-acc5-710bc5a3bcd6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258911 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258911 1111 #uuid "e0dac5b6-786f-5744-93c0-87b1055f2912" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258912 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258912 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258912 1111 #uuid "38362d27-f96d-5548-bff5-1512c06ada80" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258913 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258913 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258913 1111 #uuid "0362b06e-efe4-545c-86bd-91cb1c3508c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258914 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258914 1111 #uuid "acbfefc2-a7e5-5a3b-b04b-4a73976e50af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258915 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258915 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258915 1111 #uuid "56f00260-986c-5669-a513-075f3979051c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258916 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258916 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258916 1111 #uuid "e1267788-fb64-575f-b20c-448bc0e42758" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258917 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258918 13194139533864 true] [17592186258917 1089 "q-5031" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258917 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258917 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+1)+3x$ and the point $P(0,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{'}(0).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258917 1113 #uuid "d99c943d-c7b7-53d6-8c63-62b556330e7e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258917 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+1)+3x \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+3 \\\\ {f}^{'}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258918 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258919 13194139533864 true] [17592186258918 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258918 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258918 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258919 13194139533864 true] [17592186258918 1110 #uuid "ba51ce0d-964f-5fa5-81c2-970951106ba8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258919 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258919 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258919 1111 #uuid "4b6b26c0-f41e-56e3-9bb3-7bd483e8aab3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258920 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258921 13194139533864 true] [17592186258920 1089 "q-50629" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258920 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258920 1100 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[9]{{n}^{2}}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}{2{n}^{2}}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258920 1113 #uuid "a897110c-16a9-5e34-8f8f-61673b309ea9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258920 1145 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}.$ Rewriting this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\sqrt[9]{{n}^{2}}}=8\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2/9}},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{2}{9}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}{2{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258922 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258923 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258924 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258925 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258926 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258927 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258928 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258929 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258925 13194139533864 true] [17592186258921 1110 #uuid "f719330c-8cad-5dd6-8388-518d937a28d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258922 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258922 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258922 1111 #uuid "e82e0a94-0ef1-5341-a309-036af807e925" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258923 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258923 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258923 1111 #uuid "d17dd7b5-7079-5e55-a247-27b2c0ea73e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258924 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258924 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258924 1111 #uuid "ef7b6a8d-bcfe-50b3-a209-f5009fafc7bf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258925 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258925 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258925 1111 #uuid "b0008a49-cef9-5b3e-8b9e-c5f691191a5e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258926 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258926 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258926 1111 #uuid "0d405ad9-1854-5b2f-ba5e-5a63f59fac5d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258927 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258927 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258927 1111 #uuid "233a6e40-88e2-5eb9-86a7-69a846a8e298" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258928 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258928 1111 #uuid "66bff3b0-0197-5440-8606-f7cf19285068" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258929 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258929 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258929 1111 #uuid "4a02105c-bb09-5f55-963a-149b9d550997" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258930 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258931 13194139533864 true] [17592186258930 1089 "q-51051" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258930 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258930 1100 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}8x-1}{2x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258930 1113 #uuid "a06fc393-8cfd-59d3-ab02-f104a703cf73" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258930 1145 "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}8x-1}{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(8\\cdot 0)-1}{2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\theta }=0\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}8x-1}{2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-(1-\\operatorname{cos}8x)}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(8\\cdot \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8\\cdot x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8x} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $\\theta =8x.$ Then, since $\\theta \\to 0$ as $x\\to 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8x}={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\theta }=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}8x}{8x} & =-4\\cdot 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258932 13194139533864 true] [17592186258931 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258931 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258931 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258932 13194139533864 true] [17592186258931 1110 #uuid "afd8774b-b9d3-5d5c-a7e5-afdde01fad3d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258932 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258932 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258932 1111 #uuid "915c35b1-110c-595b-ab13-a53989edc066" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258933 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258934 13194139533864 true] [17592186258933 1089 "q-5145" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258933 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258933 1100 "Find $p$ if $2$ is a root of $f(x)=12{x}^{2}-3px.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258933 1113 #uuid "64793999-0fa3-539c-b945-76159b1213b2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258933 1145 "Since  $x=2$ is a root, we have $f(2)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=2,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(2)^{2}-3p(2) & =0 \\\\ 12(4)-6p & =0 \\\\ 48-6p & =0 \\\\ -6p & =-48 \\\\ p & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258934 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258935 13194139533864 true] [17592186258934 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258934 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258934 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258935 13194139533864 true] [17592186258934 1110 #uuid "81f591d8-00b2-566c-99df-1c6a9f783203" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258935 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258935 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258935 1111 #uuid "ab16b9c5-9977-585e-b838-599705aeba9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258936 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258937 13194139533864 true] [17592186258936 1089 "q-51567" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258936 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258936 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4585ab21f7130a45e2ff5871e0fdc66cda5483181033010519781d9725eec36a.png)\n\nThe graph of a periodic function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. What is the period of this function?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258936 1113 #uuid "d428032a-dc8a-5dbd-8dd9-59088ffcca86" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258936 1145 "Note that if we shift the graph $3$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $3$ (or a multiple of $3$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258937 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258938 13194139533864 true] [17592186258937 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258937 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258937 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258938 13194139533864 true] [17592186258937 1110 #uuid "3b6689cc-424a-5a03-b855-95578f1e475f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258938 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258938 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258938 1111 #uuid "437dcd9d-2381-5665-8812-6db4db373dcb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258939 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258940 13194139533864 true] [17592186258939 1089 "q-5174" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258939 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258939 1100 "If ${log}_{2}x=3$, then what is $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258939 1113 #uuid "51e5f7cc-f758-5b6e-9b53-61015feb7246" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258939 1145 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}x & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}x} & ={2}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${2}^{{log}_{2}x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258940 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258941 13194139533864 true] [17592186258940 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258940 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258940 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258941 13194139533864 true] [17592186258940 1110 #uuid "afb138c8-d5fe-521b-a16b-e38a7fd1ad20" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258941 1034 "8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258941 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258941 1111 #uuid "5e1cdbb0-3158-56a8-8bf1-b12a39213539" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258942 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258943 13194139533864 true] [17592186258942 1089 "q-51843" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258942 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258942 1100 "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=-3$ and ${a}_{2}=5,$ then ${a}_{4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258942 1113 #uuid "aaf574a4-1edb-5e4e-ad18-e36c426d7d2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258942 1145 "To compute ${a}_{4},$ we first need to compute ${a}_{3}.$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+2} & ={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n} \\\\ {a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =5+(-3) \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute ${a}_{4},$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2+2} & ={a}_{2+1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2+5 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{4}=7.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258943 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258944 13194139533864 true] [17592186258943 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258943 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258943 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258944 13194139533864 true] [17592186258943 1110 #uuid "740de7d4-004d-5340-938d-4b8a451d2832" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258944 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258944 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258944 1111 #uuid "27dd64a2-7bd5-5f97-9dad-e0c5611a4652" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258945 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258946 13194139533864 true] [17592186258945 1089 "q-51884" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258945 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258945 1100 "If $f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$ with $f(1)=2$ and $f(2)=7,$ then $f(3)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258945 1113 #uuid "1511ae51-ebcc-5ba6-b833-34f0d648c90d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258945 1145 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =7+2 \\\\ f(3) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(3)=9.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258947 13194139533864 true] [17592186258946 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258946 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258946 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258947 13194139533864 true] [17592186258946 1110 #uuid "eee2c678-c53b-5c59-9f8e-f6f6412565fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258947 1034 "9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258947 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258947 1111 #uuid "258fcf2a-82f0-5ea8-bd85-4a4bad015507" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258948 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258949 13194139533864 true] [17592186258948 1089 "q-52557" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258948 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258948 1100 "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=1$ and ${a}_{2}=6,$ then ${a}_{4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258948 1113 #uuid "8a11f5a8-91b1-5863-9d5d-ca50ab4f8b49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258948 1145 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =6+1 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2+2} & ={a}_{2+1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =7+6 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${a}_{4}=13.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258949 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258950 13194139533864 true] [17592186258949 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258949 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258949 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258950 13194139533864 true] [17592186258949 1110 #uuid "047ab731-6e2a-50ae-8359-c91d287ccaa3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258950 1034 "13" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258950 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258950 1111 #uuid "474158d3-9ce8-52f7-9f17-4787c83f481e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258951 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258952 13194139533864 true] [17592186258951 1089 "q-5300" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258951 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258951 1100 "What is the sum of the arithmetic series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}(-2n)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258951 1113 #uuid "ac22c321-341c-5b1a-8d5d-082d85416290" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258951 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nGiven the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}(-2n)$ in sigma notation, we note that\n\n- the total number of terms is $N=8$\n\n- the first term is ${a}_{1}=-2\\cdot 1=-2$\n\n- the last term is ${a}_{8}=-2\\cdot 8=-16$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{8} & =\\frac{8}{2}(-2+(-16)) \\\\  & =4(-18) \\\\  & =-72\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258953 13194139533864 true] [17592186258952 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258952 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258952 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258953 13194139533864 true] [17592186258952 1110 #uuid "b530797f-f38a-5d88-8eb5-a1eb5d3c16a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258953 1034 "-72" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258953 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258953 1111 #uuid "817c3779-2936-58e7-a892-3e1f60da2e7f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258954 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258955 13194139533864 true] [17592186258954 1089 "q-533" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258954 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258954 1100 "What is the value of ${\\text{i}}^{2}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258954 1113 #uuid "85fc0f28-62f8-538e-bd6b-a85278b14b50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258954 1145 "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258956 13194139533864 true] [17592186258955 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258955 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258955 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258956 13194139533864 true] [17592186258955 1110 #uuid "cf132198-cedf-5b46-8c37-ea64c36630bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258956 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258956 1111 #uuid "c1d65a6d-bf89-5a0a-82c2-a379c4c5fc50" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258957 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258958 13194139533864 true] [17592186258957 1089 "q-53707" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258957 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258957 1100 "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\nI. - Rotation by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise around a point $O$\nII. - Reflection across the line $y=x$\nIII. - Translation by $2$ units downward, then reflection across the $y$-axis" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258957 1113 #uuid "1619c077-49d6-5e48-9ec7-0e73f45a0c3d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258957 1145 "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations.\n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is a rotation, which is a rigid motion. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation II is a reflection, which is also a rigid motion. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation III is a combination of translation and a reflection. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258959 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258960 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258961 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258962 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258963 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258964 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258965 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258966 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258959 13194139533864 true] [17592186258958 1110 #uuid "0cdedff1-749e-5f66-9b0d-e97ce597ac82" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258959 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258959 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258959 1111 #uuid "59023a64-26c4-536d-8dde-931060606dd1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258960 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258960 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258960 1111 #uuid "fbef86b5-8e4f-5d00-8110-e4bf4686c160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258961 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258961 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [175921