 similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I includes stretching with different factors in the $x$- and $y$-direction. Thus, it is not a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation II is a combination of two translations, which are rigid motions. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation III is a combination of dilation of scale factor $2$ and a reflection. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258969 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258970 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258971 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258972 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258973 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258974 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258975 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258976 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258970 13194139533864 true] [17592186258968 1110 #uuid "6d48b603-1df0-5461-9d24-35d968e79fdd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258969 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258969 1111 #uuid "a3e4495b-139d-5866-a47e-ce97abbf6fc5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258970 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258970 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258970 1111 #uuid "15cf785b-2983-55a4-ad42-8fdd2f3019b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258971 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258971 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258971 1111 #uuid "bda3f3ff-f263-5ced-ae65-4f7f16e76a88" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258972 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258972 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258972 1111 #uuid "bd295e36-ebb3-5937-a21c-796f5e53a88a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258973 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258973 1111 #uuid "a70fb577-a5c2-5742-b5c1-2d14492e2ddb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258974 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258974 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258974 1111 #uuid "42bcf7ce-9945-591c-98e0-3fbaf24c2b01" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258975 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258975 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258975 1111 #uuid "332343e5-f574-585f-a7cf-a7ba25ad1cc8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258976 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186258976 1111 #uuid "c96652f2-0d0f-52e0-afb8-eaef93aa99c2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258977 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258978 13194139533864 true] [17592186258977 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258980 13194139533864 true] [17592186258977 1089 "q-53754" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258977 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258977 1100 "The equation of a curve is $3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=9.$ The normal line at the point $(3,\\,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258977 1113 #uuid "91ee7106-7624-554e-a734-a1b55bccb844" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258977 1145 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(3,\\,3).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 6x-4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-6x}{-4y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3x}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(3,\\,3)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3x}{2y})}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3/2}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(3,\\,3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(-3)+3 \\\\ y & =2+3 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258979 13194139533864 true] [17592186258978 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258978 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258978 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258979 13194139533864 true] [17592186258978 1110 #uuid "48897116-5235-5acf-94fe-bcd383de1cee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258979 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258979 1111 #uuid "90209f67-483c-5e31-a0ad-bdd02e85f44a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258981 13194139533864 true] [17592186258980 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258980 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258980 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258981 13194139533864 true] [17592186258980 1110 #uuid "7b89e891-b1fa-58ac-b17a-34e1f3ff8d2e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258981 1034 "\\,5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258981 1111 #uuid "852d25b5-37c8-5917-8e19-a7a6fff4d1e1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258982 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258983 13194139533864 true] [17592186258982 1089 "q-538" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258982 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258982 1100 "${({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{2015}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258982 1113 #uuid "e22a5106-9b86-5df9-82bd-ae96786f3991" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258982 1145 "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{({\\text{i}}^{4})}^{2015}=(1)^{2015}=1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258983 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258984 13194139533864 true] [17592186258983 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258983 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258983 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258984 13194139533864 true] [17592186258983 1110 #uuid "8df60c92-dcf7-5f84-9b2b-e78a9ed3f90b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258984 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258984 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258984 1111 #uuid "da397cf0-577d-5fc2-a133-0ee5fec95c88" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258985 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258986 13194139533864 true] [17592186258985 1089 "q-549" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258985 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258985 1100 "${\\text{i}}^{14}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258985 1113 #uuid "f8437f94-1d26-5f61-9246-7a0a4a80faa9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258985 1145 "To find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=14.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n14÷4=3\\,\\text{R}2.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{14}={\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258986 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258987 13194139533864 true] [17592186258986 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258986 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258986 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258987 13194139533864 true] [17592186258986 1110 #uuid "690025c3-3162-54f0-9482-c6813f64a78f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258987 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258987 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258987 1111 #uuid "b721c5a8-f447-5d0a-8137-886105d797b1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258988 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258989 13194139533864 true] [17592186258988 1089 "q-575" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258988 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186258988 1100 "If $a={\\text{i}}^{2}$ then ${a}^{4}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258988 1113 #uuid "feb815e0-49a2-5a08-9510-a18cd36fc9e1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258988 1145 "Using the power law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}={({\\text{i}}^{2})}^{4}={\\text{i}}^{2\\cdot 4}={\\text{i}}^{8}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=8.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n8÷4=2\\,\\text{R}0.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{8}={\\text{i}}^{0}=1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258989 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258990 13194139533864 true] [17592186258989 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258989 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258989 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258990 13194139533864 true] [17592186258989 1110 #uuid "e656be50-0e8a-5b00-a570-bb0ae760e03d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258990 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258990 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258990 1111 #uuid "841e8a1a-3c8b-5f97-965f-40251f13efe6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258991 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258992 13194139533864 true] [17592186258991 1089 "q-5759" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258991 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258991 1100 "You're buying a new computer and can choose different options: $3$ different levels of RAM, $2$ different hard drive sizes, $3$ different monitor sizes, and $4$ different colors.  How many different computer systems can you create from these choices?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258991 1113 #uuid "749914bf-d1a3-5489-91da-4a74ce2608f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258991 1145 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of computer systems} & =\\text{Number of RAM levels} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of hard drive sizes} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of monitor sizes} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of colors} \\\\  & =3\\times 2\\times 3\\times 4 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can create $72$ different computer systems." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258993 13194139533864 true] [17592186258992 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258992 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258992 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258993 13194139533864 true] [17592186258992 1110 #uuid "31c1f32f-a92e-5062-8efa-0e6b7f45f5da" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258993 1034 "72" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258993 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258993 1111 #uuid "917b7239-d1a9-58a5-ae89-76e89f4a79c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258994 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258995 13194139533864 true] [17592186258994 1089 "q-5760" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258994 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186258994 1100 "You're letting your computer make random songs using $5$ different notes, and you want the song to be $32$ notes long. All $32$ notes have the same duration. If notes can be repeated, how many different songs can your computer create?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258994 1113 #uuid "98158fcc-db0f-530b-87f1-367cdd949729" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258994 1145 "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of songs} & =\\text{Number of choices for the }1\\text{st note} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of choices for the }2\\text{nd note} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of choices for the }3\\text{rd note} \\\\  & \\times … \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of choices for the }32\\text{nd note} \\\\  & ={{5\\times 5\\times 5\\times ⋯\\times 5}_{⏟}}_{32\\text{ times}} \\\\  & ={5}^{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the computer can create ${5}^{32}$ different songs." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258995 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258996 13194139533864 true] [17592186258995 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258995 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258995 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258996 13194139533864 true] [17592186258995 1110 #uuid "c2b84597-acc5-5c3d-9c3c-d9cb9cddc42b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258996 1034 "{5}^{32}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258996 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258996 1111 #uuid "dfad00bb-0495-5532-915c-4c3d81a054c0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258997 1017 #edb/ref 17592186258998 13194139533864 true] [17592186258997 1089 "q-60150" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258997 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186258997 1100 "In a zoo, there are $12$ camels. If the ratio of camels to monkeys is $3:7,$ how many monkeys are there in the zoo?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258997 1113 #uuid "2b7399af-5c1c-5c70-8b98-f78b69b577eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258997 1145 "We start with the ratio $3:7$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of camels and the denominator represents the number of monkeys.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $12$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 4}{7\\times 4}=\\frac{12}{28}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $28$ monkeys in the zoo." 13194139533864 true] [17592186258998 1000 #edb/ref 17592186258999 13194139533864 true] [17592186258998 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186258998 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258998 1104 #edb/ref 17592186258999 13194139533864 true] [17592186258998 1110 #uuid "69b03fbc-9c09-5a05-9e92-855079d6d16c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258999 1034 "28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186258999 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186258999 1111 #uuid "ccced504-eda8-5d95-8eb9-d594c37589c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259000 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259001 13194139533864 true] [17592186259000 1089 "q-6397" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259000 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259000 1100 "Given that $k\\ge 0$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(k+3)!}{(k+1)k!}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259000 1113 #uuid "1390df05-6bca-5037-aa71-abe139ff704c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259000 1145 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(k+3)!=(k+3)\\cdot (k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $k!$ and $(k+1)$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(k+3)!}{(k+1)k!}= & \\frac{(k+3)\\cdot (k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k!}{(k+1)\\cdot k!} \\\\ = & \\frac{(k+3)\\cdot (k+2)\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)}\\cdot \\cancel{k!}}{\\cancel{(k+1)}\\cdot \\cancel{k!}} \\\\ = & (k+3)(k+2) \\\\ = & {k}^{2}+5k+6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259002 13194139533864 true] [17592186259001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259003 13194139533864 true] [17592186259001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259004 13194139533864 true] [17592186259001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259005 13194139533864 true] [17592186259001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259006 13194139533864 true] [17592186259001 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259001 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259001 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259003 13194139533864 true] [17592186259001 1110 #uuid "b54fd3d2-3af0-5e41-8978-da67e08d4bbb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259002 1034 "k+2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259002 1111 #uuid "9a4fc142-f8be-5f31-882a-e83b75f5f1ee" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259003 1034 "{k}^{2}+5k+6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259003 1111 #uuid "b73b0fee-d32e-56aa-ad42-83d1a7c3fa14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259004 1034 "{k}^{2}-3k+1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259004 1111 #uuid "9fd33fda-aef8-5fb6-b1e9-74d1da876554" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259005 1034 "{k}^{2}+2k+3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259005 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259005 1111 #uuid "3c092cc7-e57c-5648-9b3e-ba3be80ea005" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259006 1034 "k+3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259006 1111 #uuid "8dd55d92-a16d-5694-9a50-729422e0bc37" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259008 13194139533864 true] [17592186259007 1089 "q-63983" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259007 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259007 1100 "What is the index of the term $192$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=2f(n),\\,f(1)=3,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259007 1113 #uuid "4bb8271f-112b-50bc-8969-18efd4c79667" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259007 1145 "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\nf(1)=3,\\,r=2.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that $f(n)=192.$ Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ 192 & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ \\frac{192}{3} & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ 64 & ={2}^{n-1} \\\\ {2}^{6} & ={2}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n6=n-1\\,⟹\\,n=7\n$$\n\nTherefore, $192$ is the $7$th term of the sequence." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259009 13194139533864 true] [17592186259008 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259008 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259008 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259009 13194139533864 true] [17592186259008 1110 #uuid "7ba5a909-4d46-51cf-a875-7ce7b06f88a4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259009 1034 "7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259009 1111 #uuid "0279c069-a67d-5613-b216-68b81778d8dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259010 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259011 13194139533864 true] [17592186259010 1089 "q-64010" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259010 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259010 1100 "The third term of a geometric sequence is $108$ and its fifth term is $3.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259010 1113 #uuid "76388739-1ca8-5045-add3-33e80a2c5254" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259010 1145 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{5}=3$ and ${a}_{3}=108.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{108} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259012 13194139533864 true] [17592186259011 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259011 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259011 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259012 13194139533864 true] [17592186259011 1110 #uuid "2642f3cb-c89f-58d1-845c-061ebbb035a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259012 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259012 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259012 1111 #uuid "5f7157fd-8e05-5d45-a87c-b26a10f3d7b0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259013 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259014 13194139533864 true] [17592186259013 1089 "q-64678" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259013 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259013 1100 "The angle $\\theta =-{650}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259013 1113 #uuid "0e21b7ac-5788-524c-8ceb-e2c5880c1015" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259013 1145 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{650}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $({0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}].$\n\n- $-{650}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{290}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{650}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={70}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${70}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{650}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/8dff305410da2b49c69006fdb84b4de6b481ccf2a526f42b18d2eae538601bfb.png)\n\nWe see that ${70}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant I. Therefore, $-{650}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259014 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259015 13194139533864 true] [17592186259014 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259016 13194139533864 true] [17592186259014 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259017 13194139533864 true] [17592186259014 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259018 13194139533864 true] [17592186259014 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259014 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259014 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259016 13194139533864 true] [17592186259014 1110 #uuid "4a2c4789-d7f3-566b-8e6b-156289316c1f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259015 1034 "Quadrant II" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259015 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259015 1111 #uuid "40cfb998-cdc7-5a8a-9862-40b40601bcad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259016 1034 "Quadrant I" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259016 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259016 1111 #uuid "74a27f2d-4de7-5c12-bf80-2bc0547ebf19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259017 1034 "Quadrant III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259017 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259017 1111 #uuid "949b5bce-4f4d-5782-b3a1-58fb0babf6a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259018 1034 "Quadrant IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259018 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259018 1111 #uuid "73c35910-0338-58bf-9e5d-77001c9a61d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259019 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259020 13194139533864 true] [17592186259019 1089 "q-64684" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259019 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259019 1100 "The angle $\\theta =\\frac{17\\pi }{6}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259019 1113 #uuid "45b2051b-479f-542d-97fb-8807ff31a294" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259019 1145 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{17\\pi }{6}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $\\frac{17\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $\\frac{17\\pi }{6}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1c7c1d3a8d69502521dc71f24335ff79e707867161b95d6a80eaf95c0bf6e36c.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $\\frac{17\\pi }{6}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259021 13194139533864 true] [17592186259020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259022 13194139533864 true] [17592186259020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259023 13194139533864 true] [17592186259020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259024 13194139533864 true] [17592186259020 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259020 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259020 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259021 13194139533864 true] [17592186259020 1110 #uuid "3612bfb5-d8b9-5b06-9025-f70807e1466e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259021 1034 "Quadrant II" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259021 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259021 1111 #uuid "8a098b45-c2e8-5184-ad1b-26d16ee81d23" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259022 1034 "Quadrant I" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259022 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259022 1111 #uuid "712753df-61f6-5b8d-aac1-8334199f6c95" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259023 1034 "Quadrant III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259023 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259023 1111 #uuid "f1ce7416-c18b-595c-8946-699ee597f39c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259024 1034 "Quadrant IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259024 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259024 1111 #uuid "43d72985-f62c-5dcf-b5d5-ca0c6215fc29" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259025 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259026 13194139533864 true] [17592186259025 1089 "q-64685" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259025 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259025 1100 "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259025 1113 #uuid "838287b6-8054-530d-a9fc-68e77332ddb3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259025 1145 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b5397fc107a7425c822a9d00a169fa7c3624a3e29e3a8266897d459f19ac1aee.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259027 13194139533864 true] [17592186259026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259028 13194139533864 true] [17592186259026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259029 13194139533864 true] [17592186259026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259030 13194139533864 true] [17592186259026 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259026 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259026 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259027 13194139533864 true] [17592186259026 1110 #uuid "fc8f9d6b-fcbc-5fd8-9a0d-eb8398e0a69b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259027 1034 "Quadrant II" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259027 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259027 1111 #uuid "c3060ff9-5832-5483-b4f6-621dd4a0844d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259028 1034 "Quadrant I" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259028 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259028 1111 #uuid "8eb093f3-6cd5-5f4f-9458-696e29118eb4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259029 1034 "Quadrant III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259029 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259029 1111 #uuid "80d886b1-7b9b-5484-9d4b-45199d952142" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259030 1034 "Quadrant IV" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259030 1111 #uuid "d843c8f7-8868-5f25-bca3-74331a0671cb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259031 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259032 13194139533864 true] [17592186259031 1089 "q-64863" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259031 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259031 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259031 1113 #uuid "5d76d623-1c6f-5d51-9983-82a36796ab6c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259031 1145 "The angle $0$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}0=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259033 13194139533864 true] [17592186259032 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259032 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259032 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259033 13194139533864 true] [17592186259032 1110 #uuid "1854554f-c17d-57f3-91f8-95d189868d2d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259033 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259033 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259033 1111 #uuid "e6c93262-23b3-5291-822c-b2fc2431d836" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259034 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259035 13194139533864 true] [17592186259034 1089 "q-6491" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259034 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259034 1100 "What is the sum of the arithmetic series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(n+5)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259034 1113 #uuid "51f91c45-43d2-5fcb-8a9f-a87826b50b9f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259034 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nGiven the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(n+5)$ in sigma notation, we note that\n\n- the total number of terms is $N=10$\n\n- the first term is ${a}_{1}=1+5=6$\n\n- the last term is ${a}_{10}=10+5=15$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{10} & =\\frac{10}{2}(6+15) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =105\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259036 13194139533864 true] [17592186259035 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259035 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259035 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259036 13194139533864 true] [17592186259035 1110 #uuid "39483401-c887-5eab-9097-84d46f3a7fff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259036 1034 "105" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259036 1111 #uuid "81c51b21-cf4d-54d7-8ea3-bf125558d5d6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259037 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259038 13194139533864 true] [17592186259037 1089 "q-64929" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259037 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259037 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259037 1113 #uuid "8358751c-30a9-5482-b7a6-569bce8efae2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259037 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259038 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259039 13194139533864 true] [17592186259038 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259038 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259038 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259039 13194139533864 true] [17592186259038 1110 #uuid "bacd49d1-4171-50e8-bfbf-31f75f685056" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259039 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259039 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259039 1111 #uuid "93f296a3-9576-51c4-b33b-0f948582bc66" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259040 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259041 13194139533864 true] [17592186259040 1089 "q-65940" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259040 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259040 1100 "What values is **not** in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259040 1113 #uuid "c9123ac6-7632-56b3-b65f-ed8e53a479f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259040 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=-2$ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259041 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259042 13194139533864 true] [17592186259041 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259041 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259041 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259042 13194139533864 true] [17592186259041 1110 #uuid "a864d3e1-1652-5a25-9295-3c0dc4974de9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259042 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259042 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259042 1111 #uuid "9a1bacdb-2b02-5c2b-a494-eb6aa4f0049a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259043 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259044 13194139533864 true] [17592186259043 1089 "q-66412" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259043 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259043 1100 "Given $f(x)={x}^{2}-6$ and $g(x)=-x+2,$ find  $(f-g)(4).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259043 1113 #uuid "85eb992c-5f22-5219-9748-87d4d54a3ac2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259043 1145 "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$ \n\n**Method 1** \n\nTo evaluate $(f-g)(4),$ we find $f(4)$ and $g(4),$ and then subtract $g(4)$ from $f(4).$\n\nSo, first we find $f(4)$ and $g(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =(4)^{2}-6=10 \\\\ g(4) & =-(4)+2=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we subtract $g(4)$ from $f(4)$:\n\n$$\n(f-g)(4)=f(4)-g(4)=10-(-2)=12\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst we find the difference of the two functions $f$ and $g$ by subtracting $g(x)$ from $f(x)$: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =({x}^{2}-6)-(-x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6+x-2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f-g)(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(4) & =(4)^{2}+4-8=12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259044 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259045 13194139533864 true] [17592186259044 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259044 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259044 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259045 13194139533864 true] [17592186259044 1110 #uuid "6821fbd9-b4f5-5206-b8ed-cd3fb89fc36b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259045 1034 "12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259045 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259045 1111 #uuid "7638ef27-f19f-5c5f-a5c1-11acc4e29d6a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259046 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259047 13194139533864 true] [17592186259046 1089 "q-66420" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259046 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259046 1100 "Given $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-4$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+2,$ find  $(fg)(-2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259046 1113 #uuid "7f3adebb-be1f-5f32-94a7-24221953a6e1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259046 1145 "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-2),$ we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4(-2)^{2}-4=12 \\\\ g(-2) & =(-2)^{2}+2=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-2)=f(-2)g(-2)=12\\cdot 6=72\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(4{x}^{2}-4)({x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-2) & =4(-2)^{4}+4(-2)^{2}-8 \\\\  & =64+16-8 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259047 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259048 13194139533864 true] [17592186259047 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259047 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259047 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259048 13194139533864 true] [17592186259047 1110 #uuid "e28444a1-1bf8-5433-8bf8-e1265d606a0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259048 1034 "72" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259048 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259048 1111 #uuid "7629391d-2609-5c56-8323-73c227842648" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259049 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259050 13194139533864 true] [17592186259049 1089 "q-66425" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259049 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259049 1100 "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-6$ and $g(x)=-2x+2,$ find  $(f/g)(3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259049 1113 #uuid "be22410d-413d-5639-ac9e-a2e4fceb3697" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259049 1145 "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(3),$ we find $f(3)$ and $g(3),$ and then divide the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(3)$ and $g(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2(3)^{2}-6=12 \\\\ g(3) & =-2(3)+2=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(3)=\\frac{f(3)}{g(3)}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{-2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(3) & =\\frac{2(3)^{2}-6}{-2(3)+2}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259051 13194139533864 true] [17592186259050 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259050 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259050 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259051 13194139533864 true] [17592186259050 1110 #uuid "61952005-9460-5d76-b16f-7f533c1dbb6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259051 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259051 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259051 1111 #uuid "b9267840-5ce8-574d-a2fc-24a332eabb5f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259052 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259053 13194139533864 true] [17592186259052 1089 "q-66456" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259052 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259052 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)=2x+6$ and $g(x)=\\frac{x}{2}+k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259052 1113 #uuid "923497f6-8730-5b4d-abe0-ef7248d1cbbc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259052 1145 "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =\\frac{x}{2}+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =\\frac{f(x)}{2}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=2x+6$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =\\frac{f(x)}{2}+k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+6}{2}+k \\\\  & =x+3+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}+3+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ 3+k & =0 \\\\ k & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259053 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259054 13194139533864 true] [17592186259053 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259053 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259053 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259054 13194139533864 true] [17592186259053 1110 #uuid "390887ae-57b4-509e-a85d-2fcab16c4c1d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259054 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259054 1111 #uuid "90f9a494-3dc9-5916-a927-e8fd3b943a34" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259055 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259056 13194139533864 true] [17592186259055 1089 "q-66481" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259055 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259055 1100 "Find  $(f/g)(-2)$  for $f(x)={x}^{2}+x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+2x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259055 1113 #uuid "935c56eb-56da-5d6e-947a-de0e34bd7c74" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259055 1145 "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(-2),$ we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2),$ and then divide the results. \n\nFirst, we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =(-2)^{2}+(-2)=2 \\\\ g(-2) & =(-2)^{2}+2(-2)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, since the denominator of a fraction can't be zero, we can't divide $f(-2)$ by $g(-2).$\n\nTherefore, the value  $(f/g)(-2)$  is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259056 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259057 13194139533864 true] [17592186259056 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259056 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259056 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259057 13194139533864 true] [17592186259056 1110 #uuid "db91a2a4-f747-5abc-b3f2-624d8c8adf48" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259057 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259057 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259057 1111 #uuid "972fdea6-91a0-5c04-915e-c97d847aea79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259058 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259059 13194139533864 true] [17592186259058 1089 "q-66490" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259058 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259058 1100 "$-4{\\text{i}}^{-2}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259058 1113 #uuid "1ba253e5-6b77-5991-9dca-b8648ddf6d5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259058 1145 "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n-4{\\text{i}}^{-2}=-\\frac{4}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}=-\\frac{4}{(-1)}=4.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259059 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259060 13194139533864 true] [17592186259059 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259059 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259059 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259060 13194139533864 true] [17592186259059 1110 #uuid "a58bb529-ef27-5657-bf19-9e025da5b449" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259060 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259060 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259060 1111 #uuid "593fe36f-a7fe-5072-a247-39f4491b9f08" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259061 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259062 13194139533864 true] [17592186259061 1089 "q-66631" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259061 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259061 1100 "$\\operatorname{log}(\\sqrt[4]{10})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259061 1113 #uuid "eeafaca2-0567-55ba-95dd-d0ca684cdc15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259061 1145 "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[4]{10}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{10}={10}^{1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(\\sqrt[4]{10})=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{1/4})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{1/4})=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259062 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259063 13194139533864 true] [17592186259062 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259062 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259062 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259063 13194139533864 true] [17592186259062 1110 #uuid "acd6482b-f428-5998-8399-25cb88d42795" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259063 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259063 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259063 1111 #uuid "475a2c31-42f1-50cb-a261-1edd8acd5e39" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259064 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259065 13194139533864 true] [17592186259064 1089 "q-66772" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259064 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259064 1100 "$6{log}_{5}(100)-6{log}_{5}(4)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259064 1113 #uuid "77269f1f-6854-5aa0-b092-d17b19bb30c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259064 1145 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}(a)-{log}_{n}(b)={log}_{n}(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{5}(100)-6{log}_{5}(4) & =6({log}_{5}(100)-{log}_{5}(4)) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}(\\frac{100}{4}) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}(25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${5}^{2}=25$, then ${log}_{5}(25)={log}_{5}({5}^{2})=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n6{log}_{5}(25)=6\\cdot 2=12.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259065 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259066 13194139533864 true] [17592186259065 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259065 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259065 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259066 13194139533864 true] [17592186259065 1110 #uuid "51107b99-a0e6-5ec3-9377-1d83c4b7a6fa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259066 1034 "12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259066 1111 #uuid "6130991a-8ab3-5716-b016-0c243c85f322" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259067 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259068 13194139533864 true] [17592186259067 1089 "q-66785" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259067 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259067 1100 "Given that $f(x)=|x-4|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. - $x\\in (-\\infty ,3)$\nII. - $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\nIII. - $x\\in (-\\infty ,5]$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259067 1113 #uuid "4cce8534-f73e-5161-8a0a-c2664b949e49" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259067 1145 "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-4|+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-4|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-4|$ by $2$ units up to get $f(x)=|x-4|+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/56956d0f23ae6828098507712fd00525dc4f114a195716dc16e63d8475e9c143.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,3).$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in (1,\\infty )$  and $(-\\infty ,5].$ On each of these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259069 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259070 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259071 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259072 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259073 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259074 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259075 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259076 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259072 13194139533864 true] [17592186259068 1110 #uuid "14d3f168-6a19-58ff-abd8-b04a5f4655b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259069 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259069 1111 #uuid "ab26b8cb-a139-5ce9-a151-ebee17d1b6f9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259070 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259070 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259070 1111 #uuid "11c95497-a320-54ed-a88c-ad12ea0c199f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259071 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259071 1111 #uuid "d054f279-d2ac-5a3a-8060-047cfa775c71" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259072 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259072 1111 #uuid "881d65ca-2072-5588-bce2-9ed33b1a57dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259073 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259073 1111 #uuid "75b3a3b7-487c-519f-ba7a-d4d852a091ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259074 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259074 1111 #uuid "0ed2ddfb-be8c-5994-95e9-1fa71c0540c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259075 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259075 1111 #uuid "dac16ae2-2b04-54fb-8083-8201d8ca44f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259076 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259076 1111 #uuid "fe58edd6-ff34-597f-baca-916875313e30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259078 13194139533864 true] [17592186259077 1089 "q-66864" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259077 1100 "Find the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${9}^{x-5}={3}^{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259077 1113 #uuid "a0166c2e-fa1a-5455-a7a2-c570d25330ae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259077 1145 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${3}^{2},$ and then rewrite the left-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{x-5} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {({3}^{2})}^{x-5} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{2(x-5)} & ={3}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-5) & =x \\\\ 2x-10 & =x \\\\ x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259079 13194139533864 true] [17592186259078 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259078 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259078 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259079 13194139533864 true] [17592186259078 1110 #uuid "c3e9ccd4-c33e-56dc-9e3d-865956871ef6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259079 1034 "10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259079 1111 #uuid "089bec96-9182-5888-ac4f-ff6b790f83ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259080 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259081 13194139533864 true] [17592186259080 1089 "q-66880" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259080 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259080 1100 "${log}_{25}(\\sqrt{125})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259080 1113 #uuid "bf175bf7-41d9-5d9c-9ca1-8254b6c5a5b0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259080 1145 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=25$ and $X=\\sqrt{125}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $5,$ we set $b=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{25}(\\sqrt{125}) & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(\\sqrt{125})}{{log}_{5}(25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(\\sqrt{{5}^{3}})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}({5}^{3/2})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259081 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259082 13194139533864 true] [17592186259081 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259081 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259081 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259082 13194139533864 true] [17592186259081 1110 #uuid "c060282b-a228-5149-88ee-93425f941ef2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259082 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259082 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259082 1111 #uuid "b03a8b08-8e7b-5cd4-a133-f21c8ad23ce8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259083 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259084 13194139533864 true] [17592186259083 1089 "q-66887" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259083 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259083 1100 "${log}_{9}(\\sqrt{27})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259083 1113 #uuid "47a63a18-20ff-5e2a-89aa-c14176c04a06" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259083 1145 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=9$ and $X=\\sqrt{27}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $3,$ we set $b=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}(\\sqrt{27}) & =\\frac{{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{27})}{{log}_{3}(9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{{3}^{3}})}{{log}_{3}({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}({3}^{3/2})}{{log}_{3}({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259084 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259085 13194139533864 true] [17592186259084 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259084 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259084 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259085 13194139533864 true] [17592186259084 1110 #uuid "d0e6003b-62b3-5c4a-a6fd-c807d1c3bba4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259085 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259085 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259085 1111 #uuid "a37b4e09-7c5d-59e7-869c-54c3b277f653" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259086 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259087 13194139533864 true] [17592186259086 1089 "q-6690" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259086 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259086 1100 "Is the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-2x$ even, odd, or neither?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259086 1113 #uuid "04c5062c-5f4b-5176-9cc2-4587d770b021" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259086 1145 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =2(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(2{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ the function is odd." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259087 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259088 13194139533864 true] [17592186259087 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259089 13194139533864 true] [17592186259087 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259090 13194139533864 true] [17592186259087 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259087 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259087 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259088 13194139533864 true] [17592186259087 1110 #uuid "f1cdf5a9-0267-594e-ae75-94addeb8ba71" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259088 1034 "Odd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259088 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259088 1111 #uuid "cd64d0c3-583d-5c65-a565-ee541a6fd8d7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259089 1034 "Neither" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259089 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259089 1111 #uuid "bb107164-8082-5aaa-a7ea-d774a9761a12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259090 1034 "Even" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259090 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259090 1111 #uuid "950d78ae-9463-5605-901d-784797ad43e3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259091 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259092 13194139533864 true] [17592186259091 1089 "q-66901" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259091 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259091 1100 "Given that ${e}^{2h}-4=7,$ find the value of $h$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259091 1113 #uuid "c8d597e1-3a70-5551-8ca3-548ecbb3ddce" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259091 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2h}-4 & =7 \\\\ {e}^{2h} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2h} & =11 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{2h}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(11) \\\\ 2h & =\\operatorname{ln}(11) \\\\ h & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(11)$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nh\\approx 1.199\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259093 13194139533864 true] [17592186259092 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259092 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259092 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259093 13194139533864 true] [17592186259092 1110 #uuid "06df9466-5754-5c36-a3e3-d7675a140094" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259093 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259093 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259093 1111 #uuid "119305f0-b1fe-5b2f-8e3b-0d6015572b29" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259094 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259095 13194139533864 true] [17592186259094 1089 "q-66904" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259094 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259094 1100 "Given that ${e}^{-7p}+3=8,$ find the value of $p$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259094 1113 #uuid "6e5cb6a2-7bc8-5f55-bf93-6f94f39a879f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259094 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-7p}+3 & =8 \\\\ {e}^{-7p} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-7p} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{-7p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(5) \\\\ -7p & =\\operatorname{ln}(5) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}(5)$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\np\\approx -0.230\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259096 13194139533864 true] [17592186259095 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259095 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259095 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259096 13194139533864 true] [17592186259095 1110 #uuid "4a59462f-4f5b-570c-b652-5baadec3debc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259096 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259096 1111 #uuid "8cc96152-4ed2-5310-8597-d190b5027215" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259097 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259098 13194139533864 true] [17592186259097 1089 "q-66934" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259097 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259097 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}(35)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259097 1113 #uuid "1e980299-bf4e-5e95-b34b-d63085c5feea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259097 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}(35)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=35,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}(35) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(35)}{{log}_{10}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(35)}{\\operatorname{log}(8)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.544\\,07}{0.903\\,09} \\\\  & \\approx 1.71\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259098 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259099 13194139533864 true] [17592186259098 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259098 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259098 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259099 13194139533864 true] [17592186259098 1110 #uuid "49f59870-2611-597e-8a30-5301563263a2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259099 1034 "1.71" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259099 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259099 1111 #uuid "fa06c276-2514-578f-8d2c-d26527f7e6c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259100 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259101 13194139533864 true] [17592186259100 1089 "q-67043" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259100 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259100 1100 "The following arithmetic series has $13$ terms in total. Find the sum of the series.\n\n$$\n-9+(-11)+(-13)+(-15)+⋯+(-33)\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259100 1113 #uuid "1b0d02cf-7e0a-5619-ba74-9c01c7f880f0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259100 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-9,{a}_{N}=-33$ and $N=13.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{13} & =\\frac{13}{2}(-9+(-33)) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}(-42) \\\\  & =13(-21) \\\\  & =-273\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259101 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259102 13194139533864 true] [17592186259101 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259101 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259101 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259102 13194139533864 true] [17592186259101 1110 #uuid "878d60a5-6709-5ede-b238-4483f961cae3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259102 1034 "-273" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259102 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259102 1111 #uuid "45bf49dd-7841-5382-a86f-093a0a3c4e69" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259103 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259104 13194139533864 true] [17592186259103 1089 "q-67047" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259103 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259103 1100 "The following arithmetic series has $15$ terms in total. Find the sum of the series.\n\n$$\n-6+(-2)+2+6+⋯+50\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259103 1113 #uuid "4fced0be-707f-5fb0-9e9e-ae88a44c183d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259103 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-6,{a}_{N}=50$ and $N=15.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{15}{2}(-6+50) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot 44 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =330\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259104 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259105 13194139533864 true] [17592186259104 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259104 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259104 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259105 13194139533864 true] [17592186259104 1110 #uuid "67ab2d8d-687a-57cc-bd4e-e89fb5e0950a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259105 1034 "330" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259105 1111 #uuid "4eddb648-ec6d-570d-9987-b5a58e4f4ce2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259106 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259107 13194139533864 true] [17592186259106 1089 "q-67160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259106 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259106 1100 "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=2$, the $6$th term ${a}_{6}=22$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=288.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259106 1113 #uuid "dedda1be-748d-5578-a854-6406afc3ca44" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259106 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{6}-{a}_{1}}{6-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{22-2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=2$, $d=4$, and ${S}_{N}=288$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 288 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 2+(N-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\ 288 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4+4N-4) \\\\ 288 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4N) \\\\ 288 & =2{N}^{2} \\\\ 144 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259108 13194139533864 true] [17592186259107 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259107 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259107 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259108 13194139533864 true] [17592186259107 1110 #uuid "6f33194a-533e-5d5f-aeea-98fb2111edad" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259108 1034 "12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259108 1111 #uuid "e4d4730e-784d-5184-9134-019f0cdedc08" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259109 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259110 13194139533864 true] [17592186259109 1089 "q-67177" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259109 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259109 1100 "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{1}=1$ and ${a}_{5}=3,$ where ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{5}$ are the first and fifth terms of the series, respectively. What is the sum of the first $13$ terms of the series?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259109 1113 #uuid "6a41ce80-b8e8-5930-a1cd-1406aa9952fe" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259109 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe know that ${a}_{5}=3.$ So, using the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}+(5-1)d \\\\ 3 & =1+4d \\\\ 4d & =2 \\\\ d & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=1$, $d=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $N=13$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{13} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(13-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\cdot (2+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =52\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259111 13194139533864 true] [17592186259110 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259110 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259110 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259111 13194139533864 true] [17592186259110 1110 #uuid "a9f1ca61-45bb-5af2-b0d1-664076cee0bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259111 1034 "52" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259111 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259111 1111 #uuid "ceaf749a-0891-5b69-b7ba-0e8416117672" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259112 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259113 13194139533864 true] [17592186259112 1089 "q-67183" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259112 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259112 1100 "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{4}=-11$ and ${a}_{11}=-39,$ where ${a}_{4}$ and ${a}_{11}$ are the fourth and eleventh terms of the series, respectively. \nFind the sum of the first $15$ terms of the series.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259112 1113 #uuid "32b30204-fdd2-5361-b71f-01c0c6f85c10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259112 1145 "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{11}-{a}_{4}}{11-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-39-(-11)}{11-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-28}{7} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, as ${a}_{4}=-11$, using the formula for the $n$th term, we can compute ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+(4-1)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ -11 & ={a}_{1}+(-12) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=1$, $d=-4$, and $N=15$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(15-1)\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (2-56) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (-54) \\\\  & =15\\cdot (-27) \\\\  & =-405\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259114 13194139533864 true] [17592186259113 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259113 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259113 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259114 13194139533864 true] [17592186259113 1110 #uuid "ec0376b2-78bc-5aa1-867c-d5690b732c26" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259114 1034 "-405" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259114 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259114 1111 #uuid "ae38c78a-c742-5fee-b66d-6fd8a1173707" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259115 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259116 13194139533864 true] [17592186259115 1089 "q-67217" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259115 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259115 1100 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n+1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259115 1113 #uuid "d7f6ddd3-7a07-53be-a65c-aca333beb0f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259115 1145 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $0,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{0+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1+1}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}}=\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}{\\frac{3}{4}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n+1} & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259116 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259117 13194139533864 true] [17592186259116 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259116 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259116 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259117 13194139533864 true] [17592186259116 1110 #uuid "d11d1acd-f605-5019-b2ec-43e4dae29f74" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259117 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259117 1111 #uuid "847298b9-a2bc-5e6e-95ca-5505365402af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259118 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259119 13194139533864 true] [17592186259118 1089 "q-67275" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259118 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259118 1100 "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $100$ and a third term equal to $4.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259118 1113 #uuid "cd3f6efc-8ad1-5ea4-8f11-4ecf2a1e15c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259118 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=100$ and ${a}_{3}=4.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{4}{100} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259119 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259120 13194139533864 true] [17592186259119 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259119 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259119 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259120 13194139533864 true] [17592186259119 1110 #uuid "7a617679-97a1-5278-90f7-755630e05862" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259120 1034 "125" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259120 1111 #uuid "2ba2240c-2cbb-5aaf-b814-9c23b8e87e9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259121 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259122 13194139533864 true] [17592186259121 1089 "q-67277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259121 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259121 1100 "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $36$ and a fourth term equal to $\\frac{4}{3}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259121 1113 #uuid "3c4f183b-d69c-5863-8872-aaadbc068516" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259121 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=36$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{4}{3}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{3})}{36} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259123 13194139533864 true] [17592186259122 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259122 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259122 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259123 13194139533864 true] [17592186259122 1110 #uuid "12a24b21-1e39-5e3a-b35e-388b6c84b984" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259123 1034 "54" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259123 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259123 1111 #uuid "ccff46d8-51f2-52c3-9d44-6f826b2af348" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259124 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259125 13194139533864 true] [17592186259124 1089 "q-67278" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259124 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259124 1100 "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $48$ and a fourth term equal to $\\frac{3}{4}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259124 1113 #uuid "bd4b8c3e-dfee-5ef6-85a6-3f80440979fd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259124 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=48$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{48} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259126 13194139533864 true] [17592186259125 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259125 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259125 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259126 13194139533864 true] [17592186259125 1110 #uuid "2615b029-78a2-5f37-bd7f-39a215cd4284" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259126 1034 "64" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259126 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259126 1111 #uuid "35f1fe2b-5643-5b0a-907c-3906ef26c821" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259127 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259128 13194139533864 true] [17592186259127 1089 "q-67279" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259127 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259127 1100 "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $35$ and a fourth term equal to $-\\frac{56}{25}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259127 1113 #uuid "2b352d28-1238-5c21-917e-ac85575e41a6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259127 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=35$ and ${a}_{4}=-\\frac{56}{25}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{56}{25})}{35} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =-\\frac{8}{125} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{3} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{(1-(-\\frac{2}{5}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{(1+\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{(\\frac{7}{5})} \\\\  & =35\\cdot \\frac{5}{7} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259128 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259129 13194139533864 true] [17592186259128 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259128 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259128 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259129 13194139533864 true] [17592186259128 1110 #uuid "d86636ef-8a10-5440-9ad0-7bc6b121987b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259129 1034 "25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259129 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259129 1111 #uuid "0ec14a23-1c72-554b-953c-a75bf3129a46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259130 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259131 13194139533864 true] [17592186259130 1089 "q-67304" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259130 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259130 1100 "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259130 1113 #uuid "8da21356-cf78-57bd-8a24-bca56a7d11af" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259130 1145 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{8}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{8}{125})}{(\\frac{4}{25})}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{25})}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{25})}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259131 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259132 13194139533864 true] [17592186259131 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259131 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259131 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259132 13194139533864 true] [17592186259131 1110 #uuid "892a22f9-897a-55b5-b400-54bbd725f0eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259132 1034 "\\frac{4}{15}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259132 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259132 1111 #uuid "399ca58c-1a93-5aa8-a3a1-30c792e828f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259133 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259134 13194139533864 true] [17592186259133 1089 "q-67348" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259133 1100 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ what is the index of the term $90?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259133 1113 #uuid "9ea5035a-bbb0-5b32-9e84-2a69e6c2ebe9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259133 1145 "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term ${a}_{1}.$\n\nSince we are given ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{4}}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{41-13}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{4} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=13$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+3(7) \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ -8 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-8+(n-1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=90,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =7n-15 \\\\ 90 & =7n-15 \\\\ 105 & =7n \\\\ \\frac{105}{7} & =n \\\\ 15 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $90$ is the $15$th term of the sequence." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259135 13194139533864 true] [17592186259134 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259134 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259134 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259135 13194139533864 true] [17592186259134 1110 #uuid "245df91c-45b7-5cee-88ee-1e72010dc86c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259135 1034 "15" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259135 1111 #uuid "de80f496-7a51-5689-ba57-9b7b068772a3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259136 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259137 13194139533864 true] [17592186259136 1089 "q-67439" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259136 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259136 1100 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{2}{x+3}=\\frac{8}{x-1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259136 1113 #uuid "d177fcdc-42ce-5f5f-82c6-0abf2f8ef727" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259136 1145 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x+3}{2}=\\frac{x-1}{8}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $8.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4x+12}{8}=\\frac{x-1}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)\\cdot \\frac{4x+12}{-8} & =(-8)\\cdot \\frac{x-1}{8} \\\\ 4x+12 & =-x+1 \\\\ 4x+x & =1-12 \\\\ 5x & =-11 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (5x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (-11) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})\\cdot (\\cancel{5}x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (-11) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259137 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259138 13194139533864 true] [17592186259137 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259137 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259137 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259138 13194139533864 true] [17592186259137 1110 #uuid "bace9fc5-2f38-5ab3-b01e-2f59a7d5e614" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259138 1034 "-\\frac{11}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259138 1111 #uuid "34c48104-4fe1-5365-a916-797f9ea4ad0a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259139 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259140 13194139533864 true] [17592186259139 1089 "q-67580" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259139 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259139 1100 "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}$, what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259139 1113 #uuid "818d4509-0f7c-512b-8135-c5e0fbca7d0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259139 1145 "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}})=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{8}{(-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (8) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-12 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259141 13194139533864 true] [17592186259140 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259140 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259140 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259141 13194139533864 true] [17592186259140 1110 #uuid "a5e97405-b554-57b0-ad9e-c3f78a6c8f79" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259141 1034 "-12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259141 1111 #uuid "57d42ae6-dec0-587f-8e65-a76bd0d3d23f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259142 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259143 13194139533864 true] [17592186259142 1089 "q-67696" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259142 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259142 1100 "Find ${lim}_{z\\to -1}\\frac{(z-2)^{4}}{(z+2)^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259142 1113 #uuid "93e1f776-dbc1-58f3-a68d-2ac0bb33599b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259142 1145 "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{z\\to -1}\\frac{(z-2)^{4}}{(z+2)^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{z\\to -1}(z-2)^{4}}{{lim}_{z\\to -1}(z+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({lim}_{z\\to -1}(z-2))}^{4}}{{({lim}_{z\\to -1}(z+2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1-2)^{4}}{(-1+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{1} \\\\  & =81\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259143 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259144 13194139533864 true] [17592186259143 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259143 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259143 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259144 13194139533864 true] [17592186259143 1110 #uuid "f7242dff-1d2d-5dcb-a2ec-c3039ce685d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259144 1034 "81" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259144 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259144 1111 #uuid "db6c0abe-be01-5a18-89bf-81ac56e02c61" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259145 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259146 13194139533864 true] [17592186259145 1089 "q-67700" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259145 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259145 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d81155f8c9b683e85a7f00ef41ac9ddcf36e303b40ca51c6de3e5d74833d5825.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259145 1113 #uuid "ddb68ee1-fc65-5b56-84b0-11e3e63cec4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259145 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=9.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3(3)-10)^{3} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3} \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9-3)^{2} \\\\  & ={6}^{2} \\\\  & =36\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(f(x)-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)(36) \\\\  & =-36\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259147 13194139533864 true] [17592186259146 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259146 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259146 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259147 13194139533864 true] [17592186259146 1110 #uuid "9079c108-3383-5bba-a4f3-e607d0cdd310" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259147 1034 "-36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259147 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259147 1111 #uuid "99c8788e-b678-5c57-9fef-cb3884fabb5e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259148 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259149 13194139533864 true] [17592186259148 1089 "q-67702" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259148 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259148 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1786cfa3ea3b08a97e59ec148a8fe710d6e4b11fc1cbc3582c4bf925a41bf19f.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\sqrt{17-2f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259148 1113 #uuid "f14e9302-b81e-5bd4-b3e0-dc1fe5b82277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259148 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=4.$ \n\nApplying the root rule, we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\sqrt{17-2f(x)} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}(17-2f(x))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17-2\\cdot (4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259150 13194139533864 true] [17592186259149 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259149 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259149 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259150 13194139533864 true] [17592186259149 1110 #uuid "369ba6c0-530d-5f3a-85c4-0d0286705012" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259150 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259150 1111 #uuid "249be7b5-1906-53fd-b666-f188a9dbdb3f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259151 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259152 13194139533864 true] [17592186259151 1089 "q-6832" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259151 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259151 1100 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}),\\,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0$ | $b$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $a$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $-a+b+c.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259151 1113 #uuid "bb6b00ad-d0da-51b8-947a-ae3a849534d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259151 1145 "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $y=\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\na=\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi (-3)}{3})=\\operatorname{cos}(-\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2})=\\operatorname{sin}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi (3)}{2})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n-a+b+c=-(-1)+(-1)+(-1)=-1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259153 13194139533864 true] [17592186259152 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259152 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259152 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259153 13194139533864 true] [17592186259152 1110 #uuid "757137c4-df81-5d0c-9722-1fd017aa21a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259153 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259153 1111 #uuid "d07a408d-61e2-5c18-ae4f-c310d6a3fc68" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259154 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259155 13194139533864 true] [17592186259154 1089 "q-68957" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259154 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259154 1100 "${(\\frac{6}{7})}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259154 1113 #uuid "4f339d72-643b-5603-8c4c-84752670bfae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259154 1145 "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}={6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3-8}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259156 13194139533864 true] [17592186259155 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259155 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259155 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259156 13194139533864 true] [17592186259155 1110 #uuid "920bc779-3731-5cf3-8bd3-d6fec7f0b5ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259156 1034 "{6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259156 1111 #uuid "05b55f04-7cd3-5516-801f-54d823312188" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259157 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259158 13194139533864 true] [17592186259157 1089 "q-69708" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259157 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259157 1100 "Find the eighth partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2n+3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259157 1113 #uuid "5a8ef49c-d0f0-5230-8a06-784e450e8567" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259157 1145 "Let ${a}_{n}=2n+3.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2(1)+3=5 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(2)+3=7 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2(3)+3=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we notice that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2$ and first term ${a}_{1}=5.$ \n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{8}=2(8)+3=19,$ we get\n\n$$\n{s}_{8}=\\frac{8}{2}(5+19)=96.\n$$\n\nSo the eighth partial sum of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2n+3)$ is equal to $96.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259158 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259159 13194139533864 true] [17592186259158 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259158 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259158 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259159 13194139533864 true] [17592186259158 1110 #uuid "3a5431de-10c0-57fb-86ad-1c754cd133cb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259159 1034 "96" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259159 1111 #uuid "144e8652-fa68-52dc-82e6-05edd1d9d46d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259160 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259161 13194139533864 true] [17592186259160 1089 "q-6979" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259160 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259160 1100 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+x}{1-{x}^{3}}.$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259160 1113 #uuid "f8e64f3e-acff-51b4-ab2e-e9c313440acf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259160 1145 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+x}{1-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(3+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{3+0+0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259162 13194139533864 true] [17592186259161 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259161 1041 "y" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259161 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259162 13194139533864 true] [17592186259161 1110 #uuid "ff98420d-303a-5998-a7ab-08711c468a4e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259162 1034 "-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259162 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259162 1111 #uuid "b08d7f70-e837-52b5-a04f-b94a8a4abbb0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259163 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259164 13194139533864 true] [17592186259163 1089 "q-69791" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259163 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259163 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{5}x?$\n\nI. - The domain is $x>1$\nII. - It has a maximum at $x=1$\nIII. - It passes through the point $(25,2)$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259163 1113 #uuid "22db072b-47e0-5531-be05-96bd3329aa8c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259163 1145 "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{5}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fd8c4765fa1b07d58a4d0b2e90a07383f5475883a2ada747ea6ae32e7a73808b.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, we see that the domain is $x>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain. Therefore, there are no maximum values.\n\n- Statement III is true. Substituting $x=25$ into the equation of the curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{5}(25) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}({5}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259165 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259166 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259167 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259168 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259169 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259170 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259171 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259172 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259169 13194139533864 true] [17592186259164 1110 #uuid "7b376239-9277-5a5a-b27c-bad904bb69b7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259165 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259165 1111 #uuid "75311fce-4feb-567e-b92c-49d719962b96" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259166 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259166 1111 #uuid "c402868e-47ed-50be-b9a7-189ca376d7a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259167 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259167 1111 #uuid "77a8eadb-a898-5c1c-ab62-f63855a24e95" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259168 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259168 1111 #uuid "40e36838-8b21-54b1-8762-5ad5ccff12d3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259169 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259169 1111 #uuid "d660b1d0-8f74-5e2a-95a1-a3d4bfbb381a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259170 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259170 1111 #uuid "722f8723-5569-5bf5-9c17-5140e64ebe19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259171 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259171 1111 #uuid "f7bd8e97-4a84-5829-9d91-27806062c05c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259172 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259172 1111 #uuid "5162764c-a93f-5b9a-bd1e-2e9c26b3a693" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259173 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259174 13194139533864 true] [17592186259173 1089 "q-69923" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259173 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259173 1100 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=-3t,\\,\\,\\,y=2t,\\,-5\\le t\\le 0,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $15$ | $12$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $a$ | $-6$ | $b$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b+c.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259173 1113 #uuid "97d679c9-1cde-5a51-924a-06ae37ba6ab4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259173 1145 "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-4$ into $y=2t.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=2(-4)=-8.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-2$ into $y=2t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=2(-2)-4.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=-3t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=-3(0)=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=-8-(-4)+0=-4.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259175 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259175 13194139533864 true] [17592186259174 1110 #uuid "29e41ae2-ae31-55b2-8633-b421b7bdd207" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259175 1034 "-4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259175 1111 #uuid "8fc224f3-876f-5816-892b-79717b173126" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259176 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259177 13194139533864 true] [17592186259176 1089 "q-70017" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259176 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259176 1100 "If ${log}_{3}(x-6)+5=8$, then $x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259176 1113 #uuid "513d3cec-982b-533e-81bb-8c54a8125de4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259176 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(x-6)+5 & =8 \\\\ {log}_{3}(x-6) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(x-6) & =3 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}(x-6)} & ={3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}(x-6)}=x-6.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6 & ={3}^{3} \\\\ x-6 & =27 \\\\ x & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259178 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259178 13194139533864 true] [17592186259177 1110 #uuid "b50d5e7f-236c-51dc-af70-45c7389804d0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259178 1034 "33" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259178 1111 #uuid "ef22657b-07c2-55e8-850e-53f5cacb390e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259179 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259180 13194139533864 true] [17592186259179 1089 "q-70018" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259179 1100 "If $6{log}_{4}(x+11)=12$, then $x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259179 1113 #uuid "6670ae0c-7bd3-5be8-8afd-7e9eadd30b6f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259179 1145 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{4}(x+11) & =12 \\\\ {log}_{4}(x+11) & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ {log}_{4}(x+11) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}(x+11) & =2 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}(x+11)} & ={4}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}(x+11)}=x+11.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+11 & ={4}^{2} \\\\ x+11 & =16 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259181 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259181 13194139533864 true] [17592186259180 1110 #uuid "7d747a78-d797-5920-a99b-727c7561455e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259181 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259181 1111 #uuid "02211a15-7355-5260-8fcb-254354108bd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259183 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1089 "q-70042" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1100 "Given $f(x)=x+3$ and $g(x)=-2x+1,$ find $(f+g)(2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1113 #uuid "08ea8cf2-f1c0-54d8-b1f4-c3ee612c64fc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259182 1145 "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f+g)(2),$ we find $f(2)$ and $g(2),$ and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(2)$ and $g(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =2+3=5 \\\\ g(2) & =-2(2)+1=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n(f+g)(2)=f(2)+g(2)=5+(-3)=2\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(x+3)+(-2x+1) \\\\  & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f+g)(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(2) & =-2+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259184 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259184 13194139533864 true] [17592186259183 1110 #uuid "1b1f79e3-52c1-5680-8033-9d1772967ff2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259184 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259184 1111 #uuid "2af7aef1-c83a-5f90-b617-ed5f9a36c70b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259186 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1089 "q-70044" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1100 "Given $f(x)=3-2x$ and $g(x)=x+6,$ find  $(f+g)(5).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1113 #uuid "1a8dcfa2-7a22-559e-8b4a-993daded6d09" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259185 1145 "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f+g)(5),$ we find $f(5)$ and $g(5),$ and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(5)$ and $g(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =3-2(5)=-7 \\\\ g(5) & =5+6=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n(f+g)(5)=f(5)+g(5)=(-7)+11=4\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(3-2x)+(x+6) \\\\  & =9-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f+g)(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(5) & =9-5=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259187 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259187 13194139533864 true] [17592186259186 1110 #uuid "c8818533-22cd-5f3d-895a-867d995e5be4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259187 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259187 1111 #uuid "0bf8de10-d41e-55cc-bf82-e50fe6e6a886" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259189 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1089 "q-70116" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1100 "Evaluate $\\frac{2(x-3)}{4-x}$ for $x=2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1113 #uuid "275243bf-578c-5ea8-b1eb-e4e03906be2e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259188 1145 "We replace $x$ with $2$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-3)}{4-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(2-3)}{4-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(-1)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}(-1)}{\\cancel{2}} & = \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259190 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259190 13194139533864 true] [17592186259189 1110 #uuid "cc207bd0-5db3-5bf4-999b-8a51dc1a7a3b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259190 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259190 1111 #uuid "35b1c28b-4cd8-5ae3-ae71-00d05daa2f29" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259192 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1089 "q-70210" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1100 "If you deposit $$2,000$ into an account with a $20%$ annual interest rate, compounded semi-annually, how many years will it take until there is at least $$4,000$ in the account?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1113 #uuid "cb756b6f-c9b7-51dc-82e2-024fa8b2534a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259191 1145 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded semi-annually, so $n=2.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=2,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.2.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 4,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ 2,000{(1+\\frac{0.2}{2})}^{2t} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ (1+0.1)^{2t} & \\ge \\frac{4,000}{2,000} \\\\ (1.1)^{2t} & \\ge 2 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({1.1}^{2t}) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{log}(1.1) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{log}(2)}{\\operatorname{log}(1.1)} \\\\ 2t & \\ge 7.272… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{7.272…}{2}\\approx 3.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember payments are compounded semi-annually, so we must choose either $3.5$ or $4$ years.\n\n- After $3.5$ years, there is still less than $$4,000$ in the account, because $3.5<3.64.$\n\n- After $4$ years, there will be more than $$4,000$ in the account, since $4>3.64.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $4$ years until there is at least $$4,000$ in the account." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259193 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259193 13194139533864 true] [17592186259192 1110 #uuid "2fc3f2cd-a3d6-5ac2-af86-a346aff71d9e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259193 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259193 1111 #uuid "b700da5e-ba2d-5a12-ab05-324fb4f0bdd5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259194 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259195 13194139533864 true] [17592186259194 1089 "q-70355" 1319