al to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only II states that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equation II has no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259268 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259269 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259270 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259271 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259272 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259273 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259274 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259275 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259275 13194139533864 true] [17592186259267 1110 #uuid "6ca60986-ae01-560b-84be-531cbd4f220f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259268 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259268 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259268 1111 #uuid "e55f859a-b44a-521e-8f69-f6fc35f05d6a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259269 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259269 1111 #uuid "b1ae8032-4f08-58e6-be0b-5418814cf3ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259270 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259270 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259270 1111 #uuid "775af3b1-5061-5125-8b7c-b29e2ad7c0f3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259271 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259271 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259271 1111 #uuid "821db7a8-8f56-556d-9121-149c04644e3b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259272 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259272 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259272 1111 #uuid "038d8bbc-19ca-5980-b57c-1bfecd77321c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259273 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259273 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259273 1111 #uuid "4f77c179-e69a-59bd-9aee-7e02ce2890e9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259274 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259274 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259274 1111 #uuid "22358ea3-e645-529e-a108-3a8fc0a9a7c7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259275 1111 #uuid "7550839f-4794-5b19-970d-ea559dd47bd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259277 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259279 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1089 "q-70913" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1100 "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1113 #uuid "90b1010c-b5bd-524a-86d1-a7ed6794e479" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259276 1145 "First, we isolate the square root by adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+6 & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-3(-2)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+6+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-3(5)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{25-15+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=-2$ and $x=5$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=5.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259277 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259278 13194139533864 true] [17592186259277 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259277 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259277 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259278 13194139533864 true] [17592186259277 1110 #uuid "bf3b6073-83d3-5a4b-be0c-891adaa49651" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259278 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259278 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259278 1111 #uuid "b547e8bd-ee5a-56dc-abe0-5665aab273f9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259280 13194139533864 true] [17592186259279 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259279 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259279 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259280 13194139533864 true] [17592186259279 1110 #uuid "6bf043e1-fb2b-52ea-83c7-e40ff55f2741" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259280 1111 #uuid "624dc58c-08a0-55d9-80aa-dc7d9874467a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259282 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1089 "q-71525" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1100 "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-x+3$ at the point $(1,4).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1113 #uuid "37f69159-8a56-5bc8-8f1b-0361b230c90b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259281 1145 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-x+3.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-x+3)=6{x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(1)=6(1)-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{'}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259283 13194139533864 true] [17592186259282 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259282 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259282 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259283 13194139533864 true] [17592186259282 1110 #uuid "42e70d83-930f-5340-bd0c-7bd247ddc733" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259283 1034 "-\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259283 1111 #uuid "32db2457-07c5-5427-8a92-9bd848a6b907" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259285 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259287 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1089 "q-71542" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1100 "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-4x+1$ at the point $(2,5)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1113 #uuid "a51e04ce-0c90-5337-b907-f8c65ca8414a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259284 1145 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-4x+1)=6x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=6(2)-4=8,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{1}{4}+5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 0+\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\frac{21}{4}).$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259286 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259286 13194139533864 true] [17592186259285 1110 #uuid "8baad589-7eab-517d-90c5-8bf0ac079d1a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259286 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259286 1111 #uuid "2e1eb68a-e252-5e78-a0ae-e38739c43457" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259287 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259288 13194139533864 true] [17592186259287 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259287 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259287 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259288 13194139533864 true] [17592186259287 1110 #uuid "d3e89ab2-4682-5c69-9be4-fd67450d1798" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1034 "\\frac{21}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259288 1111 #uuid "0d7aaa54-efcb-5abb-8a7e-be2121262538" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259290 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259292 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259294 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1089 "q-71778" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1100 "Solve the equation $5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1113 #uuid "56a901dc-9638-58ff-af9a-f87cff2b0a14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259289 1145 "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5z.$ We can factor out the $5z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z & =0 \\\\ 5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${z}^{2}-7z+10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0 \\\\ 5z(z-2)(z-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and $z=5.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259291 13194139533864 true] [17592186259290 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259290 1041 "root-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259290 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259291 13194139533864 true] [17592186259290 1110 #uuid "aa901033-8b8a-5397-a5eb-b35c081d2689" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259291 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259291 1111 #uuid "adee0ecc-80f7-537c-884d-c7171e95523e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259293 13194139533864 true] [17592186259292 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259292 1041 "root-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259292 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259293 13194139533864 true] [17592186259292 1110 #uuid "2113cfb0-dcde-5210-8cf3-60a45e92f9f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259293 1111 #uuid "d2bf68f7-8f17-5d48-a0c8-4753c3310bc5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259295 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1041 "root-3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259295 13194139533864 true] [17592186259294 1110 #uuid "228f0d78-7b41-5269-8680-1df663ae7c7c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259295 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259295 1111 #uuid "1e5ee175-51be-52ac-916b-773e369a6a66" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259297 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1089 "q-72064" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1100 "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $C(t),$ that arrive at a bank, measured over a period of $15$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C(t)\\,\\text{(customers/minute)}$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,15]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total number of customers served.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1113 #uuid "71771640-36aa-5365-9496-11f66f198e7d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259296 1145 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over $[0,15].$ In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA\\approx C(3)Δ{t}_{1}+C(7)Δ{t}_{2}+C(12)Δ{t}_{3}+C(15)Δ{t}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $C(t)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12+12+25+6 \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of customers served over a period of $15$ minutes. So, the total number of customers is approximately\n$55.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259298 13194139533864 true] [17592186259297 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259297 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259297 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259298 13194139533864 true] [17592186259297 1110 #uuid "f22aac6e-74b5-5d2e-aa51-0c181d3d7ce6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259298 1034 "55" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259298 1111 #uuid "eee1d4ba-d69b-5847-a859-94521b94f113" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259300 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1089 "q-72091" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1100 "A student's parents promise to reward her $R(p)$ dollars if she obtains an average of $p$ points in her exams. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nWrite the units in the form \"[output units] per [input unit]\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1113 #uuid "dbe3b6c2-cec5-5e7c-9393-5197f7a69152" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259299 1145 "We have that the student's reward $R(p)$ is measured in dollars and the average exam result is measured in points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(p): & \\text{dollars} \\\\ p: & \\text{points}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $R(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{points}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"dollars per point\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259300 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259301 13194139533864 true] [17592186259300 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259300 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259300 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259301 13194139533864 true] [17592186259300 1110 #uuid "3810100f-eb13-58ea-9c35-58a116899722" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259301 1034 "Dollars per point" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259301 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259301 1111 #uuid "79cd8330-ab97-5903-b9f3-ef508c00087b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259303 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1089 "q-72324" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1113 #uuid "65630b08-d1c1-5d45-aeb6-aa5a5348d31e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259302 1145 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})]^{'}}{[{x}^{3}]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{x\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{\\cancel{x}\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2(0)\\operatorname{cos}({0}^{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259304 13194139533864 true] [17592186259303 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259303 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259303 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259304 13194139533864 true] [17592186259303 1110 #uuid "d1d352a2-c4df-56d8-8d15-407181e1e893" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259304 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259304 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259304 1111 #uuid "2829294d-bbef-5146-a8fc-e708419389ca" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259306 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1089 "q-72340" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1113 #uuid "a4310b88-b219-51d1-b627-f9ad0abc215f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259305 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{csc}x]}^{'}}{[{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}\\cancel{(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259307 13194139533864 true] [17592186259306 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259306 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259306 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259307 13194139533864 true] [17592186259306 1110 #uuid "aafa4e81-2f31-5079-8a06-e3131206cf76" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259307 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259307 1111 #uuid "16f7c0ae-c727-596d-b5e9-acd5ce0e0abf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259309 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1089 "q-72361" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1100 "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ | ${f}^{''}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{'}(x)$ | ${g}^{''}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1113 #uuid "1b60af40-7b2a-56af-abba-7a8bf8f2cce4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259308 1145 "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)+1}{g(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(f(x)+x)^{'}}{(g(x)-x)^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1} & ={\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{'}(1)+1}{{g}^{'}(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({f}^{'}(x)+1)^{'}}{({g}^{'}(x)-1)^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{''}(x)}{{g}^{''}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=1,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{''}(x)}{{g}^{''}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{''}(x)}{{g}^{''}(x)}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{''}(1)}{{g}^{''}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}=-2.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259310 13194139533864 true] [17592186259309 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259309 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259309 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259310 13194139533864 true] [17592186259309 1110 #uuid "3231fccf-4ac4-5ca8-8af9-24e84e8cd6c4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259310 1034 "-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259310 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259310 1111 #uuid "ffe5f973-b330-576f-82fc-788f11da4e0b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259312 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1089 "q-72548" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1100 "The lines $2y+10x=3$ and $y=px-3$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1113 #uuid "0e3fd00d-e0e6-58dd-a905-2ad7d3579ca9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259311 1145 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The second line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=px-3$ is $p.$\n\n- To find the slope of  $2y+10x=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+10x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =-10x+3 \\\\ y & =-5x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $2y+10x=3$ is $-5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-5\\cdot p=-1\\,⟹\\,p=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259312 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259313 13194139533864 true] [17592186259312 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259312 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259312 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259313 13194139533864 true] [17592186259312 1110 #uuid "9d2fb2ba-1a63-50be-8a2f-06d6293b98a4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259313 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259313 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259313 1111 #uuid "f6489295-a743-5a1b-ba1c-3a1a21887946" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259315 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1089 "q-72754" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/38e096b8d57404135b7fe3244e50989e66021f94bb2275c56956d8663575b4f8.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1113 #uuid "8714b293-e1b5-58d0-b1e0-a6deda18c342" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259314 1145 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 23.908\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & =112\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 67.403\\,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =23.908\\,7+67.403\\,3 \\\\  & =91.311\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $91.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259316 13194139533864 true] [17592186259315 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259315 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259315 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259316 13194139533864 true] [17592186259315 1110 #uuid "6392d35b-90a6-526e-b49f-ded054099625" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259316 1034 "91" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259316 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259316 1111 #uuid "c97c84c3-c608-56b1-bf04-384879e31964" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259318 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1089 "q-73010" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-4x}{{x}^{2}-x-2}.$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1113 #uuid "87ee7e2c-2bbd-59c8-9b69-81310098b6e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259317 1145 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-4x}{{x}^{2}-x-2} & =\\frac{2x(x-2)}{(x-2)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+1=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259318 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259319 13194139533864 true] [17592186259318 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259318 1041 "x" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259318 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259319 13194139533864 true] [17592186259318 1110 #uuid "7180fa9b-38c7-591a-b8ac-d09b14278b51" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259319 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259319 1111 #uuid "09577c60-8054-58d0-be47-1caacaf6eec7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259321 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1089 "q-73017" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1113 #uuid "4b55051a-8f37-5733-840a-d51d5d26cfae" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259320 1145 "First, notice that $9!=9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!$ and $6!=6\\cdot 5!\\,.$ Then, we can cancel the $7!$ and $5!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!} & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!\\cdot 5!}{7!\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{7!}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{6} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259321 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259322 13194139533864 true] [17592186259321 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259321 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259321 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259322 13194139533864 true] [17592186259321 1110 #uuid "708fe7b0-f364-5757-a9a4-3709636c5143" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259322 1034 "12" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259322 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259322 1111 #uuid "cb5f0847-6dca-5e86-959a-5ce40230bdf2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259324 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1089 "q-73023" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1100 "Given that $n\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1113 #uuid "6c23c670-ac3c-5d23-92d6-e55e17344fd4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259323 1145 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+1)!=(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!} & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!}{(n-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot \\cancel{(n-1)!}}{\\cancel{(n-1)!}} \\\\  & =n(n+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259325 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259326 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259327 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259328 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259329 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259329 13194139533864 true] [17592186259324 1110 #uuid "72ddcd8c-501a-56c3-af42-4b7e20f51180" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1034 "n(n-1)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259325 1111 #uuid "bbcf8c96-eec1-510a-a0f6-919ab37fe0a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259326 1034 "(n-1)(n+1)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259326 1111 #uuid "65778dde-8bff-5b72-9171-0d6d29456876" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259327 1034 "{n}^{2}(n+1)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259327 1111 #uuid "a04b3d8d-8d0a-5983-9a83-ba594f0c251b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259328 1034 "n(n-2)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259328 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259328 1111 #uuid "9a789b7d-babf-5062-84ac-2e74a8f93df2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259329 1034 "n(n+1)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259329 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259329 1111 #uuid "b6d2e29e-e230-52d1-892f-59e17d8