"q-79249" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259524 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259524 1100 "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{3x+7}=-4$\nII. - $\\sqrt{5x-6}=2$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{4x-3}=-3$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259524 1113 #uuid "a1727d27-de04-53c4-a9de-9b0d34a8d70c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259524 1145 "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and III state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and III have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259526 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259527 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259528 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259529 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259530 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259531 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259532 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259533 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259528 13194139533864 true] [17592186259525 1110 #uuid "1f2b5810-9eef-5df4-8922-1d1b9118af75" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259526 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259526 1111 #uuid "e2230ade-0920-5220-9045-cd6d1c6a1d14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259527 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259527 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259527 1111 #uuid "126cee81-9a83-5954-83a2-22c702f4a5b6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259528 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259528 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259528 1111 #uuid "57308651-1460-57b4-a7d1-30b9fe7b42ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259529 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259529 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259529 1111 #uuid "2d4c9c70-57f5-5518-a8ed-9b7f71ff2227" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259530 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259530 1111 #uuid "00f59731-286b-54c8-86e4-a1b98f41bf30" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259531 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259531 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259531 1111 #uuid "7cd7cc04-afa2-5f31-b312-8df61565bf85" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259532 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259532 1111 #uuid "b700074f-ff81-5931-a4ec-44982358a62b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259533 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259533 1111 #uuid "15950900-69d0-5101-973e-12dc011fb487" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259535 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1089 "q-79380" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1100 "If ${log}_{9}(2y-3)=1$, then $y=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1113 #uuid "036e1450-c076-5d46-8a8b-161ca824e7b5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259534 1145 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $9,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}(2y-3) & =1 \\\\ {9}^{{log}_{9}(2y-3)} & ={9}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${9}^{{log}_{9}(2y-3)}=2y-3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =9 \\\\ 2y & =12 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259536 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259536 13194139533864 true] [17592186259535 1110 #uuid "7bb5e72b-9a4d-5593-8f5b-f183f0e6e8a7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259536 1034 "6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259536 1111 #uuid "90560ae3-56f4-52be-a0b1-acd9dd6e99b2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259538 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1089 "q-79389" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1100 "If $5{log}_{4}x+3{log}_{4}x=16$, then $x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1113 #uuid "085c7d1a-1e1a-5955-8d31-2be9a5ca85e3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259537 1145 "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{log}_{4}x+3{log}_{4}x & =16 \\\\ (5+3){log}_{4}x & =16 \\\\ 8{log}_{4}x & =16 \\\\ {log}_{4}x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}x & =2 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}x} & ={4}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={4}^{2} \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259539 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259539 13194139533864 true] [17592186259538 1110 #uuid "4a4a00ad-aa7d-55a1-b4fc-d55a24214956" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259539 1034 "16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259539 1111 #uuid "20548a01-2b86-54af-86e2-47785b72dd24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259541 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1089 "q-79540" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{x-3}{2x}+\\frac{3+2x}{2x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1113 #uuid "3c0f2338-2ab2-574c-954b-5e423f2531e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259540 1145 "We have a common denominator of $2x,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-3}{2x}+\\frac{3+2x}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{x-3+3+2x}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-3+3)+(x+2x)}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{3x}{2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259542 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259542 13194139533864 true] [17592186259541 1110 #uuid "cfa6f51a-4684-5e94-8b70-ee3994a3c1cb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259542 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259542 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259542 1111 #uuid "c5ea0012-4792-591a-9de7-6bbb67cc1e4c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259544 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1089 "q-80680" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1100 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3x+4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No vertical asymptotes\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1113 #uuid "94f25a88-826e-5f93-a9c5-c2932f446753" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259543 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nNote that the equation ${x}^{2}+3x+4=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =9-16 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259545 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259545 13194139533864 true] [17592186259544 1110 #uuid "878de882-ea49-5dfb-8a79-fdd6fee775dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259545 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259545 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259545 1111 #uuid "58eeb9bd-8760-51d2-b2d7-e15ea6490c9b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259547 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1089 "q-81074" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1100 "Given that  ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{(n+1)^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1113 #uuid "752a7942-16ed-5bb1-8e0f-a8b976101dd2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259546 1145 "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{(n+1)^{2}}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{(n+2)^{2}})}{(\\frac{5}{(n+1)^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(n+1)^{2}}{5(n+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)^{2}}{(n+2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n+1}{n+2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\frac{n+1}{n+2}<1,\n$$\n\nsince the numerator is always smaller than the denominator. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0<L<1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259548 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259549 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259550 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259551 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259552 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259553 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259554 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259555 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259550 13194139533864 true] [17592186259547 1110 #uuid "dfdad0fd-561d-5abc-9287-2caf6255bd83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259548 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259548 1111 #uuid "baac2114-91db-50b1-a268-6631f4f42b1b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259549 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259549 1111 #uuid "079f0eeb-7453-5945-9b06-9e1dad690e83" 13194139533