194139533864 true] [17592186259601 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259601 1100 "Find the root of $f(x)={log}_{5}(x-1)+1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259601 1113 #uuid "116feb8e-6c77-562d-8f5b-52579754920b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259601 1145 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}(x-1)+1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}(x-1)+1 & =0 \\\\ {log}_{5}(x-1) & =-1 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}(x-1)} & ={5}^{-1} \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{6}{5}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259603 13194139533864 true] [17592186259602 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259602 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259602 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259603 13194139533864 true] [17592186259602 1110 #uuid "5db17994-04e0-5902-9afc-c0a8af4f42ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1034 "\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259603 1111 #uuid "279dcb68-2aa0-5ed9-b074-fd704ed393a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259605 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1089 "q-82002" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1100 "Find the root of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1113 #uuid "55eb1db9-fbcd-5c61-8a62-80c4dc797847" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259604 1145 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x)=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(2-x)} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ 2-x & =1 \\\\ -x & =-1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259605 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259606 13194139533864 true] [17592186259605 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259605 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259605 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259606 13194139533864 true] [17592186259605 1110 #uuid "3a5771f8-f3ac-5bd9-b0b9-18e96060513c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259606 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259606 1111 #uuid "0f457522-0e79-5d09-af53-68b18678b277" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259607 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259608 13194139533864 true] [17592186259607 1089 "q-82003" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259607 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259607 1100 "Find the root of $f(x)=4{log}_{4}(-3x)+2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259607 1113 #uuid "01cf8919-1e7e-525e-9677-8521240a49d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259607 1145 "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n4{log}_{4}(-3x)+2=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{4}(-3x)+2 & =0 \\\\ 4{log}_{4}(-3x) & =-2 \\\\ {log}_{4}(-3x) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}(-3x)} & ={4}^{-1/2} \\\\ -3x & =\\frac{1}{{4}^{1/2}} \\\\ -3x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=-\\frac{1}{6}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259609 13194139533864 true] [17592186259608 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259608 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259608 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259609 13194139533864 true] [17592186259608 1110 #uuid "b5897f55-943d-5708-b19a-e23e4b99c567" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259609 1034 "-\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259609 1111 #uuid "906c9fff-edfb-53c0-a419-d46c09fde49c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259610 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259611 13194139533864 true] [17592186259610 1089 "q-83535" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259610 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259610 1100 "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $2(x+2)^{2}-3=0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259610 1113 #uuid "4b5fab31-45d0-51ce-8ef5-97f676669b2b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259610 1145 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)^{2}-3 & =2({x}^{2}+4x+{2}^{2})-3 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+8x+8-3 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+8x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have $a=2,$ $b=8,$ and $c=5.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={8}^{2}-4(2)(5) \\\\  & =64-40 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259611 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259612 13194139533864 true] [17592186259611 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259611 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259611 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259612 13194139533864 true] [17592186259611 1110 #uuid "a5336a8b-4ba5-5097-900d-ae962c807fe8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259612 1034 "24" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259612 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259612 1111 #uuid "8d30ae83-830f-59b8-b0c6-ad96496b8c60" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259613 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259614 13194139533864 true] [17592186259613 1089 "q-83537" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259613 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259613 1100 "Consider the equation $-{x}^{2}-2x+7=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259613 1113 #uuid "36f2b2bd-57ab-5e38-8592-4e4272a98493" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259613 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=7,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(-1)(7) \\\\  & =4+28 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(D>0),$ the equation has two distinct real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259614 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259615 13194139533864 true] [17592186259614 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259614 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259614 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259615 13194139533864 true] [17592186259614 1110 #uuid "be543a50-c5a0-5c72-b7ab-c5327fad3313" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259615 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259615 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259615 1111 #uuid "2d36712e-7eca-5b2f-a624-7f2244a6921f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259616 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259617 13194139533864 true] [17592186259616 1089 "q-83538" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259616 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259616 1100 "Consider the equation $-5{x}^{2}+2x-6=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259616 1113 #uuid "57953432-f314-5c58-a78f-a4a5625bcfcb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259616 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-5,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-6,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(-5)(-6) \\\\  & =4-120 \\\\  & =-116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is negative $(D<0),$ the equation has no real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259617 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259618 13194139533864 true] [17592186259617 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259617 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259617 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259618 13194139533864 true] [17592186259617 1110 #uuid "c51d8f76-2247-5bc3-9f3c-9466b39e3049" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259618 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259618 1111 #uuid "26273ade-5abc-59f8-b122-160e069e014e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259619 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259620 13194139533864 true] [17592186259619 1089 "q-83542" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259619 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259619 1100 "Consider the equation ${x}^{2}-4x+5=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259619 1113 #uuid "8708ad07-e7c1-511e-ad81-a624c1913f98" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259619 1145 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=1,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=5,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4)^{2}-4(1)(5) \\\\  & =16-20 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is negative $(D<0),$ the equation has no real solutions." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259620 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259621 13194139533864 true] [17592186259620 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259620 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259620 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259621 13194139533864 true] [17592186259620 1110 #uuid "9e953dff-9782-5a49-9384-c0b88716c9eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259621 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259621 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259621 1111 #uuid "ae8c871a-c889-5bb8-a4e3-84cbe582680f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259622 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259623 13194139533864 true] [17592186259622 1089 "q-83593" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259622 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259622 1100 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3{n}^{5}}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259622 1113 #uuid "59b096bf-4af9-5e57-941d-caa731cefc46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259622 1145 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n}=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\cdot {n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3{n}^{5}} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=5.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259624 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259625 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259626 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259627 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259628 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259629 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259630 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259631 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259625 13194139533864 true] [17592186259623 1110 #uuid "fa99ed83-7eac-5c53-a422-b00ad94c2731" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259624 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259624 1111 #uuid "6c79b06e-20b6-545a-bf3f-4ace475d5b58" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259625 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259625 1111 #uuid "8fabf757-29a7-5810-8ad6-bd173bea819e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259626 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259626 1111 #uuid "4c35feee-56b7-527a-9eee-d30c114b58ab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259627 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259627 1111 #uuid "b3815365-f016-5d77-9ce7-512dc1e1c99f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259628 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259628 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259628 1111 #uuid "cd21ecf6-cc48-56ea-bc16-ba8db7b8c72a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259629 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259629 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259629 1111 #uuid "906d8a92-c585-5a67-8fad-0f5a15fe64dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259630 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259630 1111 #uuid "d22f00a5-7b8e-54b3-958e-6974ad2ff725" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259631 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259631 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259631 1111 #uuid "ceb00b21-f4d3-516a-a25b-c63f9d245980" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259632 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259633 13194139533864 true] [17592186259632 1089 "q-83815" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259632 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259632 1100 "According to the rational roots theorem, What **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+x-3?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259632 1113 #uuid "a045cc6b-4cfa-5c4d-a695-16b0b2a1fa9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259632 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259634 13194139533864 true] [17592186259633 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259633 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259633 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259634 13194139533864 true] [17592186259633 1110 #uuid "f1aef993-e83d-5c8c-b93e-e98f5353e4cc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259634 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259634 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259634 1111 #uuid "30905c28-cb3e-546b-9dc8-5fce5d94c32e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259635 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259636 13194139533864 true] [17592186259635 1089 "q-83863" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259635 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259635 1100 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259635 1113 #uuid "c03b7d9a-1305-5730-b73a-a062c9cbaa05" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259635 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 4,\\pm 8\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ are $-4$ and $-2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-4$ | $12$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+4)({x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & =(x+4)(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-4,1,2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-4+1+2=-1.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259636 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259637 13194139533864 true] [17592186259636 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259636 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259636 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259637 13194139533864 true] [17592186259636 1110 #uuid "82ea7c62-6cec-5fea-9f67-89017e02bd87" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259637 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259637 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259637 1111 #uuid "ec0a9244-0323-5c50-9ce3-be04162b7b0e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259638 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259639 13194139533864 true] [17592186259638 1089 "q-84333" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259638 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259638 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5b3030b99501d2f3bc7dec80c4a1eab3754096c633be6d6d309064b084a8351d.png)\n\nIs the Venn diagram above correct?" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259638 1113 #uuid "ba0cd7e7-1913-5003-b315-15357e8d1f1f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259638 1145 "For any event $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X)+P({X}^{'})=1.\n$$\n\nThe Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=\\frac{5}{8}$ and $P({X}^{'})=\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nThese probabilities satisfy the rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X)+P({X}^{'}) & =\\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the above Venn diagram is  correct." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259640 13194139533864 true] [17592186259639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259641 13194139533864 true] [17592186259639 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259639 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259639 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259641 13194139533864 true] [17592186259639 1110 #uuid "51cfe37c-18ab-5778-81da-b1dc936be0f7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259640 1034 "No" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259640 1111 #uuid "70b0e3a8-4d90-5091-bb3a-1b048582423e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259641 1034 "Yes" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259641 1111 #uuid "9b232213-17cc-5460-8d11-a63369663d07" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259642 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259643 13194139533864 true] [17592186259642 1089 "q-84428" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259642 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259642 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f4928f6453f0f4d2e26dd99753e690de0b0266f63b541bf849c8fde5c13593d1.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P({X}^{'}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259642 1113 #uuid "e42f1d76-ec91-5e77-92f4-de1b8c4d9852" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259642 1145 "The Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=\\frac{7}{13}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}^{'}) & =1-P(X) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{7}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259643 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259644 13194139533864 true] [17592186259643 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259643 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259643 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259644 13194139533864 true] [17592186259643 1110 #uuid "2110b3f7-3f9a-50ba-ba22-2e0a49a6d572" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259644 1034 "\\frac{6}{13}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259644 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259644 1111 #uuid "1e1fbceb-950d-5d19-b414-6ec16db91287" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259645 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259646 13194139533864 true] [17592186259645 1089 "q-84431" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259645 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259645 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a5e4261d260faf531e7a0eecc95eed379009f238b99f6424e598ac42c27e7c64.png)\n\nConsider the sample space $S$ with the Venn diagram given above. What is $P({Y}^{'})?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259645 1113 #uuid "eb776e32-2017-5cbf-b5c4-b276faf266d9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259645 1145 "From the given Venn diagram, we have $P(Y)=3p$ and $P({Y}^{'})=8p.$\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y)+P({Y}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ 3p+8p & =1 \\\\ 11p & =1 \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $P({Y}^{'})=8p=\\frac{8}{11}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259647 13194139533864 true] [17592186259646 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259646 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259646 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259647 13194139533864 true] [17592186259646 1110 #uuid "9f1ed6f6-03cf-5262-bad9-889b0d7a9848" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259647 1034 "\\frac{8}{11}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259647 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259647 1111 #uuid "57cdb069-ec17-5a57-807b-c34261e4751b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259648 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259649 13194139533864 true] [17592186259648 1089 "q-84489" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259648 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259648 1100 "Two fair $6$-sided dice are rolled. Define $A$ to be the event \"the sum of outcomes is equal to $5.$\" Which of the following statements are true regarding this event?\n\nI. - $P(A)=0.15$ to $2$ decimal places\nII. - ${A}^{'}$ is the event \"the sum of the outcomes is less than $5$\"\nIII. - $P({A}^{'})=0.89$ to $2$ decimal places" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259648 1113 #uuid "bbd53da4-9111-5ade-a6dc-05cf5e290645" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259648 1145 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are $36$ possible outcomes and $4$ of these have the sum of the outcomes equal to $5,$ namely $(1,4),(4,1),(2,3),(3,2).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{4}{36}=0.11\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false. The complement of $A$ is the event ${A}^{'},$ \"the sum of the outcomes is not equal to $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A)=0.11$ to $2$ decimal places, we can calculate $P({A}^{'})$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.11 \\\\  & =0.89.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259650 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259651 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259652 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259653 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259654 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259655 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259656 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259657 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259654 13194139533864 true] [17592186259649 1110 #uuid "5749a1ac-dbc4-5b78-8fcc-ffe805a26b61" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259650 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259650 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259650 1111 #uuid "1812c78b-a55b-57c3-832c-8eaffa65f449" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259651 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259651 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259651 1111 #uuid "6de1e2ae-c9eb-5c1a-af4b-7ad5418ce020" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259652 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259652 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259652 1111 #uuid "dbb099e6-0299-5d14-85a1-e2f505b0f3c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259653 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259653 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259653 1111 #uuid "9429ffb0-d529-544d-8017-1e51bef97808" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259654 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259654 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259654 1111 #uuid "5ba7c15d-d3cb-59aa-9e04-f73793693b28" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259655 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259655 1111 #uuid "e82f6fdf-cd6a-518c-bf93-5bcfbd58572d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259656 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259656 1111 #uuid "f53f81a0-20c4-5d62-822d-d15e0208bb72" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259657 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259657 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259657 1111 #uuid "616f6e0e-482f-510b-b573-5c356d87d0f5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259658 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259659 13194139533864 true] [17592186259658 1089 "q-85485" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259658 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259658 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi .$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259658 1113 #uuid "813cae57-00fa-52d6-9195-91359bae21ec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259658 1145 "The angle $\\pi $ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi =0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259660 13194139533864 true] [17592186259659 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259659 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259659 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259660 13194139533864 true] [17592186259659 1110 #uuid "2f9e6d61-b9c3-5e20-84ad-4f9374daaa4f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259660 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259660 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259660 1111 #uuid "9713ae01-932f-53c2-8604-d374f44821b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259661 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259662 13194139533864 true] [17592186259661 1089 "q-85486" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259661 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259661 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}({90}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259661 1113 #uuid "aa9617ac-766d-5c56-b9e5-48a4a947fb5a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259661 1145 "The angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}({90}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{tan}({90}^{∘})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259662 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259663 13194139533864 true] [17592186259662 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259662 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259662 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259663 13194139533864 true] [17592186259662 1110 #uuid "dc95bd45-30c4-5602-8cc2-a03cbae23f68" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259663 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259663 1111 #uuid "f1176aca-177b-56ab-8497-2f78a7b72522" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259664 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259665 13194139533864 true] [17592186259664 1089 "q-85492" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259664 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259664 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{cot}({90}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259664 1113 #uuid "b5e13a82-d333-5782-b422-ea795b8fd432" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259664 1145 "The angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}({90}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}({90}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{0}{1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}({90}^{∘})=0.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259665 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259666 13194139533864 true] [17592186259665 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259665 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259665 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259666 13194139533864 true] [17592186259665 1110 #uuid "319e1acb-cd6d-5be6-8a29-1e555e601ba7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259666 1034 "0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259666 1111 #uuid "18c427b6-038d-52cc-af20-b4aa4105e7cd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259667 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259668 13194139533864 true] [17592186259667 1089 "q-85495" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259667 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259667 1100 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{csc}({180}^{∘}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259667 1113 #uuid "2767b2a5-ba70-56dd-aba5-63a5e516b4eb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259667 1145 "The angle ${180}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({180}^{∘})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}({180}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{csc}({180}^{∘})$ is undefined." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259669 13194139533864 true] [17592186259668 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259668 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259668 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259669 13194139533864 true] [17592186259668 1110 #uuid "b96f27ba-03a4-5459-be66-b937edfcfc70" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259669 1034 "Undefined" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259669 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259669 1111 #uuid "b9928cb5-81c9-5428-bf72-d7e1de225401" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259670 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259671 13194139533864 true] [17592186259670 1089 "q-85514" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259670 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259670 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259670 1113 #uuid "dc09cb6b-cb50-55e8-8667-d714b407a48f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259670 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${30}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259672 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259672 13194139533864 true] [17592186259671 1110 #uuid "000dbb85-f45d-5ae1-a493-4f7966ffcde0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259672 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259672 1111 #uuid "434ec09f-ccb5-5c0a-8667-6647f9cb6dac" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259674 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1089 "q-86424" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1100 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $f(2)=5$ and $f(4)=23$, what is the index of the term $158?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1113 #uuid "2b2a5aa3-5b37-5fc1-a953-21f39ffa3a31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259673 1145 "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term $f(1).$\n\nSince we are given $f(2)=5$ and $f(4)=23,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{f(4)-f(2)}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23-5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(2)=5$ is $1$ jump after $f(1),$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(1)+d \\\\ 5 & =f(1)+9 \\\\ -4 & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-4+(n-1)(9) \\\\  & =-4+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $f(n)=158,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =9n-13 \\\\ 158 & =9n-13 \\\\ 171 & =9n \\\\ \\frac{171}{9} & =n \\\\ 19 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $158$ is the $19$th term of the sequence." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259674 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259675 13194139533864 true] [17592186259674 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259674 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259674 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259675 13194139533864 true] [17592186259674 1110 #uuid "4abfcb06-3ae1-5b51-bf96-dd3c3c9a2972" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259675 1034 "19" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259675 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259675 1111 #uuid "8683c5c9-de60-5c5a-b120-a61da703cf14" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259676 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259677 13194139533864 true] [17592186259676 1089 "q-87562" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259676 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259676 1100 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $9$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259676 1113 #uuid "7dfa7943-f9fd-587a-96e7-82a3ae160d16" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259676 1145 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259678 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259678 13194139533864 true] [17592186259677 1110 #uuid "b5dbac9f-85f4-59c4-8a53-10bde3633c1d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259678 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259678 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259678 1111 #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259679 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259680 13194139533864 true] [17592186259679 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259682 13194139533864 true] [17592186259679 1089 "q-88205" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259679 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259679 1100 "A stretch of factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and invariant $x$-axis is applied to the point $(5,-6).$  What is the resulting point?\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259679 1113 #uuid "76d5aa8d-6b7f-5a59-a437-5e21bbd91bff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259679 1145 "Applying a stretch of factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and invariant $x$-axis to a point means that the $y$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,-\\frac{1}{2}y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is\n\n$$\n(5,-6)↦(5,-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6))=(5,3).\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/76787875dff29fa140881c6de2ee0d7c08baf394994e31246204d21bbcf61f56.png)" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259680 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259681 13194139533864 true] [17592186259680 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259680 1041 "coordinate-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259680 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259681 13194139533864 true] [17592186259680 1110 #uuid "a4523332-86c1-57a4-af85-58822a8ad619" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259681 1034 "5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259681 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259681 1111 #uuid "58d8976a-8d6a-571f-aa3c-4dfb863d3bb8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259682 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259683 13194139533864 true] [17592186259682 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259682 1041 "coordinate-2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259682 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259683 13194139533864 true] [17592186259682 1110 #uuid "52a41c4b-0097-56b8-a60f-6552b9d773bb" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259683 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259683 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259683 1111 #uuid "2fcfc92c-4860-5df6-b3a0-3c09ec63b05a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259684 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259685 13194139533864 true] [17592186259684 1089 "q-90623" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259684 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259684 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/632da466eb26480490a49f9c5a71e0d6f95653efa749a8f117d81e016fd969ae.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\nII. - $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=3$\nIII. - $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=1$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259684 1113 #uuid "029317bc-fcf6-501c-9125-ecc74c217368" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259684 1145 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(4).$ For example, $f(1)>f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function does *not* have a global minimum at $x=3.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is smaller than $f(3).$ For example, $f(6)<f(3).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=1.$ For example, in the interval $(0.5,1.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (0.5,1.5).$ \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259686 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259687 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259688 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259689 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259690 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259691 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259692 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259693 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259690 13194139533864 true] [17592186259685 1110 #uuid "1396d91b-da49-5297-9871-ffd0d3227bab" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259686 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259686 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259686 1111 #uuid "2ab74f05-3afc-5968-87f4-be4d044e94c5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259687 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259687 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259687 1111 #uuid "e6b21ac8-297f-5559-8890-905d5525ed71" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259688 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259688 1111 #uuid "2de2d434-323b-5bd0-88c3-4a9168bb5e3d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259689 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259689 1111 #uuid "e35d3b70-777c-576e-9426-bd095fa9b141" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259690 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259690 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259690 1111 #uuid "8f387637-8b17-53e2-9637-872e8c44aabf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259691 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259691 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259691 1111 #uuid "8b88f3ab-2919-5f84-8aa0-b48c86a90793" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259692 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259692 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259692 1111 #uuid "57b09224-30af-51ef-b156-ee01bb809d0a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259693 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259693 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259693 1111 #uuid "6be2d733-af51-531c-a6ae-fb3b8f9f6ff2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259694 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259695 13194139533864 true] [17592186259694 1089 "q-914" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259694 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259694 1100 "If $\\operatorname{log}y=3$, then $y=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259694 1113 #uuid "3f443508-b423-5aae-b226-2edd43a6873e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259694 1145 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the common logarithm is $10,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}y & =3 \\\\ {log}_{10}y & =3 \\\\ {10}^{{log}_{10}y} & ={10}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${10}^{{log}_{10}y}=y.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={10}^{3} \\\\ y & =1\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259695 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259696 13194139533864 true] [17592186259695 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259695 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259695 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259696 13194139533864 true] [17592186259695 1110 #uuid "37ba8d5d-39aa-537d-aaed-64ef93471dd2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259696 1034 "1\\,000" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259696 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259696 1111 #uuid "64a61867-194c-5c5b-8079-5ef39b6bec38" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259697 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259698 13194139533864 true] [17592186259697 1089 "q-9293" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259697 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259697 1100 "If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=0.5,\\,$ ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}=9$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}=-2,$ then calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259697 1113 #uuid "770c1ff4-046b-5d4d-9ce0-c706d161310b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259697 1145 "Taking the constant factors outside of the summation signs gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{b}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{c}_{n} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}+8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n} \\\\  & =3(0.5)+2(9)+8(-2) \\\\  & =1.5+18-16 \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259699 13194139533864 true] [17592186259698 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259698 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259698 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259699 13194139533864 true] [17592186259698 1110 #uuid "078bc752-e359-55d9-ac87-b5b28ffbedb8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259699 1034 "3.5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259699 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259699 1111