n-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d03c7a1ada2f422df96b8479894197e5d388f73e1f7980155a009b6502902cbb.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=-1$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259704 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259705 13194139533864 true] [17592186259704 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259704 1041 "y" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259704 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259705 13194139533864 true] [17592186259704 1110 #uuid "5bb8367a-3808-5ada-bad9-70a6c5853a45" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259705 1034 "-1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259705 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259705 1111 #uuid "686dcbf3-9915-5d87-a73c-2fe61d91472e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259706 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259707 13194139533864 true] [17592186259706 1089 "q-92985" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259706 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259706 1100 "Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-3}+4.$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259706 1113 #uuid "f44d5537-7cca-5193-b04b-aab03731cb25" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259706 1145 "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/47d570c5b05096a0e8b7a13df510d8cd71dbee93dd2bf58dae18d7ff49ff1787.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=4$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=4.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259708 13194139533864 true] [17592186259707 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259707 1041 "y" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259707 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259708 13194139533864 true] [17592186259707 1110 #uuid "5c0b24f9-bb9d-54de-beac-975884c53c1c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259708 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259708 1111 #uuid "4b93ea71-7aef-56db-9a46-7a8bf77de11e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259709 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259710 13194139533864 true] [17592186259709 1089 "q-92990" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259709 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259709 1100 "Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{2x+1})+3.$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259709 1113 #uuid "8e2b0b19-242a-586c-83d9-b9a00d75b930" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259709 1145 "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/7df3ad89847be8197653ece86935a3e310d66a7632143e5e5218bf489f1c23d4.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=3$ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=3.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259711 13194139533864 true] [17592186259710 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259710 1041 "y" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259710 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259711 13194139533864 true] [17592186259710 1110 #uuid "692c0975-83dd-5e12-b8d5-3f74e2e8e1e1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259711 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259711 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259711 1111 #uuid "f22c16fc-f6c1-5051-834f-8ea7f4851607" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259712 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259713 13194139533864 true] [17592186259712 1089 "q-93100" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259712 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259712 1100 "Find the root of $f(x)=3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13}-6.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259712 1113 #uuid "915a85d2-04ba-5d69-9940-b94e85fbc1dd" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259712 1145 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13}-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13}-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt[6]{3x+13} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{3x+13} & =2 \\\\ (\\sqrt[6]{3x+13})^{6} & ={2}^{6} \\\\ 3x+13 & =64 \\\\ 3x & =51 \\\\ x & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[6]{3\\cdot 17+13}-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt[6]{64}-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2-6 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=17.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259713 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259714 13194139533864 true] [17592186259713 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259713 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259713 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259714 13194139533864 true] [17592186259713 1110 #uuid "f665c510-5191-5119-81c5-8160fe67a91d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259714 1034 "17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259714 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259714 1111 #uuid "0753cec7-4d3b-5556-880e-87974e8a09cf" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259715 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259716 13194139533864 true] [17592186259715 1089 "q-9331" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259715 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259715 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[6]{{n}^{5}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259715 1113 #uuid "6f9c004d-c216-5608-ad21-162c3e1dd45a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259715 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{6}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nNote that the factor of $4$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259716 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259717 13194139533864 true] [17592186259716 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259716 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259716 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259717 13194139533864 true] [17592186259716 1110 #uuid "1b342020-669f-5e04-8b85-dfad9597da56" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259717 1034 "4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259717 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259717 1111 #uuid "333eb865-1aff-5b94-a5c8-384293d669d8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259718 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259719 13194139533864 true] [17592186259718 1089 "q-9332" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259718 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259718 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[8]{{n}^{7}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259718 1113 #uuid "e09514d4-1e05-5fb3-ba5a-f0e56cf060c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259718 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{7/8}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{7}{8}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259720 13194139533864 true] [17592186259719 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259719 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259719 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259720 13194139533864 true] [17592186259719 1110 #uuid "801ab594-17cd-508c-b3db-45ed4c7ea1d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259720 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259720 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259720 1111 #uuid "ce94ff1f-e592-5911-a4d8-01d6f5dd50dc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259721 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259722 13194139533864 true] [17592186259721 1089 "q-9334" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259721 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259721 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259721 1113 #uuid "47306eda-69a1-5c4c-86bf-6d6e220f28a9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259721 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $3$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259723 13194139533864 true] [17592186259722 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259722 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259722 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259723 13194139533864 true] [17592186259722 1110 #uuid "bccb7fea-91f1-5488-9cfc-3f870d14529a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259723 1034 "3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259723 1111 #uuid "f434f7ee-2d39-5a22-9c70-41bd16dc432f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259724 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259725 13194139533864 true] [17592186259724 1089 "q-9337" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259724 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259724 1100 "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{4}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259724 1113 #uuid "b19142ea-49a5-5749-b0b5-ee3907a47a2f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259724 1145 "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4/3}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{4}{3}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $2$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259725 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259726 13194139533864 true] [17592186259725 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259725 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259725 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259726 13194139533864 true] [17592186259725 1110 #uuid "df143856-fb55-5527-a503-a0ef018edd46" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259726 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259726 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259726 1111 #uuid "a5a9a0ac-ec3f-5328-b510-e2373b85173e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259727 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259728 13194139533864 true] [17592186259727 1089 "q-956" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259727 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259727 1100 "Calculate ${log}_{8}(32)$ without using a calculator.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259727 1113 #uuid "04859e81-899a-55b9-b72c-cdf329ab2df5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259727 1145 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ and $X=32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}(32) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}(32)}{{log}_{2}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}({2}^{5})}{{log}_{2}({2}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259729 13194139533864 true] [17592186259728 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259728 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259728 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259729 13194139533864 true] [17592186259728 1110 #uuid "52b95d39-7d74-5974-8d4e-39079c7d9ed6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259729 1034 "\\frac{5}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259729 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259729 1111 #uuid "27b0bc67-9e69-591a-af43-9f9d6f672b43" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259730 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259731 13194139533864 true] [17592186259730 1089 "q-96669" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259730 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259730 1100 "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{‴}+(\\operatorname{sin}x){y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$\nII. - $5{y}^{''}{y}^{'}+3xy=4{y}^{'}$\nIII. - ${({y}^{'})}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=y$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259730 1113 #uuid "e356353f-6fc7-5ad8-bdec-cdea5919a2a0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259730 1145 "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259732 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259733 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259734 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259735 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259736 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259737 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259738 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259739 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259736 13194139533864 true] [17592186259731 1110 #uuid "6f94aaaa-9bc1-5a1d-a06c-4a240f08215e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259732 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259732 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259732 1111 #uuid "c090be31-e0fe-53b2-9454-19182ededf52" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259733 