is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259752 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259753 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259754 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259755 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259756 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259757 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259758 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259759 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259756 13194139533864 true] [17592186259751 1110 #uuid "80ce5838-d2f1-5bb7-a228-f73d437d0ff4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259752 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259752 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259752 1111 #uuid "31ec9ae4-b0d6-5be9-bc8c-de29f3265f4d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259753 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259753 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259753 1111 #uuid "fa6ea8d3-f89e-5586-84d6-4f2fb5198b6c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259754 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259754 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259754 1111 #uuid "a953b4c7-4ee6-5bc0-a8ac-ed73d3147732" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259755 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259755 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259755 1111 #uuid "2f42e654-6c96-54e8-b757-9b1eb76c5697" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259756 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259756 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259756 1111 #uuid "54293858-1d79-5dce-bab3-925acb13dc67" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259757 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259757 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259757 1111 #uuid "34a68151-c242-525f-a5da-8df577aa7e20" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259758 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259758 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259758 1111 #uuid "9dffe7bf-50cd-5aa0-8f09-7c1dc592865a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259759 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259759 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259759 1111 #uuid "112dd4e2-d13b-5fc2-92ec-691c04cd9869" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259760 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259761 13194139533864 true] [17592186259760 1089 "q-96702" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259760 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259760 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x={e}^{y}$\nII. - $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2=\\sqrt{y}$\nIII. - $4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y}=5y$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259760 1113 #uuid "6d484918-7854-547b-b753-257beeb0c7d2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259760 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{y}+x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={e}^{y}+x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259762 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259763 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259764 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259765 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259766 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259767 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259768 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259769 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259769 13194139533864 true] [17592186259761 1110 #uuid "0f7fab41-ea7c-53fd-85ac-6541129d2e5f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259762 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259762 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259762 1111 #uuid "af8323ab-ddb1-5f48-a9f7-f5692ae339da" 13194139533864 true] [1759218