" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259770 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259772 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259773 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259774 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259775 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259776 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259777 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259778 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259779 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259771 104