80 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259780 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\nII. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}y$\nIII. - $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259780 1113 #uuid "2f8c44a4-8695-5bce-9b85-93159ef8f779" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259780 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259782 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259783 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259784 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259785 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259786 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259787 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259788 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259789 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259785 13194139533864 true] [17592186259781 1110 #uuid "b143ce9c-1d2f-54f8-9822-2eb13785f2e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259782 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259782 1111 #uuid "b74d4121-19bd-519b-9f42-6e2d74a4e53e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259783 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259783 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259783 1111 #uuid "974d493e-45f3-5112-9946-af67f80df4aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259784 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259784 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259784 1111 #uuid "aae8832f-c0df-5015-9905-eda5a0e98849" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259785 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259785 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259785 1111 #uuid "8784430a-fefb-5a0e-8037-7072e097a072" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259786 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259786 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259786 1111 #uuid "e76fcc33-5ba1-50a2-b5ef-7bdb0d465af6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259787 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259787 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259787 1111 #uuid "59f18cbc-4413-5cee-a2a2-0a7277b9cd1c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259788 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259788 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259788 1111 #uuid "710af5b4-256e-512a-a5d1-86c2b6361f9a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259789 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259789 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259789 1111 #uuid "b70c79e3-f5b6-5da6-92fe-75d7cf1ec1e5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259790 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259791 13194139533864 true] [17592186259790 1089 "q-96712" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259790 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259790 1100 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{'}=1-\\sqrt{y}$\nII. - ${y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y={e}^{2}$\nIII. - $x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y}=4$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259790 1113 #uuid "a6f5f12c-8742-57e1-8431-b7e5f7c8b33f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259790 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{4y+1}{xy} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y+1}{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259792 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259793 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259794 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259795 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259796 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259797 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259798 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259799 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259796 13194139533864 true] [17592186259791 1110 #uuid "831ff5c2-b840-5fe4-b6ea-ec995d5e6729" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259792 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259792 1111 #uuid "60ed9cbb-4ba4-51aa-a0f8-7225e1688c36" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259793 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259793 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259793 1111 #uuid "31036b5b-7449-5599-8b7f-8b39b778f239" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259794 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259794 1111 #uuid "be9955de-2d2b-52bd-ad19-a9c11284301d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259795 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259795 1111 #uuid "c053f2d6-9364-55b3-954f-91552728a08d" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259796 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259796 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259796 1111 #uuid "8163967d-e6d8-56ce-9dca-756d234c3020" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259797 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259797 1111 #uuid "11bda8cf-ada4-544f-8e86-30b984916eaa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259798 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259798 1111 #uuid "f1a652c4-ff96-5e6d-b026-86d6eed744a5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259799 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259799 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259799 1111 #uuid "9e078445-c2fc-5a31-88ab-c2be941bd0f4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259801 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1089 "q-96987" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - ${x}^{2}{y}^{''}-y=2x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(1)=3$\nII. - $x{y}^{‴}-{e}^{x}{y}^{'}=9y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(0)=1,\\,{y}^{''}(0)=2$\nIII. - $2{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{y},\\,y(0)=1$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1113 #uuid "8ed0d022-e8a6-5277-8016-371d657756f9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259800 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{''}-y=2x$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(1)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $x{y}^{‴}-{e}^{x}{y}^{'}=9y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(0)=1,$ and ${y}^{''}(0)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259802 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259803 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259804 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259805 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259806 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259807 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259808 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259809 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259806 13194139533864 true] [17592186259801 1110 #uuid "939e9ad9-80a2-515c-913c-6d8916123f54" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259802 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259802 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259802 1111 #uuid "debfa9e3-f0d6-59a8-bd1a-a93ddedec8c1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259803 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259803 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259803 1111 #uuid "d5192a67-97b2-58a1-918c-4a7ae001f7db" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259804 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259804 1111 #uuid "59b7ad7c-c2a0-5250-8870-d1c274c642d1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259805 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259805 1111 #uuid "755b967b-454d-5af1-a6d7-4423160433e4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259806 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259806 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259806 1111 #uuid "7750975c-75a1-5e0e-b7af-140759e7ca53" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259807 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259807 1111 #uuid "1e4e9f1f-b4a5-52d2-a38c-f17ed129eff4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259808 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259808 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259808 1111 #uuid "e36f6f5b-7422-5092-a409-609d64e0adf7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259809 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259809 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259809 1111 #uuid "425d8c3b-9b21-5902-964b-634b329711ed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259810 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259811 13194139533864 true] [17592186259810 1089 "q-96992" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259810 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259810 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\nII. - $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\nIII. - ${y}^{'}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259810 1113 #uuid "f324e71f-0765-5434-b640-0e2263e787be" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259810 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259812 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259813 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259814 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259815 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259816 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259817 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259818 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259819 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1041 "selection" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259817 13194139533864 true] [17592186259811 1110 #uuid "36b80bee-db64-535d-aace-058b279199ff" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259812 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259812 1111 #uuid "491c18dc-e9d5-5a2b-88f5-a3bc86a065ea" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259813 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259813 1111 #uuid "6cee0851-d168-5175-af4b-4e9ec6593fd0" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259814 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259814 1111 #uuid "e20c0aff-74cc-552d-96eb-eef3a81e96ec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259815 1034 "I only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259815 1111 #uuid "f0377b83-e245-52ea-80a0-298ddfe36899" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259816 1034 "III only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259816 1111 #uuid "c6bcccdb-85b9-52d9-b7ca-5d988b9f7fb5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259817 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259817 1111 #uuid "070e48b3-a56e-5c40-9343-941d4858140e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259818 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259818 1111 #uuid "40d7f3bf-ff28-581c-96a7-f1b98edbccd3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259819 1034 "II only" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533864 true] [17592186259819 1111 #uuid "f1223c20-84d2-5fb5-bf8a-545a08f3d710" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259821 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1089 "q-97980" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-1\n$$\n\n \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=b\\pi +6n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $b?$\n\n$b =$: {{b}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1113 #uuid "70cc7789-9f93-5c83-af83-334149cbeb2c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259820 1145 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $b=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259822 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1041 "b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259822 13194139533864 true] [17592186259821 1110 #uuid "ec1a2131-1d28-5ffa-8e30-33f1d353ae90" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259822 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259822 1111 #uuid "7ec7bfd3-8f80-5b87-aa75-1ec8afd1b0ec" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259824 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1089 "q-98" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1100 "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{12}(23)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1113 #uuid "4bb0c190-0bc7-5c24-bdb9-2ae5232df4b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259823 1145 "Let's calculate ${log}_{12}(23)$ by changing the base $12$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=12,$ $X=23,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}(23) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}(23)}{{log}_{10}(12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(23)}{\\operatorname{log}(12)} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1.361\\,728}{1.079\\,181} \\\\  & \\approx 1.262\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259825 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259825 13194139533864 true] [17592186259824 1110 #uuid "546450ba-f0fc-5ac8-a380-753b82559f9e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259825 1034 "1.262" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259825 1111 #uuid "6310d092-8863-5a48-bb29-8e40b3e3e2f2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259827 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1089 "q-98030" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1100 "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(x-{20}^{∘})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx={50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x={a}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. What could be the value of $a?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1113 #uuid "579b5dc7-11ff-5357-9fa6-16fce5adbb8f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259826 1145 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-{20}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nx-{20}^{∘}={30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x-{20}^{∘}={330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x={20}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x={20}^{∘}+{330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x={50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x={350}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=350.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259828 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259828 13194139533864 true] [17592186259827 1110 #uuid "c5bb9771-544b-5e26-b5f1-fe053e541d83" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259828 1034 "350" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259828 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259828 1111 #uuid "c1013782-3d79-51a2-9739-3f2e166b9f97" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259830 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1089 "q-98152" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4b445c27e9b4b720cfcc8b8b265485ba4f62106be39acc066cf041bf9db073b2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=k{log}_{3}(bx).$ Find the value of $b+k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1113 #uuid "6efc25c1-7280-5213-84a4-9dd424baaae7" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259829 1145 "Clearly, $b\\ne 0.$ Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{b}$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{3}(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{3}1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(\\frac{1}{b},0)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(\\frac{1}{3},0)$ lies on the curve. Hence\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3},0)=(\\frac{1}{b},0)\\,\\Rightarrow \\,b=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{3}(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{3}(3x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{3}(3\\cdot 1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k{log}_{3}3 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb+k=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259831 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259831 13194139533864 true] [17592186259830 1110 #uuid "c66636a2-9208-5170-b91e-f795c24d0ed5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259831 1034 "\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259831 1111 #uuid "6382687d-7aa3-5933-9ee6-b7bb4a90620f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259833 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1089 "q-98153" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/60d178be10126417bfbfad4e7a316d43ad1fd94ca19ea72adae929500a7f13c2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=k\\operatorname{ln}(x)+c.$ Find the value of $k+c.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1113 #uuid "f292f735-dfaf-5103-802f-50b77b9a6c31" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259832 1145 "Substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k\\operatorname{ln}(1)+c \\\\  & =0+c \\\\  & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(1,c)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(1,-2)$ lies on the curve. Hence,\n\n$$\n(1,-2)=(1,c)\\,\\Rightarrow \\,c=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(e,2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\operatorname{ln}(x)+c & =y \\\\ k\\operatorname{ln}(e)-2 & =2 \\\\ k-2 & =2 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nk+c=4-2=2.\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259834 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259834 13194139533864 true] [17592186259833 1110 #uuid "289b11ae-f133-544e-a3cc-747a26d8b1cc" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259834 1034 "2" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259834 1111 #uuid "918bdc14-d8d0-512b-9b65-820ae6d0ad5c" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259836 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1089 "q-9908" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1100 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1113 #uuid "c1aeb34a-99a8-5372-b85e-e163f31127c6" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259835 1145 "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x$ and $v(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-{sin}^{2}x-{cos}^{2}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-({sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since ${sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-1)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259837 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259837 13194139533864 true] [17592186259836 1110 #uuid "150d451a-00ce-5a55-8435-8e6fa936af48" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259837 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259837 1111 #uuid "8d4fd620-0900-55af-8837-e928f5f4ee8b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259839 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1089 "q-99705" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1100 "Evaluate $\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}(100)}-\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1113 #uuid "2160c283-c3ec-5a1f-9e6b-bbbe6f28e2b8" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259838 1145 "First, we rewrite $100$ and $1\\,000$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 1\\,000 & ={10}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}(100)}-\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000)=\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})}-\\operatorname{log}({10}^{3})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=2$ and $x=3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})}-\\operatorname{log}({10}^{3})=\\frac{7}{2}-3=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259840 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259840 13194139533864 true] [17592186259839 1110 #uuid "1dc46136-de90-5e72-8301-b5f5942256c9" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259840 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259840 1111 #uuid "a2ed6d89-2369-535d-945f-1639d7ec8515" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259842 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1089 "q-99719" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1100 "$\\operatorname{log}(0.000\\,01)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1113 #uuid "cd2e08d3-b64d-5c1f-866d-cc3f34b11473" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259841 1145 "First, we rewrite $0.000\\,01$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n0.000\\,01={10}^{-5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(0.000\\,01)=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-5})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-5.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-5})=-5\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259843 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259843 13194139533864 true] [17592186259842 1110 #uuid "e2cbaf4a-f7a5-5f3a-951b-c7f20d653587" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259843 1034 "-5" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259843 1111 #uuid "68bc143c-0ae4-5757-9301-c1143621dc0f" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259845 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1089 "q-99720" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1100 "$\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1113 #uuid "4c27c663-c4ba-5f94-a868-bd140e031303" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259844 1145 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}=\\frac{1}{{10}^{1/4}}={10}^{-1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259846 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259846 13194139533864 true] [17592186259845 1110 #uuid "538406e0-44fb-5ffa-a87e-0c0b8a80f33e" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259846 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259846 1111 #uuid "fddb9305-d180-552f-8764-a6de81b573b3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259848 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1089 "q-99782" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1100 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1113 #uuid "4794869c-06a9-5c29-93c1-d60c4f9c5809" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259847 1145 "We substitute $x=-1$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5} & =\\sqrt[3]{4(-1)+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259849 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259849 13194139533864 true] [17592186259848 1110 #uuid "85f844fb-7e4e-5a95-9c72-fb00470fcfd3" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259849 1034 "1" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259849 1111 #uuid "50270b86-8bb2-51d5-82d9-8fdc05982042" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259851 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1089 "q-9983" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1100 "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $10-2+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{25}+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1113 #uuid "e2fe4fd1-88fd-50df-b7b9-458bd013b3aa" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259850 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-2}{10}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1-(-\\frac{1}{5}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1+\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(\\frac{6}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259852 13194139533864 true] [17592186259851 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259851 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259851 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259852 13194139533864 true] [17592186259851 1110 #uuid "1921c358-4dd0-59c7-bde4-fa7983f50160" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259852 1034 "\\frac{25}{3}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259852 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259852 1111 #uuid "f02455f8-7263-5aee-b6ca-44c762028d6b" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259854 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1089 "q-99877" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4317726b2c9fbe22707377e1c713e8e8271ae95f0d818ac658da02cc81d64202.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1113 #uuid "ea0c4278-e87d-566a-b51b-71d40367ec17" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259853 1145 "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))={lim}_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2.$ \nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{3}-5(2) \\\\  & =0-10 \\\\  & =-10\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259854 1000 #edb/ref 17592186259855 13194139533864 true] [17592186259854 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533864 true] [17592186259854 1041 "answer" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259854 1104 #edb/ref 17592186259855 13194139533864 true] [17592186259854 1110 #uuid "26a5bf6a-c62a-5cc2-9f6b-4f19779fd5b4" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259855 1034 "-10" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533864 true] [17592186259855 1111 #uuid "c7a975cc-631d-5d9f-a650-71675ddb4917" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259857 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1017 #edb/ref 17592186259859 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1089 "q-99965" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRight limit at 0: {{right-limit-at-0}}\n\nRight limit at 1: {{right-limit-at-1}}\n\nIf a requested limit does not exist, enter \"Does not exist\"." 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1113 #uuid "135d935c-2bd7-5669-bc52-6f55c09e6c2a" 13194139533864 true] [17592186259856 1145 "The right-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,1).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nA